window.RV_LOADED['ib-psychology-hl'] = {"name":"IB Psychology HL","topics":[{"name":"Biological Approaches to Human Behaviour","subtopics":[{"name":"Animal Research & Human Behaviour","questions":[{"ref":"PS0622","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0622\n\nEvaluate the role of animal research in understanding human behaviour.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The question used the command term *evaluate*, therefore, the response should identify, explain, and weigh the strengths and limitations of the role of animal research in understanding behaviour by looking at one behaviour. The response also needs to include an explanation of how the research is effective in understanding behaviour.\n\n**1 mark** if the question was briefly described in less detail or if the question was restated. *(e.g. This essay will evaluate the role of animal research in understanding human behaviour.) No marks are awarded for this criterion if the answer showed no clear focus on the command term and the topic in the question.*\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant \\& appropriate terminology is explained in detail *(e.g. animal models, biological approach, neuroplasticity, neurotransmission  etc)*. Because the question asks to evaluate the role of animal research, explanation on why animals are used for research along with the references in how it aids in understanding specific human behaviour is required to be given to score full marks in this criterion.\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors *(e.g. The role of animal research is explained with their value in understanding human behaviour but details relevant to why it should be used is not clearly outlined).*\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited *(e.g. the role of animal research is not properly outlined and only shows a minimal understanding of its value on understanding behaviour).* No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer showed no explanation of relevant terminology.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies are explained (e.g., aim, procedures, results \\& conclusion) and used to support the answer or the arguments stated. Studies to be used are but not limited to:\n\n-    Neurotransmission: Rogers and Kesner (2003)\n\n-    Neuroplasticity: Rosenzweig et.al (1972)\n\n-    Aggressive behaviour: Cases et.al (1995)\n\n-    Memory: Meaney et.al (1988)\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. *(e.g the aim, procedure, results \\& conclusions of the study was not clearly presented)*\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. *(e.g the study was not linked to the evaluation points mentioned) No marks will be awarded for this criterion if no relevant study was explained in detail.*\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed evaluation points *(e.g around 2-3 strengths and limitations)* of the value of using animal models in research to understand human behaviour as well as ethics related to the research chosen. *(e.g. justifying the use of animals in research, safety and protection of the animals used, practical application to animal findings etc)*\n\n**3-4 marks** if the evaluation points *(e.g. around 1-2 strengths \\& limitations)* are present and clearly explained but are not sustained. Or if the answer focused on explaining only the strengths or only the limitations.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation or discussion points are superficial and mainly descriptive *(e.g. if it only evaluates the study briefly and not the topic overall, or if the evaluation points were only stated or described but not linked back to the study) No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer showed no explanation of relevant critical thinking and/or evaluation points.*\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is an organised \\& coherent explanation of the concept and an evaluation of one or more relevant studies *(see Criterion C). (e.g. first paragraph introduces the focus on the question and related terminology, the succeeding paragraphs talk about the studies to be evaluated as well as the actual evaluation of one or more research, and the closing paragraphs or statements link the arguments back to the question)*\n\n**1 mark** if the organisation and clarity were not sustained or if the answer is incomplete or unfinished.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \n\nAnimal research has contributed greatly to our understanding of human diseases and behaviour. One reason animal models are used in human research is because animal physiology has the potential to mimic the characteristics or aspects of the biological process studied. Therefore, animal research may provide some insight into human behaviour.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Animal research has been utilised in psychology to study a wide range of behaviours such as memory, aggression, neuroplasticity to name a few. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nCertain species of animals are used more frequently than others when it comes to research such as mice, monkeys etc. This is due to the high percentage of similarity in genes and behaviour process that can be observed between these animals and humans.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ However, there are various considerations that need to be accounted before animal research can be undertaken such as the cost-benefit analysis that justifies the use of animals in research by measuring the possible benefit gained from the research and comparing it to the inevitable harm or loss that may come to the animal.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Research on animals is especially instrumental in understanding biological aspects of behaviour such as the effect of neurotransmitters on behaviour which will be demonstrated through the study of Rogers and Kesner (2003) and neuroplasticity as seen in the study by Rosenzweig (1972).$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ \n\nRogers and Kesner wanted to investigate the role of acetylcholine on the formation of spatial memories.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ They gathered 30 rats in a lab and acclimatized them to a Hebb-William maze by placing food. The goal of the experiment was to test the rat's memory when injected with scopolamine, a drug that blocks the acetylcholine receptors in the brain. Once the rats were familiar with the maze, one group was injected with scopolamine and the other group received a placebo shot. Their behaviour was measured by the number of mistakes they made during the first five trials of the day in reaching the food signifying the formation of spatial memories.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Results showed that the experimental group took longer and made more mistakes in retrieving the food. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThese results showed that acetylcholine plays a significant role in the creation of spatial memories as in its absence the rats took longer to find the food the food that was placed. One of the most significant strengths of this study lies in its potential implications in the development of drugs that could help in the treatment of patients with memory diseases such as Alzheimer's.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Additionally, this experiment was conducted in highly controlled conditions with the control placebo group helping to establish a strong cause and effect relationship. However, as with all animal research a weakness is the generalization of results from animals to humans.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Though, there exists a high level of similarity between rats and humans, it cannot be said that human physiology would react in the same way as rats and not lead to other complications arising from the excessive acetylcholine given to treat memory issues. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nThe second behaviour studied was the effect of environment stimulation on brain changes known as neuroplasticity. Rosenzweig (1972) used rats to study the effect of environmental stimuli on the changes in the brain.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The researcher used three rats from a litter and placed them either in an impoverished condition (IC) with little to no environmental stimuli like the presence of other rats or toys, maze etc. or he placed them in an enriched condition (EC) with the other rats and having access to toys and maze.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Both the groups had equal access to food and water. The rats spent 30 - 60 days before being killed to study the effect of the condition on the physiology of their brain. Results showed that the rats in the EC had an increased thickness in the cortex and had higher activity of the neurons in the cerebral cortex associated with learning and memory. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe results of the study showed how the stimuli present in the environment had a direct impact on the physiological changes in the brain indicating that our environment plays a significant role in learning and memory. This gives further evidence to the theory that with proper care and nurture, humans can increase their ability and talents. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ A strength of the study is the possible application in the field of early childhood education. Furthermore, the results were measured through an autopsy indicating high construct validity of the results. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ However, similar to most animal research the issue of theoretical generalization is present as the study was done on rats to generalize the results to the human population may not be appropriate in spite of the similarity. Also, as the enriched condition included various aspects such as social stimulation, physical activity, and toys, it is not clear which of the above has the most impact on learning and memory. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nAs seen from both the animal research cited above the implications from the research conducted are massive and can potentially impact many areas of education and drug development. While further research is required before definitive steps can be taken, it has opened up avenues of discovery for the researchers. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ On the other hand, animal research needs to be undertaken with proper justification and cost benefit analysis to avoid unnecessary harm to animals. While in the Rogers and Kesner study, there was no long-term harm to the animals, the rats were killed in the  Rosenzweig study showing the importance of cost benefit analysis as each case needs to be considered individually.  $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$Researchers must ensure they are following all regulations when considering animals for research such as the Animal Welfare Act signed in 1966 in US regulates the treatment of animals in research to abide by the ethical guidelines of research. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nIn conclusion, animal research has played a significant role in helping researchers understand various biological aspects of human behaviour. While research in this field has far reaching implications, a cost benefit analysis must be conducted to ensure that researchers are following all ethical guidelines connected with this area of research to ensure there all research participants whether human or animals are receiving equitable protection. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nWord count: 986\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nRogers, J., \\& Kesner, R. P. (2003). Cholinergic modulation of the hippocampus during encoding and retrieval. Neurobiology of Learning and Memory, 80(3), 332-342. https://doi.org/10.1016/s1074-7427(03)00063-7 \n\nRosenzweig, M. R., Bennett, E. L., \\& Diamond, M. C. (1972). Brain changes in response to experience. Scientific American, 226(2), 22-29. https://doi.org/10.1038/scientificamerican0272-22"},{"ref":"PS0643","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0643\n\nDiscuss one or more ethical considerations in animal research.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question iss explained. The question uses the command term discuss, paired with the ethical considerations question stem; therefore, the response should present a balanced discussion regarding the use of one or more ethical considerations (*e.g., the right to withdraw, anonymity/confidentiality, minimisation of harm*) when producing knowledge regarding the topic.\n\n**1 mark** if the question is not accurately explained, has limited explanation,  is simply restated, or is misinterpreted, though still related to ethical considerations (*e.g., This essay will evaluate ethical considerations in the study of topics in the biological approach; or the candidate writes in their introduction that they will discuss ethical considerations but either does not define them at any point or discusses different ethical considerations than were described in the introduction*). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer is not relevant to the question.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant and appropriate knowledge and terminology related to ethical considerations in the context of animal research are explained in detail without errors and are explicitly linked to the studies in Criterion C (*e.g., reduction, refinement, replacement, transgenic mice, autopsy, etc.*).\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors but is lacking in some detail or shows only knowledge about research methods, such as a brief explanation of the topic, with little focus on evaluation of study (*e.g., response primarily demonstrates sustained knowledge of animal research in neglect of the ethical considerations*).\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited or superficial in nature (*e.g., incorrectly describing confidentiality*) and if the answer does not demonstrate knowledge and understanding of animal research. No marks will be awarded for this criterion if no relevant terminology is explained.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments stated, and a specific link is made between the findings of the study(ies) and the question. Relevant details, including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion, from one or more studies, should be included. Studies that could be referred to include, but are not limited to:\n- An investigation on the use of transgenic mice to study MAOA: Cases et al. (1995)\n- A study on the role of acetylcholine and spatial navigation: Rogers & Kesner (2003)\n- A study on cortisol and long-term health outcomes of stress: Sapolsky (1990, 2005)\n- A study investigating the role of glucocorticoids on memory: Meaney et al. (1988)\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study is explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors (*e.g., the aim, procedure, results, conclusions of the study are not clearly presented, or there are some errors with regard to the relevance of the findings or the details of the methodology*).\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research is used with insufficient detail, errors, or superficial links to the question (*e.g., the study was not linked to the discussion of ethical considerations*). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer does not include any relevant studies.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer shows relevant, consistent, and well-developed critical thinkingpoints. Critical thinking points could include a combination of the following: strengths and limitations of the research methods used in supporting research studies and their conclusions, strengths and limitations of the relevant theories, potential applications of the findings, or an acknowledgment of complexities and alternative arguments. At least 2-3 discussion or critical thinking points are explained regarding ethical considerations.\n\n**3-4 marks**  if relevant critical thinking points are presented (as above), but they are not always fully developed. They may not be directly linked to the specific study or theory, or they may be limited in number. For example, a response might state that the study is low in internal validity without explaining what this means in reference to the specific study. At least 1-2 discussion or critical thinking points are explained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if evaluation points are superficial or contain inaccuracies (*e.g., evaluation points only stated but not explained properly*). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer does not contain relevant critical thinking points, discussion, or evaluation.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the essay has an organised structure and coherent explanation of one or more ethical considerations, including a clear introduction (which outlines the focus of the essay), body paragraphs (which outline the relevant study or studies as described in Criterion C; followed by evaluation of those studies), and concluding paragraphs or statements (which link back to the question).\n\n**1 mark** if organisation and clarity are not sustained throughout the answer (*e.g. unfinished response, lack of a clear introduction, lack of body paragraphs, and/or lack of a conclusion; a structure that is not logical or easy to follow; or incomplete description of key terms and studies*). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the response does not meet any of the standards above.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nAnimal research is frequently proposed as an ethical alternative to human research. However, there are still significant ethical concerns in studies involving living creatures. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ Supporters of animal research say that there is a utilitarian benefit: so long as research leads to knowledge or solutions that could help people, we should minimize harm as much as possible but accept that it may occur. Those who reject this argument say this is fundamentally an anthropocentric—or human-centric—approach. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Animals are living things that can feel pain and experience social bonds. Without the ability to gain animal consent, proponents of this argument say we should not use them in research to benefit humans. As a form of compromise for these arguments, psychological research involving animals is governed by principles referred to as the three Rs: replacement, reduction, and refinement. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nOne reason animals are desirable for research is because they have shorter lifespans and can demonstrate the effects of environmental factors sooner than humans. Unlike with humans, we can also exert total control over animals' environments. One example of a study where this was used to understand the impact of stress and adverse childhood experiences is Meaney et al. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Meaney and his team weaned a litter of rats from the same mother and randomly allocated them to 2 conditions. In 1, researchers groomed them with a brush. In the other, they were not groomed. When the rats reached 2 years of age, they were put into a pool of milky water, making it difficult to see a platform just below the surface of the water. The groomed mice took a more direct route to the platform, whereas the ungroomed mice looped back on their own path and had a less systematic approach to finding the platform. Researchers hypothesized that this was due to their higher levels of glucocorticoids, or stress hormones. Without grooming, they failed to express genes that helped regulate stress. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Further, their overall greater exposure to cortisol led to overstimulation and, eventually, hippocampal cell death. This explains the poor maze completion of the mice in this condition, as the hippocampus in mice also dictates spatial navigation. When the mice were euthanized and autopsied, Meaney and his team were able to confirm the reduced volume of the hippocampus. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nCases et al. genetically engineered knockout mice to study the potential role of the MAOA gene in aggressive behaviour. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Knockout mice have a gene of interest deleted, and researchers observe their behaviour to infer the role of that gene. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$These studies can yield important findings in genetics, but the lifespan and quality of life of the animals can be severely reduced. In this study, the underproduction of MAOA led the mice to have higher-than-normal average levels of serotonin and norepinephrine. After 11 days, the mice began to display violent shaking, aggressive behaviour when handled, and biting. Using a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$\nresident-intruder$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$\nprotocol, researchers introduced a control mouse into the cage with one of the MAOA knockout mice. The knockout mice would display heightened aggressive behaviour, even when introduced to control mice of the opposite sex. After being euthanised, the mice were autopsied so neurotransmitter levels could be measured. Serotonin levels were six to nine times higher in the knockout mice than in the control mice. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The study helped researchers rule out the role of social learning or environmental factors in being a primary cause of aggressive behaviour in low MAOA mice and humans. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nBoth studies were conducted relatively early in the field, and so options for replacement were developed after understanding their initial findings. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Replacement asks researchers to find alternate models or approaches to spare animals from experimentation or harm. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$This could involve using computer models to simulate the impact or interaction of multiple genetic mutations, neural network changes, or hormonal changes. However, such models cannot be developed without any direct experimentation. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Modern advancements also allow for experimentation on cell cultures. To have the most ethical study possible today, both Cases and Meaney would be expected to minimise the number of mice involved to yield as much data as possible. This is the principle of reduction. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Sharing their complete datasets after their research could also reduce the number of mice involved in similar studies, as other teams could run their own analyses using the original data. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$  Finally, refinement asks the researchers to consider how they can improve their procedures to reduce pain and provide better living conditions and care for the animals involved. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Variables unrelated to the study should be maximized. For example, in both Meaney and Cases, the mice should have been provided clean and spacious cages and a high-quality diet as control variables across all conditions. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Further, the process for euthanization should be as painless as possible.\n\nUltimately, cost-benefit analyses should be performed before and after animal studies to assess whether similar veins of research should be carried out. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$  Both Meaney and Cases contributed substantial information that guided our collective understanding of the relationship between genes, environment, and behaviour. Despite this, it is impossible to know the results of a study ahead of time. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ We did not understand the relevance to epigenetics that Meaney’s study had until several years after the study was carried out. Further, the cost-benefit analysis of animal research is complicated by the relative social complexity of humans. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ In studies of behaviour that are moderated by social relationships (like aggression), it is challenging to determine the extent to which findings from animal research also apply to humans. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Striving to improve our ability to replace, refine, and reduce animal use in research is likely the best way to mitigate concerns while also carrying out research that cannot be done on humans. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nWord count: 939\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\nCases, O., Seif, I., Grimsby, J., Gaspar, P., Chen, K., Pournin, S., Et, A. (1995). Aggressive behavior and altered amounts of brain serotonin and norepinephrine in mice lacking MAOA. Science, 268(5218), 1763-1766.\n\nMeaney, M. J., Aitken, D. H., van Berkel, C., Bhatnagar, S., $\\&$ Sapolsky, R. M. (1988). Effect of neonatal handling on age-related impairments associated with the hippocampus. Science (New York, N.Y.), 239(4841 Pt 1), 766-768. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.3340858"},{"ref":"PS0778","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0778\n\nIdentify the two groups in Meaney et al.'s (1988) study and explain their treatment conditions. \n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nThe treatment group consisted of rats handled daily by researchers from birth to weaning, with 15 minutes of grooming to simulate maternal care. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) The control group was taken away from their mothers but not handled by the researchers, serving as the baseline condition. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\n\n- 1 mark for describing the treatment groups and their specific handling.\n\n- 1 mark for describing the control group and their lack of handling.\n\n- Marks should be awarded for any similarly worded explanations to a maximum of 2 marks.\n\nReference/s:\\\n\nMeaney, M. J., Aitken, D. H., van Berkel, C., Bhatnagar, S., & Sapolsky, R. M. (1988). Effect of neonatal handling on age-related impairments associated with the hippocampus. \n\nScience, 239(4841), 766-768. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.3340858"},{"ref":"PS0779","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0779\n\nWhat were the main findings of Meaney et al.'s (1988) study on glucocorticoids and memory, and what do these findings suggest about the effects of early-life stress?\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nThe study found that rats exposed to high levels of glucocorticoids (stress hormones) in early life showed hippocampal cell loss and memory deficits in old age. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Rats in the treatment group, who received daily handling and grooming, had fewer cognitive impairments and less hippocampal damage. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) These results suggest that early-life stress, marked by high glucocorticoid exposure, can lead to long-term cognitive problems by accelerating brain cell loss, particularly in the hippocampus, a region critical for memory. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\n\n- 1 mark for describing the memory and brain structure results.\n\n- 1 mark for explaining the correlation between early-life stress and cognitive deficits.\n\n- 1 mark for linking the findings to long-term impacts on memory and brain function.\n\n- Marks should be awarded for any similarly worded explanations of the findings to a maximum of 3 marks.\n\nReference/s:\\\n\nMeaney, M. J., Aitken, D. H., van Berkel, C., Bhatnagar, S., & Sapolsky, R. M. (1988). Effect of neonatal handling on age-related impairments associated with the hippocampus. \n\nScience, 239(4841), 766-768. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.3340858"},{"ref":"PS0780","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0780\n\nMeaney et al.'s (1988) study on the role of stress hormones in memory provides valuable insights into how early-life stress can affect long-term cognitive functioning. The study emphasised the role of glucocorticoids in brain function. It introduced the concept of epigenetics, showing how early environmental factors, like maternal care, can influence gene expression and affect cognitive health later in life.\n\nDiscuss two strengths and two limitations of Meaney et al.'s (1988) study. \n\n[8 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n**Strengths may include but are not limited to the following:**\n\n**Reliability**\\\n\nThe study is highly reliable due to its use of a standardised procedure. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) The treatment group of rats was groomed in a consistent and controlled manner, which allows for replication of the experiment in other settings and increases the trustworthiness of the findings. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Applications**\\\n\nThe findings have practical applications in understanding the impact of stress on cognitive health. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) The study shows that stress hormones like cortisol can lead to hippocampal cell death, which has implications for disorders like Alzheimer's disease, suggesting that early stress management could help in cognitive health prevention. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Contribution to epigenetics**\\\n\nThe study made an important contribution to the field of epigenetics. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Although the concept of epigenetics was not fully understood then, the study highlighted how environmental factors (such as maternal grooming) can influence gene expression and affect the long-term stress response, which has influenced subsequent research in the field of epigenetics. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Limitations may include but are not limited to the following:**\n\n**Lack of ecological validity**\\\n\nThe study lacks ecological validity. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) The artificial nature of the laboratory setting, where rats were separated from their mothers and subjected to grooming by researchers, does not mimic real-world stressors, reducing the applicability of the findings to natural environments. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Ethical concerns**\\\n\nThe study raises ethical concerns as the rats were subjected to significant stress (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) (being separated from their mothers) and ultimately killed to measure brain function, which raises questions about the ethics of using animals in research and whether the benefits of the findings justify the harm caused. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Generalisability to humans**\\\n\nThe study's results are difficult to generalise to humans. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Rats have a different brain structure and response to stress than humans. Although the study provides insight into the effects of stress, its findings may not directly apply to human cognitive functions and disorders like Alzheimer's. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Longitudinal study**\\\n\nThe study has limitations in assessing long-term effects over the rats' entire lifespan. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nAlthough the rats were tested at two years of age, which is considered older in rat years, it is unclear if the study truly captured the full lifespan of the rats and the effects of early-life stress. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\n\n- 1 mark for identifying the strength or weakness. (e.g. reliability, generalisability, ethical concerns, ecological validity) \n\n- 1 mark for explaining and linking the strength or weakness to the study's design or findings.\n\n- Marks should be awarded for any similarly worded strength/weakness and explanation  to a maximum of 8 marks.\n\nReference/s:\\\n\nMeaney, M. J., Aitken, D. H., van Berkel, C., Bhatnagar, S., & Sapolsky, R. M. (1988). Effect of neonatal handling on age-related impairments associated with the hippocampus. \n\nScience, 239(4841), 766-768. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.3340858"},{"ref":"PS0782","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0782\n\nDescribe the procedure of Rogers & Kesner's (2003) study on Acetylcholine and memory.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nRogers & Kesner (2003) had 30 rats learn a maze with food placed in one corner. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) The rats were then randomly assigned to receive either scopolamine (which blocks acetylcholine receptors) or a saline solution (placebo) ten minutes before running the maze. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) They measured memory by counting the number of errors in the first and last five trials of Day 1 for encoding and the first five trials of Day 2 for retrieval. Rats who received scopolamine took longer and made more errors in learning the maze. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\n\n- 1 mark for explaining the maze learning procedure and the rat groups.\n\n- 1 mark for describing the injections and their purpose.\n\n- 1 mark for explaining the memory assessment and findings.\n\n- Marks should be awarded for any similarly worded explanations to a maximum of 3 marks.\n\nReference/s:\\\n\nRogers, J. L., & Kesner, R. P. (2003). Cholinergic modulation of the hippocampus during encoding and retrieval. Neurobiology of Learning and Memory, 80(3), 332-342\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/s1074-7427(03)00063-7"},{"ref":"PS0783","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0783\n\nHow did Rogers & Kesner (2003) ensure that confounding variables did not influence the results of their study?\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nRogers & Kesner controlled for confounding variables by using a placebo group (saline injection) to rule out the possibility that the injection itself was responsible for changes in memory. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Additionally, the injections were made directly into the hippocampus, ensuring that the effects were related to acetylcholine and not other factors. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nMarking Guidance:\n\n- 1 mark for mentioning the placebo condition.\n\n- 1 mark for explaining how the injection into the hippocampus controls for confounding variables.\n\n- Marks should be awarded for any similarly worded explanations to a maximum of 3 marks.\n\nReference/s:\\\n\nRogers, J. L., & Kesner, R. P. (2003). Cholinergic modulation of the hippocampus during encoding and retrieval. Neurobiology of Learning and Memory, 80(3), 332-342. \n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/s1074-7427(03)00063-7"},{"ref":"PS0784","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0784\n\nRogers & Kesner (2003) studied the role of Acetylcholine in memory consolidation. They found that rats injected with Scopolamine, which blocks Acetylcholine, took longer and made more errors in learning a maze. However, Scopolamine did not affect memory retrieval. These results suggest that Acetylcholine is important for encoding new memories but not for retrieving them.\n\nDiscuss two strengths and two limitations of Roger & Kesner's (2003) study. \n\n[8 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n**Strengths may include but are not limited to the following:**\n\n**High reliability**\\\n\nThe study used a controlled experimental design, including a placebo group, allowing for replication and consistency in results. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This means that the study's procedure can be repeated in other labs. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Internal validity (cause-and-effect relationship)**\\\n\nThe use of a controlled environment and random assignment of rats to the treatment and control groups help ensure that the manipulation of acetylcholine levels caused the observed effects on memory and not other confounding variables. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This enhances the internal validity of the study, making the results more convincing in establishing a direct cause-and-effect relationship between acetylcholine and memory. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Applicability (Alzheimer's Disease Research)**\\\n\nThe findings are relevant to real-world issues, particularly in understanding memory impairments like those seen in Alzheimer's disease, where acetylcholine levels are known to be low. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This means the study could have practical applications in future treatments or interventions for cognitive impairments, making it more applicable to human health. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**High Control**\\\n\nThe study controlled for extraneous variables, such as the rats' prior experiences and environments, allowing for a clearer understanding of the role of acetylcholine. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This control increases the generalizability of the findings to other experiments testing the effects of acetylcholine on memory. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Limitations may include but are not limited to the following:**\n\n**Ecological validity (artificial task)**\\\n\nThe memory task, where rats had to swim to find a platform, is artificial and does not closely resemble real-life memory tasks humans encounter. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This reduces the ecological validity of the study, meaning the findings may not translate well to everyday memory processes in humans. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Ethical concerns (use of animals)**\\\n\nThe study involved using rats, which were subjected to separation from their mothers, handled by researchers, and euthanasia at the end of the experiment. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) These procedures raise ethical concerns about the well-being of the animals, which is a critical consideration in animal research. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n**Lack of generalisability to humans**\\\n\nAlthough rats are often used as animal models for human behaviour, there are significant differences between the physiology of rats and humans. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This limits the generalisability of the findings, as it is uncertain whether the role of acetylcholine in rats will apply in the same way to humans, especially in the context of complex human memory. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Reductionist approach**\\\n\nThe study only looked at acetylcholine and its impact on spatial memory, ignoring other factors that could influence memory, such as emotion, other neurotransmitters, or environmental influences. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This reductionist approach oversimplifies memory processes and does not provide a comprehensive understanding of how memory works in a complex, real-world context. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\n\n- The answer should clearly state two strengths and two weaknesses\n\n- 1 mark for identifying the strength/weakness (e.g. reliability, validity, generalisability and ethical concerns) \n\n- 1 mark for a clear explanation of the strength/weakness\n\n- Marks should be awarded for any similarly worded strength/weakness to a maximum of 8 marks.\n\nReference/s:\\\n\nRogers, J. L., & Kesner, R. P. (2003). Cholinergic modulation of the hippocampus during encoding and retrieval. Neurobiology of Learning and Memory, 80(3), 332-342. \n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/s1074-7427(03)00063-7"},{"ref":"PS0809","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0809\n\nThe use of animal research must be justified, meaning that the potential benefits of the research must outweigh the harm caused to the animals. Researchers must demonstrate that the knowledge gained from the study cannot be obtained without using animals. \n\nIdentify and briefly explain three ethical guidelines that researchers must follow when conducting animal research. \n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n**Replacement**\\\n\nResearchers must consider alternatives to animal testing whenever possible, such as using in vitro (cell culture) or computational models. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) If animals are used, alternatives should be prioritized to reduce the number of animals involved. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Refinement**\\\n\nResearchers should refine their experimental procedures (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) to reduce the suffering and distress animals experience. This includes providing proper care and minimising any distress caused by procedures. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Reduction**\\\n\nReducing the number of animals used minimizes harm by ensuring only the essential sample size is used. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This helps avoid unnecessary suffering while still providing valid results, balancing ethics with scientific rigor. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Ethical Review Process**\\\n\nAny research involving animals must be approved by an ethics committee (e.g., an Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee). (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This committee assesses the scientific value and ethical aspects of the research to ensure compliance with ethical standards. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\n\n- 1 mark for identifying the ethical guideline (e.g. replacement, refinement, reduction, ethical review).\n\n- 1 mark for explaining why the researcher must follow the guidelines (e.g. reduce animal suffering, ensuring proper care is maintained).\n\n- Marks should be awarded for any similarly worded answer to a maximum of 6 marks."},{"ref":"PS0810","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0810\n\nExplain two advantages of using animals with shorter lifespans in research on the effects of early-life stress.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\n**Observe the long term effects**\\\n\nFirst, animals with shorter lifespans allow researchers to observe the long-term effects of early-life stress over a much shorter period of time, which is crucial when studying developmental or aging-related changes. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) For example, researchers can track the effects of childhood stress on aging rats and see how it impacts their cognitive function and physical health as they grow older. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n**Genetic inheritance**\\\n\nSecond, the use of animals that breed quickly enables researchers to study the genetic transmission of stress effects across generations, providing insights into how environmental factors influence genetics. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This allows for a better understanding of the hereditary and biological factors that might contribute to stress-related disorders in humans. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\n\n- 1 mark for identifying the advantage of using animals (e.g. long term effects, genetic inheritance). \n\n- 1 mark for explaining how this advantage helps in research on early-life stress (e.g. allows researchers to track the effects of childhood stress).\n\n- Marks will also be awarded for any similarly worded explanations to a maximum of 4 marks."},{"ref":"PS0811","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0811\n\nFriedman (2012)  and his team at the Jackson Laboratory wanted to find the signal that controls food intake and body weight. In their 1994 study (Zhang et al.), they discovered the hormone leptin by studying the OB mouse, which overeats and becomes obese due to a genetic mutation. This animal study was groundbreaking because it identified leptin as a key hormone in regulating hunger and body weight, providing new insights into obesity and metabolism.\n\nDiscuss the positive and negative impacts of using animals in this research. \n\n[8 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n**Positive factors:**\n\n**Scientific discovery**\\\n\nFriedman's research on leptin led to the discovery of a hormone that plays a key role in regulating hunger and satiety. This has profound implications for understanding obesity and could inform treatments for eating disorders and obesity. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This discovery is a significant contribution to biological and medical science, helping us understand how the brain signals satiety, which could potentially lead to therapies for people with obesity. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Limited number of animals used**\\\n\nThe research used only two mice, adhering to the principle of \"reduction,\" one of the \"3Rs\" of animal research, which encourages minimizing the number of animals used in studies. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This means the study was ethically mindful of the number of animals used, which can be seen as a positive aspect of the research. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Negative factors:**\\\n\nPhysical harm to the animals\n\nThe study involved severe procedures, including sewing the two mice together to join their circulation systems. This caused physical harm to the animals and likely resulted in pain and distress. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) According to ethical guidelines, animal research should minimise harm, but this study clearly violated that principle by subjecting the mice to unnecessary and extreme physical stress. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Lack of refinement**\\\n\nThe procedure could have been refined to reduce harm. For example, using less invasive techniques or having veterinary oversight to monitor pain and stress levels could have improved the ethical standards of the study. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) The procedure could have been refined further to ensure the mice experienced less trauma, which would have aligned better with the ethical guidelines for refinement. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\n\n- 1 mark for identifying the positive or negative impact of using animals (e.g. scientific discovery, minimum animals used, physical harm). \n\n- 1 mark for an explanation of the impact (e.g. the findings were significant to understanding obesity).\n\n- Marks will also be awarded for any similarly worded explanations to a maximum of 8 marks. \n\nReference/s:\\\n\nZhang, Y., Proenca, R., Maffel, M., Barone, M., Leopold, L., & Friedman, J. M. (1994).                     \n\nPositional cloning of the mouse obese gene and its human homologue. Nature, 372(6505), 425-432. https://doi.org/10.1038/372425a0"},{"ref":"PS0844","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0844\n\nMice and rats constitute about 95% of all animals used in laboratories, with mice being the most frequently used species in research. There are several reasons for their widespread use in psychological research. \n\nExplain two reasons why mice are predominantly used in research. \n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n**Genetic Similarity to Humans**\\\n\nMice share many genetic, physiological, and behavioral similarities with humans. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This makes them ideal models for studying human diseases, brain functions, and psychological processes, such as memory and stress. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n**Small Size and Manageability**\\\n\nMice are small and easy to handle in a lab setting. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Their size makes them less expensive to house and care for, and they require less space compared to larger animals. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n**Short Lifespan and Rapid Reproduction**\\\n\nMice have short lifespans (2-3 years) and reproduce quickly. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This allows researchers to observe changes across generations in a short period, making them useful for studying aging, development, and the long-term effects of stress or treatment. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Established research models**\\\n\nMice are widely studied, with extensive knowledge of their biology. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This makes it easier to design experiments, manipulate genetics, and compare results across studies, making them versatile for researching various psychological and medical conditions. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n**Ethical and practical considerations**\\\n\nMice are considered ethically acceptable for many types of research. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) They are seen as having lower moral status compared to higher-order animals like primates, making their use more widely accepted in research, though ethical guidelines still apply. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n**Availability and cost**\\\n\nMice are easy to obtain and inexpensive to maintain. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Their widespread availability in labs and low cost make them practical choices for a wide range of experiments. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\n\n- 1 mark for Identification of a clear reason (*e.g. genetic similarities, manageability, short lifespan*). \n\n- 1 mark justifying why mice are predominately used  for research (*e.g observe changes across generations, mice require less space, low cost and practicality*). \n\n- Marks should be awarded for any similarly worded answer to a maximum of 6 marks."},{"ref":"PS0933","marks":"22","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0933\n\nDiscuss the role of animal research in investigating hormones and/or pheromones and behaviour.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \n\nAnimal research plays a crucial role in the study of biological processes, including the influence of hormones on behaviour. A key justification for using animals in research is that many species share significant physiological similarities with humans, enabling researchers to draw meaningful insights into human behaviour from animal studies. Furthermore, animal research allows for controlled experiments and invasive procedures that may otherwise be unethical or impractical to conduct with human participants. This essay will discuss the role of animal research in understanding how hormones influence behaviour, focusing on a study conducted by Meaney et al. (1988) and Friedman's discovery of the hormone leptin with reference to research by Zhang et al. (1994). $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nThe aim of Meaney et al. (1988) was to investigate how stress hormones, called glucocorticoids, affect memory in rats. The researchers focused on how early maternal care, such as grooming, could influence how rats respond to stress later in life. The procedure included two groups of newborn rats, which included the treatment group and the control group. In the treatment group, the baby rats were separated from their mothers for 15 minutes each day over a period of three weeks. During this time, the researchers gently brushed them to imitate the natural grooming a mother rat would provide. This simulated care was meant to mimic the comforting touch a mother gives. In contrast, the control group was also separated from their mothers for 15 minutes each day, but did not receive any brushing or grooming from the researchers. As the rats aged, they were tested in a water maze to assess their memory. The task involved finding a hidden platform in a pool of milky water. The results showed that rats from the control group, who had not received grooming, had higher levels of stress hormones and performed worse in the memory test. They took longer, less direct routes to find the platform. These rats also showed damage in the hippocampus, a part of the brain important for memory. On the other hand, the treatment group had lower cortisol levels, healthier brain structure, and performed better in the maze. This suggests that early nurturing experiences can impact how stress-related genes are expressed, which in turn influences memory and behaviour. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nOne strength of using animals in this study was that it allowed for a highly controlled laboratory setting, where the procedure (such as the timing and method of grooming) was standardised. This level of control would not be possible with human participants because of ethical and practical concerns. Another strength is that rats share some physiological similarities with humans, especially in hormonal systems, meaning the study can offer useful insights into how early experiences might influence stress regulation and memory in humans. However, the artificial lab environment and the researcher-administered grooming do not reflect real-life conditions, where natural mother-infant interactions are very different. This makes it harder to apply the results to real-world human situations. In addition to this, rats do not have the same cognitive and emotional abilities as humans. Therefore, they may not respond the same way as humans in these situations.  $\\textcolor{#8c6188}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nFriedman's discovery of leptin, a hormone that helps control hunger and body weight, was made possible through animal research involving genetically modified mice. The aim of the study was to understand the biological causes of obesity and how the body signals when it has enough energy stored. The study focused on a special type of mouse called the ob/ob mouse, which is born with a mutation in the ob gene. This mutation means the mice cannot produce leptin, the hormone that normally tells the brain to stop eating. Because of this, ob/ob mice constantly feel hungry, overeat, and become severely obese, even when fed the same food as normal mice. To investigate further, Friedman used a method where two mice are surgically joined so they share a blood supply. When an ob/ob mouse was joined to a healthy mouse, the ob/ob mouse began to eat less and lose weight. This suggested that something in the normal mouse's blood, later identified as leptin, was missing in the obese mouse. To confirm this, researchers injected leptin into the ob/ob mice, and found that their appetite decreased, their activity increased, and they lost weight. The study showed that leptin is a key hormone involved in appetite regulation and in controlling eating behaviour. This was a major breakthrough in understanding the link between hormones and behaviour, and it provided a biological explanation for obesity. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe study has strong applicability, as it led to further research into how hormones influence eating behaviour. It also helped in gaining a deeper understanding of the biological factors behind obesity and laid the foundations for follow-up research with humans. However, the ecological validity of the study is limited. The conditions under which the mice were tested (such as surgical procedures and hormone injections) do not represent how eating behaviour works in everyday life, where psychological and social factors also play a role. This highlights a potential limitation of using animals in this area of research, as it restricts the ability to explore underlying influences on food choices through follow-up qualitative questions. $\\textcolor{#8c6188}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nBoth studies show how animal research can help uncover the role of hormones in behaviour, but there are important ethical and biological concerns. Animal studies often involve procedures that would be considered unacceptable in human research, such as surgical operations, hormone injections, or euthanasia. While such methods are sometimes necessary in animal research, they raise ethical concerns about animal welfare and whether the potential benefits to humans justify the harm to animals. There are also important differences between animals and humans. While hormones like cortisol and leptin function similarly across species, human behaviour is more complex and influenced by culture, emotion, and sociocultural factors. $\\textcolor{#8c6188}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nOverall, animal research continues to be a valuable tool in exploring the role of hormones in behaviour. Studies such as Meaney et al. (1988) and Friedman’s research on leptin show how hormones like cortisol and leptin affect memory, stress regulation, and eating behaviour. However, both studies are limited by the fact that there are important differences between animals and humans in terms of physiology and cognition. Other limitations include low ecological validity, ethical concerns, and issues with generalisability. Despite this, these studies have made significant contributions and discoveries that may not have been possible with humans.\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{\\textbf {[Key Term]}\\rightarrow\\textbf{[KT]}}$\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{\\textbf {[Study]}\\rightarrow\\textbf{[S]}}$\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#8c6188}{\\textbf {[Critical Thinking]}\\rightarrow\\textbf{CT}}$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Mark Scheme:}}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible ideas \\& key terms:}}}$\n\n - Hormones: Chemical messengers that influence behaviour (e.g., cortisol, leptin)\n\n - Glucocorticoids: Stress hormones (e.g., cortisol) involved in memory and neurodevelopment\n\n - Ob/ob mice: Genetically modified mice lacking leptin; used to study obesity\n\n - Leptin: A hormone produced by fat cells that signals the brain to reduce appetite and regulate energy balance\n\n$\\textcolor{#8c6188}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible critical thinking points:}}}$\n\n - Psychological similarities: Many species share significant physiological similarities with humans, enabling researchers to draw meaningful insights into human behaviour from animal studies. \n\n - Generalisability: Differences in brain structure, cognition, and social behaviour can make it challenging to apply results from animal studies to humans.\n\n - Lack of in-depth understanding: Using animals removes the potential to ask follow-up qualitative questions to gain a deeper understanding.\n\n - Reliability: Meaney's study is highly reliable due to its controlled lab setting and standardised, replicable procedures.\n\n - Application: Friedman's study on Leptin has  strong applicability, as it led to further research into how hormones influence eating behaviour.\n\n - Ecological validity: The tightly controlled conditions that are possible in animal studies may mean that the findings no longer replicate real-world settings.\n\n - Ethical concerns, including stress and physical harm: animal studies often involve procedures that would be considered unacceptable in human research, such as surgical operations, hormone injections, or euthanasia.\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible key studies:}}}$\n\n - Meaney et al. (1988): Examined how early handling affected stress hormone regulation and memory in rats, showing that early experiences influence cognitive function through hormones.\n\n - Zhang et al. (1994): Identified the obese gene in mice and its human counterpart, revealing leptin’s role in appetite regulation and obesity.\n\n$\\textcolor{#d19f24}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible structure/organisation of response:}}}$\n\n**Introduction:**\n\n - Introduce the role of animal research in research \n\n - Use the command term (state that the response will discuss)\n\n - Mention the key studies that will be discussed\n\n**First body paragraph:** \n\n - Discuss the aim, procedure and findings of your first study, eg, Meaney et al (1988)\n\n**Next body paragraph:**\n\n - Critical thinking: For example, evaluate the study in terms of the use of animals to gain insight into human behavior. \n\n**Next body paragraph:**\n\n - Discuss the aim, procedure and findings of your second study, Friedman's discovery of leptin.\n\n**Next body paragraph:**\n\n - Critical thinking: For example, evaluate the study in terms of the use of animals to gain insight into human behavior.\n\n - Address any additional areas of discussion/uncertainty related to the question, e.g ethical concerns and how animals are different to humans\n\n**Conclusion:** \n\n - Briefly sum up the response and link back to the question.\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\n\n\\\n\nMeaney, M. J., Aitken, D. H., van Berkel, C., Bhatnagar, S., & Sapolsky, R. M. (1988). *Effect of neonatal handling on age-related impairments associated with the hippocampus.* Science, 239(4841 Pt 1), 766-768.  https://doi.org/10.1126/science.3340858\n\nZhang, Y., Proenca, R., Maffei, M., Barone, M., Leopold, L., & Friedman, J. M. (1994). *Positional cloning of the mouse obese gene and its human homologue.* Nature, 372(6505), 425-432.  https://doi.org/10.1038/372425a0"}]},{"name":"Genetics & Evolution","questions":[{"ref":"PS0512","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0512\n\nOutline the role of one gene on human behaviour using a relevant study.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nThe response should have a direct answer to the question within the first few lines.\n\n*(e.g.  One gene that may influence human behaviour is the 5HTT gene……*). As the question clearly refers to the role of genes on human behaviour, it should be identified and referred to in the first few lines. A specific gene affecting a behaviour which is being investigated through the study must be mentioned, however as genetics is a vast area of study with different techniques used, it must be clearly mentioned how research on this gene is conducted. (*e.g. With the advent of new scientific methods, techniques such as gene mapping have advanced to a significant extent helping scientists to…*)\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nThere should be accurate knowledge and understanding of the topic by using all or some key terms, such as:\n\n-    Genes - Genes are made up of DNA and are the functional unit of heredity which determines factors affecting your physical and personality features. \n\n-    Genetic Inheritance - a process by which genetic information is passed through genes from parent to child.\n\n-    Gene mapping - the process of understanding the position of each gene on the chromosomes\n\n-    Epigenetics - an area of study that looks at how the interaction between genetics and environment determines the expression of genes.\n\nThe command term outline seeks to provide a summary or a brief account. Therefore, while the explanation of the effect of genes on human behaviour is essential, there should also be a focus on meeting the command term. There should be a clear summary of how the genetic influence occurs with sufficient details.\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study - including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion. *Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n-    Caspi et. al (2009) on the role of epigenetics on major depressive disorder. \n\n-    Sorensen et.al  (1992) on the role of genes in obesity \n\n-    Bouchard et. al (1990) on the role of genetics on intelligence \n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nThere should be a clear link between the study results and the topic using one of the key terms mentioned in the introduction. (*e.g. This shows a gene-environment interaction, which suggests that the short 5HTT allele, when combined with stressful life events, may influence behaviour, in this case, depression*).\n\n**Marking limits:**\n\n-    Focussing on the biological aspect of genetics and not its influence on behaviour. (*a maximum of 2 marks may be deducted*)\n\n-    A relevant study is described, but no clear links to the effect of genetics on behaviour are made as it can be tricky to make explicit links. (*a maximum of 5 marks will be awarded*)\n\n-    No explanation of the method used to study genetics in the study cited. (*a maximum of 2 marks may be deducted*)\n\n-    If the question is on $`$one' gene, then care should be taken to ensure that only one gene, such as the 5HTT gene as identified by Caspi, is used (*a maximum of 2 marks may be deducted*).\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nOne gene that may influence human behaviour is the 5HTT gene, also known as the serotonin transport gene, which is believed to influence depression. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$\n\n Genes are inherited from our parents and are considered the basic building blocks of a person. Genes contain DNA and tell our cells what to do and how to express different traits. These traits can affect our body's functions and even influence our behaviour. Depression is a mental health condition that can cause persistent feelings of sadness and loss of interest in things that used to bring joy. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nThe 5HTT gene, also known as the serotonin transport gene regulates serotonin levels, and serotonin is found to be crucial for mood regulation. Alterations in serotonin levels or functioning have been implicated in research on depression. Specifically, mutations in the 5HTT gene, such as short alleles, may affect serotonin reuptake.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nThis was studied by Caspi & colleagues in 2003. They wanted to see if the 5HTT gene plays a role in depression.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ They studied 800-plus individuals and split them into groups according to their allele types (short 5HTT alleles vs long 5HTT alleles vs long & short alleles combined.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ They assessed their level of depression regarding stressful life events and found that the participants with shorter 5HTT alleles encountered more stress and had a higher vulnerability to developing depression. This suggests short 5HTT gene alleles may predispose individuals to be vulnerable to depression under adverse conditions. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\nThis study highlights a gene-environment interaction, which suggests that the short 5HTT allele, when combined with stressful life events, may influence behaviour, in this case, depression. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\nThis shows the importance of looking at genetic influences and environmental factors when analysing behaviour.\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nCaspi, A., Sugden, K., Moffit TE., Taylor, A., Craig AW., Harrington, H., McClay, J., Mill, J., Martin, J., Braithwaite, A., Poulton R. (2006). Influence of life stress on depression: moderation by a polymorphism in the 5-HYY gene. Science, 18(301), 389-9. https://doi: 10.1126/science.1083968. PMID: 12869766."},{"ref":"PS0513","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0513\n\nDescribe one evolutionary explanation of one human behaviour using one study.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nThere should be a direct answer linked to the question within the first few lines.\n\n(*e.g. Evolution has had an impact on some human behaviours such as ...*) As the question clearly refers to an evolutionary explanation of human behaviour, it should be identified and referred to in the first few lines. A specific behaviour affected due to evolution must be mentioned along with a clear explanation of how evolution has played a role in its expression.\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nThere should be accurate knowledge and understanding of evolutionary explanations for behaviour, by using all or some key terms, such as:\n\n-    Theory of Evolution - the theory states that the behaviour of organisms evolves from generations \n\n-    Natural selection - the passing of successful behaviours from one generation to another which helps them to survive\n\n-    Sexual selection - the process by which organisms selects mate that helps them to produce offsprings with higher chances of survival\n\n-    Adaptive behaviour - behaviour that helps people to adapt in the current environment.\n\nThe command term outlines seeks to provide a detailed account, including possible causes or reasons. Therefore, while evolutionary explanations for behaviour is essential, there should also be a focus on meeting the command term. There should be a clear summary on how this adaptation occurs with sufficient details (*e.g. The reason for this behaviour can be traced back to…*).\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study - including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study. *Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n-    Wedekind et.al (1995) on the evolution of attraction in humans\n\n-    Curtis et.al (2004) on how evolution plays a role in the survival of humans\n\n-    Ronay Von and Hippel (2010) on the evolution of mate selection\n\n-    Ahs et.al (2009) on the role of fear in the survival of humans\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nThere should be a clear link between the results of the study to the topic using one of the key terms mentioned in the introduction. *(e.g. Wedekind et.al (1995)  - results from this study showed that the participants performed more risk-taking behaviour in front of the female researcher as compared to the male researcher. This could be linked to the evolutionary explanation of mate selection as since earlier times males have always had to show characteristics of bravery and courage to attract females to assure them of their capability to protect them in the wilderness*)\n\n**Marking limits:**\n\n-    Focusing on Genetics and not on the evolutionary aspects of behaviour. (*up to 4 marks may be awarded*)\n\n-    A relevant study is described but no clear links to evolution is made. (*up to 5 marks may be awarded*)\n\n-    No behaviour is mentioned specifically. (*up to 3 marks may be deducted*)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nCharles Darwin - known as the father of Evolution, theorised that only behaviours that produce the fittest offspring with the highest chance of survival are repeated through generations and others slowly wane out. Evolutionary Psychologists over the years have observed human behaviour to identify behaviours that can be rooted in evolution. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The tendencies for this behaviour is said to be passed on genetically from one generation to the next and is present across the human race. One of the behaviours that has an evolutionary explanation is fear and disgust. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$  $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  Humans tend to naturally get repelled or disgusted by things that are harmful to them and will avoid it. This tendency has been known to have a protective effect as they ensure the survival of humans by protecting them from dangerous and fatal diseases. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  Another well-known behaviour is natural selection or sexual selection. According to this theory, humans will select their mate based on their chances of producing the fittest offspring for survival. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ One study that gave evidence to the evolutionary aspect of the behaviour of disgust is by Curtis et.al (2004). $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nCurtis et.al (2004) hypothesised that disgust has evolved as a behaviour to protect humans from harmful diseases. A large study with over 40,000 respondents was conducted through an online platform. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ People were asked to rate 20 images of food on a scale from 1-5 with 1 being most disgusting. Among the images were also 7 foods associated with diseases. Results from the study showed that women consistently rated these 7 images as the worst as compared to men. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ This can be traced back to earlier times when knowledge of proper methods of cooking food was limited. There was not enough awareness on the dangers of uncooked food thereby evolution has helped humans to survive deadly foodborne diseases that could have been fatal. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nFrom the results of this study, it can be seen that women were more sensitive towards foods that were associated with diseases. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ As women are the ones who are responsible to bear children and also play a protective role in rearing children along with being the gatekeepers of nutrition in the family, the results from this study show a direct impact of evolution on women's behaviour of disgust directly related to foods that could potentially be fatal. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ Thus supporting the idea of the effect of evolution on human behaviour as it supports the survival of the human species. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nCurtis, V., Aunger, R., $\\&$ Rabie, T. S. (2004). Evidence that disgust evolved to protect from risk of disease. Proceedings of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences, 271(suppl_4). https://doi.org/10.1098/rsbl.2003.0144"},{"ref":"PS0529","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0529\n\nExplain the role of genes on human behaviour using one relevant study.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question provided in the first few sentences (e.g. *“Genetics plays a determining role in the causation of behavior. ….”)*. This can be followed by explanations of how research on this gene is conducted. (*“With the advent of new scientific methods, techniques such as gene mapping have advanced significantly, helping scientists to…..”*)\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{2. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study$\\text{\\textendash}$ including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study\\\n\n*Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n-    Kendler et.al (2006) on the role of genes on major depressive disorder.\n\n-    Caspi et. al (2009) on the role of epigenetics on major depressive disorder. \n\n-    Sorensen et.al  (1992) on the role of genes in obesity \n\n-    Bouchard et. al (1990) on the role of genetics on intelligence \n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{3. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of genes and their role on human behaviour.\\\n\n          *Important concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define include some of the   \n\n           following:*\n\n-    Genes - Genes are made up of DNA and are the functional unit of heredity which determines factors affecting your physical and personality features. \n\n-    Inheritance - a process by which genetic information is passed through genes from parent to child.\n\n-    Gene mapping - the process of understanding the position of each gene on the chromosomes\n\n-    Epigenetics - an area of study that looks at how the interaction between genetics and environment determines the expression of genes.\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study to the topic  (e.g. *“The results of the Kendler et.al (2006) study, give evidence to the role of genetics on the occurrence of a disorder like MDD as MZ twins who share 100% identical genes had significantly higher concordance rates to MDD as compared to DZ twins. This study shows how twin studies are effective in identifying the impact of genes on human behaviour by giving further evidence to the effect of genes on MDD.”)*\n\n**Marking limits:**\n\n-    Focusing on the biological aspect of genetics and not its influence on behaviour. (up to 2 marks may be deducted)\n\n-    A relevant study is described, but no clear links to the effect of genetics on behaviour are made as it can be tricky to make explicit links. (up to 5 marks will be awarded)\n\n-    If the question is on $`$one' gene, then care should be taken to ensure that only one gene, such as the 5-HTT gene as identified by Caspi, is used( up to 2 marks may be deducted)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nGenes are considered as the basic building blocks of a person which determine to a great extent their personality, their behaviour and various other aspects of living. The role of genes on human behaviour has long been an area under intensive research with continuous scientific advances that have helped further understand this field $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$. Epigenetics is a branch of study focused on understanding how specific genes can exist in an individual without being actively manifested in behaviours or disorders $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$.   This exploration enhances our comprehension of the relationship between genes and the environment, shedding light on the conditions under which certain genes are activated. Consequently, epigenetics involves the targeted isolation and in-depth examination of particular genes to unravel their patterns of expression $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. One study that used epigenetics to investigate the interaction of the 5-HTT gene with the stressors in the environment leading to the occurrence of Major Depressive disorders (MDD) is Caspi et.al (2009) $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nCaspi et. al (2009) used a sample of 26-year-old New Zealanders who were assessed yearly for their mental health. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  The sample was divided into three groups as per their 5-HTT allele - group 1 had two short alleles, group 2 had one long and one short allele and group 3 had 2 long alleles. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  Additionally, the participants also responded to a stressful life events questionnaire along with a depression screener. Results showed that participants with one or more short alleles demonstrated more symptoms of depression along with suicidal ideation. It was also seen that people with three or more stressful events had the strongest effect from groups 1 and 2 suggesting that continuous exposure to stressors has a role to play in gene expression. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  Therefore, it can be concluded that participants with the shorter allele were more prone to developing depression in the presence of stressful life events as compared to those with longer alleles. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThis helps us to understand that it is not enough for a gene to be present but environmental triggers are necessary for it to be expressed. Thus, epigenetics has helped us to further understand the gene environment interaction in the causation of human behaviours and offers possible reasons why two people with similar genes may or may not have the same personalities. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nCaspi, A., Sugden, K., Moffitt, T. E., Taylor, A., Craig, I. W., Harrington, H. L., McClay, J. L., Mill, J., Martin, J., Braithwaite, A. W., & Poulton, R. (2003). Influence of life stress on depression: moderation by a polymorphism in the 5-HTT gene. Science, 301(5631), 386$\\text{\\textendash}$389. https: //doi.org/10.1126/science.1083968"},{"ref":"PS0531","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0531\n\nExplain the effect of genetic similarities on human behaviour using one  research study. \n\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question provided in the first few sentences (e.g. *“Genetic similarities is a significant factor which helps us to understand the reason why individuals within the same family display similar characteristics….”)*\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{2. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study $\\text{\\textendash}$ including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study\\\n\n            *Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n-    Weissman et. al (2005) using family studies across generations on the incidence of depression.\n\n-    Holland et.al (1988) uses twin studies to measure the effect of genes on eating disorders.\n\n-    Bouchard et. al (1990) uses twin studies to measure the effect of genetics on intelligence\n\n-    Sorensen et.al  (1992) uses adoption studies to test the role of genes in obesity \n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{3. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of hormones and its effects on human behaviour.\\\n\n           *Important concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define include:*\n\n-    Genetic inheritance - a process by which genetic information is passed through genes from parent to child.\n\n-    Genetic similarities - a measure to understand the similarities in genetics among people who are related to each other\n\n-    Twin studies - A type of research method carried out to measure genetic similarities among twins.\n\n-    Monozygotic twins (MZ) - twins born from one single cell sharing 100% same genetic matter\n\n-    Dizygotic twins (DZ) - twins born from two different cells sharing 50% of same genetic matter.\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study to the topic (e.g. *“Bouchard et.al (1990) used twin studies to understand the role of inheritance on intelligence - Links to results of the study as he found that MZT had a concordance rate of 86% while the MZA only had 76%. These results signify the impact of genetic factors on intelligence.”)*\n\n**Marking reductions:**\n\n-    No explanation on the method used to study genetics in the study cited. (up to 2 marks may be deducted)\n\n-    Genetic similarities is not explained in detail. (up to 3 marks may be deducted)\n\n-    The study cited is not appropriate and does not refer to genetic similarities and focuses on other content such as epigenetics. (up to 4 marks maybe deducted)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nResearchers study genetics to better understand the biological basis of human behaviour. Genetic similarities help us to understand the presence of common genetic materials among humans and its correlation on the incidence of behaviour and disorders $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$. One of the earliest ways to study gene  inheritance is through twin studies using Monozygotic twins (MZ) - twins who share 100% genes as they are identical and Dizygotic twins (DZ) - twins who share 50% genes between them and are non-identical. $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  The reasoning behind using twin studies is that twins mostly share the same environment as they grow up together with the same family and are exposed to similar stressors. Therefore, any difference in the occurrence of any disorder between them can be due to the genes they share.This is measured through concordance rates which is the correlation between the similarity of inheritance and occurrence of a disorder. $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  One study that uses twin studies to investigate the role of inheritance in the occurrence of eating disorders such as Anorexia Nervosa  is Holland et al.'s research (1988). $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nHolland et al. gathered 25 female MZ and 20 female DZ twin pairs with at least one of them diagnosed with anorexia. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  The twins were put through a structured diagnostic interview for Anorexia based on DSM III. Additionally, they were asked to complete questionnaires on eating habits and personality styles. The results from the study showed that  MZ twins had a significantly higher concordance rate of anorexia - 56% as compared to the DZ twins - only 5%. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  The results of this study give evidence  to the role of genetics on the occurrence of a disorder like anorexia nervosa as MZ twins who share 100% identical rates had significantly higher concordance rates as compared to DZ twins. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  Even though the MZ twins had a higher concordance rate when compared to the DZ twins, it is still not 100% indicating that though there is genetic predisposition it is still not necessary that if one MZ twin develops anorexia the other will also develop. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThis study shows how twin studies are effective in identifying the impact of genes on human behaviour by giving further evidence to the effect of genes on eating disorders. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nHolland, A., Sicotte, N. L., & Treasure, J. (1988). Anorexia nervosa: Evidence for a genetic basis. Journal of Psychosomatic Research, 32(6), 561$\\text{\\textendash}$571. https: //doi.org/10.1016/0022-3999(88)90004-9"},{"ref":"PS0566","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0566\n\nEvaluate one or more research study/ies on one or more evolutionary explanations for human behaviour.\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The \n\nquestion used the command term *evaluate*, therefore, the response should \n\nidentify, explain, and weigh the strengths and limitations of one or more studies on evolutionary explanations for human behavior. Any human behavior can be focused upon. \n\n**1 mark** if the command term is not accurately explained or has limited explanation.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant $\\&$ appropriate terminology is explained in detail (*e.g. evolution and related theories, supporting research*). Since the question asks to evaluate studies, marks will also be awarded for criterion B for knowledge of research methods and how they are used in the study context.\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology such as explanation of evolutionary theory, human behaviour that is being focused on like disgust, mate selection or attraction is explained and has minimal errors and if the response only showed knowledge about research methods, such as a brief explanation of evolutionary explanation for behaviour with little focus on evaluation of study.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited with no evaluation of studies.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (*e.g., aim, procedures, results $\\&$ conclusion*) are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments stated. Studies to be used are but not limited to -\n\n-    Curtis, Aunger and Rabie (2004) investigated the evolution of disgust as a protection from risk of disease.\n\n-    Fessler (2006) investigated that disgust helps to compensate for the suppressed immune system.\n\n-    Chiao and Blizinsky (2010) investigated the prevalence of genetic susceptibility to depression in collectivist societies.\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (*e.g the aim, procedure, results $\\&$ conclusions of the study was not clearly presented*) \n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. (*e.g the study was not linked to the evaluation points mentioned*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed evaluation points (*e.g around 2-3 strengths and limitations such as there is low internal validity as a cause and effect relationship cannot be observed between the IV and DV due to the research method used, researcher or sample and its generalization*). \n\n**3-4  marks** of the evaluation points (*e.g around 1-2 strengths $\\&$ limitations*) are present and clearly explained but are not sustained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation or discussion points are superficial (*evaluation points only stated but not explained properly*).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is a clear link to be made at the end of the answer focusing on evolutionary explanation. The link can be made with the help of an evolutionary explanation that influences behaviour. The answer must be organized and clear with appropriate information provided. \n\n**1 mark** if the organization and clarity were not sustained throughout the answer.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nEvolution is the process by which organisms change from one generation to another as a result of changes in heritable characteristics. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ It assumes that organisms are driven by the need to survive and reproduce. The organisms that adapt to change are able to survive and produce their off-springs which is called survival of the fittest. The theory of evolution was introduced by Charles Darwin and was based on the explanation of differential fitness and natural selection. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Differential fitness is the process when organisms from the population have different traits as a result of adapting to the environmental changes. Traits that have not adapted to the environmental changes often wear out and are not passed to the offspring. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Many common behaviours such as fear, attraction, mate selection and disgust may be understood from an evolutionary point. \n\nThe study conducted by Curtis, Aunger and Rabie (2004) focuses on the behaviour of disgust as it has been evolved to a way of protecting oneself from the risk of diseases. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ There were a total of five hypotheses for the study with a total of 77,000 participants from 165 countries but only 40,000 data was selected as participants who had watched the BBC documentary on the topic were excluded. An online survey was conducted to record their responses. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The five hypotheses were: \n\n1.    Disgust should be felt more when faced with a disease-salient stimulus compared to a stimulus with less salience. \n\n2.    The behaviour of disgust should operate in a similar way across cultures. \n\n3.    Disgust should be more pronounced in females since they have to protect their offsprings. \n\n4.    Disgust should become weaker with age as the individual's reproductive potential declines.\n\n5.    Disgust should be stronger in contact with strangers than with close relatives. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe procedure involved showing twenty images to all the participants, post which they were asked to rank them based on their level of disgust on a scale of 1 (not disgusting) to 5 (very disgusting). out of the 20 images, 14 images comprised 7 pairs of disease-salient versus less salient stimuli. All participants were asked to share their demographic details prior to the survey. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe results of the study supported all five hypotheses. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\n1.    Disease-salient stimuli were rated as more disgusting than less salient ones (the image of a plate of organic-looking fluid was rated as 61$\\%$ more disgusting than the image of a plate of blue fluid that looked like a chemical).\n\n2.    Results were consistent across cultures.\n\n3.    There were gender differences as females rated the disease-salient pictures more disgusting compared to men.\n\n4.    There was a decrease in sensitivity to disgust as a result of aging. \n\n5.    Disgust was felt more for strangers. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nWith the help of this study, it is evident that disgust as a behaviour has shown evolutionary changes with respect to age, and gender. Disgust has also shown to have passed down from one generation to the next. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nThere are many strengths of a survey method. Data collection for a large sample size is easy to collect, conduct at the same time and cost effective compared to other research methods such as interviews or experiments. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ A survey method has low internal validity as causation cannot be established between the IV and DV whereas correlation can be established between variables. Due to the large sample size of 44,000 generalization of findings is high and makes the sample more representative of the population across cultures. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ On the other hand, there are few limitations to the survey method. Since the data collected is self reported data, it may not always be accurate as people have a tendency to not highlight their weakness and may want to showcase a positive image of themselves. This leads to inaccurate data and there is no way of cross checking the same. It is a self selected sample where extraneous variables cannot be controlled such as subject differences with experience of disgust, having a high threshold for disgust. These variables may impact the correlation in the results$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. Another limitation is that images were shared with participants instead of actual things which may alter the level of disgust. \n\nThe above explained study can clearly show the evolutionary explanation of disgust as a response as disgust is a biologically driven behaviour. Evolutionary theory suggests that behaviour evolves over times and through different generations by only passing off the healthy genetic material. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nWord count: 735\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nCurtis, V., Aunger, R., $\\&$ Rabie, T. (2004). Evidence that disgust evolved to protect from risk of disease. Proceedings. Biological sciences, 271 Suppl 4(Suppl 4), S131-S133. https://doi.org/10.1098/rsbl.2003.0144"},{"ref":"PS0579","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0579\n\nDiscuss one or more evolutionary explanation/s for one human behaviour.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The question used the command term discuss, therefore, the response should offer a balanced review that considers a range of factors, arguments and hypotheses. The response can also include an explanation of why it is important to evaluate research studies that investigate evolutionary explanations for human behaviour.\n\n**1 mark** if the question was briefly described in less detail or if the question was restated. (*e.g. This essay will discuss two studies that explore an evolutionary explanation for human behaviour.*)\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant $\\&$ appropriate terminology are explained without errors, and are explicitly linked to the study and the discussion points (*e.g.natural selection, sexual selection, intrasexual selection, intersexual selection*).\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors (*e.g. sexual selection means how we operate in our societies when selecting a mate*) and if the response only showed knowledge about the theory rather than exploring the methodology in the studies.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited (*e.g. natural selection means we look for certain traits in a mate*) and If the answer only shows knowledge related to evolutionary explanations for behaviour and did not explore studies that support it. \n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (*e.g., aim, procedures, results $\\&$ conclusion*) are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments stated. Studies to be used are but not limited to:\n\n - Wedekind (1995) studied MHC and attraction\n\n - Ronay & von Hippel (2010) researched on risk-taking behaviours, testosterone, and attraction\n\n - Buss (1989) investigated gender differences in mate preferences\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (e.g the aim, procedure, results & conclusions of the study was not clearly presented)\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. (*e.g the study was not linked to the evaluation points mentioned*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed evaluation points (*e.g around 2-3 strengths and limitations*) of research on evolutionary behaviours (*e.g. Studies that explore mating behaviour seem to focus on the reasons why we might be attracted to someone else, but stops short of the idea that reproduction is the objective, and negates to explain homosexual attraction*)\n\n**3-4 marks** of the evaluation points (*e.g around 1-2 strengths $\\&$ limitations*) are present and clearly explained but are not sustained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation or discussion points are superficial and mainly descriptive (*e.g if the theory was evaluated instead of the study, or if the evaluation points were only stated or described but not linked back to the study*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is an organised & coherent explanation of evolutionary explanations for behaviour and an evaluation of one or more relevant studies (see Criterion C). (*e.g. first paragraph introduces the focus on the question, has a clear thesis and related terminology is defined, the succeeding paragraphs link back to the argument stated in the introduction, an evaluation is made based on the strengths and weaknesses of the research studies covered*).\n\n**1 mark** if the organisation and clarity were not sustained throughout the answer. (*e.g unfinished response, incomplete description of key terms and studies*)\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nIf humans were not able to adapt to the world around them, survival would be impossible. As such, we have developed evolutionary behaviours that ensure the likelihood of survival; this is called natural selection $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$. In particular, sexual selection is the notion that the human species looks for traits in their partners that are likely to engender reproductive success and survival of future generations. Intrasexual selection is when individuals of the same sex compete with each other for access to mates $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$, whereas intersexual selection is when individuals choose a mate based on desirable traits, such as strong shoulders as a sign of strength in men, or wide hips that convey suitability for child rearing $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. While there is empirical evidence to support the existence of sexual selection, this is just one factor that explains mate selection, it would be reductive not to acknowledge the importance of both sociocultural and cognitive factors $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$. Evolutionary explanations of behaviour, in terms of mate choice, and competing with others to win potential mates, can be discussed with reference to research conducted by Wedekind (1995) and Ronay and von Hippel (2010). $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nWedekind (1995) wanted to determine whether someone's major histocompatibility complex (MHC) gene would affect their mate choice $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$.  MHC genes play a major role in the immune system in that they release molecules that help us to recognise pathogens and psychologists argue that we are attracted by smell to those with different MHC genes to ensure the strength of the immune system in our offspring. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ In this quasi-experiment, 49 male students and 44 female students were asked to take part. The men were asked to wear a t-shirt for two days, taking care not to wear deodorant or eat spicy food. The women were then asked to rank the smell of the t-shirts in a cardboard box with a smelling hole: three of the seven boxes contained T-shirts from men with MHC similar to the woman's own; three contained T-shirts from MHC dissimilar men; and one contained an unworn T-shirt as a control. Results demonstrated that women scored male body odours as more pleasant when they differed from their own MHC than when they were more similar. It can, therefore, be concluded that the MHC genes of a potential mate affect our mate choice, supporting the evolutionary notion that mates are chosen to engender reproductive success [S]$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$.\n\nWedekind's (1995) study was well-controlled, with researchers enacting control measures to ensure that other smells were not affecting the judgement of the female participants $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. The researchers took care not to bias the women's decision by putting each t-shirt in a box, so they were unaware of the characteristics of the wearer, protecting the integrity of the results $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. As the researchers could not manipulate the genetic make-up of the participants, this was not a true experiment and no strong cause-and-effect relationship can be established. Despite confirmation of the original hypothesis, it is quite limiting to consider that the decision about pursuing a mate is solely dependent on smell; there are other factors that play a role in the decision-making process $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. In a real-life setting, the MHC scent may be masked by other smells, and so perhaps the same results would not be obtainable in a natural environment $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$.\n\nAccording to intrasexual selection, individuals of the same sex will compete with each other to win the attention of potential mates. Ronay and von Hippel (2010) aimed to explore whether males would engage in increasingly risky behaviour in front of an attractive female$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. 96 Australian male skateboarders were asked to do a trick they could complete 50$\\%$ of the time in front of a middle-aged male researcher, and then in front of an attractive 18-year old researcher who was blind to the aim of the study. Saliva samples were also collected to measure the testosterone levels of the skateboarders. Results demonstrated that greater risks were taken with the female researcher, in that the skateboarders pursued the trick more times, even after bad execution. The skaters also demonstrated higher levels of testosterone after performing in front of the female researcher, showing that there is a biological drive for risk-taking in the presence of a potential mate. Conclusions of this experiment indicate the level of competitiveness among the skateboarders as a need to demonstrate attractive traits to the female researchers, such as strength, skill and dominance.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ This highlights the evolutionary concept of intrasexual selection, where individuals of the same sex will compete with each other to win the attention of potential mates, thus increasing the likelihood of reproductive success. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$. \n\nRonay and von Hippel's (2010) study is well-controlled and had a test and experimental condition to show how the skateboarders would perform under $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$normal\" conditions, and how this changed when an attractive female was present; the researchers were able to isolate this variable $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. The conditions of the experiment were fairly natural and at an outdoor skatepark, although ecological validity may have been affected by having researchers present. This study also made use of method triangulation in testing for testosterone levels as well as assessing risky behaviour, further strengthening the validity of the results $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. While it is clear that the female researcher had an effect on the behaviour of the skateboarders, it is difficult to say whether this behaviour is linked to reproduction, as a goal of attracting the female's attention $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. \n\nIn conclusion, evolutionary psychology determines that humans have genetically adapted to their environment in ways that ensure we have the best characteristics which are then passed down the generations $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. Evidence available in the field supports the notion that we have biological mechanisms such as a drive for genetically dissimilar sexual partners, or a surge of hormones in a situation where we are confronted with a potential partner, which kickstarts behaviours that reveal our desirable traits to the opposite sex $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. The evidence, then, supports the idea of sexual selection as an evolutionary behaviour. A great deal of research in this area is done in a laboratory setting, which leads to high control and protection against extraneous variables. However, as these studies are based on evolutionary arguments, their premises are not falsifiable, and we cannot make strong claims in terms of cause and effect. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. Furthermore, human beings are complex and make decisions based on a variety of factors; it is somewhat reductive to reduce behaviour to biological mechanisms - humans make conscious decisions when selecting a partner and are not governed only by biological drives $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$.\n\nWord count: 984\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nWedekind, C., Seebeck, T., Bettens, F., & Paepke, A. J. (1995). MHC-dependent mate preferences in humans. Proceedings of the Royal Society of London. Series B: Biological Sciences, 260(1359), 245-249.\n\nRonay, R., & von Hippel, W. (2010). Power, testosterone, and risk-taking. Journal of Behavioural Decision Making, 23(5), 473-482."},{"ref":"PS0621","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0621\n\nEvaluate studies related to the influence of genetic similarities to one or more human behaviours.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The question used the command term *evaluate*, therefore, the response requires the candidate to make an appraisal of one or more studies related to genetic similarities. The focus of the evaluation should be upon the study/studies, not genes and/or genetic similarities. Although both strengths and limitations should be addressed, this does not have to be evenly balanced.\n\n**1 mark** if the question was briefly described in less detail or if the question was restated. *(e.g. Some of the research studies that explain Genetic similarities) No marks are awarded for this criterion if the answer showed now clear and explicit focus on the command term and topic in the question.*\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant & appropriate terminology is explained in detail *(e.g. inheritance, genetic similarities, twin studies, family studies, terms related to research methods such as correlational study, case study, longitudinal and terms related to ethical considerations such as - deception, consent etc.)* Because the question asks to evaluate studies, students need to show an understanding of the research used in this field followed by explaining its value in understanding human behaviour to score full marks in this criterion.\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors *(e.g. Genetic similarities is explained along with research but there is no explanation of the research methods used in the studies cited.)*  \n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited *(e.g. Genetic similarities is explained but the research cited shows minimal understanding of research terminology with errors in its usage).* No marks are awarded for this criterion if no relevant terminology was explained.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies are explained *(e.g., aim, procedures, results \\& conclusion)* and used to support the answer or the arguments stated. Studies to be used are but not limited to:\n\n-    Correlational studies - Kendler et.al (2006), Sorensen et.al (1992), Holland et.al (1988) \n\n-    Case study - Bouchard et.al (1990), Weissman et.al (2005)\n\n**3-4 marks**  if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. *(e.g the aim, procedure, results & conclusions of the study was not clearly presented)*\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. *(e.g the study was not linked to the discussion points mentioned) No marks are awarded for this criterion if no relevant study/ies are explained.*\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, well-balanced appraisal of both strengths and limitations of the studies *(e.g. methodological, cultural, and gender considerations, ethical concerns regarding the study and validity and reliability of the study, around 2-3 strengths and limitations)*\n\n**3-4 marks** if the evaluation points are only based on either strengths only or limitations only, and/or if the answer explains around 1-2 strengths and limitations only.\n\n**1-2 marks** will be awarded if the evaluation is based mostly on the topic and not on the evaluation of studies. Or if the evaluation points were only stated but not explained *(e.g. the answer stated that the study is limited in terms of the generalisability of its findings but it was not explained why).* No marks are awarded for this criterion if no relevant critical thinking or evaluation points are explained.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is an organised \\& coherent explanation of the research related to the concept *(e.g. first paragraph introduces the focus on the question and related terminology, the succeeding paragraphs talk about the studies to be evaluated as well as the actual evaluation of one or more research, and the closing paragraphs or statements link the arguments back to the question)*\n\n**1 mark** if the organisation and clarity were not sustained or if the answer is incomplete or unfinished.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \n\nResearchers study genetics to better understand the biological basis of human behaviour. Genetic similarities help us to understand the presence of common genetic materials among humans and its correlation on the incidence of behaviour and disorders. Some of the most common ways to study genetic similarities is through twin studies and family studies. Both of these research types require rigorous research methods that will give quantifiable data for analysis. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nOne of the common research methods used are natural experiments which are correlational in nature.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ In this type of experiment, the Independent variable is naturally occurring and is not manipulated by the researcher. Additionally, these are known as correlational as the premise of the study is to establish the relationship between genetic similarities and behaviour.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ One study that will be discussed in this essay demonstrating the use of correlational studies is Kendler et.al (2006). The other most commonly used research method is case study. Case studies are often longitudinal and help to ascertain the change in behaviour over a period of time. Additionally, case studies use method triangulation helping to establish the validity of the research.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ This will be shown through the Weismann et.al (2005) study. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nKendler et.al (2006) conducted a correlational study using twins to investigate the effect of genes on depression. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ These studies use monozygotic twins (MZ) - twins who share 100% genes as they are identical and dizygotic twins (DZ) - twins who share 50\\% genes between them and are non-identical. Around 15,000 twins were identified from the twin registry in Sweden and data was gathered through interviews based on the DSM IV criteria for depression. The reasoning behind using twin studies is that twins mostly share the same environment as they grow up together with the same family and are exposed to similar stressors. Therefore, any difference in the occurrence of any disorder between them can be due to the genes they share. This is measured through concordance rates which is the correlation between the similarity of inheritance and occurrence of a disorder. Results showed that female MZ twins had the highest concordance rates followed by male MZ twins. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nTherefore, the results give evidence to the correlation between genes and behaviour as MZ twins had higher concordance rates indicating the more similarity in genes the higher chances of depression among twins. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ A significant strength of the study was the large sample size leading to increased generalizability as the results can now be applied to both types of twins. Furthermore, other studies have confirmed these findings increasing the reliability of the study such as Bierut et.al (1999) etc. However, a limitation of this research is the questionable validity of the diagnosis as it was a telephonic interview. Also, the symptoms were mainly self-reported which could explain the gender bias seen in the results. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ \n\nWeissman et.al (2005) conducted a longitudinal case study on families to test for the effect of genes on depression. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Sample was collected from three generations over a span of 20 years with the grandparents having a clinical diagnosis. The children and grandchildren were interviewed by different clinicians through surveys and observations. Results showed that if a grandparent was diagnosed with depression, the grandchild had an increased chance more than 50% to develop the disorder. Additionally, it was also found that an incidence of depression in both grandparents and parents raised the chances of the child getting the disorder. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nResults demonstrate the effect of genetic similarity on depression as having the genes for depression in the family increase the chances of a person developing the disorder. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ As it was a case study, the presence of method triangulation such the use of interviews and clinical diagnosis increases the validity of the research findings.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Additionally, the study is longitudinal increasing the reliability of the research. However, the presence of confounding variables in the study such as the amount of time spent with the parent or grandparent was not accounted for which could have affected the results as the environment could also play a role in the diagnosis of depression. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nBoth studies cited above show a clear link between genetics and depression furthering the argument that depression could be inherited through genetics. As seen, true experiments help enhance the validity of the argument as the independent variable (genetic similarity) is not manipulated by the researcher and is naturally occurring.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Furthermore, this being a correlational study, helped researchers to identify the relationship that exists between the variables of genetics and depression. However, caution must be used as correlation is different from causation, thereby it cannot be said that genetics is the sole cause of depression rather its presence only helps to ascertain the likelihood of a person developing the disorder.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Another advantage of using this type of studies is the large sample size used as seen in both studies. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nWhile correlation is necessary in the research on genetic similarities, researchers should also include triangulation of data to raise the validity of the studies as seen in the second study cited. Thus, a mixed-method design is more effective in establishing the validity of the conclusions drawn linking genetics and behaviour. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nWord count: 849\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nKendler, K. S., Gatz, M., Gardner, C. O., \\& Pedersen, N. L. (2006). A Swedish national twin study of lifetime major depression. American Journal of Psychiatry, 163(1), 109-114. https://doi.org/10.1176/appi.ajp.163.1.109 \n\nWeissman, M. M., Wickramaratne, P., Nomura, Y., Warner, V., Verdeli, H., Pilowsky, D. J., Grillon, C., \\& Bruder, G. E. (2005). Families at high and low risk for depression. Archives of General Psychiatry, 62(1), 29. https://doi.org/10.1001/archpsyc.62.1.29"},{"ref":"PS0766","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0766\n\nDefine the following key terms: twin studies, adoption studies, and pedigree studies. Then, explain how each helps in understanding the role of genetics in psychological disorders.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n**Twin Studies**\\\n\nTwin studies compare the similarity of psychological traits or disorders between identical twins (MZ), who share 100% of their genes, and fraternal twins (DZ), who share 50% of their genes. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Twin studies help us understand the role of genetics in psychological disorders by checking if identical twins are more likely to both have the same disorder than fraternal twins, which suggests that genes play a part. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)  \n\n**Adoption Studies**\\\n\nAdoption studies investigate the psychological traits or disorders of adopted individuals, comparing them to their biological parents (genetic influence) and adoptive parents (environmental influence). (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nAdoption studies help separate the effects of genetics and environment by showing whether adopted people are more like their biological parents (genetics) or their adoptive parents (environment). (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Pedigree Studies**\\\n\nPedigree studies track the inheritance of traits or disorders in families across generations to identify genetic patterns. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)  Pedigree studies help identify whether certain psychological disorders run in families, indicating a potential genetic predisposition to those disorders. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\n\n- 1 mark for defining twin studies.\n\n- 1 mark for defining adoption studies.\n\n- 1 mark for defining pedigree studies \n\n- 1 mark explaining the type of study (twin, adoption and pedigree) and its relevance in understanding genetics.\n\n- Marks should be awarded for any similarly worded explanations to a maximum of 6 marks"},{"ref":"PS0916","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0916\n\nExplain how twin and/or kinship studies are used to investigate the role of genetic similarities in one behaviour.\n\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nTwin studies, especially those that compare monozygotic and dizygotic twins, assist researchers with establishing the extent to which behaviors like depression correspond with increasing genetic similarity. Monozygotic twins share 100$\\%$ of their genes, while dizygotic twins share 50$\\%$. If researchers find that concordance - the rate of similarity in a behavior -  correlates with the percentage of shared DNA, it indicates that a behavior is influenced by genetics. Concordance comparisons across mono and dizygotic twins may also reveal the role of the environment. \n\nKendler et al. interviewed over 15,000 twins who were registered in the Swedish Twin Registry to better understand how genetic similarity would relate to the development of depression.  Through phone interviews, the researchers established whether or not participants had a history of depression, and a history of treatment for depression. In addition, researchers asked questions to reveal the extent to which each set of twins had a shared living environment in childhood. Other questions were asked to determine if events in adulthood may have exacerbated each individual’s risk of developing depression.\n\nThis study found that concordance rates for depression were higher in monozygotic twins than dizygotic twins. When comparing concordance rates by sex, female monozygotic twins had higher concordance rates than male monozygotic twins. The number of years participants spent living with their twin did not correlate with diagnosis of persistent major depression. Overall, researchers concluded heritability of depression at 0.38, in line with other completed research. \n\nThis study demonstrates how twin research assists with understanding behavior by comparing genetic similarity. By comparing rates of depression between monozygotic and dizygotic twins, differences in concordance can support heritability, especially when the amount of time living together is taken into account.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Mark Scheme:}}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible ideas \\& key terms:}}}$\n\n - Monozygotic (identical) twins share the same DNA, while dizygotic (fraternal) share half, like other sibling types\n\n - Concordance rates describe the frequency at which two individuals share the same trait\n\n - Shared environment can also play a role in increasing concordance for some traits, and strong genetic similarity studies take measures to account for this\n\n - If monozygotic twin concordance rates are higher than dizygotic twin concordance rates, chances are higher that a trait is heritable \n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible key studies:}}}$\n\n - Skre et al.'s (1993) twin study investigating genetic influences on anxiety disorders\n\n - Holland et al.'s (1988) twin study investigating genetic influences on anorexia\n\n - Kendler et al.'s (2006) twin study investigating genetic influences on depression\n\n - Bouchard et al.'s (1990) twin study into genetic influences on intelligence\n\n - True et al.'s (1993) twin study investigating genetic influences on PTSD symptoms among U.S. Vietnam veterans\n\n - Weissman et al.'s (2005) kinship (parental) study of MDD\n\n - Yehuda et al.'s (2000, 1998) kinship (parental) studies investigating symptoms of PTSD among adult children of Holocaust survivors.\n\n$\\textcolor{#d19f24}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible structure/organisation of response:}}}$\n\n**Direct Answer (DA)**: Begin with a statement identifying how researchers may use a twin or kinship study to better understand the role of genetic similarity in behavior, e.g., $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Twin studies, especially those that compare monozygotic and dizygotic twins assist researchers with establishing to what extent behaviors like depression correlate with genetic similarity.\" Introduce the relevant study (*e.g., Kendler et al.*) to contextualize the discussion.\n\nParagraph 1: **Define key terms (KT)**: A description of twin and/or kinship study methodology should be included. Concordance should also be defined. If using a twin study, define and distinguish monozygotic twins and dizygotic twins. Depending on the study selected, it may be relevant to define gene expression, genetic vulnerability, or gene x environment interactions.\n\nParagraph 2: Describe the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of one twin or kinship **study (S)**.\n\nClosing statement: Show the **link (L)** between the results of the study and the question. Explain what the study demonstrates in relation to establishing a relationship between genetic similarity and behavior."}]},{"name":"Hormones & Pheromones","questions":[{"ref":"PS0305","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0305\n\nExplain the effect of one pheromone on one human behaviour.\n\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the responses include all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nThe first few sentences provide a direct answer to the question (*e.g. “Pheromones can influence human behaviour by triggering subconscious responses such as attraction, arousal, or social bonding.” / “Pheromones can impact human behaviour by subtly influencing attraction and mate selection through subconscious chemical signals.”*)\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of what pheromones are and how they affect human behaviour by linking pheromones to a specific behaviour. The answer is focused on the command term **‘explain’**, which seeks to give a detailed account including reasons and causes related to *pheromones*. (e.g. *Definition of pheromones, the role of pheromones to any human behaviour such as attraction*).\n\nExamples of some chemicals linked to pheromones are MHC or major histocompatibility complex (linked to mate selection, body odour preferences & immune system response), AND or androstadienone (*linked to elevated mood & arousal, attention to emotional cues*) & estratetraenol (*linked to attraction, trust, and social bonding*). It is best to add information on the ambiguous nature of pheromones.\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study – including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion, where the study’s aim is linked to pheromones.\\\n\n*Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n- McCoy & Pitino (2002) study on whether synthesised female pheromones increase sociosexual behaviour of women.\n\n- Lundstrom & Olsson (2005) studied the effects of androstadienone and its effect on women’s mood.\n\n- Cutler, Friedman, & McCoy’s (1998) research focused on investigating whether synthesised male pheromones increase sociosexual behaviour of men. \n\n- Wedekind’s (1995) research on the effect of MHC and the role it plays in mate selection in humans.\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study described to pheromones (*e.g. ‘The results of the research showed that the androstadienone increased women’s mood in the presence of a male experimenter and it had no effect when the experimenter was a female. The research clearly shows that androstadienone as a pheromone intensifies women’s reactions to men and also signals sexual attractiveness towards the opposite gender.’*)\n\n[9 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nPheromones are chemical messengers that carry stimulation and play an important role in mating behaviour for mammals.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ There are still debates about the existence of pheromones in human behaviour with supporting and non supporting evidence.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Many pheromones usually have a distinct smell. Mammals have a separate structure called the vomeronasal organ and is located in the anterior nasal cavity. This organ is missing in most humans and hence the debate with the existence of pheromones. $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ One example of a chemical associated with pheromones is AND or androstadienon.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ \n\nOne study conducted by Lundstrom and Olsson (2005) studied the effects of androstadienone.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Androstadienone is a chemical component of sweat and a derivative of testosterone.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  The participants were 37 females who were exposed to two conditions, condition 1 being exposed to androstadienone and condition 2 being a control condition. In both conditions the participants were in front of a male or a female experimenter. The experimenters used questionnaires to measure the mood of the participants.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nThe results of the research showed that the androstadienone increased women’s mood in the presence of a male experimenter and it had no effect when the experimenter was a female.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nThe research clearly shows that androstadienone as a pheromone intensifies women’s reactions to men and also signals sexual attractiveness towards the opposite gender.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$  \n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link"},{"ref":"PS0510","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0510\n\nExplain how one hormone may affect behaviour using one relevant study.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nThere should be a direct answer to the question within the first few lines. A specific hormone affecting a behaviour which is being investigated through the study must be mentioned. (*e.g. One hormone that is linked to behaviour is cortisol which is often associated with stress and verbal declarative memory.*)\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nThere should be accurate knowledge and understanding of the topic by using all or some key terms, such as:\n\n-    Hormones - chemical substances that help pass information from one part of the body to another through the bloodstream.\n\n-    Endocrine system - a network of glands that helps to control various bodily functions through hormones.\n\n-    Target cells - Cells that receive the messages from the hormones as they have the specific receptors for the hormones\n\n-    Receptors - detect signals from different hormones/neurotransmitters\n\nThe focus of the response should be on the effects of hormones on human behaviour and not its physiological functions. This should be done in a rational and easy-to-understand manner.\n\nThe command term $`$explain' seeks to provide a detailed account including possible causes or reasons. Therefore, while the explanation of the effect of hormones is essential there should also be a focus on meeting the command term. The possible reasons for a change in that behaviour due to hormone fluctuations should also be considered in the essay (*e.g. This could be due to changes in…*)\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study - including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study.\\\n\n *Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n-    Newcomer et.al (1999) on the effect of Cortisol on Verbal declarative memory\n\n-    Ronay and von Hippel (2010) on the effect of testosterone in risk-taking behaviour of males\n\n-    Mc Gaugh and Cahill (1995) on the effect of adrenaline on flashbulb memories\n\n-    Zethraeus et. al (2009) on the effect of oestrogen and testosterone on human economic behaviour\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nThere should be a clear link between the results of the study and the topic using one of the key terms mentioned in the introduction. For example, using Mcgaugh and Cahill (1995) on the effect of adrenaline on flashbulb memories  a possible link would be *$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Adrenaline released during a stressful situation such as the dramatic story in the first experiment led to the formation of flashbulb memories in the participants as measured by their performance on the recognition task 2 weeks later.$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$*\n\n**Marking limits:**\n\n-    Mixing up hormones with neurotransmitters. (*no marks will be awarded*)\n\n-    A relevant study is described but no clear links to the effect of hormones on behaviour are made (*a maximum of 5 marks will be awarded*)\n\n-    Wrong hormone linked to the behaviour (*a maximum of 3 marks may be deducted*)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nHormones are chemicals secreted by glands in the endocrine system directly in the bloodstream. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  As they are secreted in the bloodstream and not in the synaptic gaps, it takes time to produce an effect on behaviour but they are more long-lasting than a typical neurotransmitter response. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The hormones bind themselves to specific receptor sites known as target cells bringing about a change in behaviour.Hormones affect human behaviour in myriad ways as a single hormone can have effects on multiple behaviours. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Testosterone, a hormone produced by the testes in males is known for its effect on aggression and aggressive behaviour. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ However, there is also evidence of its effect on risk-taking behaviour seen in males as suggested by the results of the study conducted by Ronay and von Hippel (2010). $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\n   \n\nRonay and von Hippel (2010) carried out a study on 96 young adult male skateboarders to find out if testosterone affected their risk-taking behaviour. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Participants were divided into two conditions - one with a male researcher and the other with an attractive female researcher. All the participants were asked to practise two $`$types' of tricks which they could do fairly well - an $`$easy' trick and a $`$difficult' trick. After this, they were asked to attempt the same tricks either in front of the same male researcher or an attractive female researcher. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Their behaviour was coded in one of the three ways, a successful attempt, crash landing or aborted attempt. Biometric measurements of saliva samples were collected to measure the level of testosterone. Results from the study suggested that participants in the female researcher condition took greater risks as they had fewer aborted attempts in the $`$difficult' tricks. In addition, testosterone was higher in this group as measured by saliva samples. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe study concluded that testosterone had an effect on the risk-taking behaviour of males as they were willing to attempt more difficult tricks and did not abort it in the presence of an attractive female researcher. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ This can be clearly linked to the increased level of testosterone as there was a clear difference in the performance between the two groups  which occurred due to the presence of the female researcher. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\nTestosterone is a reproductive hormone that is associated with the sexual behaviour of males having clear links to the mating behaviour observed in males. As can be seen from the study, the skateboarders performed the $`$difficult' tricks with fewer aborted attempts meaning they were willing to take more risks in the presence of a female researcher as compared to when a male researcher was present indicating the mating behaviour linked to the presence of increased levels of testosterone. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nRonay, R., $\\&$ Von Hippel, F. (2010). Power, testosterone, and risk-taking. Journal of Behavioral Decision Making, 23(5), 473-482. https://doi.org/10.1002/bdm.671"},{"ref":"PS0511","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0511\n\nExplain how one pheromone can affect behaviour, using one study.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nThere should be a direct answer to the question within the first few lines.\n\n(*e.g. One study that explains the role of pheromones on human behaviour Doucet et a.'s research in 2009.*) A specific pheromone affecting a behaviour which is being investigated through the study must be mentioned. However, it should be pointed out that this area is still under research and all research/evidence only gives an indication of the possible role of pheromones in human behaviour. (*e.g. There is research suggesting the possibility of the pheromones playing a role in human mating behaviour.*)\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nThere should be accurate knowledge and understanding of the topic by using all or some key terms, explained in a cohesive manner such as:\n\n-    Pheromones - the focus of the response should be on the possible effects of Pheromones in human behaviour. There should be a clear explanation for why there is no complete evidence of its role in human behaviour but it is generally known to be present in animals.  This should be done in a rational and easy-to-understand manner.\n\n-    Vomeronasal organ (VNO) - An olfactory organ not fully developed in humans said to influence mating and social behaviour.\n\n-    Olfactory senses - to detect odours and smells \n\n-    Androstadienone (AND) - a pheromone secreted in male sweat that may be potentially attractive to females\n\n-    MHC genes - major histocompatibility complex (MHC), a group of genes influencing our immune system response\n\nThe command term explain seeks to provide a detailed account including possible causes or reasons. While the explanation of the effect of pheromones is essential, there should also be a focus on meeting the command term. The types of behaviours in which pheromones are considered to be impactful must be considered. For example: *Pheromones may impact behaviours that take place between two individuals such as bonding, mating etc.*\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study - including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study. *Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n-    Wedekind et.al (1995) on the potential role of MHC genes on mating behaviour.\n\n-    Doucet et.al (2009) on the effect of secretion of the mammary glands on an infant's sucking behaviour. \n\n-    Cutler, Friedman and McCoy (1998) on investigating if a synthesised pheromone can increase socio sexual behaviour of men\n\n-    Lundstrom and Olsson (2005) on the effect of AND on women in the presence of men\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nThere should be a clear link between the results of the study and the topic using one of the key terms mentioned in the introduction. For example: *Wedekind et.al (1995) on the potential role of MHC genes on mating behaviour - Females rated the t-shirts with MHC genes different from their own as highly pleasant when compared to their rating on the t-shirts with similar MHC genes. This links to the potential role of Pheromones as they are considered to affect human behaviour through their sense of smell.*\n\n**Marking limits:**\n\n-    Mixing up pheromones with hormones. (*no marks will be awarded*) \n\n-    A relevant study is described, but no clear links to the effect of pheromones on behaviour are made as it can be tricky to make explicit links. (*a maximum of 5 marks will be awarded*)\n\n-    Jumping straight to the research without a clear explanation on how Pheromones work (*a maximum of 3 marks may be deducted*)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nPheromones are considered to be chemical messengers that work by interacting between individuals. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The olfactory senses are responsible for the transmission of pheromones as one of the ways they are secreted in the human body is through sweat.  They are theorised to work through the Vomeronasal organ (VNO)found in mammals giving them an advanced sense of smell. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The smell then triggers the hypothalamus in the brain stimulating emotions such as attraction, sexual arousal etc. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ While the role of Pheromones is observed clearly in animals, their effect on human behaviour is still an area under research, as even though this organ is present in humans, it is not as well-developed as animals. Pheromones can potentially effect human mating behaviour as suggested in the study done by Wedekind et.al (1995). $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nWedekind et al. (1995) conducted a study on 49 female and 44 male university students. He aimed to test whether MHC genes (Major Histocompatibility Complex) influence human mate preferences. MHC genes play an important role in the immune system and vary between individuals based on the alleles inherited from their parents. Evolutionary theory suggests that people may be attracted to partners with MHC genes different from their own, as this could increase the immune strength of their offspring.  $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nAll participants were genetically tested to identify their MHC type. The male participants were not allowed to wear deodorant and were asked to sleep in the same plain T-shirt for two nights. They were also instructed not to engage in sexual activity or other behaviours that might affect their natural body odour. The female participants used a nasal spray to reduce the risk of colds and were tested mostly during the second week of their menstrual cycle. In the laboratory, the women were presented with seven boxes containing T-shirts — three from MHC-similar men, three from MHC-dissimilar men, and one unworn control shirt. They rated each odour for pleasantness.  $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe results showed that women generally rated the odours of MHC-dissimilar men as more pleasant than those of MHC-similar men. This suggests that biological factors, such as MHC-related body odour, may play a role in attraction.\n\n $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nTherefore, the study provides support for the possible role of pheromone-like signals in human attraction, as women generally rated the odours of MHC-dissimilar men as more pleasant. The finding that preferences were reversed in women taking oral contraceptives suggests that hormonal factors influence MHC-based odour preferences. Since the women only smelled the T-shirts, the results indicate that body odour may act as a biological cue in attraction, although this does not prove the existence of human pheromones.  $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link"},{"ref":"PS0527","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0527\n\nDescribe one study on the effect of one hormone to human behaviour.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\n A direct answer to the question provided in the first few sentences (e.g. *“Hormones affect human behaviour in various ways, one of which is…… One study that demonstrates this effect is….”)*\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{2. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study $\\text{\\textendash}$ including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study\n\n            *Relevant studies include but are not limited to: *\n\n-    *Newcomer et.al (1999,) on the effect of Cortisol on Verbal declarative memory*\n\n-    *Ronay and von Hippel (2010), on the effect of testosterone in risk-taking behaviour of males*\n\n-    *Mc Gaugh and Cahill (1995), on the effect of adrenaline on flashbulb memories*\n\n-    *Zethraeus et. al (2009), on the effect of oestrogen and testosterone on human economic behaviour*\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{3. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of one hormone and its effects on human behaviour.\n\n           Important concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define include:\n\n-    Laboratory experiment $\\text{\\textendash}$ An experiment in a lab with well defined IV and DV\n\n-    Independent variable (IV $\\text{\\textendash}$ Variable that is manipulated in the experiment\n\n-    Dependent Variable (DV) $\\text{\\textendash}$ Variable that is measured in the experiment.\n\n-    Cause and effect relationship $\\text{\\textendash}$ The relationship between two variables with one causing another.\n\n-    Hormones $\\text{\\textendash}$  chemical substances that help in passing information from one part of the body to other through the bloodstream. Provide one specific hormone, such as but not limited to the following: cortisol, adrenaline, oxytocin \n\n-    Target cells $\\text{\\textendash}$  Cells that receive the messages from the hormones as they have the specific receptors for the hormones\n\n-    Receptors - detect signals from different hormones/neurotransmitters\n\n-    Human behaviour - focus on a specific human behaviour related to the hormone in focus\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study to the topic (e.g. *Mcgaugh and Cahill (1995) concluded that adrenaline released during a stressful situation such as the dramatic story in the first experiment led to the formation of flashbulb memories in the participants as measured by their performance on the recognition task 2 weeks later. This result clearly suggests that adrenaline plays a role in the formation of flashbulb memories.)*\n\n**Marking reductions:**\n\n-    Mixing up hormones with neurotransmitters. (no marks will be awarded)\n\n-    A relevant study is described but no clear links to the effect of hormones on behaviour are made (a maximum of 5 marks will be awarded).\n\n-    The aim, methodology and results of the study is not described properly(marks will be deducted based on the context of the study).\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \n\nHormones are described as chemicals secreted by glands in the endocrine system directly in the bloodstream having various effects on human behaviour$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$. As they are secreted in the bloodstream and not in the synaptic gaps, it takes time to produce an effect but they are more long-lasting than a typical neurotransmitter response. The hormones bind themselves to specific receptor sites known as target cells bringing about a change in behaviour $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. Research in this field is often carried out in lab conditions with a clearly defined IV and DV to determine a strong cause-and-effect relationship $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$.  A  lab experiment conducted by Newcomer et al in 1999 gives evidence of the effect of the hormone Cortisol on Verbal declarative memory (human behaviour) $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$.\n\n   \n\nNewcomer et.al (1999) carried out a study on a population of students and employees from Washington University Medical Centre. Participants were divided into three conditions - group one in a high cortisol condition, ingesting 60 mg of cortisol similar to those experiencing a highly stressful event. Group 2 ingested 40 mg of cortisol similar to a moderately stressful condition. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The last group was a control group that had a placebo pill. All participants underwent a baseline test to ensure there were no individual differences in their cognitive performance as it could become a confounding variable. The participants were asked to memorise a prose passage and were tested for the number of errors made. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Results showed that the high cortisol group performed the worst and the control group performed the best. This clearly indicates the effect of the hormone cortisol on verbal declarative memory as high levels of the hormone significantly impaired human behaviour. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\n The study had a well-defined independent variable - the different levels of cortisol ingested by the participants and an accurate measurement for the dependent variable which was the number of errors made by the participants while recalling the passage. Additionally, this was a double-blind experiment ensuring that both the participants and the experimenters were unaware of who belonged in which group thereby ensuring a strong cause-and-effect relationship $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. Thus, the participant's memory can be linked to the increased level of cortisol as there was a clear difference in the performance between the three groups  which occurred due to the changes in the level of cortisol  $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$.\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nNewcomer, J. W., Selke, G., Melson, A. K., Hershey, T., Craft, S., Richards, K., Alderson, A. (1999). Decreased memory performance in healthy humans induced by Stress-Level cortisol treatment. Archives of General Psychiatry, 56(6), 527$\\text{\\textendash}$533. https://doi.org/10.1001/archpsyc.56.6.527"},{"ref":"PS0543","marks":"22","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0543\n\nDiscuss the potential role of pheromones in human behaviour\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained and addressed consistently throughout the response. The question used the command term *discuss* paired with a topic, therefore, the response should offer a balanced review of pheromones. This could involve perspectives, issues, or uncertainties regarding understanding pheromones.\n\n**1 mark** if the question is either simply restated, misinterpreted (*though still related to pheromones, e.g. This essay will evaluate research on pheromones*), or if the overall response does not address the prompt (*e.g. the candidate writes in their introduction that they will discuss pheromones, but primarily evaluates research*).\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant $\\&$ appropriate concepts and terminology related to pheromones are explained in detail (*e.g.androstadienone, estratetraenol, sexual selection, vomeronasal organ etc.*). Some marks will also be awarded for criterion B for knowledge of research methods and how they are used in the study context.\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors (*e.g. pheromones are described accurately but additional vocabulary terms – such as the vomeronasal organ, bioassays, etc – are not correctly applied*), or if the response only shows strong methodological knowledge.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited (*e.g. having inaccurate definitions of pheromones or being unable to describe why a chemical may be a pheromone*).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (*e.g., aim, procedures, results $\\&$ conclusion*) are thoroughly explained and used to support the answer. Relevant studies include, but are not limited to:\n- Savic et al (2001), which used PET to study neural responses to two potential pheromones, androstadienone and estratetraenol\n- Wedekind et al (1995), a study which suggests that women may perceive the smell of men with differing major histocompatibility complexes as more attractive\n- Zhou et al (2014), which tested the role androstadienone and estratetraenol may play in sex perception\n- Lundstrom & Olsson (2005), which explored andostadienone’s influence on women’s mood\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (*e.g the aim, procedure, results $\\&$ conclusions of the study were not clearly presented*)\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. (*e.g the study was not linked to a broader discussion of pheromones or one of multiple studies presented is not related to the topic*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and elaborated discussion regarding pheromones. Candidates should ideally develop 3 or more distinct claims throughout their discussion. For this prompt, this may include discussing biological structures for detecting pheromones, the influence of social and cultural factors in mate selection (other than scent), challenges replicating research findings related to specific pheromones, and/or the need to separate ‘scent’ from ‘pheromones’ in variable operationalisation.\n\n**3-4 marks** if 1-2 lines of discussion (see above) are present and explained but are not fully developed and linked back to the prompt.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the claims within the discussion are superficial, mainly descriptive (*e.g the claims were only stated or described but not linked back to research or the overall topic*), repetitive, or if the included critical thinking primarily centers on strengths and limitations of research studies (rather than the topic).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organisation}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is an organised $\\&$ coherent explanation of one or more pheromones, and an evaluation of one or more relevant studies (see Criterion C). (*e.g. first paragraph introduces the focus on the question and related terminology, the succeeding paragraphs talk about related research/considerations in interpreting them as support for pheromones, a synthesis paragraph which presents and explores a range of claims, and the closing paragraphs or statements summarise how the prompt was addressed*)\n\n**1 mark** if organisation and clarity were not sustained.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\nPheromones are heavily disputed in the scientific community for many reasons. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ Pheromones, in non-human species, are chemicals secreted or emitted outside of an organism’s body that other members of that species detect. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Pheromones can serve a wide range of communicative purposes in animals: to share information about health, mating availability, or fear. But the existence and function of pheromones in humans is widely contested, in part because there is a lack of convincing research to support its existence in humans. However, there may be some limited biological evidence for pheromones in humans. Two studies that highlight these areas of discussion are Zhou et al and Wedekind et al, both of which sought to identify or test the role of potential human pheromones in behaviors related to sexual selection. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nZhou et al explored two specific chemical substances: estratetraenol (EST) and androstadienone (AND). $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ Both of these substances potentially communicate information about the sex status of members of another species, and if they could reliably produce a specific behavior or perception, they could be considered human pheromones. EST is produced by women, and AND is produced by men. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Using a diverse sample involving both heterosexual and homosexual men and women, Zhou et al wanted to understand whether or not being exposed to these substances would influence the perception of gender of androgynous stick figures. In a double-blind study, each participant was administered a dose of either AND, EST (which was mixed with cloves to hide any potential unpleasant or detectable smell of the chemical), or a control also mixed with cloves, asked to view the stick figure’s motion on the screen, and then asked to report whether the figure was more masculine (male) or feminine (female). Researchers found that heterosexual women and homosexual men were more likely to perceive the sex of the stick figure as male when they had been exposed to AND, and heterosexual men were more likely to perceive the stick figure as female when exposed to EST. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ The results of this study could suggest that these substances communicate important information to members of our species who experience sexual and/or romantic attraction to members of our biological sex. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nUnlike Zhou, Wedekind does not attempt to isolate a specific pheromone, but suggests that there is some information in our body odor that somewhat reliably communicates about a potential partner’s “genetic fit.” $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ Researchers hypothesized that because there is an evolutionary advantage to mating with people with varied immune system genetics, referred to as major histocompatibility complexes (MHCs), that women would find the scents of men with relatively different MHCs more attractive. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Also a lab experiment, researchers asked men with varying MHC compositions to wear a plain shirt for multiple days while avoiding the use of artificial scents or deodorants. These t-shirts were then laid in cardboard boxes, and women not on any form of contraception were asked to smell a set of seven of these t-shirts. Approximately half of these shirts belonged to men with very different MHCs than them, and half were similar. One was an unworn control. The women were asked to rate the attractiveness, pleasantness, and sexiness of each shirt’s smell. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ The study found that women tended to rate shirts worn by men with different MHCs as more pleasant than the shirts worn by men with more similar MHCs. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$  Because producing offspring with a partner with different MHCs produces children with higher birth weight and other health indicators that favor survival, it could be the case that this advantageous preference is something that has been naturally selected for over time in humans. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ While Wedekind does not isolate a specific chemical, it could support the idea that there is something olfactory that is being secreted and detected by humans which carries meaning to other members of our species. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nOverall, a combination of challenges with research and also realistic use of pheromones make it difficult to assert that they play a significant role in human behavior. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ Experimental studies like Zhou et al and Wedekind use rigorous controls to eliminate confounding variables and allow us to conclude that the pheromone is what is causing the change in each study’s dependent variable. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$  This strength actually also serves as a limitation: for pheromones to exist in humans, we must see that they are reliably used by all members of a species to communicate information in everyday environments. In Wedekind, the t-shirts accumulated scents over multiple days without the use of perfumed detergents or soaps. This is not typical. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$  In Zhou et al, the concentrations of AND and EST were magnitudes higher than what would ever be secreted by one person on one day. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nHumans manipulate their own scents, and nearly all cultures have hygienic practices that use perfumes and other artificial scents to enhance our desirability to prospective partners. In other words, we would not be smelling and making mating choices based on pheromones, but on perfumes. In addition, the organ that mammals use to sense pheromones – the vomeronasal organ – is vestigial in most humans, with no active sensory neurons associated with this region of the nose. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ This means we would not be able to identify how humans can sense pheromones without identifying an alternative biological mechanism that is unique to humans, which is unlikely. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$  Finally, the populations of these studies tend to be Western – which, keeping in mind that some human populations lack the gene that produces body odor, threatens the assumptions of studies such as Wedekind that pheromones may be both released and understood by all members of a species.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nOverall, there seem to be many difficulties in presuming the use of pheromones to influence mating behavior in humans, ranging from poor generalizability to real world scenarios to biological practicalities of sensing pheromones from other members of our species.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nWord count: 979\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\nWedekind, C., Seebeck, T., Bettens, F., & Paepke, A. J. (1995). MHC-dependent mate preferences in humans. Proceedings. Biological sciences, 260(1359), 245–249.  https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.1995.0087\n\nZhou, Wen, Xiaoying Yang, Kepu Chen, Peng Cai, Sheng He, and Yi Jiang. \"Chemosensory Communication of Gender through Two Human Steroids in a Sexually Dimorphic Manner.\" Current Biology 24.10 (2014): 1091-095."},{"ref":"PS0547","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0547\n\nDiscuss the role of hormones on behaviour or cognition.\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained and addressed consistently throughout the response. The question used the command term *discuss* paired with a topic, therefore, the response should offer a balanced review of hormones. This could involve perspectives, issues, or uncertainties when it comes to understanding the role of hormones in behaviour or cognition.\n\n**1 mark** if the question is either simply restated, misinterpreted (*though still related to pheromones, e.g. This essay will evaluate research on hormones*), or if the overall response does not address the prompt (*e.g. the candidate writes in their introduction that they will discuss hormones, but primarily evaluates research*).\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant $\\&$ appropriate concepts and terminology related to hormones are explained in detail (*e.g. pituitary gland, target cells*). Some marks will also be awarded for criterion B for knowledge of research methods and how they are used in the study context.\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors (*e.g. hormones are described accurately but additional vocabulary terms – such as a permissive effect – are not correctly applied*), or if the response only shows strong methodological knowledge.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited (*e.g. having inaccurate definitions of hormones or being unable to clearly specify behaviours or cognitive processes related to one or more hormones*).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (*e.g., aim, procedures, results & conclusion*) are thoroughly explained and used to support the answer. Relevant studies include, but are not limited to:\n- McGaugh and Cahill (1995) on adrenaline and flashbulb memory\n- Zak (2009) on testosterone and aggression\n- Baumgartner (2008) on oxytocin and trust\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (*e.g the aim, procedure, results & conclusions of the study were not clearly presented*)\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. (*e.g the study was not linked to a broader discussion of hormones or one of multiple studies presented is not related to the topic*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and elaborated discussion regarding hormones. Candidates should ideally develop 3 or more distinct claims throughout their discussion. For this prompt, this may include discussing the complexities of attributing behavior on a single biological factor, challenges in measuring hormones, and/or competing explanations for the behaviours/cognitive functions discussed.\n\n**3-4 marks** if 1-2 lines of discussion (see above) are present and explained but are not fully developed and linked back to the prompt.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the claims within the discussion are superficial, mainly descriptive (*e.g the claims were only stated or described but not linked back to research or the overall topic*), repetitive, or if the included critical thinking primarily centers on strengths and limitations of research studies (rather than the topic).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organisation}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is an organised $\\&$ coherent explanation of one or more hormones, and an evaluation of one or more relevant studies (see Criterion C). (*e.g. first paragraph introduces the focus on the question and related terminology, the succeeding paragraphs talk about related research/considerations in interpreting them as support for the role of a hormone, a synthesis paragraph presents and explores a range of claims, and the closing paragraphs or statements summarise how the prompt was addressed*)\n\n**1 mark** if organisation and clarity were not sustained.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\nHormones are chemicals which are released and regulated by the endocrine system. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  They travel through our bloodstream to target cells, and when it makes a connection with a receptor site on those cells they either increase or decrease its function. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  Some hormones, like testosterone and adrenaline, can have impacts on brain function. In contrast to neurotransmitters, however, hormones can take longer to produce changes in behaviour and cognition. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Testosterone facilitates aggressive behaviour and also correlates with risk seeking behaviour because of its effect of impairing prefrontal cortex activity. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Adrenaline is linked to the fight or flight response and can activate the amygdala. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Overall, these two hormones present examples of how hormones can impact both behaviour and cognitive processes. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nRonay and von Hippel wanted to understand the relationship between testosterone and risk-seeking behaviours. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Ronay and von Hippel recruited 96 young adult, male skateboarders at a park. The researchers asked the participants to do one easy trick and one difficult trick, which they could reliably complete about half of the time. They were asked to repeat each trick 10 times. After a short break, they were asked to do the same again but this time in front of either the same male researcher that had recorded observations of their tricks in the first round, or an attractive female researcher. For the same tricks, the researchers recorded the number of times they were successful at landing their difficult trick, how many times they bailed out before attempting, and how many times they had a crash landing. High rates of bailing out from the trick early correlates with low risk-taking, and high rates of crash landing correlates with high risk-taking. Salivary testosterone was measured to understand the role testosterone may play in risk-taking. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe researchers found that participants were more willing to take risks and follow through with their difficult trick when they were in front of the attractive female researcher. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Also, testosterone in each participants’ saliva was measured, and it was greater in the group which was in front of the female researcher. The researchers suggested that testosterone impacts the function of the prefrontal cortex, a region of the brain associated with higher order decision-making. It could be that this has developed as an evolutionary mechanism: upon seeing a suitable partner, testosterone is released and as a cascading series of responses throughout the brain and body, the part of the brain responsible for telling us to not take risks is impaired. This can reduce fear and second-guessing and may have been selected for overtime as risk-taking can allow a male to demonstrate their physical fitness and competitiveness. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThis was a well controlled study that took place in naturalistic settings, improving the external validity of the study. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ There are some limitations to this study that interfere with our ability to understand the role of testosterone, however. Most critically, it is difficult to standardize the selection of difficult tricks. It is unclear if all of the tricks were of a similar level of difficulty for each participant. If participants selected tricks that were either too difficult to complete or too easy, the biological response to the task may be altered. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$  Additional participant characteristics, such as testosterone level differences among participants, may also interfere with the findings. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Random allocation was used, and there was a pretest posttest design allowing for participants to be measured both with an unattractive and attractive researcher present. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ This control, however, leads to potential concerns with order effects, like fatigue. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nAn example of a hormone’s impact on cognition is adrenaline’s impact on emotional learning, specifically related to flashbulb memory creation. McGaugh and Cahill explored the role of adrenaline and the flight-or-fight response in the creation of flashbulb memories. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ In part one of their study, they randomly allocated participants to two conditions: one saw 12 slides and heard a visceral story about a car accident in which a boy had his limbs severed. The participants heard many details about his trip to the hospital and the work of surgeons who reattached his limbs. In the other condition, participants heard a comparatively dull story about a family who came to visit someone in a hospital and witnessed a safety drill while there. The first condition was designed to create a greater emotional experience, whereas the latter was not. Researchers hypothesized that memory would be greater in the first condition. Sure enough, participants both rated the vivid, shocking story as more emotional, and recalled more details from the story and slides than the mundane story condition. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nIn a follow up study, researchers administered propranolol, a beta blocker that blocks the target cells for the hormone adrenaline. When the study was repeated as a 2x2 factorial design (one level being the propranolol, the other being the intensity of the story), the condition which heard the high-emotion story and was administered the beta blocker recalled information at the same rate and accuracy as participants in the mundane story conditions. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThis suggests that adrenaline, which is a stress hormone that activates the amygdala, plays an important role in creating memories from emotionally arousing events, such as flashbulb memories. Because the amygdala is associated with emotional learning, blocking activation of this brain region reduces learning to the same rate as ‘safe,’ ordinary events. The use of a true experiment allows us to understand this cause-and-effect relationship between adrenaline, amygdala activation, and flashbulb memories. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ The study is highly replicable as well, and so it’s possible to establish the reliability of these findings. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Yet, a tradeoff of this study is that the high control over variables reduces our ability to presume that this is what we would observe in real life, outside of the confines of a lab. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nOverall, both studies demonstrate the impacts of hormones on both behaviour and cognition. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nWord count: 979\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\nMcGaugh, J. L., Cahill, L., & Roozendaal, B. (1996). Involvement of the amygdala in memory storage: interaction with other brain systems. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America, 93(24), 13508–13514. https:// doi.org/10.1073/pnas.93.24.13508\n\nRonay, R., & von Hippel, W. (2010). The presence of an attractive woman elevates testosterone and physical risk taking in young men. Social Psychological and Personality Science, 1(1), 57–64. https:// doi.org/10.1177/1948550609352807"},{"ref":"PS0565","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0565\n\nEvaluate one or more study/ies on the effect of one or more hormones on human behaviour.\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\n**Criterion A: Focus on the question**\\\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The \n\nquestion used the command term *evaluate*, therefore, the response should \n\nidentify, explain, and weigh the strengths and limitations of one or more studies on hormones and its effect on human behavior.\n\n**1 mark** if the command term is not accurately explained or limited explanation.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\\\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant $\\&$ appropriate terminology is explained in detail (e.g. hormones, effect of hormones on human behaviour). Since the question asks to evaluate studies, marks will also be awarded for criterion B for knowledge of research methods and how they are used in the study context.\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors and if the response only showed knowledge about research \n\nMethod (brief explanation of hormones with little focus on evaluation of study). \n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited with no evaluation of studies.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}\\ }$\\\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (e.g., aim, procedures, results $\\&$ \n\nconclusion) are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments \n\nstated. Studies to be used are but not limited to:\n\n$1.$ De Dreu et al (2012) - the role of oxytocin in inter-group conflict\n\n$2.$ Scheele et al (2012) - oxytocin promoting fidelity\n\n$3.$ Kosfeld et al (2005) - role of oxytocin in interpersonal trust\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (e.g the aim, procedure, results $\\&$ conclusions of the study was not clearly presented) \n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. (e.g the study was not linked to the evaluation points mentioned)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\\\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed evaluation points (e.g around 2-3 strengths and limitations) of research on one bias (there is high internal validity as a cause and effect relationship can be observed between the IV and DV ). \n\n**3-4 marks** of the evaluation points (e.g around 1-2 strengths $\\&$ limitations) are present and clearly explained but are not sustained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation or discussion points are superficial (evaluation points only stated but not explained properly).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\\\n\n**2 marks** if a clear link to be made at the end of the answer focusing on hormone, behaviour and the study used. The link can be made with the help of hormones and how it affects any one human behaviour. The answer must be organized and clear with appropriate information provided. \n\n**1 mark** if the organization and clarity were not sustained throughout the answer.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nHormones are chemical messengers that are released from the endocrine glands directly into the bloodstream to influence behaviour such as aggression, mating behaviour. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The effect of hormones is long lasting and affects important bodily functions such as growth, metabolism and reproduction$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$There is very little voluntary control on the production and effect of hormones. Hormones alter the chances of a behaviour occurrence in response to environmental factors (gene activation or gene suppression). This essentially means that when a hormone binds to a receptor it launches a sequence of changes as a result of environmental stimuli. This explains how oxytocin promotes fidelity.\n\nThere are a variety of hormones produced in the body, a few examples are oxytocin, cortisol, adrenaline$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. \n\nThis answer will focus on oxytocin and its influence on human behaviour. Oxytocin is produced in the hypothalamus and is transported into the bloodstream by the pituitary gland. It plays a role in sexual reproduction (also released with every kiss or hug), childbirth (oxytocin is released from stimulation of nipples during breastfeeding) and social bonding. It is also called a love and bonding hormone. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$The study conducted by Scheele et al (2012) on oxytocin promoting fidelity is explained below. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nThe research conducted by Scheele et al in 2012, aimed to demonstrate whether oxytocin would lead to influencing men in a relationship to keep greater distance from female strangers and whether it promotes fidelity$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. There were a total 86 heterosexual male adults in the study, out of which some were singles and others in a stable relationship$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. It was a double-blind experiment using an independent measures design. A researcher administered either oxytocin or a placebo intranasally to the two groups of participants. All participants took part in 2 independent tasks, one of which was the $`$stop-distance paradigm'. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$In this scenario, participants were to be positioned at one end of the room with a mark on the floor, while an attractive female experimenter was to stand on the other end of the room. The participants were asked to move slowly towards the female experimenter and stop at a point that made them uncomfortable. A variable controlled throughout was that the experimenter maintained the same appearance over all the trials.\n\nResults found that in the oxytocin group, men in a monogamous relationship kept a greater distance between themselves and an attractive woman compared to single men. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ It was also found that in the oxytocin group men in a relationship tend to stay away from an attractive woman who was not their partner. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nIn the second part of the same experiment, a task called the $`$approach/avoidance task' was introduced in which participants viewed a series of pictures on a screen with a viewing distance of 50m. Each picture was shown for two seconds. There were a total of four types of photos, positive social or attractive women, positive non-social or beautiful landscapes, negative social or mutilations and negative non-social or dirt. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ A joystick was provided to all participants and they were instructed to pull the joystick if they liked the picture which would increase the picture's size. They were also told that if they didn't like the picture, they were to push the joystick backwards which would reduce the images' size. The results of this part of the experiment showed that the only group of pictures affected by oxytocin and relationship status was the positive social group (pictures of attractive women). The participants in the oxytocin group had slower reaction time and they were more reluctant to pull the joystick if they were in a relationship. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ These trials showed that oxytocin promotes fidelity in men who are in a relationship. \n\nThe research had several evaluation points. The strengths of the research are that there was no evidence of bias as due to its use of a double-blind measure. The results also show more credibility as the experiment had two parts to show the relationship between the independent and dependent variable. The study also has high internal validity as a cause and effect relationship can be established between the IV and DV as others variables were kept constant so that they do not affect the changes in the DV. This was done to see the effect of oxytocin (IV) on fidelity (DV).  There was also less effect of extraneous variables as it was a controlled lab experiment. The study also addressed ethical considerations showing evidence of physical or psychological harm to the participants. Another strength of a lab experiment is that it has high replicability due to its standardized procedure. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nThe limitations of the study are that the extraneous variables like duration of the relationship, problems in a relationship have not been taken into consideration which can alter the results as these factors also affect loyalty in a relationship. The attractiveness of the experimenter is a subjective factor which may have affected the results of the study. Another limitation of a lab experiment is that it may not always reflect real life situations as people behave differently in a lab setting versus their natural environment. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nThis study clearly supported how the hormone oxytocin has an effect on the fidelity in relationships in men. Other research methods such as semi interviews may also be used to understand factors that influence fidelity in men and women as it can help to collect qualitative data to understand the topic in depth. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nWord count: 906\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nScheele, D., Striepens, N., Güntürkün, O., Deutschländer, S., Maier, W., Kendrick, K. M., \\& Hurlemann, R. (2012). Oxytocin modulates social distance between males and females. The Journal of neuroscience : the official journal of the Society for Neuroscience, 32(46), 16074-16079. https://doi.org/10.1523/JNEUROSCI.2755-12.2012"},{"ref":"PS0690","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0690\n\nExplain one ethical consideration related to one study investigating hormones and/or pheromones and behaviour.\n\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\nOne ethical consideration in studying the effect of hormones on behaviour is informed consent, which should be obtained in writing before a research study begins. This is evident in Newcomer et al.'s study on cortisol and memory, which ensured that participants fully understood the potential risks before agreeing to participate in the study. \n\nNewcomer et al.'s study aimed to investigate whether high levels of the stress hormone cortisol affect verbal declarative memory (which is the ability to recall facts and knowledge).\n\nIn the study, participants were randomly split into three groups. The first group was given a high dose of cortisol (160 mg per day) to simulate the levels experienced during a major stress event. The second group received a lower dose (40 mg per day), mimicking a minor stress event. The third group acted as a control and was given a placebo (a tablet with no active ingredient). Participants listened to and recalled parts of a prose paragraph to test their memory. \n\nThe results showed that those who received the highest dose of cortisol performed the worst on the memory test but only after 4 days of exposure. However, their memory returned to normal after they stopped taking the hormone. The study concluded that high cortisol levels for extended periods interferes with verbal declarative memory.\n\nTo ensure that ethical standards were met, the researchers gained informed consent, which involved explaining the risks of taking cortisol, such as temporary physical side effects, including changes in blood pressure or immune response. Participants were informed about the study's purpose, the tasks they would perform, and the potential side effects. This process of providing clear information and ensuring participants voluntarily agreed is called informed consent. Gaining informed consent also involves informing participants about their rights, such as being able to withdraw at any time without penalty.\n\nNewcomer et al.'s study highlighted the importance of acquiring scientific knowledge through ethical measures, as seen in their acquisition of consent. While the study provided valuable insights into how stress hormones like cortisol affect memory, ethical considerations such as informed consent are essential to protecting the rights and well-being of the participants involved.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Mark Scheme}}$:\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible ideas \\& key terms:}}}$\n\n - Informed consent is an ethical consideration ensuring participants understand the risks and agree to participate voluntarily.\n\n - Informed consent requires clear communication about the study's purpose, procedures, potential risks, and the right to withdraw.\n\n - Ethical research practices protect participants' physical and psychological well-being while maintaining scientific integrity.\n\n - Ethical considerations are particularly important in studies involving hormones and their physiological and behavioral effects.\n\n - Other ethical considerations such as anonymity, confidentiality, deception, undue stress and harm, may also be used granted that the study tackles the specified ethical consideration.\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible key studies:}}}$\n\n - Include only one study on hormones and/or pheromones and focus on one ethical consideration of the study. For example: Newcomer et al. (1999) focused on cortisol and memory, and highlighted the use of informed consent.\n\n - Other studies that can be used are, but not limited to, the following:\n\n     - Studied the influence of cortisol on memory and the ethical consideration in focus is undue stress and harm: Buchanan & Lovallo (2001)\n\n     - Investigated the role of testosterone on trust, the ethical consideration in focus is protection from harm: Bos et al. (2010)\n\n     - Investigated the relationship between MHC (Major Histocompatibility Complex), which may be associated with pheromones in humans, and attractiveness, the ethical consideration in focus can be debriefing: Wedekind & Furi (1997)\n\n$\\textcolor{#d19f24}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible structure/organisation of response:}}}$\n\n - **Direct Answer (DA)**: Begin with a statement identifying an ethical consideration, *e.g.*, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$*One ethical consideration in studying the effect of hormones on behavior is informed consent.\" Introduce the relevant study (e.g., Newcomer et al.*) to contextualize the discussion.\n\n - Paragraph 1: **Define key terms (KT)**: Define informed consent, its importance in research, and how it protects participants' well-being. Link to one study involving hormones and/or pheromones\n\n - Paragraph 2: Describe the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of one **study (S)** linked to hormones and/or pheromones. Highlight specific ethical considerations used, such as informed consent, informing participants about potential stress and/or harm, and ensuring the right to withdraw.\n\n - Closing statement: Show the **link (L)** between the results of the study and the question. Explain how the study demonstrates ethical practices in research, emphasising the importance of informed consent in ensuring both participant safety and scientific rigour.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\n\n\\\n\nNewcomer, J. W., Selke, G., Melson, A. K., Hershey, T., Craft, S., Richards, K., \\& Alderson, A. L. (1999). *Decreased memory performance in healthy humans induced by stress-level cortisol treatment*. Archives of General Psychiatry, 56(6), 527-533. https://doi.org/10.1001/archpsyc.56.6.527"}]},{"name":"The Brain, Neuroplasticity, and Neurotransmitters","questions":[{"ref":"PS0304","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0304\n\nExplain neuroplasticity.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the responses include all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nThe first few sentences directly answer the question (*e.g. “Neuroplasticity is the brain's ability to reorganise itself by forming new neural connections throughout life.” / “Neuroplasticity refers to the brain's capacity to adapt and change in response to experiences, environmental influences, learning, and injury, allowing for forming new neural pathways and modifying existing ones.*). \n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nThe answer should be focused on the command term ‘explain,’ which gives a detailed account including reasons and causes (*e.g. Defining neuroplasticity, the process of neuroplasticity*). There is accurate knowledge and understanding of the process of neuroplasticity in relation to human behaviour. Key terminology such as the following may be used to describe neuroplasticity: *long*$\\text{\\textemdash}$*term potentiation, neurogenesis, dendritic branching, synaptic pruning, and changes in neural connections.* \n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study – including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion. The aim should be linked to neuroplasticity.\\\n\n*Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n- Draganski et al. (2004) studied the changes in grey matter due to training\n\n- Maguire et al. (2000) research on taxi drivers and their memory.\n\n- Gaser & Schlaug (2003) investigated the effect of brain plasticity in musicians.\n\n- Draganski et al. (2006) studied the effect of brain plasticity in medical students.\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the appropriate study to neuroplasticity (e.g. *‘The research clearly showed that as a result of learning a new activity like juggling, the neuroplasticity increased for the juggling group compared to the other group. There was also a correlation between the juggling performance and the amount of change in the brain plasticity.’*)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nNeuroplasticity refers to the brain's ability to reorganize and adapt by forming new neural connections, which is crucial for learning and memory.  At the level of a single neuron, it is called synaptic plasticity, and when brain area X assumes the functions of brain area Y, it is known as cortical remapping. Neuroplasticity happens on a day$\\text{\\textemdash}$to$\\text{\\textemdash}$day basis as a result of any new learning as a result of brain reshaping. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nLong$\\text{\\textemdash}$term potentiation strengthens synapses through repeated firing, enhancing memory formation and learning processes.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Neurogenesis, the birth of new neurons, fosters cognitive flexibility and adaptation to new information. Additionally, synaptic pruning refines neural circuits, optimizing efficiency and shaping behavior and cognitive processes such as decision$\\text{\\textemdash}$making and problem$\\text{\\textemdash}$solving. $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nResearch conducted by Draganski et al. (2004) aimed to find if the human brain can change structure in response to environmental demands.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ A group of self$\\text{\\textemdash}$selected samples was chosen and divided into two groups $\\text{\\textemdash}$ jugglers and non jugglers. The researchers made sure that none of the participants had any prior experience with juggling. At the start of the experiment, a brain scan was conducted for all participants.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Participants in condition 1 learned juggling for a period of 3 months, after which a second scan was conducted. Then, the participants spent another three months where they were instructed not to practice juggling. The third brain scan was performed after this. Condition 2 was the control group, where the participants were not exposed to juggling and continued with their daily lives. All 3 scans were conducted for these participants as well.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nThe results showed that the first scan did not have much difference for the participants of the two groups. At the second scan, the juggler group had significantly more grey matter in some areas of the cortex,mostly seen in the mid$\\text{\\textemdash}$temporal area in both hemispheres. The third scan showed that the grey matter was still more in the condition 1 participants than the control group.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nThe research clearly showed that as a result of learning a new activity like juggling, the neuroplasticity increased for the juggling group compared to the other group. There was also a correlation between the juggling performance and the amount of change in the brain plasticity. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ \n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link"},{"ref":"PS0508","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0508\n\nExplain neuroplasticity by using evidence from one study.\\\n\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the answer includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question is provided in the first few sentences. The question uses the command term explain; therefore, the response should provide a detailed account of neuroplasticity, including possible causes or reasons, in relation to a research study (*e.g., “Neuroplasticity is considered to be . . .;” “Neuroplasticity is the process of . . .”)*\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of key topics and supporting details are expressed in the answer. A minimum of 2 key terms should be used in the answer. Important concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define could include, but are not limited to:\n\n- Brain plasticity: ability of the brain to reorganise its functions through changes to the neural pathways\n\n- Dendritic branching: process of thickening of dendrites often seen when new habits/responses are formingNeural pathways: organisation of neurons that helps in communication flow\n\n- Neural pruning: reduction or shrinkage of unused neural pathways\n\n- Neurogenesis: process by which new neurons are formed in the brain\n\n- Synapses: the junctions between two neurons where information passes \n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nThe answer includes a clear focus on describing one study, including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study, where the study is linked to the topic. Relevant studies that could be referred to include, but are not limited to:\n\n- Study on taxi driver's change in hippocampal volume: Maguire (2000)\n\n- Study on the juggler's change in the grey matter of the brain: Draganski et al. (2004)\n\n- Effect of listening to Mozart on spatial reasoning skills: Rauscher et al. (1993)\n\n- Effect of childhood poverty on the amount of grey matter in the hippocampus and amygdala: Luby et al. (2013)\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study to the topic is present (*e.g., Maguire's (2000) study on taxi driver's change in hippocampal volume links to changes in the environment (profession as taxi drivers*). \n\n**Marking limits:**\n\n- If the answer provides a clear explanation of one relevant study and explains key terms relevant to the topic without an explicit link between the result of the study and the topic in question, a maximum of 6 marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer focuses on explaining a relevant study only, without explaining neuroplasticity, a maximum of 5 marks will be awarded. \n\n- If the answer focuses on a study that is not relevant to the topic, a maximum of 4 marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer focuses only on describing neuroplasticity, with no mention of a relevant study, a maximum of 3 marks will be awarded. \n\n- If the answer describes an incorrect topic (e.g., localisation), no marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer describes two relevant studies, the marks will only be given on the first study mentioned.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nNeuroplasticity is defined as a process that explains how the brain changes itself to suit the demands of the environment. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The brain is considered to be plastic in nature, meaning that it is malleable and can change itself depending upon the environment. This is known as brain plasticity. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The brain consists of millions of neurons that pass information to each other, forming neural pathways. These neural pathways get strengthened through dendritic branching, which occurs when neurons are firing continuously, leading to a growth in dendrites. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ This branching leads to structural plasticity or changes in the structure of the brain. However, as the brain is plastic in nature, the reverse of this also occurs, known as neural pruning, wherein synapses shrink and disappear after a period of low or no usage. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ One study that demonstrated neural pruning is Draganski et al. (2004). $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nDraganski et al. (2004) wanted to investigate if learning a skill, such as juggling, would lead to changes in the brain’s structure. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ A self-selected sample of 24 young volunteers was divided into two conditions: one group who learned juggling and another who did not, as a control group. A baseline MRI test was conducted on all participants, after which the experimental group learnt the juggling routine. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ A second MRI was done once they had mastered the routine. While the first MRI showed no differences between the two groups, the second MRI showed the jugglers had a significantly larger amount of grey matter in the mid-temporal region as measured through VBM (Voxel-based morphometry), a technique used to assess the differences in the anatomy of the brain. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ After three months, once the jugglers had stopped practising their routine and could no longer perform it, a third scan was conducted. This scan showed a decrease in the grey matter in the same parts of the brain. The mid-temporal region of the brain was the part of the brain with the most growth, as juggling iss a skill involving visual-motor coordination, and it helps in visual memory. There was no change in the grey matter of the control group across the three scans. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe results from this study can be linked to the concept of neuroplasticity because the increased grey matter in the experimental group after the second scan could have occurred due to dendritic branching.  The jugglers were continuously practising a new skill until they were an expert in it, which showcases the effect of the environment on the brain. There is further evidence of plasticity in this study, as the grey matter decreased once the jugglers had stopped practising and could no longer perform the routine successfully, which shows the process of neural pruning. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\nIt can be concluded that neuroplasticity is a complex phenomenon dependent upon stimulation from the environment, for example spending time practising a new skill, which can lead to structural changes in the brain.\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nDraganski, Bogdan & Gaser, C $\\&$ Busch, Volker $\\&$ Schuierer, G $\\&$ Bogdahn, Ulrich $\\&$ May, Arne. (2004). Neuroplasticity: changes in grey matter induced by training. Nature. 427. 311-2."},{"ref":"PS0528","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0528\n\nDescribe one study on neuroplasticity.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question provided in first few sentences (e.g. *“Research in the field of neuroplasticity …A study that demonstrates the process of neuroplasticity and its effect on human behaviour is ….”)*\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{2. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study $\\text{\\textendash}$ including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study\n\n            *Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n-    *Maguire (2000) on taxi driver's change in hippocampal volume*\n\n-    *Draganski (2004) on the juggler's change in the grey matter of the brain*\n\n-    *Rauscher et.al (1993) effect of listening to Mozart on spatial reasoning skills*\n\n-    *Luby et. al (2013) on the effect of childhood poverty and the amount of grey matter in the hippocampus and amygdala*\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{3. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of neuroplasticity and its on human behaviour.\n\n           *Important concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define include:*\n\n-    Laboratory experiment - An experiment in a lab with well defined IV and DV\n\n-    Independent variable (IV - Variable that is manipulated in the experiment\n\n-    Dependent Variable (DV) - Variable that is measured in the experiment.\n\n-    Correlational study - a study that looks at the relationship between two variables\n\n-    Imaging techniques - techniques that use radioactive or magnetic waves to view the structure and functions of the brain such as MRI, fMRI etc.\n\n-    Brain plasticity - the ability of the brain to reorganise its functions by changes to the neural pathways.\n\n-    Neurogenesis - the process by which new neurons are formed in the brain.\n\n-    Synapses - the junction between two neurons where information passess\n\n-    Neural pathways - the organisation of neurons that helps in communication flow\n\n-    Neural pruning - the reduction or shrinkage of unused neural pathways\n\n-    Dendritic branching - the process of thickening of dendrites often seen when new habits/responses are forming. \n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study to the topic  (e.g. *Results from the study conducted by Draganski (2004) can be linked to the concept of Neuroplasticity. The increased grey matter seen after the second scan could have occurred due to dendritic branching as the jugglers were continuously practising a new skill until they were experts in it - which shows the effect of environment on grey matter.)*\n\n**Marking reductions:**\n\n-    A relevant study is described, but no clear link to neuroplasticity is made (a maximum of 5 marks).\n\n-    Neuroplasticity is described in general, but no focus on how it occurs in the study is present - does not meet the demands of the question as it specifically requires a description of research on neuroplasticity (a maximum of 6 marks if the study is relevant and linked to the topic)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nResearch in the field of neuroplasticity is quite recent with much of it being conducted on humans in the last 40 - 50 years $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$. One of the reasons for this could be that  changes in the brain are difficult to investigate and require  the use of various imaging techniques such as MRI, CT scans etc, which were developed in the mid 1900's. Neuroplasticity is described as  a process that explains how the brain changes itself to suit the demands of the environment $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$. The brain consists of millions of neurons that pass information to each other, forming neural pathways. These neural pathways get strengthened through dendritic branching, indicating that neurons are firing continuously, leading to a growth in their dendrites. This leads to structural plasticity or changes in the structure of the brain $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. A study that demonstrates neuroplasticity in taxi drivers is Maguire et.al (2000) $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$.\n\nMaguire et. al (2000) wanted to investigate if the continuous practice of a skill, such as driving, would lead to brain structure changes. A sample of 16 right-handed male taxi drivers who had a driving experience of a minimum of 1.5 years in addition to passing the knowledge test - a requirement to obtain a taxi licence in London, were gathered. An MRI scan was used, and the participant's grey matter was scanned and later compared to 50 other right-handed males who were not taxi drivers. Technology such as the VBM (Voxel-based morphology) was used, which helps to determine the density of the grey matter and pixel counting which helps to determine the size of the area being measured. Results from the VBM showed that the taxi drivers with many years of experience had a higher volume of the right posterior hippocampus, an area in the brain associated with spatial memory. This indicates that dendritic branching had occurred as part of the process of neuroplasticity in the brains of the taxi drivers. The pixel counting also showed a larger area of posterior hippocampus in the taxi drivers as compared to the non-taxi drivers as a result of passing the knowledge test and driving continuously for the past few years.  $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The findings above suggest that neuroplasticity was evident due to changes observed in the taxi driver's brain.\n\nThe study was a quasi-experiment as the independent variable (taxi drivers or non-taxi drivers) naturally occurred. Additionally, it was a correlational study as VBM results clearly showed that the more years the participants were driving caused changes to the brain structure helping to show a clear link between environment and brain plasticity $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$. The results from this study can thus be linked to the concept of Neuroplasticity as there is a clear indication of the changes in the grey matter of the participant's brain due to the advanced imaging techniques used. \n\nIt can be concluded that neuroplasticity is a complex phenomenon dependent upon the stimulation from the environment - spending time practising a skill, which can lead to structural changes in the brain, as demonstrated in the study by Maguire (2000) $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$.\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nMaguire, E. A., Gadian, D. G., Johnsrude, I. S., Good, C. D., Ashburner, J., Frackowiak, R. S. J., & Frith, C. D. (2000). Navigation-related structural change in the hippocampi of taxi drivers. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America, 97(8), 4398$\\text{\\textendash}$4403. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.070039597"},{"ref":"PS0532","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0532\n\nDescribe one study on localisation of brain function in relation to human behaviour.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question provided in the first few sentences (e.g. *“One study that supports localization is Harris & Fiske's research in 2006 investigating the role of the amygdala in managing emotions..”)*\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{2. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study $\\text{\\textendash}$ including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study\\\n\n*Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n-    Milner (1966) case study on the function of the hippocampus in the brain\n\n-    Maguire (2000) on taxi driver's spatial ability and the hippocampus\n\n-    Draganski (2004) on juggler's visual processing in the mid-temporal area\n\n-    Harris and Fiske (2006) on the amygdala as the emotional centre of the brain\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{3. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of localisation of brain function in relation to human behaviour as supported by a relevant study.\\\n\n*Important concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define include:*\n\n-    Localization of brain function - different brain areas are associated with specific cognitive functions such as thinking, decision making etc.\n\n-    Hippocampus - brain area associated with memory\n\n-    Amygdala - brain area associated with managing emotions\n\n-    Brain imaging techniques - Methods used to study the structure and functions of the brain such as MRI, fMRI etc.\n\n-    fMRI - Functional Magnetic resonance imaging used to study the functions of the brain\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study to the topic  (e.g. *“Draganski (2004) found that when the participants had learned and practised a new skill of juggling the grey matter in their mid-temporal area had significantly increased. These results give evidence to the theory of localization as the mid-temporal area is associated with visual memory which would be necessary to learn the skill of juggling.”)*\n\n**Marking reductions:**\n\n-    No specific brain area is mentioned nor associated with a function. (up to 4 marks may be deducted)\n\n-    The study cited is not appropriate to localization but focuses on other concepts such as neuroplasticity which are similar. (up to 4 marks may be deducted)\n\n-    Localisation of brain function and a relevant study are both clearly described but there is no explicit link between the result of the study and localisation of brain function. (a maximum of 6 marks may be awarded)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nLocalization of function in the brain was among of the earliest ways researchers tried to understand how the brain works. While many of the early theories have been discarded due to insufficient evidence, this theory still continues to hold weight. Localization of function refers to the concept that different brain areas are associated with specific cognitive functions such as thinking, decision making, impulse control, language, among other things. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$  One of the lobes in the brain is the temporal lobe within which the amygdala is situated. $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  The amygdala is known as the emotional centre of the brain known for its role in managing emotions and its associated behaviours. $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  One study that supports localization is Harris & Fiske's research in 2006 investigating the role of the amygdala in managing emotions. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nHarris and Fiske (2006) aimed to investigate the role of the amygdala and the prefrontal cortex while being exposed to distressing images. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ They gathered 22 University undergraduates and randomly allocated them to two different conditions. This study used an fMRI - an advanced brain imaging tool that helps to identify the specific functions of the different brain areas as the patient can perform different tasks while within the machine while being connected to the scanner. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The participants in group 1 saw images of objects whereas participants in group 2 saw images of homeless people or addicts. Their brain activity was measured while viewing these images in the fMRI. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Results from the study showed that participants in group 1 did not show any brain activity in the amygdala whereas participants who viewed homeless people and addicts did. This could be as viewing distressing images of people may have caused the participants to have an emotional reaction leading to the activation of amygdala. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe results of this study give evidence to the functions of the amygdala as one that is associated with managing emotions as viewing distressing images activates this brain region among the participants as found by the fMRI scanner. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$  This study effectively shows localization of function is present and specific brain areas are responsible for different functions of the brain.\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nHarris, L. T., & Fiske, S. T. (2006). Dehumanizing the lowest of the low. Psychological Science, 17(10), 847$\\text{\\textendash}$853. https: //doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9280.2006.01793.x"},{"ref":"PS0533","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0533\n\nExplain localisation of brain function.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question provided in the first few sentences (e.g. *“Cognitive functions are localized in specific areas of the brain as it helps to improve brain processing while allowing brain plasticity….”)*\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{2. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study $\\text{\\textendash}$ including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study\\\n\n            *Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n-    Milner (1966) case study on the function of hippocampus in the brain\n\n-    Tiernay et.al (2001) on the language centres in the brain.\n\n-    Draganski (2004) on juggler's visual processing in the mid-temporal area\n\n-    Harris and Fiske (2006) on the amygdala as the emotional centre of the brain\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{3. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of hormones and its effects on human behaviour..\\\n\n           *Important concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define include:*\n\n-    Localization of brain functions  - different brain areas are associated with specific cognitive functions such as thinking, decision making etc.\n\n-    Frontal lobe - One of the brain lobes which helps in decision making \n\n-    PET scans - Positron Emission Tomography helps to study the function of the brain\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study to the topic  (e.g. *“Draganski (2004) found that when the participants had learned and practised a new skill of juggling the grey matter in their mid-temporal area had significantly increased. These results give evidence to the theory of localization as the mid-temporal area is associated with visual memory which would be necessary to learn the skill of juggling.”)*\n\n**Marking reductions:**\n\n-    No specific brain area is mentioned nor associated with a function. (up to 4 marks may be deducted)\n\n-    The study cited is not relevant to localization but focuses on other concepts such as neuroplasticity which are similar. (up to 4 marks may be deducted)\n\n-    Localisation of brain function and a relevant study are both clearly described but there is no explicit link between the result of the study and localisation of brain function. (a maximum of 6 marks may be awarded)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nResearchers have been studying the brain since earlier times to gain a better understanding of how it works. One of the emerging theories that aids in this is the localization of brain function. This refers to the idea that different areas of the brain are responsible for different functions $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$. In addition, there has been evidence suggesting that functions of one area of the brain can be overtaken by another in case of trauma, injury or illness further supporting the theory of localization. One of the most important functions of the brain include language processing and language centres in the brain are located in the left hemisphere near to the frontal lobe $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. One study that supports localization of the language function in the brain is by Tiernay et. al (2001). $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nTiernay et. al conducted a case study on patient MA to study the language centres of the brain. MA had a lesion on his left frontal lobe in infancy but had grown to become fluent in the spoken language as well as the sign language as his parents were deaf. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  PET scan imaging was used to compare the brain areas of MA to 12 control participants who were also fluent in sign and spoken language. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  The participants were asked to recount an incident while being scanned. Results from the scans revealed that the left hemisphere was primarily more active compared to other brain regions for all the control participants. However, it was interesting to note that for patient MA the right hemisphere was more active indicating that this part of the brain had overtaken the language functions due to the injury on the left hemisphere. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  Therefore, it can be concluded that normally it is the left hemisphere of the brain which is responsible for language functions, however in MA's case, it was on the right due to his childhood injury. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe results of this study give evidence to the theory of localization and explains how it occurs as all the control participants had an active left hemisphere during the language tasks but MA had an active right hemisphere due to his injury. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$  Therefore, we can understand that different brain areas are associated with specific cognitive functions but this is not fixed and one area can overtake another area's functions as seen in MA. As this was a case study, it was also noted that MA had reduced visuo-spatial abilities - functions which are associated with the right hemisphere. This gives further explanation to localization as when the right hemisphere overtook the tasks of the left hemisphere, its own functions were somewhat compromised helping us to understand better how localization of brain function works. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nTierney, M., Varga, M., Hosey, L. A., Grafman, J., & Braun, A. R. (2001). PET evaluation of bilingual language compensation following early childhood brain damage. Neuropsychologia, 39(2), 114$\\text{\\textendash}$121. https: //doi.org/10.1016/s0028-3932(00)00106-8"},{"ref":"PS0534","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0534\n\nDescribe one technique used to study the brain in relation to behaviour, using one research study.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question provided in the first few sentences (e.g. *“One technique used to study the brain is through using advanced brain imaging techniques such as the magnetic resonance imaging or MRI… ”)*\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{2. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study related to the techniques to study the brain which includes the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study\\\n\n            *Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n-    Maguire (2000) studies neuroplasticity using MRI scans. \n\n-    Harris and Fiske (2006) on the activation of amygdala using an fMRI machine.\n\n-    Tiernay et. al (2000) used PET scans to analyse language centres in the brain.\n\n-    Draganski (2004) used MRI to study neural pruning in jugglers.\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{3. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nTo answer the command term describe, the response should show an accurate knowledge and understanding of at least one technique to study the brain in relation to human behaviour.\\\n\n           *Important concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define include some of the \n\n           following:*\n\n-    MRI scans (magnetic resonance imaging) - a brain scanning technique which uses magnetic waves helping to study the structure of the brain\n\n-    fMRI scans (functional magnetic resonance imaging) -  a brain scanning technique which uses magnetic waves helping to study the function of the brain\n\n-    PET scans (positron emission tomography) - Positron Emission Tomography uses radioactive waves which helps to study the function of the brain\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study to the topic  (e.g. *Draganski (2004) used MRI scans to study the grey matter in the mid-temporal area of the participants. The researchers further used VBM to analyse the results of the MRI and arrived at the conclusion that participants had higher grey matter in the region specific to visual memory after they learned juggling. However, a post MRI scan showed that the grey matter had reduced once they stopped practising the skill. Thus, MRI scanning are helpful to further our understanding of neural pruning..”)*\n\n**Marking reductions:**\n\n-    Techniques of studying the brain are confused with research methods (up to 5 marks may be deducted if the study used is relevant to the topic)\n\n-    The study cited is not relevant to the topic such as Rogers and Kesner (2003) or Rosenzweig, Bennet & Diamond (1972) as neither use any brain imaging techniques (up to 4 marks may be deducted)\n\n-    The response does not focus on the topic and no link is present. For eg. in the Mcgaugh and Cahill (1995) study, the response may mention an imaging technique such as MRI, fMRI etc but the focus is on the concept - flashbulb memories and not on how the technique was used to study the brain (up to 4 marks may be deducted)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nThe use of brain imaging techniques in research opened up new doors for scientists to study the brain in multiple ways. The use of these methods in psychological research  are relatively recent paving the way for many discoveries. Brain imaging techniques are neuroimaging techniques that create images of the brain which help the researchers to study the changes that occur in the brain both functional and structural $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$. Magnetic resonance imaging or MRI scans are one of the most widely used non-invasive techniques that help to understand the structural changes occurring in the brain including the changes in size of the grey matter in different areas of the brain, the density of the grey matter etc. $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  MRI scans use the body's natural magnetic field, radioactive waves and a computer to create 2D or 3D images $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. Neuroplasticity is a concept that has been discovered through the use of these MRI scans. One study that used MRI scans to understand neuroplasticity is Maguire (2000). $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nMaguire et. al (2000) wanted to investigate if the continuous practice of a skill, such as driving, could change the brain's structure leading to neuroplasticity. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  A sample of 16 right-handed male taxi drivers was used who had a driving experience of minimum of 1.5 years in addition to passing the Knowledge test - a requirement to obtain a taxi licence in London. MRI scanning was used and the participant's grey matter was scanned and later compared to 50 other right-handed males who were not taxi drivers. Additionally, the VBM (Voxel-based morphology) was used to analyse the MRI scans to determine the density of the grey matter along with pixel counting which helps to measure the size of the area being measured. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  Results from the VBM showed that the taxi drivers with many years of experience had a higher volume of right posterior hippocampus, an area in the brain associated with spatial memory. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  This indicates that dendritic branching had occurred as part of the process of neuroplasticity in the brain of the taxi drivers. The pixel counting also showed a larger area of posterior hippocampus in the taxi drivers as compared to the non-taxi drivers as a result of passing the knowledge test and driving continuously for the past few years.  $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  The findings above suggest that neuroplasticity was evident due to changes observed in the taxi driver's brain.\n\nThe use of MRI scans in the study helped to determine the structural changes in the grey matter of the taxi drivers which was evident when compared to the images from the MRI scans of the non-taxi drivers. Further the use of VBM and pixel counting helps to give accurate results in a numerical form that can be compared and help researchers reach a logical conclusion to the research. Thereby, MRI imaging technique helped researchers to discover the concept of neuroplasticity in the brain which was not possible before as researchers had no way to study the brain of a live human being. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nMaguire, E. A., Gadian, D. G., Johnsrude, I. S., Good, C. D., Ashburner, J., Frackowiak, R. S. J., & Frith, C. D. (2000). Navigation-related structural change in the hippocampi of taxi drivers. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America, 97(8), 4398$\\text{\\textendash}$4403. https: //doi.org/10.1073/pnas.070039597"},{"ref":"PS0535","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0535\n\nExplain how one technique is used to study the brain in relation to human behaviour, with reference to one study. \n\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\n A direct answer to the question provided in the first few sentences (e.g. *“Brain imaging techniques such as the fMRI scan study human behaviour by mapping brain activity through detecting changes in blood flow…”* Towards the end of the response, the focus can be redirected to the question by….. *“Thus, it can be seen how the imaging technique used was effective in helping researchers to understand…..”)* \n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{2. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study related to techniques to study the brain in relation to human behaviour $\\text{\\textendash}$ including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study\\\n\n           *Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n-    Maguire's (2000) study on neuroplasticity using MRI scans. \n\n-    Sharot et. al (2007) used fMRI scans to understand flashbulb memories.\n\n-    Tiernay et. al (2000) used PET scans to analyse language centres in the brain.\n\n-    Draganski (2004) used MRI to study neural pruning in jugglers.\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{3. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of how different techniques are used to study the brain in relation to human behaviour.\\\n\n           *Important concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define include:*\n\n-    MRI scans (magnetic resonance imaging) - a brain scanning technique which uses magnetic waves helping to study the structure of the brain\n\n-    fMRI scans (functional magnetic resonance imaging) -  a brain scanning technique which uses magnetic waves helping to study the function of the brain\n\n-    PET scans (positron emission tomography) - Positron Emission Tomography uses radioactive waves which helps to study the function of the brain\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study to the topic  (e.g. *“Tiernay et.al (2000) found that when the control participants were performing language tasks in the PET scanner, the left hemisphere of the brain was getting activated. However, in the case of patient MA it was the right hemisphere that was active, leading to the conclusion that when one part of the brain gets injured another part can take over its functions. These results were found due to the detailed and colourful images that were released from the PET scans which clearly showed the areas that were active during the tasks.”)*\n\n**Marking reductions:**\n\n-    Techniques of studying the brain are confused with research methods (up to 5 marks may be deducted if the study used is relevant to the topic)\n\n-    The study cited is not relevant to the topic (up to 4 marks may be deducted)\n\n-    The response does not focus on the topic and no link is present (up to 4 marks may be deducted)\n\n-    To meet the command term, it should also be explained why the particular technique was chosen. For example referring to Draganski (2000) - *“The MRI was selected as an appropriate scanning technique by the researchers as they wanted to investigate the structural changes in the mid-temporal region after the jugglers stopped practising the skill”*. Not meeting command term expectation may lead to a deduction of up to 3 marks. \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nBefore the advent of the brain imaging techniques in research, researchers could only study the brain of dead human beings as autopsy was the most common method used. It was after the industrial revolution in the 19th century that modern medicine practices began to emerge, one of which was the use of X-rays and radioactive waves in imaging. This added immense value to the study of the brain as researchers could now understand the changes that were happening in a live brain and the factors that influence it leading to numerous discoveries in various cognitive areas such as memory $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$. One such technique is known as the fMRI imaging which helps psychologists to understand the functions of the brain. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$  This technique is invasive as a contrast material is injected in the patient's vein which helps to show the level of activation in the different brain areas $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. Additionally, the participants are asked to perform certain activities while being in the scanner to identify the brain area associated with the cognitive activity performed $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$.In 1977, Brown and Kuik proposed the concept of flashbulb memories which stated that when a highly emotional event occurs we tend to have accurate and long-lasting memories of the event. While the theory was proposed in 1977, Sharot et.al in 2007 conducted a study using fMRI to give biological evidence to this phenomena. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nSharot et. al (2007) conducted a quasi experiment three years after the 9/11 attack on a sample of 24 participants from New york city. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  The participants were placed in an fMRI machine and presented a list of verbal cues along with the words \"Summer” or “September”. They had to associate each of the verbal cues  with both of these words and recall a memory associated with it. The resulting brain images were analysed to see the level of activation in the amygdala known as the emotional centre of the brain. Results showed that participants who were closer to the event had a higher activation of the amygdala as well as being able to recall the event with clear and accurate details compared to the participants who were further away. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ These participants also reported having high confidence levels in their memories being accurate. It can be concluded that the level of activation of the amygdala indicates the emotional importance attached to the event which was followed by the vividness and accuracy of the memories leading to the biological evidence for flashbulb memories. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nfMRI scans were instrumental in helping the researchers understand how emotions trigger the memory in human beings as the level of activation of the amygdala was critical in understanding the intensity of the emotions associated with the memory. Further, as participants were in the fMRI while recalling the entire memory the changes in the activation of the brain could be noted from one task to another as for participants who were closer to the event it was less active when recalling an event from the summer as compared to recalling an event from September. Thereby, fMRI imaging technique was chosen as it helped researchers to discover the functions associated with different areas of the brain and give biological evidence to cognitive concepts. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nSharot, T., Martorella, E. A., Delgado, M. R., & Phelps, E. A. (2007). How personal experience modulates the neural circuitry of memories of September 11. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America, 104(1), 389$\\text{\\textendash}$394. https: //doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0609230103"},{"ref":"PS0536","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0536\n\nOutline the role of one agonist to human behaviour, using one study.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\n A direct answer to the question provided in the first few sentences (e.g. *“Agonists are described as substances in the brain that increase the chances of an action occurring  …”. Towards the end of the response, the following statement can be included to conclude how the agonist works in the study….. “Thus, it can be seen how acetylcholine works as an antagonist in the study cited above”)*\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{2. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study $\\text{\\textendash}$ including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study\\\n\n            *Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n-    Passamonti et.al (2012) on the effect of serotonin on reaction to emotional stimuli\n\n-    Antonova (2011) - on the role of acetylcholine in spatial memory\n\n-    Crockett et. al (2010) effect of serotonin on prosocial behaviour\n\n-    Setiawan et.al (2013) on the effect of alcohol on mood\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{3. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of at least one agonist, how it functions and how it may affect human behaviour.\\\n\n           *Important concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define include:*\n\n-    Neurotransmitter - chemical messengers that pass information through a wide network of neurons distributed all across the brain\n\n-    Synapse - the junction between two neurons where information passess\n\n-    Agonists - a chemical substance that activates the receptor sites to help produce a reaction\n\n-    Exogenous vs. Endogenous - agonists produced in the body known as endogenous agonists or they may be introduced in the form of drugs known as exogenous agonists\n\n-    Dendrites -  This is the branch-like place where the different neurons contact each other to pass information \n\n-    Neurons - These are the nerve cells spread across the body that help in passing information from one part to the other. \n\n-    Serotonin - A neurotransmitter functioning as endogenous agonists that aids in mood regulation\n\n-    Heroin - A drug functioning as an exogenous agonist that regulates reward processing, moods, pain relief etc.\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study to the topic (e.g. *“Crockett et. al (2010) found that when participants were given an SSRI, they were more prone to prosocial behaviour. This is evident as participants in condition 1 were less likely to push the person off the bridge to save the people on the train than the participants in the placebo condition. This indicates that serotonin is an agonist as it promotes the prosocial behaviour reaction in the synapse”.)*\n\n**Marking reductions:**\n\n-    Agonists are not explained and only a study on neurotransmitters is present. (up to 5 marks may be deducted)\n\n-    The study cited is not appropriate to the topic such as Lapidus et.al which focuses on agonists. (up to 4 marks may be deducted)\n\n-    The response does not focus on the topic and no proper link is present. For example in the Antonova (2011) study the focus is on the neurotransmitter acetylcholine but does not mention how it works as an antagonist (up to 4 marks may be deducted)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nOne agonist that influences human behaviour is serotonin. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$  Neurotransmitters such as serotonin are released into the gap between neurons after which they bind with the receptors on the dendrites of the post-synaptic neuron. In doing so, these neurotransmitters impact the chances of an action occurring in the next neuron. Neurotransmitters can be affected by agonists which are substances that can increase the chances of an action occurring, or they can amplify the effects of a neurotransmitter. Agonists act by binding to the receptors present at the end of the dendrites  $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ Agonists may be produced in the body in the form of hormones or other neurotransmitters known as endogenous agonists.  They may also be introduced into the body in the form of drugs, known as exogenous agonists.  $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. One study that investigated the effect of the endogenous agonist and neurotransmitter serotonin on brain activity and reactions to emotional stimuli is the investigation done by Passamonti et. al (2012). $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nPassamonti et. al (2012) aimed to investigate the effect of reduced serotonin in our interpretation of threats from the environment leading to impulsive behaviour.The study had 19 healthy participants both men and women with a mean age of 25. The participants were then divided into 2 conditions, condition 1 consumed a tryptophan-depleting drink which reduced serotonin levels, and condition 2 drank a placebo. The participants were then placed in an fMRI machine and were shown images of happy, neutral, or sad faces. The brain activity of the participants in condition 1 showed a reduced activation of the prefrontal cortex (PFC) compared to condition 2 when they were viewing the angry faces. The fMRI also showed that expected communication between the PFC and the amygdala when viewing the angry faces was disrupted in the participants with less serotonin. The amygdala is a brain structure involved in processing emotions such as fear and aggression. As serotonin can inhibit activation of the amygdala, lack of communication means that any communication to inhibit amygdala activation may not take place. This could potentially lead to increased aggression. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThis gives evidence to the role of serotonin as an agonist. When serotonin levels are reduced, this is associated with reduced activation of the prefrontal cortex and reduced communication between the PFC and the amygdala. Which may, in turn, influence levels of aggression. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nPassamonti, L., Crockett, M. J., Apergis-Schoute, A. M., Clark, L., Rowe, J. B., Calder, A. J., & Robbins, T. W. (2012). Effects of acute tryptophan depletion on Prefrontal-Amygdala connectivity while viewing facial signals of aggression. Biological Psychiatry, 71(1), 36$\\text{\\textendash}$43. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biopsych.2011.07.033"},{"ref":"PS0537","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0537\n\nOutline the role of one antagonist using one research study.\n\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question is provided in the first few sentences. The question sues the command term outline; therefore, the response should provide a summary or a brief account of the role of antagonists in relation to a research study (*e.g., $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Antagonists can be described as chemical substances introduced in the body that block receptors from passing on information . . .$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$*)\n\n \n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{2. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nThe answer includes a clear focus on describing one study,, including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study, where the study is linked to the topic.  Relevant studies that could be referred to include, but are not limited to:\n\n- Study on the role of Scopolamine in blocking acetylcholine receptors: Antonova (2011)\n\n- Study on the role of ketamine blocking glutamate receptors: Lapidus et al. (2014)\n\n- Study on the role of haloperidol on blocking dopamine receptors: Mirte et al. (2016)\n\n- Study on the role of scopolamine on blocking acetylcholine receptors: Troster $\\&$ Beatty (1989)\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{3. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of key topics and supporting details are expressed in the answer. A minimum of 2 key terms should be used in the answer. Important concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define could include, but are not limited to:\n\n- Antagonists: chemical substances introduced in the body that block receptors from passing on information\n\nDendrites: the branch-like places where different neurons contact each other to pass along information \n\n- Ketamine: a drug that has a significant effect on the reduction of depression symptoms\n\nNeurons:  nerve cells that are spread across the body andhelp in passing information from one part of the body to the others\n\n- Neurotransmitters: chemical messengers that pass information through a wide network of neurons distributed all across the brain\n\n- Scopolamine: a drug that blocks the receptors associated with the neurotransmitter acetylcholine\n\nSynapse: the junction between two neurons where information passes\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study to the topic is present (*e.g., $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Troster and Beatty (1989) found that injecting the drug scopolamine had an effect on the participant's memory, as those who received the drug scored the lowest in the free recall test. This gives evidence to scopolamine's role as an antagonist, as it effectively blocked the acetylcholine receptors known for their role in memory and reduced the participants' score on the memory test.$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$*).\n\n**Marking reductions:**\n\n- If the answer provides a clear explanation of one relevant study and explains key terms relevant to the topic without an explicit link between the result of the study and the topic in question, a maximum of 6 marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer focuses on explaining a relevant study only, without explaining the topic, a maximum of 5 marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer focuses on a study that is not relevant to the topic (*e.g., Passimonti et al., which focuses on serotonin as an agonist*), a maximum of 5 marks will be awarded. \n\n- If the answer focuses only on describing the topic, with no mention of a relevant study, a maximum of 3 marks will be awarded. \n\n- If the answer describes an incorrect topic (*e.g., System 1 or automatic thinking*), no marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer describes two relevant studies, the marks will only be given on the first study mentioned.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nNeurotransmitters pass information from one neuron to another through the receptors located on each of the branches of the neurons known as dendrites. Antagonists are chemical substances introduced in the body that block the receptors from passing on the information, thus delaying or stopping the neurons from passing on the message in the brain. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$. Antagonists are sometimes given to the patient in the form of drugs to avoid excessive reactions due to the overexcitation of the neurotransmitters.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. One such antagonist is ketamine, an excitatory neurotransmitter, which blocks the glutamate receptors known for their role in causing depression. A study was conducted by Lapidus et al. (2014) on the role of the antagonist ketamine in blocking glutamate receptors and its effect on behaviour.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nLapidus et al. (2014) conducted a lab experiment, gathering 20 participants with a diagnosis of Major Depressive Disorder (MDD) and randomly allocated them to 2 conditions. Group 1 was given a nasal dose of ketamine and Group 2 was given a placebo. The participants were asked to fill in a rating scale for depression one day after the treatment. Results from the scale showed that participants in Group 1 had decreased depression symptoms as compared to participants from the control group. We can thus conclude that ketamine is useful in the treatment of depression, as it reduces the symptoms of depression by lowering the amount of glutamate present in the synapses. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nFrom the results of this experiment, the role of ketamine as an antagonist can be clearly seen, as it blocked the glutamate receptors and reduced the symptoms of depression in the participants from the experimental group. The results are evident as the same effect was not seen in the participants of the control group. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nLapidus, K., Levitch, C. F., Perez, A. M., Brallier, J. W., Parides, M. K., Soleimani, L., Feder, A., Iosifescu, D. V., Charney, D. S., & Murrough, J. W. (2014). A randomized controlled trial of intranasal ketamine in major depressive disorder. Biological Psychiatry, 76(12), 970$\\text{\\textendash}$976. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biopsych.2014.03.026"},{"ref":"PS0545","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0545\n\nDiscuss neuroplasticity, with reference to one or more research studies.\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained and addressed consistently throughout the response. The question used the command term *discuss* paired with a topic, therefore, the response should offer a balanced review of neuroplasticity. This could involve perspectives, developments in knowledge, or uncertainties when it comes to understanding neuroplasticity.\n\n**1 mark** if the question is either simply restated, misinterpreted (*though still related to pheromones, e.g. This essay will evaluate research on neuroplasticity*), or if the overall response does not address the prompt (*e.g. the candidate writes in their introduction that they will discuss neuroplasticity, but primarily evaluates research*).\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant $\\&$ appropriate concepts and terminology related to neuroplasticity are explained in detail (*e.g. long term potentiation, dendritic branching, neural networks, etc.*). Some marks will also be awarded for criterion B for knowledge of research methodology.\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors (*e.g. neuroplasticity is described accurately but additional vocabulary terms – such as long term potentiation, neural pruning, etc – are not correctly applied*), or if the response only shows strong methodological knowledge.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited (*e.g. having inaccurate definitions of neuroplasticity or being unable to describe any of the mechanisms that relate to neuroplasticity, with or without formal vocabulary*).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (*e.g., aim, procedures, results $\\&$ conclusion*) are thoroughly explained and used to support the answer. Relevant studies include, but are not limited to:\n- Maguire (2000), on London taxi driver’s structural differences from the general population as a result of specialized navigation\n- Draganski (2004), on the impact of learning a discrete skill (juggling) on brain structure\n- Bremner et al (2008), on the relationship between neuroplasticity and post-traumatic stress disorder\n- Tierney et al (2001), a study which used PET scans to study language development\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (*e.g. the aim, procedure, results & conclusions of the study were not clearly presented*)\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. (*e.g. the study was not linked to a broader discussion of neuroplasticity or one of multiple studies presented is not related to the topic*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and elaborated discussion regarding neuroplasticity. Candidates should ideally develop 3 or more distinct claims throughout their discussion. For this prompt, this may include discussing the mechanisms that promote neuroplasticity, techniques used to assess neuroplasticity, the correlational nature of many studies of neuroplasticity, and potential applications of findings.\n\n**3-4 marks** if 1-2 lines of discussion (see above) are present and explained but are not fully developed and linked back to the prompt.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the claims within the discussion are superficial, mainly descriptive (*e.g. the claims were only stated or described but not linked back to research or the overall topic*), repetitive, or if the included critical thinking primarily centers on strengths and limitations of research studies (rather than the topic).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organisation}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is an organised & coherent explanation of neuroplasticity, and a discussion supported by one or more research studies, see Criterion C. ( *e.g. first paragraph introduces the focus on the question and related terminology, the succeeding paragraphs talk about related research/considerations in using the studies to understand neuroplasticity, a synthesis paragraph which presents and explores a range of claims, and the closing paragraphs or statements summarise how the prompt was addressed*)\n\n**1 mark** if organisation and clarity were not sustained.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\nNeuroplasticity describes the brain's ability to reorganize itself by forming new neural connections throughout life. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  These connections are shaped by the use or disuse of neural networks. Long term potentiation describes the strengthening of synaptic connections through repeated use. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  As a series of neurons, or network, continues to be activated, the brain eventually dedicates more proteins to extend the dendrites of the postsynaptic neurons to facilitate further communication. With lack of use, dendritic pruning may occur. This describes the reduction of synaptic connections. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  This natural process allows the brain to remain efficient. Two studies that explore long term potentiation and neural pruning are Maguire and Draganski. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nThe aim of Maguire was to investigate potential brain differences between the generalized population and a specialized profession. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  She hypothesized that taxi drivers, who must constantly recall spatial navigation memories as they navigate around the city, might have structural differences in parts of the brain related to that function. Maguire used a set of control scans from an MRI database, and recruited 16 right-handed, male taxi drivers from London. All of them had to have passed a knowledge test which grants UK taxi drivers with their permit to operate and had to have been professionally driving for at least 1.5 years. Voxel based morphometry and pixel counting were used to measure the density and volume of the hippocampus respectively. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  Maguire found that when comparing the control scans with the taxi drivers’ scans, the taxi drivers had larger posterior hippocampi, and significantly smaller anterior hippocampi. The size of the posterior hippocampus correlated positively with the number of years the drivers had been working. In other words, this specialized skill and recall seemed to be related to the brain structure of the drivers. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  Executing these same functions repeatedly led to the long-term potentiation and dendritic branching, which led to an increase in both density and volume of those neural networks. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  While cause and effect cannot be determined from this study, the correlation between number of years driving and hippocampal volume suggests that experience does play a role in the extent of changes. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Bias was reduced in this study through its single-blind design: the researchers did not know which brain scan belonged to which participant group. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nDraganski explored how learning a new skill might change the structure of the brain. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ 24 volunteers were allocated to either a juggling condition or a non-juggling control group. All participants underwent a pre-test scan to serve as a comparison. Those in the juggling group were asked to master a simple juggling technique and let the researchers know when they had learned it. At this point, the juggling group participants underwent a second, post-test scan. They were then told to stop juggling, and after three months a final scan was taken. MRIs were taken around the same time for the control group. Voxel based morphometry was also used in this study. The researchers found that there were no changes in the density of any region of the brain for the control group. But in the juggling group, the density of the mid-temporal area increased after learning the skill and decreased after ceasing practice of juggling. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ This suggests that long term potentiation occurred in the first part of the experiment, and synaptic pruning in the three months after they stopped juggling. The use of a skill like juggling allowed researchers to observe changes in the brain over time. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  Unlike Maguire, this is a true experiment and so causation can be inferred. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ There may be some sample bias in Draganski, however - all participants were in their early 20s and so similar rates of plasticity may not be observed in older people. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Finally, there may be some issues with internal validity as the participants were outside of the observation of researchers for most of the study. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nThere are many applications for research on neuroplasticity. It has informed therapeutic strategies for stroke patients, who can regain lost functions through targeted exercises that encourage the brain to rewire itself. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Similarly, understanding neuroplasticity has shed light on the potential for recovery in children who have suffered from neglect, suggesting that interventions can lead to improvements in cognitive functions previously thought to be permanently impaired. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ However, it's important to note that many studies on neuroplasticity, including studies like Maguire, are correlational. It is not possible to ethically study the impacts of certain variables of interest using experimental human studies: this includes trauma, socialization, education quality, or access to extracurricular programming. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Because these variables are explored retrospectively or in the context of natural experiments, they do not establish cause-and-effect relationships. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nIn conclusion, the study of neuroplasticity through research by Maguire, Draganski, and others provides valuable insights into the brain's remarkable ability to adapt and reorganize itself in response to learning and experience. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ While the correlational nature of much of this research presents challenges in establishing causality, the findings have significant implications for education, rehabilitation, and understanding the biological foundations of human behavior and cognition.\n\nWord count: 820\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\nDraganski B, Gaser C, Busch V, et al. Neuroplasticity: changes in grey matter induced by training. Nature. 2004 Jan;427(6972):311-312. DOI: 10.1038/427311a. PMID: 14737157.\n\nMaguire, E. A., D. G. Gadian, I. S. Johnsrude, C. D. Good, J. Ashburner, R. S. J. Frackowiak, and C. D. Frith. \"Navigation-related Structural Change in the Hippocampi of Taxi Drivers.\" Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 97.8 (2000): 4398-403."},{"ref":"PS0564","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0564\n\nDiscuss how one or more neurotransmitters influence human behaviour.\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\\\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The \n\nquestion used the command term *discuss* which means a considered $\\&$ balanced review of arguments, factors, or hypotheses must be addressed. \n\n**1 mark** if the command term is not accurately explained or there is a limited explanation.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\\\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant $\\&$ appropriate terminology is explained in detail (e.g. neurotransmitters, the process of neurotransmission). \n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors and if the response only showed knowledge about research \n\nMethod (brief explanation of neurotransmitters with the help of a study). \n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}\\ }$\\\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (e.g., aim, procedures, results $\\&$ \n\nconclusion) are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments \n\nstated. Studies to be used are but not limited to $-$\n\n$1.$ Antonova et al (2011) - Acetylcholine\\\n\n$2.$ Fisher, Aron and Brown (2005) - Dopamine \\\n\n$3.$ Crockett et al (2010) - Serotonin \n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (e.g the aim, procedure, results & conclusions of the study was not clearly presented) \n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\\\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed evaluation points (e.g around 2-3 strengths and limitations) of research on one bias (there is high internal validity as a cause and effect relationship can be observed between the IV and DV, ecological validity, generalization of findings). \n\n**3-4 marks** of the evaluation points (e.g around 1-2 strengths $\\&$ limitations) are present and clearly explained but are not sustained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation or discussion points are superficial (evaluation points only stated but not explained properly).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\\\n\n**2 marks** if there is a clear link to be made at the end of the answer focusing on neurotransmitters and the study used. The link can be made with the help of a behaviour affected by the neurotransmitter. The answer must be organized and clear with appropriate information provided. \n\n**1 mark** if the organization and clarity were not sustained throughout the answer.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nNeurotransmitters are chemical messengers used for communicating information with the help of an electrical impulse passed on from one neuron to another. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\n Neurons are nerve cells that are the building blocks of behaviour. Through the neuron's terminals, neurotransmitters are released and  help to send electrochemical messages to the brain so that people can respond to stimuli from the environment or changes in the body. They are stored in the neurons' terminal buttons and when the message has been passed they either break down or reabsorbed by the terminal buttons. This process is known as reuptake. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nThe process of neurotransmission takes place when signals across the synapses are transferred with the help of neurotransmitters. Neurotransmitters are broadly divided into agonists and antagonists depending on the role they play. Antagonists are chemicals which counteract the action of a neurotransmitter resulting in prevention of a signal from being passed further. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Neurotransmitters influence many human behaviours such as mood, memory, sexual arousal and mental illness. For this response, the focus will be on dopamine $\\&$ acetylcholine $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nDopamine is a neurotransmitter associated with motivation, learning and pleasure. Acetylcholine plays a role in memory, learning, attention, arousal and involuntary muscle movement. Following studies explain the influence of neurotransmitters to behaviour in detail. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nThe study conducted by Antonova et al in 2011 studied the effect of Acetylcholine (ACh) on memory. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\n The aim of the study was to investigate how blocking of the ACh receptor sites by scopolamine affected spatial memory in participants. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe sample were 20 adult males who were divided into two conditions. In condition one participants were injected with scopolamine and in condition two they were injected with a placebo 90 minutes before the experiment. The participants were put into an fMRI machine while completing a task to navigate and reach a pole. Once the participants reached the pole in the arena task the screen went blank for 30 seconds and started from a different location where the participants had to use their spatial memory to reach the pole again. The task was repeated six times for each participant. Participants then returned 3-4 weeks later and redid the experiment with opposite treatment to the original study. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nThe results of the experiment showed that participants injected with the antagonist scopolamine made more mistakes and had trouble remembering their way to the pole compared to the participants who were in the placebo condition. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The participants had a significant reduction in the activation of the hippocampus compared to when they received a placebo. The ACh availability in the hippocampus impaired the memorization of information for participants in condition 1. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nThe study has several evaluation points. The strengths of the study are there is high internal validity due to the cause and effect relationship between the independent and dependent variable. This essentially means that the changes in the DV are solely due to the change in the IV and not due to extraneous variables. Informed consent was acquired from the participants before the study. The study has high replicability as it was a lab experiment having clear and organized experimental procedures may result in ease of replicability. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nThe limitations of the study are that the sample is restricted to males hence it cannot be generalized to females. This also results in limited generalizability. Due to the way a lab experiment is designed, the participants may show demand characteristics like altering their natural behaviour to agree/disagree with the researchers views or suffer from stress of performing as they are not aware of the aim of the study. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nThe second study explaining the effect of a neurotransmitter on behaviour is Fisher, Aron and Brown (2005) study aimed to investigate the effect of dopamine on romantic love. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ The sample consisted of 17 participants with ten men and seven women who were in love with their partners. The mean age of all participants was 21 years and the mean duration of being in love was 7 months. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe procedure involved all participants being placed in a functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) scanner and they were shown photographs while their brains were being scanned. There were four parts to the experiment. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\n$1.$ For 30 seconds each participant viewed a photograph of the person they loved.\\\n\n$2.$ A 40-second filler activity which counts back from a given number for all participants. \\\n\n$3.$ For 30 seconds participants viewed a photograph of an emotionally neutral acquaintance.\\\n\n$4.$ 20 seconds of counting back from a number. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nAll steps were repeated six times and the procedure lasted for 12 minutes for the participants. The results of the study found that in response to the photographs of their loved ones, activation was observed in dopamine-rich neural systems, primarily the ventral tegmental area (VTA) and caudate nucleus. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ They are called the dopaminergic pathways, which means it generates and transmits dopamine and increases dopamine-related activity in the brain. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The study clearly explains the role of romantic love with dopamine-rich areas of the brain that are associated with reward and motivation. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nThe study has several evaluation points. The strengths of the study are there is high internal validity due to the cause and effect relationship between the independent and dependent variable. This essentially means that the changes in the DV are solely due to the change in the IV and not due to extraneous variables such as if the person has slightly had an argument with a partner or is planning to end the relationship due to unresolved issues. These points may act as an extraneous variable and affect the results. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ The study can be replicated for further studies as the procedure is standardized for all participants which makes the results reliable. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nThe limitations of the study are that the sample size is small which limits generalization. Extraneous factors such as issues in relationships, degree of commitment are subjective factors which are not taken into consideration and may affect the results as the changes in the DV will not be because of the IV in such cases. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nBoth the studies show the influence of neurotransmitters on behaviour. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nWord count: 1024\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nAntonova, E., Parslow, D., Brammer, M., Simmons, A., Williams, S., Dawson, G. R., $\\&$ Morris, R. (2011). Scopolamine disrupts hippocampal activity during allocentric spatial memory in humans: an fMRI study using a virtual reality analogue of the Morris Water Maze. Journal of Psychopharmacology, 25(9), 1256-1265. doi:10.1177/0269881110379285\n\nAron, A., Fisher, H., Mashek, D. J., Strong, G., Li, H., & Brown, L. L. (2005). Reward, Motivation, and Emotion Systems Associated With Early-Stage Intense Romantic Love. Journal of Neurophysiology, 94(1), 327-337. https://doi.org/10.1152/jn.00838.2004"},{"ref":"PS0617","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0617\n\nDiscuss one or more ethical considerations in research investigating the relationship between the brain and behaviour. \n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The question used the command term discuss, therefore, the response should offer a considered and balanced review that includes a range of arguments, factors, or hypotheses that consider the ethical concerns that could arise out of research investigating the relationship between brain and behaviour. An explanation of the ethical concern followed by how it appears in the research needs to be evident. \n\n**1 mark**  if the question was briefly described in less detail or if the question was restated. *(e.g. This essay will discuss the ethical concerns in research investigating the relationship between brain and behaviour) No marks will be awarded for this criterion if answer shows no clear and explicit focus on the demands of the command term and the question.*\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant $\\&$ appropriate terminology is explained in detail *(e.g. informed consent, physical or psychological harm, confidentiality of data, right to withdraw etc.). Since the question specifies one or more ethical considerations, the answer may focus on depth of explanation for one ethical consideration or breadth of understanding on two or more ethical considerations. These terminologies should be linked to the study/ies in Criterion C.*  \n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained but has minimal errors *(e.g. An explanation of the ethical concern is given but there is no explanation on how it appears in the chosen study and how it violates the rights of the participants is not outlined, and/or if there are some errors leading to inaccurate definitions of terminologies)*\n\n**1-2 marks**  if the explanation of relevant terminology has errors and/or limited, and/or superficial *(e.g. the ethical concern is not identified correctly or the explanation has errors and there is no mention of how it appears in the research).* No marks will be awarded for this criterion if no relevant terminology was explained.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed discussion points *(e.g. difficulty in ensuring anonymity and confidentiality of data in case studies, use of deception in the placebo groups, changes to ethical approach over time, focus on protection of participants, importance of debriefing in the research). There should be around 2-3 discussion points, explained and linked to the study/ies mentioned in Criterion C.*\n\n**3-4 marks** if the discussion points *(e.g. simple explanations but shows two different perspectives)* are stated and explained but are not sustained, and with minimal errors and/or if the answer focused on the strengths only or limitations only and if there are only 1-2 discussion points explained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation or discussion points are superficial and mainly descriptive *(e.g. if it only evaluates the study briefly and not the topic overall, or if the discussion points were only stated or described but not linked back to the study, stating an ethical consideration without explanation).* No marks will be awarded for this criterion if no critical thinking or discussion points were explicitly stated and explained in relation to the topic and study/ies.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks**  if there is an organised \\& coherent explanation followed by an evaluation of one or more relevant studies (see Criterion C \\& D ). *(e.g. first paragraph introduces the focus on the question and related terminology, the succeeding paragraphs talk about the studies to be evaluated as well as the actual evaluation of one or more research, and the closing paragraphs or statements link the arguments back to the question)*\n\n**1 mark** if organisation and clarity were not sustained or if the answer is incomplete or unfinished.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nResearch in the field of brain and behaviour is essential for scientists to understand the functions of the brain and its impact on behaviour. Some of the areas that come within this are research on brain and memory, research on neuroplasticity, localisation etc. However, as with any psychological research, protecting research participants is critical and in order to ensure this, various ethical guidelines that govern research have been imposed, such as that of the British Psychologist Association (BPA). $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nTwo of the most recurring ethical concerns in this field are issues of consent and use of deception in the study. Informed consent is the act of gaining approval from the participant prior to the start of the experiment. This is essential as the participant should be aware of the aim of the experiment as well as the research methods in order to give an informed consent.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ This is done to ensure transparency between the researcher and the participants and help build a positive relationship between them. The second often recurring ethical issue is the use of deception.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Deception involves lying or hiding information pertinent to the research from the participants. This is done as sometimes sharing the true aim of the experiment might lead to demand characteristics from the participants leading to reduced validity of the results. However, it is still considered unethical as it negates the value of the consent gained from the participants as they were lied to in the beginning. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The ethical issue of consent will be explored through HM's case study and use of deception will be demonstrated through the study by Freed et.al (2002). $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nMilner (1966) studied patient HM for a period of 50 years. HM had undergone an accident at age 7 and as result of which had to surgically remove a part of his hippocampus to stop the seizures which started after the fall.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ However, after the surgery it was found that HM had suffered from anterograde amnesia and couldn't recall any events from after the accident for more than 15 seconds. This was an unusual opportunity for researchers to understand how memory worked and thus began the 50 year long case study on HM through which they were establish the importance of the hippocampus in the conversion of short term to long term memories. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe ethical issue that arose out of HM's case study was informed consent. As stated above, the participant needs to be fully informed of the research prior to the start and in HM's case, even though he would be told of the study and may also comprehend some information, he wouldn't be able to recall giving the consent. Though, it can be argued that his family members had given the consent, he was still an adult at the time of the start of the experiment, thus making it imperative that his consent should be legally gained. Also, as this was a longitudinal case study, it involved long-term infringement upon his privacy that HM had no way to control. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nThe second study that will be discussed in this essay was conducted by Freed et al (2002). $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ He conducted a study to understand the role of dopamine on the treatment of Parkinson's disease by using a sample of 40 patients between the ages of 34 - 75. These participants were then divided into two groups, the experimental group who underwent a surgery with a novel method of dopamine neurons transplant while the control group underwent a false surgery. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Results showed that the nerve transplant had helped reduce symptoms of Parkinsons most noticeably in the younger patients. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe ethical issue of deception is clearly demonstrated in this study as the control group were deceived into thinking that they had undergone a surgery. This gives rise to immense ethical issues as the ensuing anxiety of undergoing a surgery followed by the stress of post-surgery was present. This is even more apparent as the population were slightly older participants who possibly had other chronic conditions further impacting their stress levels. This is referred to as deception of the placebo effect as all placebo groups are in effect deceived during and after the experiment. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nFrom the research studies cited above, the two ethical issues of deception and consent can be clearly seen. However, while both these issues endangered the rights of the participants in some ways, we also need to conduct a cost-benefit analysis to understand why it occurred and how it was unavoidable. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ While, it is easier to understand the case study of HM as at least parental and family consent was obtained and HM was not psychologically capable of giving informed consent. Apart from that full precautions were taken to ensure anonymity of his data as he was called HM through the period of the research to preserve his anonymity. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ However, the issue of deception in the second research is slightly more glaring as the participants thought they had undergone a real surgery and went through the psychological trauma associated with it for quite a while. Once again, to ensure a strong cause and effect relationship, it was essential for the psychologists to ensure that there was a placebo group in order to further strengthen the results of the study and the efficacy of the treatment.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$  It must be noted that any research in this field has massive implication in the medical industry with a possibility of the treatment being advised to other patients and thus researchers have the responsibility ensure the efficacy of the treatment being suggested with sound and valid research. One possible way to mitigate the effects of the deception would be to conduct intensive debriefing sessions with regular follow ups to ensure no long term impact was felt by the participants. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nIn conclusion, we can say that while ethical guidelines established by BPA have served to streamline research conducted in this field, there are times when the rationale behind the research and its possible implications must be considered and decisions needs to be made on an individual basis. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nWord count: 994\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nMilner, B., Corkin, S., \\& Teuber, H. (1968). Further analysis of the hippocampal amnesic syndrome: 14-year follow-up study of H.M. Neuropsychologia, 6(3), 215-234. https://doi.org/10.1016/0028-3932(68)90021-3\n\nFreed, C. R., Greene, P., Breeze, R. E., Tsai, W., DuMouchel, W., Kao, R., Dillon, S., Winfield, H. N., Culver, S., Trojanowski, J. Q., Eidelberg, D., \\& Fahn, S. (2001). Transplantation of embryonic dopamine neurons for severe Parkinson's disease. The New England Journal of Medicine, 344(10), 710-719. https://doi.org/10.1056/nejm200103083441002"},{"ref":"PS0618","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0618\n\nDiscuss research methods used in investigating the relationship between brain and behaviour.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The question used the command term *discuss*, therefore, the response should offer a considered and balanced review that includes a range of arguments, factors, or hypotheses that consider the research methods used in studies that investigate the relationship between brain and behaviour. The behaviour specified in the question can be any cognitive process but has to be explicitly identified. \n\n**1 mark** if the question was briefly described in less detail or if the question was restated. *(e.g. This essay will discuss the research methods used in research investigating the relationship between brain and behaviour.*) No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer shows no clear and explicit focus on the demands of the command term and the question.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant\\ & appropriate terminology is explained in detail *(e.g.: true experiments, Quasi experiments, case studies etc.)*. Since the question specifies research methods, one or more research methods may be used , the answer may focus on depth of explanation for one research method or breadth of understanding on two or more research methods. These terminologies should be linked to the study/ies in Criterion C. \n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained but has minimal errors *(e.g. An explanation of the research method is given but there is no explanation of its characteristics and  how it is used in the chosen study,  and/or if there are some errors leading to inaccurate definitions of terminologies).*  \n\n**1-2 marks** if the explanation of relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited, and/or superficial *(e.g. the research method is not identified correctly or the explanation has errors and there is no mention of how it appears in the research).* No marks will be awarded for this criterion if no relevant terminology was explained.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies *(e.g., aim, procedures, results & conclusion) are explained (e.g., aim, procedures, results \\& conclusion)* and used to support the answer or the arguments stated. Studies to be used are but not limited to:\n\n-    Case study - Milner (1966),  Squire (2002)\n\n-    Quasi experiment - MAguire (2000), Sharot (2007)\n\n-    True experiments - Antonova (2011), Draganski et al (2003)\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors and/or is limited. *(e.g the aim, procedure, results \\& conclusions of the study was not clearly presented)*\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. *(e.g the study was not linked to the discussion points mentioned, or if the study was not explained but just mentioned briefly or described superficially).* No marks will be awarded for this criterion if no relevant research was explained.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed discussion points *(e.g. internal validity of the study, ecological validity, ability to determine cause and effect relationship between the variables, ethical concerns in the research).* There should be around 2-3 discussion points, explained and linked to the study/ies mentioned in Criterion C.\n\n**3-4 marks**  if the discussion points *(e.g. simple explanations but shows two different perspectives)* are stated and clearly explained but are not sustained, and with minimal errors and/or if the answer focused on the strengths only or limitations only and/or if there are only 1-2 discussion points explained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation or discussion points are superficial and mainly descriptive *(e.g. if it only evaluates the study briefly and not the topic overall, or if the discussion points were only stated or described but not linked back to the study, stating the research method without explanation)*. No marks will be awarded for this criterion if no critical thinking or discussion points were explicitly stated and explained in relation to the topic and study/ies.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is an organised \\& coherent explanation followed by an evaluation of one or more relevant studies (see Criterion C \\& D ). *(e.g. first paragraph introduces the focus on the question and related terminology, the succeeding paragraphs talk about the studies to be evaluated as well as the actual evaluation of one or more research, and the closing paragraphs or statements link the arguments back to the question)*\n\n**1 mark** if the organisation and clarity were not sustained or if the answer is incomplete or unfinished.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \n\nResearch in the field of brain and behaviour is essential for scientists to understand the functions of the brain and its impact on behaviour. One of the behaviours that is commonly observed is memory. The relationship between brain area and its impact on memory is investigated through the use of various research methods in psychology. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nWhile there are various research methods used in this field, the two most commonly used are case studies and true experiments.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Case studies are characterized by the in-depth study of an individual or a small group of people with the same characteristics.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Another feature of case studies is the use of methodological triangulation, this means that multiple methods of data collection such as observation, interviews, surveys are done to gain data.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The second type of research method is true experiments. True experiments are characterized by the presence of well-defined variables. An independent variable is manipulated by the researcher, in order to see the effect on a dependent variable, and participants are randomly assigned to conditions. True experiments are often conducted in a lab setting with control variables helping to establish the causality of the relationship.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Additionally, apart from this, most research also uses advanced technologies to understand the structure and function of the brain, such as MRI and fMRI.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The two studies that will be discussed in this essay are Millner (1966) case study on HM and the true experiment conducted by Antonova (2011). \n\nMilner (1966) conducted a longitudinal study on patient HM for a period of 50 years.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  HM had met with an accident at age 7 and as a result had to surgically remove a part of his hippocampus to stop the seizures which started after the fall.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ However, after the surgery it was found that HM had suffered from anterograde amnesia and couldn't recall any events from after the accident for more than 15 seconds. This was an unusual opportunity for researchers to understand how memory worked and thus began the 50 year long case study on HM using various methods such as interviews, observations, cognitive testing and also MRI to study the structure of his brain specifically the hippocampus. Through this they were able to establish the importance of the hippocampus in the conversion of short term to long term memories. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nHM is a classic example of how case studies are used for research in this field as it was an in-depth study on one person and was conducted over a long period of time.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Additionally, it also used method triangulation as HM was observed in a lab setting and at his home as well as interviewed and also underwent MRI testing.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  A significant strength of this study is the role it played in establishing the understanding of the hippocampus as a key player in the conversion of memories. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$However, as it is a case study it has low generalizability as HM faced unique circumstances with his injury, thus the results cannot be generalized to the rest of the population.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ \n\nThe second study that will be discussed in this essay was a true experiment conducted by Antonova (2011).$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ He investigated the effect of neurotransmitters on the encoding of spatial memories. Participants were  divided into two conditions - an experimental group where they were injected with scopolamine and a control group with a placebo injection. Scopolamine is a beta blocker that inhibits the neurotransmitter response and would help the researcher to understand the effect of neurotransmitter acetylcholine.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ They were then placed in an fMRI machine and were asked to play a video game in which they had to navigate to a pole. The results showed that in the experimental group there was a reduced activation of the hippocampus as compared to the placebo group signifying the importance of acetylcholine and its role on spatial memory.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nThis was a true experiment as the IV ( activation of the hippocampus) was clearly measured through the use of the fMRI scanner and the DV (presence of scopolamine) was clearly manipulated as the participants were put into two groups. Further this was a double blind test meaning neither the researchers nor the participants knew which group they belonged to.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ A significant strength of the study is the high internal validity due to its ability to control variables as well as a double blind test furthering the cause and effect relationship.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ However, it has reduced ecological validity as the test was conducted in highly artificial conditions and in a real-life situation there would be many factors that could influence the formation of spatial memories.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ \n\nThe research cited have used two different research methodologies and contribute meaningfully to gain an understanding in this field. While case studies that are conducted over an extended period of time help researchers to gain a deep understanding of how the changes in the brain structure affect the behaviour (memory) of the person and how this effect can change over a period of time as it was found that while HM could not ever remembering performing any cognitive tests his time for completing the tests did reduce overtime indicating that his procedural memory was intact.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ One can argue that case studies are more holistic in nature while true experiments can be reductionistic as they are often conducted with defined variables. However, true experiments on the other hand, have an immense value due to their high causality helping researchers to pinpoint exactly how the brain impacts behaviour.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ \n\nThus we can conclude that, while both these methodologies contribute in their own way, it is essential to continue research in this field to ensure that the conclusions drawn from the research are reliable and valid.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ \n\nWord count: 994\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\n Milner, B., Corkin, S., \\& Teuber, H. (1968). Further analysis of the hippocampal amnesic syndrome: 14-year follow-up study of H.M. Neuropsychologia, 6(3), 215-234. https://doi.org/10.1016/0028-3932(68)90021-3\n\nFreed, C. R., Greene, P., Breeze, R. E., Tsai, W., DuMouchel, W., Kao, R., Dillon, S., Winfield, H. N., Culver, S., Trojanowski, J. Q., Eidelberg, D., \\& Fahn, S. (2001). Transplantation of embryonic dopamine neurons for severe Parkinson's disease. The New England Journal of Medicine, 344(10), 710-719. https://doi.org/10.1056/nejm200103083441002"},{"ref":"PS0619","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0619\n\nEvaluate one or more studies related to neuroplasticity and its influence to human behaviour.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The question used the command term *evaluate*, therefore, the response requires the candidate to make an appraisal of one or more studies related to neuroplasticity. The focus of the evaluation should be upon the study/ies, not neuroplasticity. Although both strengths and limitations should be addressed, this does not have to be evenly balanced.\n\n**1 mark**  if the question was briefly described in less detail or if the question was restated. *(e.g. Some of the research studies that explain Neuroplasticity.) No marks will be awarded for this criteria if the answer did not show clear and explicit focus on addressing the command term and the topic in question.*\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant \\& appropriate terminology is explained in detail *(e.g. neuroplasticity, neural pruning, dendritic thickening, neural pathways, terms related to research methods such as - lab experiment, Independent variable (IV), dependent variable (DV), MRI, fMRI  and terms related to ethical considerations such as  - deception, consent etc.)* Because the question asks to evaluate studies, students need to show an understanding of the research method used in this topic followed by explaining its value in understanding human behaviour to score full marks in this criterion.\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors *(e.g. Neuroplasticity is explained along with research but there is no explanation of the research methods used in the studies cited.)* \n\n**1-2 marks**  if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited *(e.g. Neuroplasticity is only briefly stated or described but the research cited shows minimal understanding of research terminology with errors in its usage).* No marks will be awarded for this criterion if no relevant terminology was explained.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, well-balanced appraisal of both strengths and limitations of the studies, around 2-3 strengths and limitations *(e.g. methodological, cultural, and gender considerations, ethical concerns regarding the study and validity and reliability of the study) Strengths and limitations should be mentioend and explained in relation to the study/ies presented in Criterion C. (e.g. One limitation of research studies on neuroplasticity is the use of correlational research to link physiological processes in the brain to human behaviour. However, this is may not show cause and effect relationship between the process of neuroplasticity and behaviour.)*\n\n**3-4 marks** if the evaluation points are only based on either strengths or limitations or if the answer only shows around 1-2 strengths and limitations of relevant studies on neuroplasticity.\n\n**1-2 marks** will be awarded if the evaluation is based mostly on the topic and not on the evaluation of studies or if the evaluation is limited or superficial *(e.g. stating the specific limitation but not explaining what it is about or how it is related to the topic or the study/ies)*. No marks are awarded for this criterion if no relevant evaluation is explained in detail.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is an organised \\& coherent explanation of the research related to the topic in the question *(e.g. first paragraph introduces the focus on the question and related terminology, the succeeding paragraphs talk about the studies to be evaluated as well as the actual evaluation of one or more research, and the closing paragraphs or statements link the arguments back to the question).*\n\n**1 mark** if the organisation and clarity were not sustained or if the answer is incomplete or unfinished.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nResearchers have found a correlation between behaviour and the development of certain brain regions. It is argued that our behaviour directly effects our brain and some regions of the brain regions correspond to the demands of our environment. The understanding that our brain regions can change and adapt as per the demands of our environment has opened new ways and helped researchers realise the potential of the brain and how it can be trained effectively. One such phenomenon found by researchers is known as neuroplasticity. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nNeuroplasticity is defined as a process that explains how the brain changes itself to suit the demands of the environment.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The brain is considered to be plastic in nature, meaning that it is malleable and can change itself depending upon the environment, this is known as brain plasticity.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The brain consists of millions of neurons that pass information to each other, forming neural pathways. These neural pathways get strengthened through dendritic branching, indicating that neurons are firing continuously, leading to a growth in its dendrites.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ This leads to structural plasticity or changes in the structure of the brain. Research in this field is often quantitative in nature and is conducted under highly controlled lab conditions with the use of latest scanning technologies such as MRI to understand the changes that are occurring in the brain.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Laboratory experiments randomly allocated participants to conditions. An independent variable is then manipulated in order to see the effect on a dependent variable that can be easily measured. Apart from that, the extraneous variables are often controlled to help establish a strong cause and effect relationship.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Quasi-experiments, on the other hand, can be utilised when the random allocation of participants to groups is not possible. Two studies investigating neuroplasticity conducted by Draganski (2004) and by Maguire (2000) will be evaluated in this essay.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ \n\nDraganski (2004) wanted to investigate if learning a skill, such as juggling, would lead to changes in the brain's structure.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ A self-selected sample of 24 young volunteers was divided into two conditions - one who learned juggling and the other was a control group. A baseline MRI test was conducted on all participants, after which the experimental group learnt the juggling routine. A second MRI was done once they had mastered the routine, and a final third one was done after a couple of months when they had stopped practising the routine and could not do it successfully. While the first MRI showed no differences between both the groups, the second MRI showed the jugglers had a significantly larger grey matter in the mid-temporal region as measured through VBM (Voxel-based morphometry) a technique used to assess the differences in the anatomy of the brain. After a period of three months, once the jugglers had stopped practising their routine and were unable to perform it, a  third scan was conducted, showing a decrease in the grey matter in the same parts of the brain. The mid-temporal region of the brain was the part of the brain with the most growth as it was a skill involving visual-motor coordination and it helps in visual memory. There was no change in the grey matter of the control group.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nThe results from this study can be linked to the concept of Neuroplasticity as the increased grey matter after the second scan could have occurred due to dendritic branching as the jugglers were continuously practising a new skill until they were an expert in it - the effect of environment. There is further evidence of plasticity as it could be seen that the grey matter had decreased once the jugglers had stopped practising and were no longer able to perform it successfully, showing the process of neural pruning.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ One of the key strengths of this study is the use of MRI scans which helped to measure the changes in the grey matter that occurred after each of the stages.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Additionally, the baseline pre-test helped to establish the size of the grey matter already present effectively eliminating it as a confounding variable in the study. However, as the participants were allowed to go home during the period between the stages, there is no control on how much they would have practiced the skill of juggling which could have acted as an extraneous variable effecting the results of the study.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nMaguire et. al (2000) wanted to investigate if the continuous practice of a skill, such as driving, would lead to changes in the brain's structure.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ A sample of 16 right-handed male taxi drivers was used who had a driving experience of minimum of 1.5 years in addition to passing the Knowledge test - a requirement to obtain a taxi licence in London. MRI scanning was used and the participant's grey matter was scanned and later compared to 50 other right-handed males who were not taxi drivers. Technology such as the VBM (Voxel-based morphology) was used which helps to determine the density of the grey matter along with pixel counting which helps to determine the size of the area being measured.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Results from the VBM showed that the taxi drivers with many years of experience had a higher volume of right posterior hippocampus, an area in the brain associated with spatial memory. This indicates that dendritic branching had occurred as part of the process of neuroplasticity in the brain of the taxi drivers.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The pixel counting also showed a larger area of posterior hippocampus in the taxi drivers as compared to the non-taxi drivers as a result of passing the knowledge test and driving continuously for the past few years.  $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The findings above suggest that neuroplasticity was evident due to changes observed in the taxi driver's brain.\n\nOne of the strengths of the study is it's a quasi-experiment as the Independent variable (taxi drivers or non-taxi drivers) was naturally occurring.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Additionally, it was a correlational study as VBM results clearly showed that the more years the participants were driving caused changes to the brain structure helping to show a clear link between environment and brain plasticity.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ However, the all male sample is a clear disadvantage as it means the results cannot be generalized to the other half of the population as women may learn spatial skills differently from males. The results from this study can thus be linked to the concept of Neuroplasticity as there is clear indication to the changes in the grey matter of the participant's brain due to the advanced imaging techniques used.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ \n\nAs can be seen from both the research cited above, the usage of advanced scanning techniques such as MRI is essential as it helps scientists to understand the level of changes taking place inside the brain and makes it easier to measure it. Additionally, the use of lab experiments makes it easier to establish the cause and effect relationship between the environmental demands and neuroplasticity in the brain.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ However, one of the limitations of using lab experiments is the data is often a snapshot and does not include the changes that could happen in the brain due to repeated exposure to the environment for an extended period of time.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ One way to overcome this is the use of case studies such as the one by Luby et.al (2003) who conducted a longitudinal research to test the effect of childhood poverty and the amount of grey matter in the hippocampus and amygdala. However, research in this field has far reaching implications as it helps scientists understand the functions of the brain better and understand the value of human environment interactions in a scientifically quantifiable manner.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$  \n\nIn conclusion, both Draganski (2004) and Maguire et al. (2000) provide compelling evidence for the concept of neuroplasticity by demonstrating how environmental demands can lead to structural changes in the brain. Draganski’s study is particularly strong due to its controlled experimental design and use of baseline MRI scans, though the lack of control over individual practice time is a limitation. Maguire’s quasi-experimental approach offers valuable insight into real-world applications of neuroplasticity, with clear links between experience and hippocampal development, though the gender-biased sample limits generalisability. Overall, these studies highlight the brain’s remarkable ability to adapt, supporting the growing understanding of its malleability in response to environmental stimulation. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ \n\nWord count: 1240\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nDraganski, Bogdan & Gaser, C \\& Busch, Volker \\& Schuierer, G & Bogdahn, Ulrich \\& May, Arne. (2004). Neuroplasticity: changes in grey matter induced by training. Nature. 427. 311-2.\n\nMaguire, E. A., Gadian, D. G., Johnsrude, I. S., Good, C. D., Ashburner, J., Frackowiak, R. S. J., \\& Frith, C. D. (2000). Navigation-related structural change in the hippocampi of taxi drivers. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America, 97(8), 4398-4403. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.070039597"},{"ref":"PS0681","marks":"22","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0681\n\nDiscuss localisation of function in the brain. \n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nThere is significant research evidence to suggest that the human brain displays some localisation of function. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ This means that different parts of the brain are specialised in different functions (e.g., memory). Therefore, if a person were to experience damage to a particular brain area, they may experience difficulties with, or loss of, the corresponding function. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Understanding if certain parts of the brain are localized for specific functions is a valuable area of research with some significant implications. For example, knowledge gained can predict future outcomes and consequences for patients with brain tumours, or for those who have experienced brain damage from an accident.  A great deal of research has suggested that some memory functions may be localized in the hippocampus. To discuss the possibility of localization of memory function in the hippocampus, we can examine Milner's case study of HM (as cited in Scoville & Milner, 1957) and Maquire's (2000) MRI study of London taxi drivers who have extensive experience memorising and navigating the streets of London. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nOne study that supports localisation of function is Milner's case study of HM. To treat ongoing seizures, which may have been the result of a bike accident as a child, HM underwent surgery to remove his hippocampus and surrounding tissue on both sides of his brain. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nAfter the operation, the seizures improved, but HM showed some problems with his memory. As a result of HM's memory problems, Milner conducted a longitudinal case study with HM. The case study aimed to understand the extent and possible causes of HM's memory impairments. Milner used many different methods (methodological triangulation) to carry out her case study. A detailed case and medical history was obtained. Observations were made of HM and detailed field notes were taken. Milner conducted interviews with HM, his doctors, and his family members. She also conducted cognitive tests with HM and a reverse mirror drawing task where HM was asked to trace an outline of a star, but he could only see the mirrored reflection. He did this once daily over a few days (as cited in Squire, 2009). $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe results of the case study showed that HM's short-term memory seemed to operate okay, and he could remember things as long as he was continually focused on them. However, he could not form new long-term memories, and if he stopped focusing on something it was forgotten. Furthermore, each time he performed the star task, he had no memory of ever doing it before, but his performance kept improving. Therefore, he showed improvements in the performance of new skills, suggesting that the formation of procedural memory is different from other types of long-term memories. He could improve at new procedural tasks but he could not remember learning those tasks. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThese results showed that HM could not acquire new long-term declarative (explicit) memories, but he could still form new long-term procedural memories (knowing how to do things). This suggests that the hippocampus, which was removed from his brain, is important for the formation and consolidation of long-term explicit memories. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Thus, the formation and consolidation of new long-term declarative memories may be localized in the hippocampus. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nThe extent to which this study supports the idea that some memory functions are localized in the hippocampus depends on the strengths and limitations of the study. Therefore, it is essential to evaluate the research. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ \n\nThis case study collected rich, detailed data using methodological triangulation. Furthermore, the data gathered from these multiple sources corroborated with each other. This helps add to the credibility of the findings. Furthermore, the study observed HM in his natural setting over an extended period which increases the ecological validity. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nHowever, several potential limitations need to be considered. Firstly, HM had surrounding brain tissue removed along with his hippocampus. Thus, we cannot be certain that the removal of the surrounding brain tissue has not impacted his memory. The lack of confidence in the findings is also partly because this was a case study, and no independent variable was manipulated. Therefore, no cause-and-effect relationship can be established between the hippocampus and the formation of new long-term declarative memories. Furthermore, the case study was on a single patient; therefore, it may be difficult to generalise these results to a wider population. However, other research studies provide triangulated evidence for the role of the hippocampus in memory, adding further credibility to the findings. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nMaguire's (2000) study further supports the idea that some memory functions may be localised in the hippocampus. The study aimed to investigate changes in the human brain resulting from extensive memorisation and navigation experience. Participants were 16 healthy, right-handed male London taxi drivers chosen due to their extensive memorisation and driving experience. Their MRI scans were compared to those of 50 healthy, right-handed, non-taxi drivers. Magnetic fields and radio waves were used by the MRI scanner, and these were converted into 3D images of brain structure. Hippocampal grey matter volume was measured for each participant. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe results showed that the taxi drivers had significantly larger posterior hippocampi compared to non-taxi drivers, indicating that the extensive memorisation and navigation of the streets of London may have resulted in neuroplastic changes in the hippocampi. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ This suggests that the hippocampi may play a significant role in memory and spatial navigation, providing further support for the idea that some memory function is localised in the hippocampus. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ \n\nAs with Milner's case study of HM, this study has not established a cause-and-effect relationship between memory and the hippocampus. In this case, it is because the researchers have conducted a quasi-experiment where no variables were manipulated. The participants were already taxi drivers (or not), and participants could not randomly be allocated to conditions. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nThe use of MRI in this study can, however, be considered a strength as it means that data was quantitatively and objectively measured, which reduces the possibility of researcher bias. Researcher bias was further reduced by ensuring that the researchers assessing each MRI were blind to whether it belonged to a taxi driver or a control participant.  $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ \n\nAlthough both of these studies suggest that the hippocampus may specialise in memory functions, it is important to note that many areas of the brain can perform multiple functions. Furthermore, different brain areas often collaborate and integrate information from diverse brain regions. The human brain is sophisticated, and there is still a great deal that we do not know about it. It is difficult and overly simplistic to definitively attribute a single function to a single area of the brain. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nIn conclusion, there appears to be a reasonable body of triangulated evidence (including Milner's Case Study of HM, and Maguire's MRI research) to suggest that one of the localized functions of the hippocampus is that it plays a major role in memory functions. However, suggesting that memory is entirely localised in the hippocampus would be overly simplistic. Most brain functions, including memory, require a coordinated approach from several brain regions.  [F] text  $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nWord count: 1164\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nMaguire, E. A., Gadian, D. G., Johnsrude, I. S., Good, C. D., Ashburner, J., Frackowiak, R. S., & Frith, C. D. (2000). Navigation-related structural change in the hippocampi of taxi drivers. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, 97(8), 4398-4403. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.070039597 \n\nScoville, W. B., & Milner, B. (1957). Loss of recent memory after bilateral hippocampal lesions. Journal of Neurology, Neurosurgery &amp; Psychiatry, 20(1), 11-21. https://doi.org/10.1136/jnnp.20.1.11 \n\nSquire, L. R. (2009). The legacy of patient H.M. for Neuroscience. Neuron, 61(1), 6-9. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuron.2008.12.023 \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Markscheme}}$:\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question is explained. The question uses the command term discuss; therefore, the response should provide a balanced assessment of the concept of localisation that considers a range of arguments or factors. Focus on the question should be maintained throughout the response. \n\n**1 mark** if the question is not accurately explained, has limited explanation, or is simply restated (e.g., the essay mentioned that it will discuss localisation but no additional explanation was provided). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer is not relevant to the question.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant and appropriate knowledge and terminology related to localisation is explained in detail without errors and are explicitly linked to the studies in Criterion C (e.g., $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$\n\nDifferent parts of the brain are specialised in different functions (e.g., memory). Therefore if a person were to experience damage to a particular brain area, they may experience difficulties with, or loss of, the corresponding function$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$\n\n). \n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors but is lacking in some detail or shows only a brief explanation of the theory/topic (e.g, certain functions are localised in certain brain areas).\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited or superficial in nature (e.g., if the response suggests that all brain function is localised). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if no relevant terminology is explained or if the response describes lateralisation, or hemispheric specialisation, instead of localisation.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$<enter>\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments stated, and a specific link is made between the findings of the study(ies) and the question. Relevant details, including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusions, from one or more studies, should be included. Studies that could be referred to include, but are not limited to:\n\n- Case study of patient HM who had his hippocampus removed: Milner (1966)\n\n- MRI investigation of London taxi drivers with extensive experience memorising and navigating the streets of London: Maguire (2000)\n\n- Case study investigation of patient SM on the role of the amygdala in fear: Feinstein et al. (2011)\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study is explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors (e.g., the aim, procedure, results, and conclusions of the study are not presented, or there are some errors with regard to the relevance of the findings or details of the methodology).\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research is used with insufficient detail, errors, or superficial links to the question. No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer does not include any relevant studies.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer shows relevant, consistent, and well-developed critical thinking points. Critical thinking points could include a combination of the following: strengths and limitations of the research methods used in supporting research studies and their conclusions, strengths and limitations of the relevant theories, potential applications of the findings, or an acknowledgment of complexities and alternative arguments. At least 2-3 discussion or critical thinking points are explained (e.g., $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$This case study collected rich detailed data, making use of methodological triangulation. Furthermore, the data gathered from these multiple sources corroborated with each other. This helps add to the credibility of the findings$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$ OR $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The human brain is sophisticated, and there is still a great deal that we do not know about it. It is difficult and overly simplistic to definitively attribute a single function to a single area of the brain.$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$).\n\n**3-4 marks** if relevant critical thinking points are presented (as above), but they are not always fully developed. They may not be directly linked to the specific study or theory, or they may be limited in number. For example, a response might state that the study is low in internal validity without explaining what this means in reference to the specific study. At least 1-2 discussion or critical thinking points are explained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if critical thinking points are superficial or contain inaccuracies (e.g., evaluation points only stated but not explained properly). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer does not contain relevant critical thinking points, discussion, or evaluation. \n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the essay has an organised structure, including a clear introduction (which outlines the focus of the essay), body paragraphs (which outline the relevant study or studies as described in Criterion C; followed by evaluation of those studies), and concluding paragraphs or statements (which link back to the question by highlighting how the each of the studies either supports or refutes the idea that some brain functions are localised).\n\n**1 mark** if the organisation and clarity are not sustained throughout the answer (e.g., unfinished response, lack of a clear introduction, lack of body paragraphs, and/or lack of a conclusion; a structure that is not logical or easy to follow; or incomplete description of key terms and studies). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the response does not meet any of the standards above.\n\n[6 marks]"},{"ref":"PS1642","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS1642\n\nDescribe the role of neurons in human behaviour.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\nNeurons are cells that are the building blocks of the nervous system. The process of neurotransmission (and the release of neurotransmitters) relies on neurons. Neurotransmission is the process of sending electrochemical messages throughout the nervous system, via neurons that release neurotransmitters. Neurotransmission is essential for everyday functioning, including basic survival functions, movement, cognitive processes and emotions. The nervous system is made up of bundles of nerve cells called neurons. When a message travels along the axon of a neuron, it takes the form of an electrical impulse. Once the message reaches the axon terminals, chemical messengers called neurotransmitters are released from the terminal sacs into the synapse. These neurotransmitters bind with receptor sites on the dendrites of the postsynaptic neuron like a key fitting into a lock. Depending on the neurotransmitter and the receptors in the synapse, the postsynaptic neuron is either stimulated (excitatory) or inhibited (inhibitory). In doing so, neurotransmission can influence behaviour and cognition.\n\nOne study that highlights the role of neurons (and neurotransmitters) in human behaviour is Antonova et al. (2011). Antonova aimed to investigate how changes in the neurotransmitter acetylcholine affect spatial memory and brain activity. The study used a repeated measures experimental design in which participants were randomly assigned to receive either scopolamine, which blocks acetylcholine receptors (on the postsynaptic neuron), or a placebo, and then completed a virtual reality Arena Task designed to test spatial memory while undergoing fMRI scanning to measure brain activity, particularly in the hippocampus. The results showed that participants performed worse on the spatial memory task and exhibited reduced hippocampal activation in the scopolamine condition compared to the placebo condition. The study concluded that acetylcholine plays a key role in memory and hippocampal functioning, demonstrating that neurotransmitter activity influences cognitive processes, and highlighting that the process of neurotransmission (and the release of neurotransmitters) relies on neurons. The study also shows that neuron activity can directly affect human behaviour such as memory.\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible ideas \\& key terms:}}}$\n\nNeurons: specialised cells that transmit information in the nervous system\n\nStructure of a neuron: dendrites, soma (cell body), axon, axon terminals\n\nElectrochemical transmission:\n- electrical impulse along the axon (action potential)\n- chemical transmission across the synapse\n\nNeurotransmitters (e.g. acetylcholine, dopamine, serotonin)\n\nSynapse: gap between neurons where neurotransmitters are released\n\nRole: neurotransmitters increase or decrease the likelihood of a neuron firing.\n\nReceptor sites: specific binding sites on the postsynaptic neuron\n\nRole in behaviour: neurons enable communication that allows for changes in behaviour and cognition.\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible key studies:}}}$\n\n- Antonova et al. (2011): acetylcholine and spatial memory\n- Fisher et al. (2005): dopamine and romantic love\n\n$\\textcolor{#d19f24}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible structure/organisation of response:}}}$\n - **Direct Answer (DA)**: Begin with a statement that immediately starts describing or defining the term neuron.\n - Following paragraphs: **Define key terms (KT)**: Provide a detailed description of the role of neurons in human behaviour.\n - Next paragraph: Describe the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of a **study** highlights the role of neurons **(S)**.\n - Closing statement: Show the **link (L)** between the results of the study and the question. between the results of the study and the question. How do the findings of this study highlight the role of neurons in human behaviour?\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\n\\\nAntonova, E., Parslow, D., Brammer, M., Simmons, A., Williams, S., Dawson, G. R., & Morris, R. (2011). Scopolamine disrupts hippocampal activity during allocentric spatial memory in humans: An fMRI study using a virtual reality analogue of the Morris Water Maze. Journal of Psychopharmacology, 25(9), 1256–1265."}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"PS0514","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0514\n\nExplain one ethical consideration on one research study on the brain and human behaviour. \n\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nThe response should have a direct answer on the topic posed in the question within the first few lines. *(e.g. There are various ethical guidelines in psychological research such as …*) As the question clearly refers to ethical considerations, it should be made clear that any violation of ethical guidelines can be considered an ethical issue/consideration and should be referred to in the first few lines. A specific ethical guideline/s which has been violated in the context of the study from within the sub-topic of brain and behaviour must be mentioned along with a clear explanation. (*e.g. Therefore, we can understand how this could be considered an ethical consideration in the study of…*)\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nThere should be accurate knowledge and understanding of the topic by using all or some key terms such as:\n\n1.    Consent - giving details about the experiment to the participants and receiving permission from them \n\n2.    Anonymity - keeping the name and other details that might reveal the person's identity confidential.\n\n3.    Rights to withdraw - the rights of the participant to withdraw from the experiment anytime they want to.  Even after the experiment is over, they can choose to withdraw their data\n\n4.    Deception - Giving the participant false information about the aim of the study\n\n5.    Undue psychological stress - psychological stress that the participant may face during the experiment\n\n6.    Physical harm - the possibility of physical harm the participant may face during the experiment\n\n7.    Animal welfare and protection  - Laws established to protect the welfare and life of the animals used in research.\n\nThe command term in the question is $`$explain'. The response should provide a detailed account, including possible causes or reasons. Therefore, while explaining ethical violations is essential, there should also be a focus on meeting the command term. There should be a clear explanation of the topic and why these ethical violations would occur in studies like these (*e.g. This could occur due to…*).\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study - including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study. *Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n-    Milner (1966) case study on HM\n\n-    Antonova (2011) on the role of neurotransmitters on behaviour \n\n-    Squire (2002) case study on Eugene Pauly\n\n-    Rosenzweig, Bennet $\\&$ Diamond (1972) on the role of neuroplasticity on human behaviour using animal studies. \n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nThere should be a clear link between the results of the study to the topic using one of the key terms mentioned in the introduction. (*e.g. Rosenzweig, Bennet & Diamond (1972) used rats to understand neuroplasticity and its possible effects on human behaviour. In order to understand the changes that had taken place in the rat's brain, they were killed, and a brain study was done which is in direct conflict with the ethical guideline on animal welfare which states that no harm should come to animals as a consequence of a study. This study had a clear ethical violation as even though there could be a justification for the use of such methods, it is still undeniably unethical*)\n\n**Marking limits:**\n\n-    Not selecting an appropriate study from the topic. (*no marks will be awarded*) \n\n-    Focussing on ethical guidelines and not ethical issues of the study. (*up to 4 marks will be awarded depending on the research cited*)\n\n-    A relevant study is described but no clear links to ethics are made. (*up to 4 marks will be awarded*)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nEthical guidelines in psychological research are very important, and it is critical that all research follows these guidelines to be recognised and included as part of the wider database. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$  $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Various Psychological organisations around the world, such as APA (American Psychological Association) and BPS (British Psychological Society), are responsible for issuing and enforcing these guidelines.  They are created to protect the participants during and after research. One of the common ethical guidelines is informed consent - this refers to the process of receiving consent from the participants before the start of the experiment. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ It is to be noted that parental consent is also required in the case of participants younger than 16 years. This is done to ensure that the participants have complete information about the study they will participate in and agree to share their data as part of the research process. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ All necessary information regarding the study topic and its procedure should be mentioned in the form the participants must sign before the experiment starts. Additionally, the consent sheet should also include the rights of the participants, such as ensuring their confidentiality, anonymity, and reiterating their right to withdraw from the study at any time they want. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ One of the most well-known case studies conducted was on HM by Milner in 1966, which clearly shows the violation of this ethical guideline. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nHM was a 27-year-old man who was suffering from continuous seizures since the age of 7 when he was involved in a bicycle accident. After various treatments, his posterior and anterior hippocampus were surgically removed as a last attempt to treat his seizures. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Though his seizures completely stopped, it was observed that he could not convert short-term memories to long-term memories. Milner then used HM as a case study up until he died in 2008 to examine the role of the hippocampus in the conversion of memories and performed various tests and procedures on him. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ He was continually subjected to a series of MRI scans, cognitive tests, interviews, observations, etc, for an extended period of time to test the effect of his surgery. Furthermore, after his death, his brain was extracted and autopsied to study it in further detail. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe ethical guideline violated in the HM case study was informed consent. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ This could have occurred as he was not physically capable of giving consent to participate in the experiment. Even if he did, he would not remember it after a while as he had a complete loss of episodic memories. Though there is evidence of family consent, it is still essential to note that at the time of the study, HM was a grown man, and his consent should have been present, especially as he was continually subjected to a series of tests that were quite repetitive as they wanted to test if he is able to create procedural memories without him being aware of it. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ It can be argued that while this can be considered as a violation, it also needs to be evaluated as a cost-benefit model as findings from his case study have been used to advance researcher's knowledge of memories and help them to develop strategies and drugs that could be used help in the treatment of diseases such as Alzheimer's etc. However, the ethical concerns regarding HM are valid as he gave up his entire life to science without even being actively aware of doing so. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nMilner, B., Corkin, S., $\\&$ Teuber, H. (1968). Further analysis of the hippocampal amnesic syndrome: 14-year follow-up study of H.M. Neuropsychologia, 6(3), 215-234. https://doi.org/10.1016/0028-3932(68)90021-3"},{"ref":"PS0515","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0515\n\nExplain one research method used in the study of the hormones and behaviour.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nThe response should focus on the topic posed in the question within the first few lines (*e.g. One research method used to study hormones and behaviour is true experiments.*). As the question clearly refers to one research method, only one research method used to study hormones and behaviour should be mentioned along with a clear explanation (*e.g. Experiments are a quantitative method of research where an independent variable (IV) is clearly manipulated and its effect on the dependent variable(s) (DV) is closely measured.*).\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nThere should be accurate knowledge and understanding of the topic using all or some key terms, such as:\n\n-    True experiment - an experiment when the IV is manipulated, and participants are randomly allocated to conditions\n\n-    Case study - involves various research methods and detailed investigation of one individual or a small group of people.\n\n-    Natural experiment - an experiment when the IV cannot be manipulated and is a naturally occurring phenomenon without randomly allocating participants. \n\n-    Variables - IV, or the independent variable, is the one that is manipulated, and the DV or dependent variable is measured.\n\n-    Random allocation - participants are placed randomly in any of the conditions\n\n-   Quasi-experiments - random allocation of participants to conditions is not possible\n\n \n\nThe command term Explain is defined as $`$to provide a detailed account including possible causes or reasons'. Therefore, while explaining research methods is essential, there should also be a focus on meeting the command term. There should be a clear explanation of the topic and why it has been selected for use in the study context (*e.g. This research method would be most appropriate in studies like these as…*).\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study - including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study. *Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n-    Newcomer et al. (1999) on cortisol- True experiment\n\n-   Kandasamy et al. (2014): cortisol and risk aversion - a true experiment\n\n-    Goetz et al. (2014): Testosterone and aggression - a true experiment\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nThere should be a clear link between the study results and the question. (*e.g. This study shows a clear manipulation of the IV - level of the hormone cortisol (high, low and placebo) to see the effect on the DV (memory). This study helps to show how and why experiments are used to study…*)\n\n**Marking limits:**\n\n-    Including a research study related to the brain and behaviour or genes and behaviour, rather than hormones and behaviour (*a maximum of 3 marks will be awarded*) \n\n-   No clear description of the research method is given. (*a maximum of 6 marks can be awarded*)\n\n-   Not selecting an appropriate study from the unit. (*no marks will be awarded*)\n\n-   A relevant study is described, but, no clear links to research methods are made. (*a maximum of 5 marks can be awarded*)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nOne research method used to study hormones and behaviour is true experiments. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ \n\nExperiments are a quantitative method of research where an independent variable (IV) is clearly manipulated and its effect on the dependent variable(s) (DV) is closely measured. Furthermore, the researchers randomly allocate participants to conditions.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ True experiments allow the researcher to establish a cause-and-effect relationship between the IV and the DV and are highly standardised, increasing internal validity. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Another feature of experiments is the ability to control extraneous variables to further strengthen the cause-and-effect relationship between variables. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Researchers sometimes manipulate aspects of physiology (e.g., hormone levels) to see the effect on behaviour or vice versa. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nNewcomer et.al (1999) conducted a study to test the role of the stress hormone cortisol on verbal declarative memory. He assigned participants randomly to three different conditions - a high cortisol group who received 160 mg of cortisol each day of the condition equating to a highly stressful situation. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The second condition had a low level of cortisol around 40 mg similar to a low stressful event and the third was a placebo group. The  experiment was carried on for four days and the participants were tested thrice at different times for their memory by asking to recall a prose passage. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Baseline test was conducted to establish that there were no significant differences in their memory. Results from the study revealed that cortisol impairs memory function as the group with the highest cortisol showed significantly poorer recall when compared to the other groups. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\n \n\nFrom the methodology of the study cited above it can be clearly seen how this research used a true experiment to establish a strong cause and effect relationship between stress and memory as it was able to provide evidence that stress hormone cortisol impairs memory negatively as can be seen in the performance of the participants in condition 1. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$  This cause and effect can be further strengthened as participants were randomly allocated to conditions, decreasing bias and allowing for individual participant differences to be evenly distributed across conditions. Extraneous variables were controlled as the baseline memory of all participants was also established prior to the start of the study. Furthermore, all participants completed the same tests, under the same conditions, with the only difference being the level of cortisol. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ This helps to isolate the effect of the IV. Therefore, this study shows a clear manipulation of the IV - level of the hormone cortisol (high, low and placebo) to see the effect on the DV (memory). This study helps to show how and why experiments are used to study the effect of hormones on human behaviour such as memory. $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ \n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nNewcomer, J. W., Selke, G., Melson, A. K., Hershey, T., Craft, S., Richards, K., $\\&$ Alderson, A. (1999). Decreased memory performance in healthy humans induced by Stress-Level cortisol treatment. Archives of General Psychiatry, 56(6), 527-533. https://doi.org/10.1001/archpsyc.56.6.527"}]}]},{"name":"Cognitive Approaches to Human Behaviour","subtopics":[{"name":"Cognitive Processing","questions":[{"ref":"PS0518","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0518\n\nExplain the schema theory.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\n*A direct answer to the question provided in the first few sentences (e.g. “Schema theory explores the idea that our schemas affect and/or influence our perception of an event or idea…”)*\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of schema theory.\\\n\nImportant concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define include: *A schema is a knowledge structure that allows organisms to interpret and understand the world around them. Schemata are a method of organising information that allows the brain to work more efficiently.*\n\n- *Piaget's theory of cognitive development put the concept at the forefront of cognitive science. Contemporary conceptions of schema evolved in the 1970s and 1980s.*\n\n- *The widespread use of computers in the last decades of the 20th century also affected theories of how people store and use information in the brain.*\n\n- *There are four main types of schemas. These are centred around objects, the self, roles, and events.*\n\n- *Schemas can be changed and reconstructed throughout a person's life. The two processes for doing so are assimilation and accommodation.*\n\n- *Schemas have been pivotal in influencing theories of learning as well as in teacher instruction methods.*\n\nThe command term $`$explain' is addressed. This requires to give a detailed account of the concept of schema and schema theory, including possible reasons or causes for the theory in relation to one research linked to the cognitive approach to understanding behaviour. \n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{2. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study $\\text{--}$ including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study\\\n\n*Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n- Bradford and Johnson (1972) Associated titles of text\n\n- Loftus and Palmer (1974) leading questions on schema theory\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link exploring schema theory in relation to the study and in reference to the question *(e.g. $`$Schemas allow us to form expectations. A schema is a cognitive structure that provides a framework for organising information about the world, events, people and actions. In Bartlett's study, $`$the War of the Ghosts' participants remembered the main gist of the story but changed unfamiliar elements to make sense of the story using terms that were more familiar to their own cultural expectations. The story remained coherent, although it became notably shorter at each reproduction. Bartlett concluded that remembering is an active process and memories are not copies of experiences, but rather reconstructions using our own internal schemas to create them.')*\n\nMarking limits:\n\n- Schema and schema theory are not explained in the opening paragraph.\n\n- Responses should clearly relate the study to the cognitive approach and must focus on the cognitive aspects of the research.\n\n- Explaining more than one study will mean marks are only given to the first study described.\n\n- Describing a study but schema theory is not explicitly addressed *(maximum 3 marks awarded)*\n\n- Explaining schema theory but does not refer to an appropriate study *(maximum 4 marks awarded)*\n\n- Clear links between what are schemas, the schema theory and the study are needed to gain marks in the top mark band. *(If the link is only implicit a maximum of 6 marks will be awarded)*\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nA schema is a cognitive structure that provides a framework for organizing information about the world, events, people and actions. Schema allows us to form expectations. These are integrated chunks of knowledge stored in long-term memory. They are mental representations of categories (from our knowledge, beliefs and expectations) and about particular aspects of the world such as people, objects, events, and situations.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ \\\n\nThere are three different types of schemas: scripts - provide information about the sequence of events that occurs in particular contexts (e.g going to a restaurant, visiting the dentist, attending class). Self-schemers organized information we have about ourselves (information stored in our memory about our strengths and weaknesses and how we feel about them). Social schemers (stereotypes) - represent information about groups of people (e.g Americans, Egyptians, women, accountants, etc.).$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nSchemas perform a variety of functions: they organize information in memory, in that, once a schema for school is activated, then this will also activate other schema representations associated with school stereotypes (classroom, teacher,, rules) and scripts for dentist visits (memories for pain associated with homework tasks and punishments etc.).$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ These schemas can be activated to increase information processing efficiency because it is much more efficient in terms of mental resources to have a prepared script of what to do in a given situation, which allows for the ability to engage simultaneously in other cognitively demanding activities.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ They generate a set of expectations about objects, events and people and regulate behaviour in accordance with this. They tend to be relatively stable and resistant to change which ensures continuity in the ways we process information and therefore the ways we act.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nIn their 1972 study, Bransford and Johnson investigated schema theory by presenting participants with a passage about a routine activity—doing laundry.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The crucial aspect was the manipulation of context: one group received the title \"Laundry Day,\" while the other received \"Moving Day.\"$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The results showed that participants with the appropriate schema, \"Laundry Day,\" demonstrated better recall and comprehension than the other group. This experiment highlighted the impact of pre-existing mental frameworks, or schemata, on information processing.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ It emphasized that our existing knowledge structures significantly influence how we encode, interpret, and remember new information. Bransford and Johnson's study has been influential in shaping our understanding of how schema contribute to cognitive processes, emphasizing the importance of context and prior knowledge in memory and comprehension.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link"},{"ref":"PS0540","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0540\n\nDescribe rational or controlled thinking using one research study.\n\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the answer includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question is provided in the first few sentences. The question uses the command term describe; therefore, the response should give a detailed account of rational or controlled thinking in relation to a research study (*e.g., $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Rational thinking or System 2 thinking is an analytical and controlled type of thinking. One study that describes System 2 thinking was conducted by Alter & Oppenheimer in 2007.$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$*).\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of the key topics and supporting details are expressed in the answer. A minimum of 2 key terms should be used in the answer. Important concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define could include, but are not limited to:\n\n- Decision-making: a process of choosing one option from available alternatives\n\n- System 2/Rational/Controlled Thinking: a slow, deliberate, and analytical mode of reasoning that requires conscious effort and is used for complex decision-making or problem-solving\n\n- Thinking: a process of analysing and drawing conclusions from the information\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nThe answer includes a clear focus on describing one study, including the  aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study, where the study is linked to the topic. Relevant studies that could be referred to include, but are not limited to: \n\n- Study on if unconscious thinking leads to better performance than rational or controlled thinking: Bonke et al. (2014)\n\n- Investigation of individual differences in reasoning: Stanovich $\\&$ West (2000)\n\n- Investigation on how font affects thinking: Alter $/&$ Oppenheimer (2007)\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study to the topic is present  (*e.g., $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$With the help of the research conducted, controlled or rational thinking and how they affect decision-making can be understood.$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$*).\n\n**Marking limits:**\n\n- If the answer provides a clear explanation of one relevant study and explains key terms relevant to the topic without an explicit link between the result of the study and the topic in question, a maximum of 6 marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer focuses on explaining a relevant study only, without explaining controlled thinking, a maximum of 5 marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer focuses on a study that is not relevant to the topic, a maximum of 4 marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer focuses only on describing the controlled thinking, with no mention of a relevant study, a maximum of 3 marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer describes an incorrect topic (*e.g., System 1 or automatic thinking*), no marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer describes two relevant studies, the marks will only be given on the first study mentioned.\n\n[9 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nRational or System 2 thinking is a slow, analytical, and logical type of thinking, which is controlled and analytical in nature. This type of thinking is prone to fewer errors and is used to make decisions with thought. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$  Rational or controlled thinking means carefully thinking about all the information, analysing it step by step, and using logic to make decisions. It's different from intuitive thinking, which relies on quick shortcuts and can sometimes cause mistakes in judgment because of biases. System 2 thinking is a type of thinking under the dual-processing model, which was introduced by Travesky & Kahneman. Thinking and decision-making are two different cognitive processes. Thinking is the process of analyzing information and drawing conclusions from the information provided, whereas decision-making is the process of choosing one option from available alternatives.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ This answer will focus on System 2 thinking or rational thinking. It also requires more time to make decisions and is goal directed. \n\nA study to understand System 2 thinking was conducted by Alter & Oppenheimer in 2007. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$The aim of the study was to investigate how font affects thinking. The sample consisted of 40 Princeton students who completed the Cognitive Reflections Test (CRT). The test was made up of 3 questions and measured to see whether people use fast thinking to answer the question (and get it wrong) or use slow/controlled thinking (and get it right). $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$All the participants were divided equally into 2 conditions. In Condition 1, half of the students were given the CRT in an easy-to-read font, while in Condition 2, the other half were given the CRT in a difficult-to-read font. \n\nResults of the study found that 10$\\%$ of students answered all 3 questions correctly in Condition 1, while 65$\\%$ of students answered all 3 correctly in Condition 2.\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThere is evidence of System 2 thinking, which is controlled/rational, present in this study. Difficult to read fonts caused participants to engage in System 2 thinking, which is more conscious and slow. It made them focus more, thereby they ended up giving the right responses.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$  In Condition 1, participants used automatic thinking as a result of an easy to read font, which made them more prone to errors. \n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nAlter, A. L., Oppenheimer, D. M., Epley, N., $\\&$ Eyre, R. N. (2007). Overcoming intuition: \n\nMetacognitive difficulty activates analytic reasoning. Journal of Experimental Psychology: \n\nGeneral, 136(4), 569-576. https://doi.org/10.1037/0096-3445.136.4.569"},{"ref":"PS0553","marks":"22","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0553\n\nDiscuss the schema theory.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The question used the command term *‘discuss’*; therefore, the response should offer a considered and balanced review that includes a range of arguments, factors, or hypotheses that consider the extent to which schema theory contributes towards the understanding of memory as a cognitive process. \n\n**1 mark** if the question was briefly described in less detail or if the question was restated. (*e.g. This essay will discuss Schema Theory.*)\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant $\\&$ appropriate terminology is explained in detail (*e.g. schemas, assimilation, accommodation, schema congruent, schema incongruent, etc*). Because the question asks to discuss the schema theory, students need to show an understanding of how schema theory is a proposed memory model and can explain some features associated with memory to score full marks in this criterion.\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors (*e.g. schema theory is explained using key terms, but how it contributes to understanding memory is not mentioned*). \n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited (*e.g. schema theory is explained but with minimal understanding of its process with errors in the usage of terminology*).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (*e.g., aim, procedures, results *$\\&$* conclusion*) are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments stated. Studies to be used are but not limited to:\n\n-   Bartlett’s (1932) study of schema processing as part of reconstructive memory\n\n-   Loftus and Palmer’s (1974) study on reconstructive memory\n\n-   Bergman and Roediger (1999) replicated Bartlett’s study.\n\n-   Brewer and Treyens’s (1981) study on the role of office schemas on recall\n\n-   Bransford and Johnson’s (1972) study on contextual conditions for encoding and recall.\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (*e.g. the aim, procedure, results *$\\&$* conclusions of the study was not clearly presented*)\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. (*e.g. the study was not linked to the discussion points mentioned*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed discussion points, around 2-3 discussion points (*e.g. methodological considerations related to research, comparing with the other memory models, interpretation and application of the research findings*).\n\n**3-4 marks** if 1-2 discussion points are present and clearly explained but are not sustained (*e.g. descriptive response or limited explanations but shows two different perspectives, or if the discussion points are underdeveloped*).\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation or discussion points are superficial and mainly descriptive (*e.g. if it only evaluates the study briefly and not the topic overall, or if the discussion points or evaluation was only stated or described but not linked back to the study*).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is an organised $\\&$ coherent explanation of schema theory and an evaluation of one or more relevant studies (*see Criterion C*). (*e.g. first paragraph introduces the focus on the question and related terminology, the succeeding paragraphs talk about the studies to be evaluated as well as the actual evaluation of one or more research, and the closing paragraphs or statements link the arguments back to the question*)\n\n**1 mark** if the organisation and clarity were not sustained or if the response is not completed.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nSchemas are considered as mental blocks representing our prior knowledge. One of the essential components of Schemas lies in the way it assists us to organize and recall information in our daily lives linked by our prior experience to the topic. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ As humans grow and learn, new schemas or blocks of information are created known as Assimilation. However, we are constantly being bombarded by new information which is absorbed in our existing schemas through a process known as Accommodation. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nWhile schemas play a crucial role in our understanding of how memory works it is especially useful in identifying one of the main errors in memory not accounted for in any of the previous models – memory distortion. This is experienced when our brain creates false memories or reconstructs memory with flaws mainly due to our memory associating schema congruent items together. Schema congruence s\\is referred to the process of categorizing items together under the same category because of our association with it. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$This essay will look at how schemas affect our memory recall through the Bartlett (1932) study and further review how our memory is affected by schema congruent and incongruent items by the Brewer and Treyens (1981) study. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\n Bartlett (1932) gathered British participants and told them a Native American legend filled with unfamiliar elements. The participants were put into two groups – serial reproduction wherein they had to recall the story to another person and repeated reproduction when they had to recall the story over a period of weeks, months, and years. While there was no significant difference between the groups, Bartlett found that the story on retelling had become much shorter with some details being adapted to the participants' own cultural schemas and familiar elements in a process known as rationalization. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe results from this study give evidence to memory distortions and the role played by schemas in reconstructing memory. While the results were pivotal in how memory recall was understood, this study had significant limitations. Firstly, there was no cause and effect as the IV, separation into groups based on type of recall did not affect the DV – memory recall. Furthermore, if culture is to be considered as a variable, there was no way to verify that cultural schema played a role in memory distortion as the results were not compared to another group of Native Americans. However, in spite of this, Bartlett’s study has been known to be reliable as a follow-up study conducted by Bergman and Roediger (1999) produced similar results. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nTo dive deeper into how memory gets distorted, Brewer and Treyens (1981) conducted a study to understand if we tend to recall schema-congruent items more easily rather than schema incongruent ones. The sample comprised 86 Psychology students who were asked to wait in an office before the experiment began. Unknown to them, the experiment had already begun as once they were called to another room, they were asked to recall items they saw in the office. The participants were divided into 3 conditions – free verbal recall, free drawing recall and verbal recognition condition. In condition 3, participants were presented with a list of items and asked to tick the ones they remember seeing in the office. Results showed that in condition 3 participants tended to recall more schema incongruent items whereas in condition 1 and 2 they recalled more schema congruent items. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThese results give evidence to how the way we recall memory affects the accuracy of our memory. When participants were asked to recall items freely there was a higher chance of distortion but when presented with a list there was higher accuracy. Though there were some ethical concerns with the use of deception in the study, it had to be done to avoid demand characteristics increasing the validity of the study. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nSchema theory has helped to further the understanding of the concept of memory distortions better leading to more awareness of the effect of schemas such as the formation of stereotypes. This has been one of the major implications of research in this field as it has a direct impact on understanding the basis of discrimination and bias between groups of people. However, concerns in the research field still exist as schemas cannot be observed directly and have no biological evidence lending support to the credibility of its existence. The research cited above only refers to its effects being observed but not in any biological sense. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nIn conclusion, schema theory has helped to bridge the gap in understanding of memory as a whole and have provided a more holistic view of memory with an understanding of how environment could affect it. \n\nWord count: 784\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nBartlett, F. F. C., & Burt, C. (1933). REMEMBERING: a STUDY IN EXPERIMENTAL AND SOCIAL PSYCHOLOGY. British Journal of Educational Psychology, 3(2), 187–192. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.2044-8279.1933.tb02913.x \n\nBrewer, W. F., & Treyens, J. C. (1981). Role of schemata in memory for places. Cognitive Psychology, 13(2), 207–230. https://doi.org/10.1016/0010-0285(81)90008-6"},{"ref":"PS0562","marks":"22","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0562\n\nDiscuss one or more research study/ies related to one model or theory of thinking and decision-making.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The \n\nquestion used the command term *discuss* which means a considered $\\&$ balanced review of arguments, factors, or hypotheses. \n\n**1 mark** if the command term is not accurately explained or has limited explanation.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant $\\&$ appropriate terminology is explained in detail (*e.g. thinking, decision making, dual processing model: system 1 and system 2 thinking*). \n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors and if the response only showed knowledge about the study, such as a brief explanation of thinking and decision making with little focus on evaluation of study.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited with no evaluation of studies.\n\n \n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (e.g., aim, procedures, results $\\&$ conclusion) are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments \n\nstated. Studies to be used are but not limited to - \n\n-    Tversky and Kahneman (1974) investigated if the order of numbers in a row would affect how participants would estimate the value if they were multiplied. \n\n-    Bechara et al (2000) studied the decision making of participants with damage to their ventromedial prefrontal cortices and healthy participants.\n\n-    Alter $\\&$ Oppenheimer (2007) investigated how font affects thinking.\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (*e.g the aim, procedure, results $\\&$ conclusions of the study was not clearly presented*) \n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. (*e.g the study was not linked to the evaluation points mentioned*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed discussion $\\&$ evaluation points (*e.g around 2-3 strengths and limitations such as there is high internal validity as a cause and effect relationship can be observed between the IV and DV, the use of independent or repeated measures design on the results, high or low generalization due to the sample characteristics*). \n\n**3-4  marks** if the discussion or evaluation points (*e.g. around 1-2 strengths $\\&$ limitations*) are present and clearly explained but are not sustained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the discussion or evaluation points are superficial (*e.g. evaluation points only stated but not explained properly*).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is a clear link to be made at the end of the answer focusing on thinking and decision making and the study used. The link can be made with the help of system 1 and system 2 thinking. The answer must be organized and clear with appropriate information provided. \n\n**1 mark** if the organization and clarity were not sustained throughout the answer.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nThinking and decision-making are cognitive processes used extensively in our daily lives. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Thinking is the process of analyzing and drawing conclusions from the information provided whereas decision-making is the process of choosing one option from available alternatives. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ There are two types of independent thinking systems, system 1 and system 2 thinking. System 1 thinking is the automatic, fast and instinct based thinking while system 2 thinking is slow, analytical and logical type of thinking. System 1 thinking is where heuristics (mental shortcuts) are used for decision making. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ These two thinking systems are based on the Dual processing model and it helps to process information with the help of system 1 and 2 thinking. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nThe first study was conducted by Tversky and Kahneman (1974) with an aim to study if the order of numbers in a row would affect how participants would estimate the value if they were multiplied. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The sample consisted of high school students. All participants were divided in two conditions. In condition one, the $`$ascending condition' the numbers 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8 were presented and participants had to estimate the total value in 5 seconds. In the second condition, the $`$descending condition' the numbers were presented in the format of 8,7,6,5,4,3,2,1 where a second set of participants had 5 seconds to estimate the value. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The results were that the ascending group (condition 1) had a median estimate of 512 whereas the descending condition (condition 2) had a median estimate of 2250. The actual value was 40,320. This was because the first number seen by the participants seems to have influenced their responses. There was no time to calculate in 5 seconds and hence based on the first few multiplications they had to tell the value. When the numbers were smaller, the estimate of the value was smaller. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The study clearly shows how system 1 thinking is used as the fast, automatic response is given by the participants. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nThe strengths of the study are that an independent measures design is used where different participants are taken in both conditions to avoid understanding the true aim of the study. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$  This increases the accuracy of the results. The sample consisted of high school students and can be generalized to the same age group. It was a highly controlled lab experiment which increases replicability and shows a cause and effect relationship between the IV and DV.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$  The median value was used in the results which helps exclude any outliers that can be seen if the mean value was calculated. There is no evidence of mental or physical harm to the participants. The study also has some limitations. There is deception for the participants as they were not told the true aim of the study. The participants may also have varying levels of interest and aptitude for Math which can help them to calculate faster than other participants. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nThe second study was conducted by Alter $\\&$ Oppenheimer, 2007 with an aim to investigate how font affects thinking. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The sample consisted of 40 Princeton students who completed the Cognitive Reflections Test (CRT). The test was made up of 3 questions and measures to see whether people use fast thinking to answer the question (and get it wrong) or use slow thinking (and get it right). Half of the students were given the CRT in an easy-to-read font (condition 1), while the other half were given the CRT in a difficult-to-read font (condition 2). The results of the study found that 10$\\%$ of students answered all three questions correctly in condition 1, 65$\\%$ of students answered correctly in condition 2. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  This was because difficult to read fonts caused participants to engage in system 2 thinking which is more conscious and slow. \n\nThe strengths of the study are that it was a lab experiment which had a controlled environment hence high internal validity and less influence of extraneous variables.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ It also took informed consent from the students before the study and does not have evidence of any physical or mental harm.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ While the limitations of the study are that it only involved Princeton undergraduate students which is not representative of the general population hence the results may not generalize to other groups of participants. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ \n\nBoth the studies show the working of system 1 and system 2 thinking and decision making is influenced by these systems. It is important to note that no one system is better than the other, it depends on the situation where an individual uses either of the thinking systems. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nWord count: 790\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nTversky, A., $\\&$ Kahneman, D. (1974). Judgment under uncertainty: Heuristics and biases. *Science, 185*(4157), 1124-1131. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.185.4157.1124\n\nAlter, A. L., Oppenheimer, D. M., Epley, N., $\\&$ Eyre, R. N. (2007). Overcoming intuition: Metacognitive difficulty activates analytic reasoning. *Journal of Experimental Psychology: General, 136*(4), 569-576. https://doi.org/10.1037/0096-3445.136.4.569"},{"ref":"PS0591","marks":"22","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0591\n\nDiscuss one or more ethical considerations in research related to cognitive processing.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The \n\nquestion used the command term discuss, which means a considered & balanced review of arguments, factors, or hypotheses. (*e.g. Ethical guidelines, which psychologists must adhere to when conducting research, help ensure the protection of research participants and the wider community.*)   \n\n**1 mark** if the command term is not accurately explained or has limited explanation.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant & appropriate terminology is explained without errors, and are explicitly linked to the study and the discussion points (*e.g. what is cognitive processing, schema theory, models of memory, ethical considerations and their use in research*). \n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained but has minimal errors and if the response only showed knowledge about ethical considerations in cognitive processing with less focus on discussing arguments on ethical considerations. Or if the knowledge focused only on cognitive processing and did not include knowledge related to ethical considerations.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited with discussion points\n\n \n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (e.g., aim, procedures, results & conclusion) are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments \n\nstated. Studies to be used are but not limited to - \n\nBrewer and Treyens (1981) investigated the role of schema in memory encoding and retrieval. \n\nAnderson and Pichert (1978) studied the effect of schema processing on memory encoding and retrieval. \n\nGlanzer and Cunitz (1966) studied the serial positioning effect. \n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (*e.g. the aim, procedure, results & conclusions of the study was not clearly presented*)\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. (*e.g. the study was not linked to the evaluation points mentioned*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed evaluation and discussion points (*e.g. around 2-3 strengths and limitations and arguments based on ethical considerations such as informed consent, right to withdraw, protection from physical and psychological harm*). \n\n**3-4  marks** of the evaluation and discussion points (*e.g. around 1-2 strengths $\\&$ limitations, 1-2 arguments for discussion*) are present and clearly explained but are not sustained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation or discussion points are superficial (evaluation points and discussion points were only stated but not explained).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is a clear link to be made at the end of the answer focusing on ethical considerations in cognitive processing and the study used. The answer must be organised and clear, with accurate information provided. One may begin by defining key terminology and then moving to the specific study, followed by an evaluation and discussion points.\n\n**1 mark** if the organisation and clarity were not sustained throughout the answer. (*e.g. unfinished response, incomplete description of key terms and studies*)\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nEthical guidelines, which psychologists must adhere to when conducting cognitive research, help ensure the protection of research participants and the wider community. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Cognitive processing relates to mental processes such as memory, thinking, and decision-making. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Some common and important ethical guidelines in psychological research are informed consent, anonymity, confidentiality, right to withdraw, avoiding deception, no undue stress/harm, and debriefing. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The ethical consideration of avoiding deception can be discussed with reference to Brewer and Treyens (1981), while the importance of informed consent can be discussed with reference to Glanzer and Cunitz (1966). Both studies investigated aspects of cognitive processing.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nDeception can involve lying to a participant, or it can involve not revealing all relevant details (i.e., deception by omission). $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Avoiding deception means that where possible, all details of a study should be truthfully revealed to a participant before the commencement of the study. Deception is sometimes necessary to prevent participants from working out the aim of the study, to avoid demand characteristics. In some instances, if minor deception was not used, researchers would not get a true indication of how mental processes guide behaviour. If the collection of valid data necessitates some deception, such deception should be essential, and minor and should not cause any harm. In terms of cognitive processing, schema theory states that our prior knowledge (stored in the form of schemas) influences our processing, storage, and retrieval of information. Schemas are mental representations of everything in our experience and knowledge. They are stored in our minds and are built from prior experiences, knowledge, and beliefs. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nBrewer and Treyens (1981) conducted research that aimed to investigate the role of schema in the encoding of memory and retrieval of memory. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ A total of 86 participants were told that they were waiting in an $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$office\" until the researchers were ready for them to begin the experiment. This was untrue, as waiting in the office was part of the research. The room had multiple objects that are found in an office like a desk and typewriter. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ It also consisted of objects not found in an office, such as a skull and a piece of bark. After this, the participants were called into another room one by one and given a questionnaire to recall the objects they had seen. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$The results of the study found that participants were more likely to remember items that are regular office items like a desk and typewriter. It was also found that participants were more likely to have false memories of items that were not actually in the office setting but were rated high on schema expectancy of an office.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The study demonstrates how schema influences memory recall, as more participants remembered objects congruent with what their schema expected. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nIn this study, valid results could not have been obtained if participants were aware of the true aims of the research. Had they known the true aims of the study, they may have intentionally memorised items in the room. Without deception, the researchers would not have had an accurate picture of how schemas influence memory recall. While avoiding deception is important for protecting participants from stress or embarrassment, this study was still ethical, despite the use of deception. The deception was minor and did not cause any harm to the participants. The participants were debriefed after the study, where the deception was revealed. Therefore, this study highlights the relevance of the ethical consideration of avoiding deception when studying cognitive processing. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nAnother important ethical consideration is informed consent. Informed consent should be voluntarily gained from all participants before the study begins. It should be in writing and should be signed by each participant. Parental or guardian consent is also required if a person is under the age of 16-18 (country dependent) or has any mental impairment. Informed consent should clearly outline what participation in the study will involve. It should outline the participants' rights, and how those rights will be protected (*e.g., the right to withdraw*). $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$        \n\n        \n\nMemory is a cognitive process in which we encode, store and retrieve information. The multi-store model of memory (MSM) suggests that memory consists of three memory stores, sensory, short term, and long-term. Each of these stores differed in duration and capacity of memory storage.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nResearch conducted by Glanzer and Cunitz investigated the serial position effect and provided support for short-term and long-term memory being separate storage systems. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The serial position effect is the tendency to recall the first and the last items on a list better than items in the middle. All the participants were asked to memorise a list of words followed by free recall. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ In condition 1 participants were asked to recall immediately after hearing the words. In condition 2, researchers introduced a delay between the end of the list and the start of recall with a filler task of 30 secs. The purpose of the filler task was to prevent the rehearsal $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Results of the immediate recall condition showed that participants were better at remembering words at the start of the list, called the primacy effect (because they had moved to long-term memory), and at the end of the list called the recency effect (because they were still in short-term-memory). Results of the delayed recall condition showed that participants were able to recall the words from the start of the list but were no longer able to recall the words from the end of the list. The results showed that there are different memory stores with different memory mechanisms. Due to the filler task, the recency effect was not seen because without rehearsal, 30 seconds exceeded the duration of long-term memory. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nTo meet ethical guidelines, informed consent would need to be obtained from all participants before this study. Participants were all over the age of 16, therefore, they could provide their own informed consent. The informed consent would need to outline what their participation would involve and that their participation was voluntary. For example, they would need to be made aware that they would undergo a series of simple recall tests. They would also be informed about how their data would be securely stored, and how it would be used. Participants would also need to be guaranteed before they signed the informed consent, that their data would be kept confidential, and that they can withdraw themselves and their results at any time without consequence. Thus, highlighting how informed consent helped to protect participants' rights in this study. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nThe two studies discussed demonstrate the importance of avoiding deception and informed consent when conducting cognitive research into schema theory and memory. These are two of many important ethical guidelines that need to be considered when researching cognitive processing. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ It is worth noting, however,  that the relative importance of different ethical considerations when investigating cognitive processing may vary depending on the context or hypothesis of the study. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nWord count: 1125\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nBrewer, W. F., & Treyens, J. C. (1981). Role of schemata in memory for places. Cognitive Psychology, 13(2), 207-230. https://doi.org/10.1016/0010-0285(81)90008-6\n\nGlanzer, M., & Cunitz, A. R. (1966). Two storage mechanisms in free recall. Journal of Verbal Learning & Verbal behaviour, 5(4), 351-360. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0022-5371(66)80044-0"},{"ref":"PS0592","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0592\n\nEvaluate the schema theory.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The \n\nquestion used the command term *evaluate*, therefore, the response should \n\nidentify, explain, and weigh the strengths and limitations of schema theory. \n\n**1 mark** if the command term is not accurately explained or has limited explanation.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant $\\&$ appropriate terminology is explained in detail without errors, and is explicitly linked to the studies in Criterion C (e.g. *what is a schema, types of schema, elements of the schema theory*).\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors and if the response only showed knowledge about research methods, such as a brief explanation of the theory with little focus on evaluation of study.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited with no evaluation of studies.\n\n \n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (*e.g., aim, procedures, results *$\\&$* conclusion*) are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments \n\nstated. Studies to be used are but not limited to - \n\n-   Brewer and Treyens (1981) investigated the role of schema in the encoding of memory  and retrieval of memory.\n\n-   Bartlett (1932) studied if memory is reconstructive in nature. \n\n-   Anderson and Pichert (1978) studied the effect of schema processing on memory encoding and retrieval. \n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (*e.g the aim, procedure, results *$\\&$* conclusions of the study was not clearly presented*) \n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. (*e.g the study was not linked to the evaluation points mentioned*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed evaluation points (*e.g around 2-3 strengths and limitations such as there is high internal validity as a cause and effect relationship can be observed between the IV and DV, researcher or participant bias*). \n\n**3-4  marks** of the evaluation points (*e.g around 1-2 strengths *$\\&$* limitations*) are present and clearly explained but are not sustained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation or discussion points are superficial (*evaluation points only stated but not explained properly*).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is a clear link to be made at the end of the answer focusing on schema theory and the study used. The answer must be organised and clear with appropriate information provided. \n\n**1 mark** if the organisation and clarity were not sustained throughout the answer. (*e.g unfinished response, incomplete description of key terms and studies*)\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nSchemas are the mental representation of everything in our experience and knowledge.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ Schema stored in our mind are built from prior experiences, knowledge and beliefs.There are three types of schema, namely social schema which is our interpretation of the world and others.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The second is self schema which is our perception of ourselves and last type is called script schema which is a sequence of events to be carried out. All these types constitute what we know as schema theory which is an organisation and categorization of knowledge from the world around us. As a cognitive process, schema theory influences the way we perceive, process, and remember any kind of information. Schema theory can be evaluated with reference to studies conducted by Bartlett (1932) and Brewer and Treyens (1981). $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nThe strengths of schema theory are that application of schema theory is extensively used in the psychological treatment under Cognitive Behaviour theory (CBT).$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ This theory is testable in nature as there is enough empirical evidence to support it. It also helps to understand how memory works in our mind and how information is categorized.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Schema theory also has some limitations such as it has low construct validity as schema is difficult to operationalize and is a vague concept.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ It also has low predictive validity as it is difficult to predict accurately what an individual will recall in certain situations. It is also difficult to explain where and how schemas are formed in the brain.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nThe study is conducted by Bartlett (1932) with an aim to study if memory is reconstructive in nature. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$He told all participants a native American legend called ‘The War of the Ghosts’. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$The participants in the study were British and the story was filled with unknown names and concepts for them to hear. All participants were divided in two conditions, one group was asked to use repeated reproduction (condition 1), where participants heard the story and were told to reproduce it after a short time and then to do it again repeatedly.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The second group was told to use serial reproduction, in which they had to recall the story on their own and repeat it to another person (condition 2). The results of the study found that there was no significant difference in recollection in both the conditions. Participants had shortened the story and omitted details they thought were unimportant according to them. Many details of the story were changed  by some participants to match the British cultural background as a result of cultural schema. For example, canoe was changed to boat.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nThe study helps to understand how schemas influence memory. It showed that our existing knowledge significantly impacts how we interpret, encode, store, and retrieve information. Bartlett concluded that memory is a reconstructive process which is influenced by our schema.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nThe strengths of the study are that participants gave informed consent before the study. There was no mental or physical harm done on the participants. There is high replicability as it is an experiment method.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ While the limitations of the study are that there is low ecological validity as this type of a study may not be the most accurate way to measure recall. There is low construct validity because memory is not operationalized by the researcher and participants may look at it differently.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nThe second research is conducted by Brewer and Treyens (1981) with the aim to investigate the role of schema in the encoding of memory and retrieval of memory.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ A total of 86 participants were told to sit in an office setting where the room had multiple objects that are found in an office like a desk and typewriter.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ It also consisted of objects not found in an office, such as a skull and a piece of bark. After this, the participants were called into another room one by one and given a questionnaire to recall the objects they had seen. The questionnaire comprised 131 objects- 61 were in the room, and 70 were not. Out of 86, 30 participants gave the written recall test, 29 did the drawing recall, and 27 did the verbal recognition test. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$The results of the study found that participants were more likely to remember items that are regular office items like desk and typewriter. It was also found that participants were more likely to have false memories of items that were not actually in the office setting but were rated high on schema expectancy of an office.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nThe study demonstrates how schema influences memory recall. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$For example, more participants remembered objects congruent with what their schema expected them to find in an office like desk and chair compared to objects that are not usually found in an office like a skull.\n\nThe strengths of the study are that informed consent was acquired from all participants before the study. There is evidence of high internal validity as cause and effect can be established between the IV and DV.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ There is high ecological validity as it was a natural office setting. The testability of the research is also high. The limitations are that the study has low population validity as the sample is not representative of the entire population which results in low generalization.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nBoth the studies explained above demonstrate how schema theory works.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ To add, we can say that schema theory is supported by an ample amount of research and can provide us with an explanation of how we form mental representations of information we learn through experience, and how we categorise this information. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$  However, it is good to note that although the schema theory can explain how new information is categorised through our existing knowledge, it does not account for situations where new information cannot be linked with existing knowledge. This limits the schema theory’s ability to explain how new information is organised.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ \n\nWord count: 972\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nBartlett, F. C. (1932). Remembering: A study in experimental and social psychology. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.\n\nBrewer, W. F., & Treyens, J. C. (1981). Role of schemata in memory for places. Cognitive Psychology, 13(2), 207–230. https://doi.org/10.1016/0010-0285(81)90008-6"},{"ref":"PS0601","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0601\n\nDiscuss one or more models of memory using one or more studies.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** If the issue mentioned in the question is explained. The command term is discuss; therefore, the response should consider the key elements of one or more models of memory. Opinions or arguments must be expressed clearly and substantiated with relevant research. The response can also explain why it is important to investigate or gain an understanding of models of memory.\n\n**1 mark** if the question was briefly described in less detail or if the question was just restated. (*e.g. this essay will discuss the multi-store model of memory*)\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant $\\&$ appropriate knowledge related to the chosen model(s) of memory is explained (*e.g. the multi-store model and/or the working memory model and/or the levels of processing model*). \n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors but lacks some detail (*e.g. an explanation of the multi-store model doesn’t mention the duration and capacity of each memory storage*).\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited or superficial (*e.g. suggesting that information for sensory memory is transferred to STM if it is rehearsed*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (*e.g., aim, procedures, results *$\\&$* conclusion*) are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments stated and a specific link is made between the findings of the study and the question. Studies that could be referred to include but are not limited to:\n\n-   Working Memory Model: Robbins et al., (1996)\n\n-   Multi-Store Model: Glanzer $\\&$ Cunitz (1966)\n\n-   Levels of Processing Model: (Craik $\\&$ Tulving, 1975)\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (*e.g. the aim, procedure, results *$\\&$* conclusions of the study was not clearly presented*) \n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with insufficient detail and errors or superficial links to the question.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed critical thinking points. Critical thinking points *could* include but are not limited to a combination of the following: Strengths and limitations of the research methods used in supporting research studies (*as well as the conclusions*), potential applications of each model, strengths and limitations of each model, an acknowledgement of complexities and alternative arguments etc. (*e.g at least 2-3 strengths and limitations or other relevant critical thinking points are explained*), \n\n**3-4 marks** if relevant critical thinking points are presented (*e.g  at least 1-2 strengths and limitations mentioned above or other relevant evaluation points are explained*) but may not always be fully developed or may be limited in number.\n\n**1-2 marks** If critical thinking points are superficial, contain inaccuracies or are not explicitly relevant. (*e.g only evaluation or other critical thinking points were stated but not explained*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is an organised $\\&$ coherent explanation of one or more models of memory with a suitable supporting study for each model (see Criterion C)  and relevant critical thinking. The essay has an organised structure including a clear introduction, body paragraphs (*e.g. where models of outline, followed by supporting studies and evaluation*) and a conclusion with statements or arguments that link back to the question.\n\n**1 mark** if the organisation and clarity were not sustained throughout the answer. (*e.g. unfinished response, incomplete description of key terms and studies*)\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nMemory is the process by which we manipulate, encode, store and recall information. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Models of memory attempt to explain this process. Memory is an essential component of learning and is closely linked with intelligence. Investigating models of memory and how this essential cognitive process works can play an important role in improving memory and in diagnosing memory disorders. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ The levels of processing and working memory models can be discussed with reference to research studies conducted by Robbins et al., (1996) and Craik $\\&$ Tulving. (1975)\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ \n\nThe Working Memory Model primarily explains short-term memory. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ Our working memory has limited capacity and allows us to hold $\\&$ manipulate information in our conscious awareness. Working memory is made up of separate subsystems. The first being the central executive, which is the control system. The three slave systems are known as the visuospatial sketchpad, the phonological loop and the episodic buffer. The visuospatial sketchpad deals with visual information and spatial awareness. The phonological loop deals with auditory information and is made up of the phonological store and the articulatory process. Finally, the episodic buffer is a temporary back-up storage for chunks of integrated visual and auditory information. A key assumption behind the Working Memory Model is that if two working memory tasks make use of two separate slave systems we should be able to effectively engage with each at the same time. However, if they both require the use of the same ecomponent, we will have diminished performance.  $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nRobbins et al., (1996) aimed to examine the effect of simultaneously engaging in two tasks that either utilize the same or different parts of working memory. 20 male chess players (of mixed ability) sat between two chessboards, with a partition between each. Chess pieces were positioned on one of the boards (out of view). The partition was removed, and participants were given 10 seconds to memorise the position of the pieces before the partition was reinstated. Participants had to recreate the cheese pieces on the other board (a visio-spatial sketchpad task) while completing either a visio-spatial interference task (typing a series of predetermined numbers into a keyboard) or a phonological loop interference task (verbally repeating the word ‘the’ in time to a metronome). Results showed poorer performance on both tasks when they required the same parts of working memory. These results lend support to the Working Memory Model  by emphasising the unique role and limited capacity of each separate slave system. If there was only one system of working memory then the ability to rearrange the chess pieces into the correct position should be equally affected regardless of the type of interference. The fact that visuo-spatial interference had a far greater detrimental effect on a visuo-spatial task suggests that there are different short-term working memory systems for processing different information. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ \n\nWhile this study provides support for some of the key elements of the working memory model, it is important to evaluate the study in order to determine the extent to which it can be used in support of the working memory model. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ \n\nOne strength is that it was a true experiment. The researchers manipulated an independent variable (visuospatial sketchpad or phonological loop interference task) in order to assess the impact on performance. Participants were also randomly allocated to conditions, therefore, a cause and effect relationship could be established. A further strength is that researchers could tightly control variables (such as using identical chess tasks) across each condition. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nThere were, however, potential extraneous variables that could reduce internal validity. The researchers had no way of controlling whether those in the visuo-spatial interference condition activated their inner voice (articulatory process) whilst they were completing the task. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nA further limitation is that participants were all male chess players from Cambridge, meaning they may not be representative of your average person, limiting the potential for generalisation.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nThe Levels of Processing Model is an alternative memory model $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ that is not limited to short-term memory. The model suggests that memory should be viewed as a process, where the strength of a memory is dependent on how deeply we have engaged with, or processed information. With in-depth engagement or processing resulting in strong memory traces and superficial processing producing weak memory traces. In-depth processing could involve considering the meaning of something, whereas shallow or superficial processing might focus on the structure of a word. Medium levels of processing which would fall somewhere between the two.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nCraik and Tulving’s experiment (1975) aimed to investigate whether more in depth processing would lead to greater recall. The study used a repeated measures design with counterbalancing. 30 participants were shown a series of words and answered a question about each word. The type of questions asked varied from in depth (focusing on meaning) to superficial (focusing) on structural aspects of each word. The dependent variable was the number of words remembered and this was measured via a surprise recognition test. Results were as expected, with participants recognising the most words from the in-depth processing condition and the least from the superficial or shallow processing condition. Thus providing support for the model. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ \n\nAs this study was also an experiment, many of the evaluation points are similar to that of Robbins et al, with researchers establishing a cause and effect relationship between depth of processing and recall.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ \n\nBoth models have research support yet we cannot be certain with regard to the claims made by either model. This is because cognitive processes such as memory cannot be observed directly and as such, researchers are left to make inferences based on the data. The levels of the processing model can effectively explain why we can remember things that we may not actively rehearse, however, it is somewhat simplistic in that it doesn’t distinguish between varying types of long-term memories. The working memory model provides an in-depth overview of working memory and can explain some real world memory phenomena. However, both models are insufficient as they cannot explain the biological processes that underpin the formation of a memory trace.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$  \n\nWord count: 997 words\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nCraik, F. I. M., & Tulving, E. (1975). Depth of processing and the retention of words in episodic memory. Journal of Experimental Psychology: General, 104(3), 268-294.\n\nRobbins, T. W., & Everitt, B. J. (1996). Neurobehavioural mechanisms of reward and motivation. Current Opinion in Neurobiology, 6, 228-236."},{"ref":"PS0611","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0611\n\nEvaluate one or more models of memory\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question is explained. The question uses the command term discuss. Therefore, the response should offer a balanced review considering various factors, arguments, and hypotheses. The response can also explain why it is essential to evaluate research studies that investigate how models of memory help to demonstrate human memory functionality.  \n\n**1 mark** if the question is not accurately explained, has limited explanation, or is simply restated  (*e.g., This essay will evaluate two studies that explore two different models of memory and how they help to show the structure of human memory*). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer is not relevant to the question.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant and appropriate knowledge and terminology are explained in detail without errors and are explicitly linked to the studies in Criterion C (*e.g., Multi-Store Model, Working Memory Model, Long-Term Memory, Short-Term Memory, Phonological Loop, Episodic Buffer, Visuospatial Sketchpad*).\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors but is lacking in some detail or shows only knowledge about research methods, such as a brief explanation of the topic with little focus on evaluation of the study (*e.g., in the working memory model, there are three more different stores for short-term memories than there are in the Multi-Store Model, showing why we can multitask*).\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited or superficial in nature (*e.g., if there is no recognition of the differences between models that are introduced*), or if the answer only shows knowledge related to memory models and does not explore studies that support it. No marks will be awarded for this criterion if no relevant terminology is explained. \n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments stated, and a specific link is made between the findings of the study(ies) and teh question. Relevant details, including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion, from one or more studies, should be included. Studies that could be referred to include, but are not limited to:\n\n- Investigation of the role of the hippocampus on memory formation: Milner (1966)\n\n- Exploration of primacy and recency effects to investigate the role of both long-term and short-term memory: Glanzer $\\&$ Cunitz (1966)\n\n- Investigation of the effect that articulatory suppression would have on the short-term memory store: Landry $\\&$ Bartling (2011)  \n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study is explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors (*e.g., the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study are not presented, or there are some errors with regard to the relevance of the findings or details of the methodology*).\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research is used with insufficient detail, errors, or superficial links to the question. (*e.g., the study was not linked to the evaluation points mentioned*). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer does not include any relevant studies.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer shows relevant, consistent, and well-developed critical thinking points. Critical thinking points could include a combination of the following: strengths and limitations of the research methods used in supporting research studies and their conclusions, strengths and limitations of the relevant theories, potential applications of the findings, or an acknowledgement of complexities and alternative arguments. At least 2-3 discussion or critical thinking points are explained (*e.g., the working memory model separates short-term memory into three stores and it can be seen from multitasking exercises; the fact that we can perform two dissimilar tasks at the same time demonstrates that there may in fact be separate stores for different functions*). \n\n**3-4 marks** if relevant critical thinking points are presented (as above), but they are not always fully developed. They may not be directly linked to the specific study or theory, or they may be limited in number. For example, a response might state that the study is low in internal validity without explaining what this means in reference to the specific study. At least 1-2 discussion or critical thinking points are explained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation points are superficial or contain inaccuracies (e.g., evaluation points only stated but not explained properly). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer does not contain relevant critical thinking points, discussion, or evaluation.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the essay has an organised structure, including a clear introduction (which outlines the focus of the essay), body paragraphs (which outline the relevant study or studies as described in Criterion C; followed by evaluation of those studies), and concluding paragraphs or statements (which link back to the question).\n\n**1 mark** if the organisation and clarity are not sustained throughout the answer (*e.g. unfinished response, lack of a clear introduction, lack of body paragraphs, and/or lack of a conclusion; a structure that is not logical or easy to follow; or incomplete description of key terms and studies*). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the response does not meet any of the standards above.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nHuman memory allows us to encode, store, and retrieve information from past experiences in a way that helps us to process the present and predict the future. Not only is memory vital for survival in the environment, it dictates how we learn, determines the way we make decisions, and helps to form our understanding of who we are as individuals.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$. The structure of our memory systems and the way that information is stored has long been a focus of cognitive psychology. Understanding how memory works is incredibly helpful in fields of clinical psychology, education, and marketing.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$. Atkinson $\\&$ Shriffin (1986) first introduced the Multi-Store Model (MSM), which posited that both short-term and long-term memory stores were located in different regions of the brain, with rehearsal and recall determining movement between each.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. Later, Baddeley $\\&$ Hitch (1974) challenged the notion that short-term memory is a single store, proposing that there are three different area that deal with different stimuli: the Phonological Loop handles auditory information, the Visuospatial Sketchpad is used for visual stimulus and spatial memories, and the Episodic Buffer ties all of the information together in a sequence. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. While models of memory help us to understand how memory functions, models are hypothetical representations, and their strengths and limitations need to be evaluated before accepting them as precise.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. \n\nMilner (1966) aimed to study the role of the hippocampus on memory formation. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. In a longitudinal study on a brain-damaged patient, H.M, who had his hippocampus removed to prevent dangerous seizures, the researcher performed observations, psychometric testing, cognitive testing, interviews, and MRI scans to assess the effect that this level of damage to the brain would have on H.M's ability for recall. H.M could not acquire new semantic and episodic memories, suggesting that the hippocampus is responsible for transfer of information from short-term to long-term memory. However, he was able to remember his childhood very well. H.M also had some capacity for procedural memories, showing these are not stored in the same way. Working memories would be remembered after a short time, if rehearsed, but then forgotten after a time.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. As H.M was able to remember his childhood, but could not encode new long-term memories, yet was able to perform procedural tasks, this study allows us to reach the conclusion that short-term and long-term memory stores are distinct systems.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. \n\nAs Milner (1966) was a case study, it is not able to be replicated to strengthen the findings, however, because it was longitudinal in nature, Milner was able to observe H.M's memory functionality and behaviour over time.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$.The study is high in ecological validity, as H.M was being observed in his natural environment and the researcher was able to witness him performing everyday tasks. While the study did rely on the researcher's observations and subjective reports, Milner also used several methods to triangulate the data, providing more credibility to the study's conclusions.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. The Milner study contributed greatly to our understanding of how memory works, helping to give rise to the formation of memory models in psychology, like the Multi-Store Model (MSM), that posits the distinct stores of the short-term memory and long-term memory.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$.\n\nLandry $\\&$ Bartling (2011) designed a dual task technique to investigate if articulatory suppression would influence recall of a written list of phonologically dissimilar letters in a series.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. When two tasks command the functionality of the same short-term memory store, interference will affect the efficacy level when encoding memory. 34 psychology undergraduates took part in this independent sample design. The experimental group read a series of numbers while carrying out an articulatory suppression task and the control group did that same task without articulatory suppression. Each trial was scored for the accuracy of recall.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. The mean percent of accurate recall was higher in the control group, supporting the hypothesis that if one area of the short-term memory is overloaded, the phonological loop in this case, the recall performance will be poorer. We can conclude from this experiment that articulatory suppression prevents rehearsal in the phonological loop because of overload, providing evidence for the three separate stores as posited by the Working Memory Model (WMM).$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. \n\nMemory models are hypothetical representations of how our memory works, making a complex process easy to understand while highlighting structural functionality.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. However, oversimplifying the memory system undermines the importance of the influence that emotions, culture, and individual differences could have on the way that memory works.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. Milner (1966) and Landry & Bartling (2011) have provided evidence to support that different types of memory function as separate entities, with the WMM building complexity around the framework that the MSM provided. Both models provide helpful information that allows us to understand perception, attention, and recall.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. It is clear that there are still gaps in our knowledge, for example, neither model explains why we are able to recall information without much rehearsal, while when we make every attempt to revise and encode knowledge, we still cannot remember it. Further research will help to inform future models of memory and can help to inform the gaps in our knowledge and address the limitations of the models we presently refer to.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. \n\nWord Count: 850\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nLandry P, $\\&$ Bartling C (2011). The Phonological Loop and Articulatory Suppression. American Journal of Psychological Research, 7, 79-86.\n\nMilner, B. (1966). Amnesia following operation on the temporal lobes. In H. M. (pp. 209-219). Springer."},{"ref":"PS0717","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0717\n\nOutline three main premises or features of the multi-store model of memory.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\nMemory consists of separate storage systems. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nThe three memory storage systems are sensory memory, short-term memory (STM) and long-term memory (LTM).  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\nMemories move in fixed sequential order from one store to the next. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\nEach storage system has a specific capacity and duration. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nEach (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) represents **1 mark** should be awarded. Marks should be awarded for any of the above responses to a maximum of **3 marks**. Similarly worded responses or additional accurate responses should also be awarded marks."},{"ref":"PS0718","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0718\n\nDefine the multi-store model’s concepts of capacity and duration.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\nCapacity refers to the amount of information that can be held in a specific storage system (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nDuration refers to how long information can be held in a specific storage system (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nAny similarly worded responses should also be awarded marks. Each (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) represents **1 mark** should be awarded."},{"ref":"PS0719","marks":"2","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0719\n\nOutline two (2) functions of sensory memory according to the multi-store model.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\nTo temporarily store all sensory information that is received by our senses in its raw original form. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nTo hold sensory information long enough for it to be transferred to short-term-memory if needed. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nTo prevent overload of information by ensuring that not all information from our senses makes its way to short-term memory. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nAny similarly worded responses should also be awarded marks up to a maximum of two. Any relevant/accurate responses should also be awarded marks. Each (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) represents 1 mark should be awarded."},{"ref":"PS0720","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0720\n\nDescribe the capacity and duration of sensory memory according to the multi-store model.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\nThe capacity is potentially unlimited. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nThe duration is very short, ranging from a fraction of a second to a few seconds. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nAny similarly worded responses should also be awarded marks up to a maximum of two."},{"ref":"PS0721","marks":"4","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0721\n\nDescribe the processes that take place for a memory to make its way to our short-term memory store. Begin your description at the entry point of memory. Your description should make use of the correct terminology from the multi-store model. Finally, explain what happens to information that does not make it to short-term memory.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\nThe entry point of memory is sensory memory. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nAll sensory information that reaches our senses is temporarily stored in relevant sensory registers in sensory memory. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nInformation that is attended to is transferred to short-term memory. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nAfter temporary storage, any information that is not attended to will be permanently lost or forgotten. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nAny similarly worded responses should also be awarded marks up to a maximum of **4 marks**."},{"ref":"PS0722","marks":"2","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0722\n\nDescribe the capacity and duration of short-term memory according to the multi-store model.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\nThe capacity is 7 plus or minus 2 pieces of information. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nThe duration is approximately 18-20 seconds. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nAny similarly worded responses should also be awarded marks up to a maximum of two."},{"ref":"PS0723","marks":"2","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0723\n\nOutline the main function of short-term memory (STM) according to the multi-store model.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\nTo temporarily hold a limited amount of information in our conscious awareness long enough (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) for us to be able to manipulate it to perform everyday functions such as problem-solving and decision-making. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nAny similarly worded responses should also be awarded marks up to a maximum of two."},{"ref":"PS0724","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0724\n\nAccording to the multi-store model, outline one way in which we can extend the duration of short-term memory (STM) and one way in which we can extend the capacity of STM.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\nWe can extend the duration of STM by repeating or rehearsing information. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nWe can extend the capacity of STM by chunking information into meaningful groups. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nAny similarly worded responses should also be awarded marks up to a maximum of two."},{"ref":"PS0725","marks":"2","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0725\n\nAccording to the multi-store model, list two locations from which our short-term memory store receives information.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n- Sensory memory (after we pay attention to it) (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n- Long-term memory (LTM) (receives information from LTM for conscious awareness) (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n Any similarly worded responses should also be awarded marks up to a maximum of two."},{"ref":"PS0726","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0726\n\nDescribe the functions and features of long-term memory according to the multi-store model. In your response, refer to capacity, duration, retrieval, forgetting and how information is received in LTM.\n[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\nLong-term memory is the permanent storage location for memory. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nIf information is repeated or rehearsed enough in STM it moves to LTM (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) for permanent storage. \n\nThe capacity of LTM is potentially infinite. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nThe duration of LTM is potentially forever or permanent. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nWhen we want to retrieve something from LTM it is moved to STM. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nForgetting is most likely the result of retrieval failure. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nAny similarly worded responses should also be awarded marks up to a maximum of **five (5) marks**."},{"ref":"PS0727","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0727\n\nDescribe two (2) strengths and two (2) limitations of the multi-store model.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n**Strengths**\n\n- The model was groundbreaking at the time and many memory models since have been based on this model. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n- It was the first model to make the clear distinction between short-term memory and long-term memory. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n- Several research studies provide support for elements of the model. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Limitations**\n\n- The model is overly simplistic memory is likely more complex than the model suggests. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n- The working memory model suggests that STM is far more complex than the model suggests.  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n- It does not differentiate between different types of LTM. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Evidence suggests that there are different types.\n\n- It does not differentiate between different types of rehearsal. Likely, basic repetition is not sufficient to transfer information to the LTM. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Meaningful rehearsal is likely to be important.\n\n- It cannot explain why we forget things, even though we have rehearsed them to great lengths. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n- Memory is not observable, therefore, elements of the model cannot be verified. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n- The model does not explain why we can remember things in the long term that have never been rehearsed. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\n Any similarly worded or accurate responses should also be awarded marks. A maximum of **two (2) marks** should be awarded for strengths and a maximum of **two (2) marks** for limitations."},{"ref":"PS0728","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0728\n\nIdentify the type of memory that the working memory model describes.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\nShort-term-memory (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) or working memory. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for either response to a maximum of **1 mark**."},{"ref":"PS0729","marks":"2","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0729\n\nDefine the term working memory (working memory model).\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\nWorking memory is the part of our memory that has a limited capacity (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) and is responsible for holding and manipulating information in our conscious awareness at any given time. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded explanations to a maximum of **2 marks**."},{"ref":"PS0730","marks":"4","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0730\n\nIdentify the three (3) subsystems and the main controlling system outlined by the working memory model.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\nCentral executive (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\\\n\nPhonological loop (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\\\n\nEpisodic buffer (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\\\n\nVisiosppatial Sketchpad (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"PS0731","marks":"4","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0731\n\nDescribe three (3) features or functions of the central executive subsystem in the Working Memory Model.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n- It is a control system that regulates information flow between the different slave systems (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n- It allocates the necessary resources to each slave system (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n- It has limited capacity (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n- It is modality-free and can accept information in a variety of formats (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded or additional accurate points to a maximum of **3 marks**."},{"ref":"PS0732","marks":"2","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0732\n\nFill in the gaps in the the following sentence. According to the working memory model, the visuospatial sketchpad deals with ___________ information and _________ awareness.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\nvisual  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\\\n\nspatial (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"PS0733","marks":"3","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0733\n\nFill in the gaps in the the following sentence. According to the working memory model, the phonological loop deals with __________ information and is made up of the _____________   __________ and the ___________  ____________.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\nauditory (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\\\n\nphonological store (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\\\n\narticulatory process (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should only be awarded for the word auditory if it appears first. Marks should be awarded for phonological store and articulatory processes regardless of whether they appear second or third."},{"ref":"PS0734","marks":"4","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0734\n\nDescribe the working memory model’s concepts of the phonological store and the articulatory process.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\nThe phonological store is often referred to as the inner ear. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) It listens to external sounds and then stores them temporarily. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Allowing auditory information to be briefly retained before being processed further or forgotten. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Whereas the articulatory process is often referred to as the inner voice. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) The articulatory process allows you to mentally/internally rehearse words and information. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This allows you to maintain auditory information in working memory.(:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nSimilarly worded accurate statements should also be awarded marks. A maximum of **two (2) marks** should be awarded for the description of the phonological store. A maximum of **two (2) marks** should be awarded for the description of the articulatory process."},{"ref":"PS0735","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0735\n\nDescribe three features or functions of the episodic buffer according to the working memory model.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\nAny **three** of the following would be acceptable:\n\nIt is a temporary backup store within working memory. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nIt holds chunks of multi-model information (e.g., visual and auditory). (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nIt combines different types of information (e.g., visual and auditory) so that it can be easily comprehended as a whole. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nIt communicates with working memory and long-term memory. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nIt allows information to pass in both directions between working memory and long-term memory. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nIt has a limited capacity. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded or additional accurate points to a maximum of **3 marks**."},{"ref":"PS0736","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0736\n\nApply your knowledge:\\\nCaitlin was busy studying for her IB Psychology exams. She usually finds it relaxing to listen to music but was frustrated because it was difficult to study and listen to her favourite Taylor Swift songs at the same time. Her dad suggested that she should try listening to his classical music (instrumental) CDs while she studies, instead of her regular music. To Caitlin’s surprise, this was a successful approach. Use your knowledge of the working memory model to explain why this might be the case.\n[7 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\nThe phonological loop is one of the slave systems of the working memory model. It deals with auditory information and is made up of the phonological store and the articulatory process. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) The phonological store temporarily stores external sounds that we hear (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$), while the articulatory process allows us to mentally/internally rehearse words. Each of these components has limited capacity. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nWhen studying, Caitlin would make use of her articulatory process to mentally rehearse the information she was trying to learn.  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) When she tried to listen to music with lyrics (eg., Taylor Swift), these lyrics would be competing for the limited capacity available in her phonological loop. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Therefore, there would likely be interference and studying would be difficult.  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nHowever, when listening to music without lyrics, Caitlin’s phonological store would be required to temporarily listen to and store the sounds of the music.  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) At the same time, her phonological loop would be required to mentally rehearse the information she is studying. As these two tasks use separate subsystems, they are not competing for the limited available capacity. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Therefore, there is less interference and it would be easier to do both things simultaneously.  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nAny accurate statements that are similarly worded should also be awarded marks up to a maximum of **7 marks**. However, the response must contain the following information:\n\n- Identification of the relevant components of working memory: Phonological loop, phonological store, articulatory process\n\n- Acknowledgement of the limited capacity of the phonological loop.\n\n- Identification of the articulatory process being used when listening to music with lyrics and when studying.  \n\n- Identification of the phonological store being used when listening to music without lyrics. \n\n- The presence of lyrics in the music would compete with the limited capacity of the phonological store, thereby creating interference and making studying difficult.\n\n- The absence of lyrics in the music would not compete with the limited capacity of the phonological store. This would result in no interference and studying would be easier."},{"ref":"PS0750","marks":"4","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0750\n\nThe Dual process model of thinking and decision-making suggests that we have two distinct modes of thinking, identified as System 1 and System 2.  Identify two alternative names for each of these two systems.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\nSystem 1: intuitive (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) automatic (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) quick (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\\\n\nSystem 2: rational (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) controlled (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) slow (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\n**1 mark** should be awarded for each correct response to a maximum of **2 marks** for system 1 and **2 marks** for system 2."},{"ref":"PS0751","marks":"2","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0751\n\nAccording to the dual process model, identify one reason System 1 thinking might be faster than System 2.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer/s}}$:\n\n- System 1 makes use of heuristics  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)  whereas System 2 does not (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n- System 1 is not likely to consider alternative explanations or options whereas System 2 is likely to (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n- System 1 is more likely to rely on hunches when making decisions (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) whereas system 2 thinking involves conscious reasoning (which takes more time)  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded point to a maximum of **2 marks**. If more than one reason is identified, marks should only be awarded for the first reason listed."},{"ref":"PS0752","marks":"2","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0752\n\nAccording to the dual process model, system 1 is more likely to use heuristics. Define the term heuristics and explain how the use of heuristics can speed up the decision-making process.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nHeuristics are mental shortcuts that allow us to focus on key elements of a problem or situation while ignoring less pertinent information.  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)  This speeds up decision-making because less content needs to be processed.  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded definition and explanation to a maximum of **2 marks**."},{"ref":"PS0753","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0753\n\nAccording to the dual process model, explain two reasons why System 1’s reliance on heuristics may result in more errors in decision-making.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nAny **two** of the following would be acceptable:\n\nUsing heuristics allows us to focus on key elements of a problem or situation while ignoring less pertinent information. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)  In doing so, important information may be overlooked. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\nHeuristics may be easily influenced by cognitive biases, (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)  increasing the likelihood of error. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\nHeuristics allow for the simplification of complex problems  (:span$\\color{red}\n\n\\checkmark$) , potentially leading to oversimplification and, therefore, inaccurate decisions.  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded or additional accurate explanation to a maximum of **4 marks** (with **2 marks** being allocated for each explanation)."},{"ref":"PS0754","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0754\n\nOther than heuristics, explain two additional reasons why the dual process model suggests that System 1 is considered to be more likely than System 2 to make mistakes.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nAny **two** of the following would be acceptable.\n\nSystem 1 thinking is quicker than system 2 thinking. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This speed may result in more errors. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nIn contrast to System 2, System 1 thinking is not likely to consider alternative explanations or options when making decisions. This means that superior or more accurate choices or explanations are overlooked. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nSystem 1 thinking requires less mental/cognitive effort than system 2 thinking. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This lack of cognitive effort may result in more errors. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nSystem 1 thinking is more prone to being influenced by biases than System 2,  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) increasing the likelihood of error.  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded or additional accurate explanation up to a maximum of **4 marks** (with **2 marks** being allocated for each explanation)."},{"ref":"PS0755","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0755\n\nBriefly discuss the accuracy of the following statement in relation to the dual process model, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$When making a decision, we only use system 1 or system 2.”\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n- The use of the word only in this statement is inaccurate (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) as it is overly simplistic (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n- It would be accurate to state that in some situations (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) we only use System 1 thinking,(:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n- In many situations, we make use of System 1 to make a quick or intuitive judgement or decision, (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) but we may then pass the information to our System 2 for further consideration. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n- Activation of System 1 is activated before System 2 (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) and in some instances, we are satisfied with our System 1 conclusion and do not pass the information onto System 2. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded or additional accurate points to a maximum of 6. Both inaccuracies and accuracies need to be stated. If only accuracies or inaccuracies are stated then marks should be awarded to a maximum of **3 marks**."},{"ref":"PS0756","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0756\n\nDescribe two strengths and limitations of the dual process model of thinking and decision-making.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n**Strengths:**\n\n- Elements of the theory are supported by empirical research. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n- The model has some explanatory power, in that it can explain some real-world phenomena. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) It can provide a potential explanation for why intelligent people can sometimes get what should be relatively simple answers wrong. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n- The model can account for how we can successfully make quick decisions (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) whilst dealing with vast amounts of information at once.(:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n**Limitations:**\n\n- Cognitive processes such as decision-making cannot be directly observed by researchers,  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)  therefore, inferences must be made from observable behaviour.  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n- Some psychologists suggest that the theory is overly simplistic  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) and that we have more than two modes of thinking. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n- As decision-making processes cannot be directly observed,  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) the theory can neither be verified nor falsified. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n- We cannot be certain that research results support the model,  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)  therefore, the interpretation of research findings may be influenced by researcher bias.  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nAny other accurate strengths and limitations, or any similarly worded strengths and limitations should also be awarded marks up to a maximum of **6 marks**. Both strengths and limitations must be outlined. If only strengths or limitations are outlined, marks should be awarded to a maximum of **3 marks**."},{"ref":"PS0918","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0918\n\nDescribe the Working Memory Model with reference to one study.\n\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \n\nBaddley & Hitch's Working Memory Model (1974) is a hypothetical representation of how our memory encodes, stores, and retrieves information. It focuses entirely on short-term memory, known as working memory. According to the model, our working memory has a limited capacity and is responsible for holding and manipulating information in our conscious awareness at any given time.  The model separates short-term memory into three separate stores: the visuospatial sketchpad for the processing of visual information, the phonological loop for processing auditory information, as well as the episodic buffer that acts as a temporary storage system connecting short-term memories to long-term memories. These separate stores are referred to as slave systems, and the Central Executive is a control system that regulates the flow of information between each of these different slave systems. One major premise of the model is that If two tasks make use of the same component of working memory, they cannot be performed successfully together. This is due to their limited capacity and because rehearsal needs to take place for short-term memories to be retained. If one of the stores is trying to process competing information, articulatory suppression, the disruption of information encoding, can occur. Landry & Bartling's (2011) study can be used to support the Working Memory Model.\n\nLandry and Bartling aimed to investigate the effects of articulatory suppression on short-term memory performance. 34 undergraduate students were presented with a list of letters to remember. Participants were divided into two groups: the experimental group engaged in articulatory suppression by repeating the numbers $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$1\" and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$2\" while trying to recall the letters, while the control group repeated nothing. After a brief presentation of the letters, participants were asked to recall them in the correct order. Results revealed that those in the articulatory suppression condition recalled significantly fewer letters than those in the control group. The study concluded that articulatory suppression negatively affects short-term memory performance by disrupting the rehearsal process crucial for encoding and recalling information in working memory.\n\nThis study evidences the working memory's separate stores by showing what happens when two tasks simultaneously engage the same subsystem (here, the phonological loop for letter and number repetition). This leads to interference, whereas tasks using different subsystems (*eg., visual and verbal*) do not. By attempting tasks that both use the phonological loop, that aspect of the working memory becomes overloaded, and encoding is disrupted. The fact that we are able to multitask using separate stores but not the same one, as is shown in Landry & Bartling (2011), supports Baddeley & Hitch's Working Memory Model. \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Mark Scheme:}}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible ideas \\& key terms:}}}$\n\nWorking Memory Model: The central executive - controls the slave systems and directs our attention. Slave systems: visuospatial sketchpad, phonological loop, and episodic buffer. Articulatory suppression - when one of the working memory stores is reaching capacity and function is being disrupted due to overload. \n\nMulti-Store Model: Memory consists of 3 separate storage systems (sensory, STM, & LTM), each with a specific capacity and duration. Information linearly moves through each store.\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible key studies:}}}$\n\nGlanzer & Kunitz (1966) investigated the serial position effect as evidence for two separate stores of memory: short-term and long-term. \n\nBaddeley et al. (1973) utilised a dual task activity to investigate the separate stores of the phonological loop and the visuo-spatial sketchpad. \n\nBaddeley et al. (1975) investigated how word length effect would affect recall in order to explore how articulatory suppression affects working memory due to capacity restraints.\n\n$\\textcolor{#d19f24}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible structure/organisation of response:}}}$\n\n**Paragraph 1:**\n\n - Describe your chosen model in some detail.\n\n**Paragraph 2:**\n\n - Outline the aim, procedure, results and conclusion of your chosen study. \n\n**Paragraph 3:**\n\n - Link the study back to the question by explaining how the methodology helps to support the theory as you have described it in your introduction. \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\n\n\\\n\nLandry, P., \\& Bartling, C. (2011). *The phonological loop and articulatory suppression.* American Journal of Psychological Research, 7(1), 79-86."},{"ref":"PS0922","marks":"22","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0922\n\nEvaluate one or more models of thinking and decision-making.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \n\nModels of thinking and decision-making aim to explain how individuals process information and make judgments. The Dual Process Model distinguishes between two systems of thinking: System 1, which is fast, intuitive, and automatic, and System 2, which is slower, more deliberate, and analytical. Furthermore, the model suggests that system 1 uses heuristics (mental shortcuts), whereas system 2 does not. Finally, unlike system 1, system 2 tends to consider alternatives before reaching a final conclusion, making it less prone to error and biased thinking.  $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  In order to evaluate the Dual Processing Model, it is important to examine its strengths, limitations, and empirical support from studies such as Alter and Oppenheimer (2007) and Englich and Mussweiler (2001). \n\nEnglich and Mussweiler (2001) explored the influence of anchoring (a type of heuristic) on judicial decisions, examining whether a prosecutor's sentence recommendation would influence the judgment of trial judges. In their experiment, judges were presented with a case of alleged rape and asked to recommend a sentence. One group was given a high anchor (a very severe sentence of 34 months), while the other group was given a low anchor (a much milder sentence of 2 months). The study found that judges were significantly influenced by the anchor provided, often recommending sentences closer to the anchor. The results confirmed that judges exposed to the high anchor (34 months) recommended an average sentence of 28.70 months, while those given the low anchor (2 months) recommended an average sentence of 18.78 months. This supports the model by showing how, through the use of heuristics, System 1 thinking can overpower System 2 thinking, leading to biased decision-making. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nIt is important to critically evaluate Englich and Mussweiler's supporting study to determine how much support it can provide for the dual processing model. A key strength of the study is its high reliability due to its controlled experimental design. One key feature of this design was the independent samples design, where participants were randomly assigned to either the high-anchor or low-anchor condition, ensuring that the results were not influenced by individual differences. The researchers controlled for the case materials used in both conditions, ensuring that the content of the case was identical for both groups, except for the anchor provided (either 34 months or 2 months). The same questionnaire was given to both groups, ensuring that the participants were asked the same questions in the same format, which makes the study replicable. In addition to this, the findings have strong real-world applications. The study illustrates how anchoring bias can affect decision-making, even in professional contexts like courtrooms. A limitation of the study is its generalisability. The sample was small, consisting of only 19 young trial judges with limited experience (an average of 9.34 months). This raises concerns about whether the findings can be applied to older, more experienced judges who may be less susceptible to cognitive biases. The study's sample was also predominantly male (15 out of 19), which may limit the generalisability to female judges or judges from different cultural backgrounds. $\\textcolor{#8c6188}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nAnother key study that supports the Dual Process Model is Alter and Oppenheimer (2007), who investigated whether presenting questions in a difficult-to-read font would lead to more correct answers on the Cognitive Reflection Test (CRT). Prior research suggested that disfluent fonts promote System 2 thinking, which is more analytical and deliberate. Based on this, the researchers hypothesised that participants exposed to a hard-to-read font would be more likely to engage System 2 and perform better. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nForty Princeton undergraduates were randomly assigned to one of two conditions: CRT questions in either an easy-to-read or difficult-to-read font. CRT questions are designed to trigger incorrect answers when individuals rely on quick, intuitive judgments (System 1), rather than reflective thinking (System 2). Results showed that participants provided more correct answers in the difficult-to-read font condition, suggesting that the hard-to-read font activated their rational System 2 thinking, making them less likely to make use of heuristics. However, the participants in the easy-to-read condition likely used heuristics and their intuitive System 1, leading to quicker but incorrect responses. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThis study highlights the different modes of thinking outlined by the Dual Process Model, thus suggesting that the model benefits from having some explanatory power. However, critical evaluation is essential. $\\textcolor{#8c6188}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nOne limitation is the use of an independent groups design, which introduces potential participant variability. Some participants may have had higher cognitive ability or more experience with CRT questions, reducing internal validity. While all were Princeton students, which implies generally high intelligence, this does not eliminate the risk of prior exposure to similar tasks. Participants were randomly assigned, and the researchers manipulated the IV (font type) while controlling other variables, such as using standardised questions. This qualifies as a true experiment, allowing for cause-and-effect conclusions between font type and performance. However, while the study links font difficulty to more correct answers, it does not directly prove that System 2 thinking was used, as cognitive processes are not directly observable. This highlights a key limitation of the Dual Process Model: many supporting studies infer, rather than demonstrate, support for the theory. $\\textcolor{#8c6188}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nIt is evident that the Dual Process Model provides a valuable framework for understanding decision-making by clearly distinguishing between System 1 (fast, automatic, intuitive) and System 2 (slow, deliberate, analytical) thinking. This distinction helps to clarify how different cognitive processes influence our judgments and decisions. The model is supported by numerous empirical studies, such as those by Alter and Oppenheimer (2007) and Englich and Mussweiler, which demonstrate how System 1 thinking can lead to biases and errors in thinking. However, the model has notable weaknesses. It can be overly simplistic, as human cognition is more complex and sometimes decisions involve a blend of both systems, rather than acting independently. Additionally, many of the studies supporting the model are conducted in controlled laboratory settings, which may not fully reflect real-world decision-making and raise concerns about the ecological validity of the findings. Moreover, the boundaries between System 1 and System 2 are not always clear,  and some decisions may incorporate both intuitive and analytical elements, complicating the classification of cognitive processes into just two systems. $\\textcolor{#8c6188}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nOverall, while the Dual Process Model provides useful insights into how we make decisions, this approach ignores how complex thinking really is and how different mental processes can work together. Therefore, a more holistic perspective is needed to better capture the full range of factors that influence decision-making in real-world contexts.\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{\\textbf {[Key Term]}\\rightarrow\\textbf{[KT]}}$\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{\\textbf {[Study]}\\rightarrow\\textbf{[S]}}$\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#8c6188}{\\textbf {[Critical Thinking]}\\rightarrow\\textbf{CT}}$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Mark Scheme:}}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible ideas \\& key terms:}}}$\n\n - Thinking: The process of using information and mental representations to make sense of the world, solve problems, or make decisions.\n\n - Decision-making: The process of choosing between alternatives, often involving reasoning and judgment.\n\n - Dual Process Model: A model suggesting two types of thinking: System 1: Fast, intuitive, automatic, and based on heuristics. System 2: Slow, analytical, deliberate, and logical.\n\n - Heuristics: Mental shortcuts used in System 1 that often lead to cognitive biases.\n\n - Anchoring Bias: The tendency to rely heavily on the first piece of information offered (the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$anchor”) when making decisions.\n\n$\\textcolor{#8c6188}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible critical thinking points:}}}$\n\n - Generalisability: Many supporting studies, such as Alter and Oppenheimer (2007) and Englich & Mussweiler (2001), were conducted with specific populations (*e.g., Princeton University students, inexperienced judges*). This limits generalisability to broader populations, particularly across cultures, ages, and levels of expertise.\n\n - Reliability: The model is supported by highly replicable, controlled lab experiments. \n\n - Applicability: The model has strong real-world relevance, especially in understanding biases in fields like law, finance, and healthcare.\n\n - Validity: Internal validity is strong due to the controlled nature of studies (*e.g., random assignment, manipulation of independent variables*). However, ecological validity is often low because tasks do not reflect natural decision-making contexts.\n\n - Reductionist: The model may also be considered reductionist, as it oversimplifies thinking into two discrete systems and doesn't account for how they may interact or blend.\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible key studies:}}}$\n\n - Englich & Mussweiler (2001) - Anchoring bias in judicial sentencing. Judges were influenced by arbitrary sentencing recommendations, showing how System 1 overrides System 2.\n\n - Tversky & Kahneman (1974) - Anchoring in numeric estimation tasks. Participants gave biased estimates based on starting values (*e.g., ascending vs descending multiplication*).\n\n - Tversky & Kahneman (1986) - Framing effect: how positive vs. negative presentation influences decisions.\n\n - Alter and Oppenheimer (2007) - Difficult-to-read font can activate System 2 thinking, resulting in greater accuracy in thinking and decision-making.\n\n$\\textcolor{#d19f24}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible structure/organisation of response:}}}$\n\n**Introduction:**\n\n - Define thinking and decision-making.\n\n - Introduce and briefly describe the model\n\n - Use the command term (state that the response will evaluate the Dual Process Model) \n\n - Mention the key studies that will be evaluated. \n\n**First body paragraph:** \n\n - Summary of the aim, procedure and findings of Englich & Mussweiler (2001) study \n\n - Explain  how the findings link to the dual processing model \n\n**Next body paragraph:** \n\n - Critical thinking: For example, evaluate the study in terms of ecological validity and generalisability.\n\n**Next body paragraph:**\n\n - Summary of the aim, procedure and findings of Alter and Oppenheimer (2007)\n\n - Explain how the findings link to the dual processing model.\n\n**Next body paragraph:** \n\n - Critical thinking: For example, evaluate the study in terms of ecological validity and generalisability.\n\n**Next body paragraph:** \n\n - Additional areas of discussion/uncertainty related to the dual processing model e.g the model has notable weaknesses as can be overly simplistic, as human cognition is more complex and sometimes decisions involve a blend of both systems, rather than acting independently.\n\n**Conclusion:** Briefly sum up the response and link back to the question by providing an overall evaluative comment related to the model.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAlter, A. L., Oppenheimer, D. M., Epley, N., \\& Eyre, R. N. (2007). *Overcoming intuition: Metacognitive difficulty activates analytic reasoning.* Journal of Experimental Psychology: General, 136(4), 569-576. https://doi.org/10.1037/0096-3445.136.4.569 \n\nEnglich, B., \\& Mussweiler, T. (2001). *Sentencing under uncertainty: Anchoring effects in the courtroom.* Journal of Applied Social Psychology, 31(7), 1535-1551.  https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1559-1816.2001.tb02687.x"},{"ref":"PS0923","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0923\n\nDescribe one model of thinking and decision-making.\n\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \n\nModels of thinking and decision-making aim to explain how individuals process information and make judgments. One model of thinking and decision-making is the Dual Process Model. This model suggests that there are two distinct systems of thinking. System 1 which is fast, automatic, intuitive, and often uses mental shortcuts known as heuristics. While efficient, it can lead to errors in judgment. System 2, in contrast, is slower, more deliberate, logical, and analytical. It requires more cognitive effort and is typically used in more complex or unfamiliar situations. Finally, unlike system 1, system 2 tends to consider alternatives before reaching a final conclusion, making it less prone to error and biased thinking. A key study that helps to describe the Dual Process Model was conducted by Englich and Mussweiler (2001). \n\nEnglich and Mussweiler (2001) investigated the influence of anchoring bias, a type of heuristic, in judicial decision-making. The researchers used an independent samples design and presented 19 young trial judges with a legal case involving alleged rape. One group was given a high anchor (a very severe sentence of 34 months), while the other group was given a low anchor (a much milder sentence of 2 months). The study found that judges were significantly influenced by the anchor provided, often recommending sentences closer to the anchor. The results confirmed that judges exposed to the high anchor (34 months) recommended an average sentence of 28.70 months, while those given the low anchor (2 months) recommended an average sentence of 18.78 months. This shows how System 1 thinking (automatic and fast) can use heuristics to overpower System 2 thinking (deliberate, analytical, and logical), leading to biased decision-making. This supports the Dual Process Model by showing how cognitive biases can affect judgments when System 1 overrides the more effortful System 2. \n\nOverall, the Dual Process Model helps explain why people often make quick but biased decisions, especially when under time pressure or uncertainty. By distinguishing between intuitive, heuristic-driven System 1 thinking and more deliberate, analytical System 2 thinking, the model offers valuable insight into how people think and make everyday decisions.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Mark Scheme:}}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible ideas \\& key terms:}}}$\n\n - Thinking: The process of using information and mental representations to make sense of the world, solve problems, or make decisions.\n\n - Decision-making: The process of choosing between alternatives, often involving reasoning and judgment.\n\n - Dual Process Model: A model suggesting two types of thinking: System 1: Fast, intuitive, automatic, and based on heuristics. System 2: Slow, analytical, deliberate, and logical.\n\n - Heuristics: Mental shortcuts used in System 1 that often lead to cognitive biases.\n\n - Anchoring Bias: The tendency to rely heavily on the first piece of information offered (the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$anchor”) when making decisions.\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible key studies:}}}$\n\n - Englich & Mussweiler (2001) - Anchoring bias in judicial sentencing. Judges were influenced by arbitrary sentencing recommendations, showing how System 1 overrides System 2.\n\n - Tversky & Kahneman (1974) - Anchoring in numeric estimation tasks. Participants gave biased estimates based on starting values (*e.g., ascending vs descending multiplication*).\n\n - Tversky & Kahneman (1986) - Framing effect: how positive vs. negative presentation influences decisions.\n\n - Alter and Oppenheimer (2007) - Difficult-to-read font can activate System 2 thinking, resulting in greater accuracy in thinking and decision-making.\n\n$\\textcolor{#d19f24}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible structure/organisation of response:}}}$\n\n**Introduction:**\n\n - Define thinking and decision-making.\n\n - Describe the dual processing model \n\n - Use the command term and introduce the study e.g A key study that helps to describe the Dual Process Model was conducted by Englich and Mussweiler (2001). \n\n**First Body paragraph** \n\n - Explain the aim, procedure and findings of Englich & Mussweiler (2001) study \n\n - Explain how the findings link to the model of thinking and decision making (the dual processing model)\n\n**Conclusion:** Briefly sum up the response and link back to the question.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\n\n\\\n\nEnglich, B., \\& Mussweiler, T. (2001). *Sentencing under uncertainty: Anchoring effects in the courtroom.* Journal of Applied Social Psychology, 31(7), 1535-1551.  https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1559-1816.2001.tb02687.x"},{"ref":"PS0971","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0971\n\nDescribe the multi-store model of memory with reference to one study.\n\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\nThe multi-store model of memory suggests that memory consists of 3 separate storage systems: sensory memory, short-term memory (STM) and long-term memory (LTM), and that information moves in a fixed sequential order from one store to the next. \n\nSensory memory temporarily stores all sensory information that is received by our senses in its raw original form. It holds sensory information long enough for it to be transferred to short-term memory if needed. Sensory memory has a duration of approximately a fraction of a second and an unlimited capacity. If information in sensory memory is attended to, it moves into short-term memory (STM); however, if it is not attended to, it is permanently lost.\n\nSTM has a capacity of 7 plus or minus two pieces of information, and it can hold things for a duration of approximately 18-20 seconds. The function of STM is to temporarily hold a limited amount of information in our conscious awareness long enough for us to be able to manipulate it to perform everyday functions such as problem-solving and decision-making. We can extend the duration of STM by repeating or rehearsing information, and we can extend the capacity of STM by chunking information into meaningful groups. If information is repeated or rehearsed enough in STM, it will be moved to LTM.\n\nLTM is our permanent memory store, with potentially unlimited capacity and duration. When we need to access information from our LTM, it is temporarily transferred back to our STM. The theory suggests that in LTM, forgetting often occurs due to retrieval failure.\n\nOne study that supports elements of the Multi-Store Model is Glanzer and Cunitz. The aim was to test the idea that our memory has separate storage systems. Using an independent groups design, 240 army enlisted men were assigned to different conditions. All participants heard the same list of words and were asked to recall them either immediately or after a 30-second delay, which included a distraction task. In the immediate recall condition, participants showed a primacy effect (strong recall of words towards the start of the list) and a recency effect (strong recall of words at the end of the list). In the delayed recall condition, the recency effect disappeared, while the primacy effect remained. This is because the words at the start of the list had been rehearsed long enough to enter LTM, and the words at the end were still in STM. Without rehearsal, 30 seconds exceeds the duration of STM.\n\nThe study supports some elements of the multi-store model. In particular, it suggests that STM and LTM are separate storage systems and STM has a limited duration. It also supports the idea that rehearsal helps move information into LTM.\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible ideas \\& key terms:}}}$\n\n - 3 separate storage systems\n\n - Sensory Memory\n\n     - sensory registers\n\n     - unlimited capacity \n\n     - duration of a fraction of a second. \n\n - Short-term memory (STM)\n\n     - capacity of 7 Plus or minus 2 bits of information \n\n     - duration 18-20 seconds (can be extended by repetition). \n\n     - temporary store\n\n- Long-term memory (LTM)\n\n     - unlimited capacity and duration. \n\n     - permanent storage\n\n - Information in sensory memory that is attended to moves to STM; if not it is lost. If rehearsed enough in STM, it moves to LTM; if not, it is lost.\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible key studies:}}}$\n\n - Glanzer and Cunitz (1966): Serial Position Effect provides support for separate STM and LTM memory stores.\n\n - Scoville and Milner (1957): This case study with HM supports the claim that STM and LTM are separate stores and that information must pass from STM to LTM.\n\n - Peterson and Peterson (1959): This study demonstrates that STM has limited duration without rehearsal.\n\n$\\textcolor{#d19f24}{\\underline{\\textrm{Possible structure/organisation of response:}}}$\n\n - **Direct Answer (DA)**: Begin with a statement that immediately starts describing or defining the multi-store model of memory.\n\n - Following paragraphs: **Define key terms (KT)**: Provide a detailed description of the model.\n\n - Next paragraph: Describe the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of a **study** that provides support for the MSM **(S)**.\n\n - Closing statement: Show the **link (L)** between the results of the study and the question. How do the findings of this study support elements of the MSM.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\n\n\\\n\nGlanzer, M., \\& Cunitz, A. R. (1966). Two storage mechanisms in free recall. Journal of Verbal Learning and Verbal Behavior, 5(4), 351-360. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0022-5371(66)80044-0"}]},{"name":"Emotion & Cognition","questions":[{"ref":"PS0307","marks":"9","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0307\n\nExplain how one research method is used in studying emotion and cognition.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the responses include all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question is provided in the first few sentences (*e.g. “One research method used in studying the processes involved in emotion and cognition are questionnaires. This method collects data by asking individuals a set of standardised questions, often in written form.” / “This answer will focus on the questionnaire method. It is a common method to obtain data from participants where the questions can be open*$\\text{\\textemdash}$*ended or not.” / One study that used this method is Neisser & Harsch (1992) who examined participants’ memories of the Challenger Space Shuttle explosion.*)\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of research methods. The response is focused on the command term ‘explain’, which asks to give a detailed account, including reasons and causes of one research method in studying emotion & cognition. (*e.g. Define and explain the research method in focus such as interviews, experiments, questionnaires, case studies, observation etc. / “A questionnaire is a method used to obtain data from participants where the questions can be open*$\\text{\\textemdash}$*ended or close*$\\text{\\textemdash}$*ended.” / “The experiment method is commonly used to study the relationship between the IV and DV. It helps to establish a cause*$\\text{\\textemdash}$*and*$\\text{\\textemdash}$*effect relationship between the two variables”*).\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study – including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion, where the study’s aim is linked to research methods on the topic of emotion & cognition or the effect of emotion on cognitive processes.\\\n\n*Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n- Using questionnaires, Neisser and Harsch (1992) investigated the role of flashbulb memory.\n\n- Talarico and Rubin (2003) studied flashbulb memory and its effect on emotions and its consistency through questionnaires \n\n- Sharot et al.’s (2004) quasi$\\text{\\textemdash}$experiment investigated the role of biological factors on flashbulb memories.\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study to the topic (e.g. *With the help of the questionnaire method, all participants were able to give data at the same time and the results from both questionnaires were compared for a better understanding of the recall accuracy in case of flashbulb memory theory.*).\n\n[9 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nResearchers use a variety of methods to obtain and analyse data. There are two main types of research, namely qualitative research such as interviews and case studies, and quantitative research, such as experiments, correlational research, and survey questionnaires.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nThis answer will focus on the questionnaire method.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ It is a common method to obtain data from participants where the questions can be open or closed$\\text{\\textemdash}$ended. It is a self$\\text{\\textemdash}$report technique that is cost$\\text{\\textemdash}$effective as it can be administered to multiple participants at the same time.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ One study that used this method is Neisser & Harsch (1992) who examined participants’ memories of the Challenger Space Shuttle explosion.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  Questionnaire was given to 106 participants the day after the incident asking them about the circumstances when they heard the news. They had to fill a similar questionnaire after the incident and another 3 years after the incident. They were also asked how accurate their memories were on a scale of 1 to 5.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nThe results of the study showed that only 11 out of 44 participants remembered to have filled this questionnaire before. The consistency of the responses was very low. Participants correctly answered only 2.95 questions out of 7 (42%). $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nWith the help of the questionnaire method, all participants were able to give data at the same time and the results from both questionnaires was compared for better understanding of the recall accuracy in case of flashbulb memory theory.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ \n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link"},{"ref":"PS0522","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0522\n\nExplain how emotion may influence one cognitive process.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question provided in the first few sentences *(e.g. “Emotion is a state of mind determined by mood, with cognitive factors increasingly seen as important in the experience of emotion. Lazarus' (1982) cognitive appraisal theory stresses the importance of cognition when as we experience an event, thoughts proceed physiological arousal and the sensation of emotion (which occur simultaneously). Therefore some cognitive processing must occur before an individual experiences an emotional response to an event. This can be linked to flashbulb memories.”)*\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of emotion and the process\n\nImportant concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define include: \n\n- *Emotion and perception - Perception is biased by schemas. Perceptual defense- where emotionally threatening stimuli takes longer to perceive.*\n\n- *Emotion and memory - Memory can be affected by schemas. flashbulb memory, repression and contextual emotional effects.*\n\n- *Emotion and decision making - Emotions, especially negative ones, can lead to changes in decision-making.* \n\nThe command term $`$explain' is addressed. \n\nThis requires students to give a detailed account of emotion which includes possible reasons or causes for the behaviour in relation to one research study linked to the cognitive approach to understanding behaviour.   \n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study – including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study\\\n\n*Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n- Brown and Kulik (1977) Flashbulb memories\n\n- Hardy and Legge (1968) Perceptual defence with high and low emotional words\n\n- Speisman et al. (1964) Cognitive appraisal and arousal\n\n- Bower (1981) Encoding and retrieval based on emotional states\n\n- Hirst et. al (2011) FBM and accuracy over time\n\n- Holmes (1990) Repression over time\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link exploring emotion to one study and in reference to the question *(e.g. $`$Brown and Kulik (1977) found that when participants were asked to recall the circumstances they found themselves in when they first received the news about some significantly emotional events, participants have vivid memories of where they were, what they did and what they felt.')*\n\nMarking limits:\n\n- Emotion, its related parts and what cognitive process you will be discussing are not explained in the opening paragraph.\n\n- Responses should clearly relate emotion to one cognitive process and must focus on the cognitive aspects of the research.\n\n- Explaining more than one study will mean marks are only given to the first study described.\n\n- Describing a study but the type of cognitive process is not explicitly addressed *(maximum 3 marks awarded)*\n\n- Explaining emotion and a cognitive process but does not refer to an appropriate study *(maximum 4 marks awarded)*\n\n- Clear links between emotion, the cognitive process and the study are needed to gain marks in the top mark band. *(If the link is only implicit a maximum of 6 marks will be awarded)*\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nEmotion is a state of mind determined by mood, with cognitive factors increasingly seen as important in the experience of emotion. Lazarus' (1982) cognitive appraisal theory stresses the importance of cognition as when we experience an event, thoughts proceed physiological arousal and the sensation of emotion (which occur simultaneously). Therefore some cognitive processing must occur before an individual experiences an emotional response to an event. This can be linked to flashbulb memories.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$\n\nA flashbulb memory is a very detailed and vivid $`$snapshot' of the moment and circumstances in which  emotionally arousing news was heard.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The $`$flashbulb' terminology indicates the events will be registered like a photograph; it will be accurate in detail.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ The defining feature of a $`$flashbulb' memory is not the memory of the event, but a memory of its reception context -  where you were when you heard the news.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ It is assumed that they are very resistant to forgetting; that the details of the memory will continue to be intact and accurate because of the emotional arousal at the time the memory was encoded.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ For example, some individuals can report, in exceptional detail, when and how they heard the news of the September 11 US terrorist attacks, as well as exactly what they were doing at the time and their exact feelings and reactions in response to the news.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ There is a positive relationship between strong emotion at the time of encoding and exceptional details of these memories.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Brown and Kulik (1977) aimed to study whether shocking events are recalled more vividly and accurately compared to other events.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ They gathered 80 US participants who were asked questions about ten events. Nine of the events were mostly assassinations or attempted assassinations of well-known American personalities ( JF Kennedy, Martin Luther King). The tenth was a self-elected event of personal relevance and feeling of surprise. The examples include the death of a friend or a family member or a serious accident. Participants were asked to remember the circumstances they found themselves in when they heard the news about the ten events. They were also asked to indicate how often they had rehearsed (over the years) information about each event.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nThe results show that participants had vivid memories of where they were, what they did and what they felt when they first heard about the shocking public events. The participants also said they had similar memories of their own shocking personal events. The results indicated that $`$flashbulb' memory is more likely for unexpected and personally relevant events. This led the researchers to suggest $`$the photographic nature of $`$flashbulb' memories'. Brown and Kulik suggested that flashbulb memory is caused by physiological arousal and physiological emotional arousal, for example, activity of the amygdala.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ \n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link"},{"ref":"PS0602","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0602\n\nWith reference to one or more research studies, discuss the influence of emotion on cognition.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question is explained. The question uses the command term *discuss*; therefore, the response should consider the influence of emotion on one or more cognitive factors in a way that encompasses a variety of arguments. Opinions must be expressed clearly and substantiated with relevant research. The response can also explain why it is important to investigate the influence of emotion on cognition. \n\n**1 mark** if the question is not accurately explained, has limited explanation, or is simply restated (*e.g., This essay will discuss studies investigating how emotion influences cognition*). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer is not relevant to the question.\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant and appropriate knowledge and terminology related to emotion and cognition are explained in detail without errors and are explicitly linked to the studies in Criterion C (*e.g., a definition of a relevant cognitive process(es); an accurate explanation of a relevant theory such as Flashbulb Memory Theory or the Somatic Marker Hypothesis*).  \n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors but lacks in some detail or shows only knowledge about research methods, such as a brief explanation of the theory with little focus on evaluating the study/ies.\n\n**1-2 marks**  if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited or superficial in nature. No marks will be awarded for this criterion if no relevant terminology is explained.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments stated, and a specific link is made between the findings of the study(ies) and the question. Relevant details, including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion, from one or more studies, should be included. Any study or combination of studies related to the influence of emotion on one or more cognitive factors would be acceptable. Studies that could be referred to include, but are not limited to:\n\n- An investigation into the formation of flashbulb memories after Margaret Thatcher's - Resignation: Conway et al. (1994)\n\n- An investigation into flashbulb memory theory by examining memories of the 9/11 terrorist attacks: Talarico $\\&$ Rubin (2003)\n\n- The role of the amygdala & emotion in decision making: Bechara et al. (1999)\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study is explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors (*e.g. the aim, procedure, results, and conclusions of the study are not presented, or there are some errors with regard to the relevance of the findings or details of the methodology*).\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research is used with insufficient detail, errors, or superficial links to the question. No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer does not include any relevant studies.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer shows relevant, consistent, and well-developed critical thinking points. Critical thinking points could include a combination of the following: strengths and limitations of the research methods used in supporting research studies and their conclusions, strengths and limitations of the relevant theories, potential applications of the findings, or an acknowledgement of complexities and alternative arguments. At least 2-3 discussion or critical thinking points are explained.\n\n**3-4 marks** if relevant critical thinking points are presented (as above) but are not always fully developed. They may not be directly linked to the specific study or theory, or they may be limited in number. For example, a response might state that the study is low in internal validity without explaining what this means in reference to the specific study. At least 1-2 discussion or critical thinking points are explained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if evaluation points are superficial or contain inaccuracies (*e.g., evaluation points only stated but not explained properly*). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer does not contain relevant critical thinking points, discussion, or evaluation.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** If the essay has an organised structure, including a clear introduction (which outlines the focus of the essay), body paragraphs (which outline the relevant study or studies as described in Criterion C; followed by evaluation of those studies), and concluding paragraphs or statements (which link back to the question by making a statement about the influence of emotion on one or more cognitive processes).\n\n**1 mark** if the organisation and clarity are not sustained throughout the answer (*e.g. unfinished response, lack of a clear introduction, lack of body paragraphs, and/or lack of a conclusion; a structure that is not logical or easy to follow; or incomplete description of key terms and studies*). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the response does not meet any of the standards above.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nFlashbulb Memory (FBM) Theory suggests that emotion does affect the cognitive process of memory. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ Memory is the process by which we manipulate, store, and retrieve information and experiences. According to FBM theory, emotional events are remembered in more vivid detail than ordinary memories. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$  It is important to understand the influence of emotion on memory because of potential implications related to the extent to which we can trust our emotionally charged memories. The influence of emotion on memory can be discussed by examining FBM theory with reference to studies conducted by Conway et al. (1994) and Talarico $\\&$ Rubin (2003). $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nAccording to FBM theory, flashbulb memories are remarkably resistant to fading over time, making them more accurate, vivid, and enduring compared to regular memories. The theory also states that people can often recall specific details about flashbulb memories, such as: where they were when they heard about the event, what activity they were engaged in, how they were informed, their personal reactions, and the responses and reactions of those around them. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ It is worth noting that a great deal of debate surrounds the validity of flashbulb memory theory. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nOne study that provides support for FBM theory is Conway et al. (1994). The aim of the study was to investigate the formation of flashbulb memories after hearing the news of UK Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher's resignation. The procedure involved 369 participants from inside  and outside the UK. Each of them was given a flashbulb memory questionnaire (FBQ) 10-14 days after Thatcher's resignation. The questionnaire asked them to describe their memory of when they learned of the resignation, without prompting. Each participant had their memory re-tested 11 months later. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe results showed that the UK participants had exceptionally vivid and accurate memories 11 months later, with 86% having flashbulb memories. However, the majority of non-UK participants showed high levels of forgetting, consistent with ordinary memory decay. The researchers concluded that the UK participants formed long-lasting and vivid flashbulb memories because of the emotional significance of the event. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Therefore, this study provides support for FBM theory and suggests that strong emotions may have a positive effect on memory. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ \n\nHowever, the extent to which this study provides support for FBM theory and the influence of emotion on memory depends on how robust the research was; therefore, it is essential that we evaluate the study and its conclusions. As this study made use of a questionnaire after the event to collect the data, no independent variable was manipulated. As such, the researchers have not conclusively established that it is the emotional nature of the memories that has enhanced participant recall. Furthermore, the researchers did not question participants until 10-14 days after the resignation. By this time, the original memory may have already been corrupted. This means that when they compared the 11-month-old memory with the original memory, they may have been comparing two flawed memories, which reduces internal validity and confidence in the findings significantly. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nFurther research investigating FBM theory and the influence of emotion on cognition was conducted by Talarico $\\&$ Rubin (2003). $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ Their aim was to investigate the vividness and accuracy of flashbulb memories compared to ordinary memories. 52 university students filled out questionnaires about their memories of 9/11 and an ordinary event from the days leading up to 9/11. These questionnaires were completed one day after the 9/11 terrorist attacks. The researchers then gave them a follow up questionnaire at different intervals of 7 days, 42 days, and almost 1 year later. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe results showed that the rate of memory decline and recall accuracy was similar between the ordinary memory and the 9/11 memory, with both being highly inaccurate. Despite this, participants were more confident in their 9/11 memory than their ordinary memory and reported that these memories were more vivid. The researchers concluded that while emotion may not make memories more accurate, it may make people believe that those memories are more accurate, meaning that people are more confident in these memories. Overall, this study did not provide support for Flashbulb Memory Theory, but it does provide some support for the role of emotion in our personal memory experience. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ While the study does not suggest that emotion in any way improves the accuracy of our memories, the study does suggest that emotion may cause us to believe that it does. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ \n\nWhile Talarico $\\&$ Rubin's (2003) study also suffers from an inability to establish cause and effect, it does have a number of controls in place that enhance internal validity when compared to the previous study. Firstly, the initial recordings of participants' memories of the event took place the day after the event. This means that the memories were still likely to be fresh in their minds. Therefore, a comparison between the initial memories and the future recollections is more meaningful. Furthermore, unlike the previous study, the researchers had a control condition which involved comparing the flashbulb memory to memories of an ordinary event, which increased the internal validity of the study. Talarico & Rubin also noted that many other research studies have reached the same conclusions as them, thus increasing the reliability of the findings and conclusions. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nIn conclusion, while Conway et al's (1994) study provides support for FBM theory and the positive influence of emotion on memory, there are significant concerns with the validity of the research and its conclusions. Research conducted by Talarico $\\&$ Rubin (2003) suggests that emotion may impact our perception of our own memories by making us more confident in them, but emotion does not necessarily improve the accuracy of memories. Talarico $\\&$ Rubin's (2003) more robust research study concluded that flashbulb memories suffer from the same issues with decay over time as ordinary memories. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ It would be worth investigating whether these unreliable, emotionally charged memories are measurably more vivid than regular memories. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nWord count: 966 words\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nConway, M. A., Anderson, S. J., Larsen, S. F., Donnelly, C. M., McDaniel, M. A., McClelland, A. G., Rawles, R. E., $\\&$ Logie, R. H. (1994). The formation of Flashbulb Memories. Memory $\\&$ Cognition, 22(3), 326-343. https://doi.org/10.3758/bf03200860\n\nTalarico, J. M., \\& Rubin, D. C. (2003). Confidence, not consistency, characterizes flashbulb memories. Psychological Science, 14(5), 455-461. https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-9280.02453"},{"ref":"PS0646","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0646\n\nEvaluate one or more research study/ies related to the influence of emotion on cognition.\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained and addressed consistently throughout the response. The question used the command term *evaluate* paired with a *research* question stem, therefore, the response should identify, explain, and weigh the strengths and limitations of one or more studies on emotion’s impact on cognition. These responses can also discuss advancements and challenges in completing research on the topic, though this should ideally be linked to the studies presented.\n\n**1 mark** if the question is either simply restated, misinterpreted (though still related to emotion and cognition, *e.g. This essay will evaluate flashbulb memory*), or if the overall response does not address the prompt (*e.g. the candidate writes in their introduction that they will evaluate research, but only discusses how emotion may impact cognition*).\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant \\& appropriate concepts and terminology related to emotion and cognition are explained in detail (*e.g. flashbulb memory or other effects of emotion and cognition, the somatic marker hypothesis, special mechanism hypothesis, recall, etc.*). Because the question asks to evaluate studies, marks will also be awarded for criterion B for knowledge of research methods and how they are used in the study context.\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors (*e.g. effects of emotion on cognition are described accurately but additional vocabulary terms $\\text{\\textendash}$ such as flashbulb memory, the somatic marker hypothesis, etc $\\text{\\textendash}$ are not correctly applied*), or if the response only shows strong methodological knowledge.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited (*e.g. providing an unsubstantiated effect of emotion on cognition, or conflating emotion and cognition with other concepts such as models of memory or thinking and decision-making*), and if the answer does not demonstrate knowledge and understanding of research methodology.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (*e.g., aim, procedures, results \\& conclusion*) are thoroughly explained and used to support the answer or the arguments stated. Studies to be used are but not limited to:\n- Neisser and Harsch (1992), on how the accuracy and confidence of flashbulb memories change over time\n- Sharot et al (2007), provide biological support for flashbulb memories\n- Kulkofsky et al (2011), suggest that culture and emotion interact to produce culturally-distinct trends in flashbulb memories\n- Luethi et al (2009), on how cortisol and stress may have different impacts on different types of memory\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors (*e.g. the aim, procedure, results \\& conclusions of the study were not clearly presented*).\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments (*e.g. the study was not linked to the evaluation points mentioned or one of the multiple studies presented is not related to the topic*).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed evaluation points (*e.g. around 2-3 strengths and limitations*) of research on emotion and cognition. While this depends on the studies selected, common trends in research on emotion and cognition include high internal and low ecological validity, varied construct definitions for flashbulb memory, and possible cultural bias.\n\n**3-4 marks** of the evaluation points (*e.g around 1-2 strengths \\& limitations*) are present and clearly explained but are not sustained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation or discussion points are superficial and mainly descriptive (*e.g the evaluation points were only stated or described but not linked back to the study*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is an organised \\& coherent explanation of one or more relevant studies, and an evaluation of those studies (*see Criterion C*). (*e.g. first paragraph introduces the focus on the question and related terminology, the succeeding paragraphs talk about the studies to be evaluated as well as the actual evaluation of one or more research studies, and the closing paragraphs or statements link the arguments back to the question*)\n\n**1 mark** if organisation and clarity were not sustained.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nFlashbulb memories are described as particularly vivid, detailed, and accurate recollections of events that are potentially ‘protected’ from the process of forgetting. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Initially, theorists proposed a special biological mechanism that suggested that a part of the brain became activated by surprising and emotionally laden events, and this mechanism was what created memories that are sharper than others. There is evidence for related biological processes, as seen in McGaugh and Cahill. Overall, though, the currently favoured explanation for flashbulb memory is best described as importance-driven, suggesting that personal relevance increases the likelihood of strong emotions and, therefore, flashbulb memories. Some studies, such as Neisser and Harsch, say that what best characterizes flashbulb memory is actually high confidence. Both studies present their own strengths and limitations in discussing emotion and cognition. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nMcGaugh and Cahill explored the role of adrenaline and the flight-or-fight response in the creation of flashbulb memories. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ In part one of their study, they randomly allocated participants to two conditions: one saw 12 slides and heard a visceral story about a car accident in which a boy had his limbs severed. The participants heard many details about his trip to the hospital and the work of surgeons who reattached his limbs. In the other condition, participants heard a comparatively dull story about a family who came to visit someone in a hospital and witnessed a safety drill while there. The first condition was designed to create a greater emotional experience, whereas the latter was not. Researchers hypothesized that memory would be greater in the first condition. Sure enough, participants rated the vivid, shocking story as more emotional, and recalled more details from the story and slides than the mundane story condition. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nIn a follow-up study, researchers administered propranolol, a beta blocker that blocks the target cells for the hormone adrenaline. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ When the study was repeated as a 2x2 factorial design (one level being the propranolol, the other being the intensity of the story) $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$, the condition which heard the high-emotion story and was administered the beta blocker recalled information at the same rate and accuracy as participants in the mundane story conditions. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThis suggests that adrenaline, a stress hormone that activates the amygdala, plays an important role in creating memories from emotionally arousing events, such as flashbulb memories. Because the amygdala is associated with emotional learning, blocking the activation of this brain region reduces learning to the same rate as ‘safe,’ ordinary events. The use of a true experiment allows us to understand this cause-and-effect relationship between adrenaline, amygdala activation, and flashbulb memories. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ The study is also highly replicable, so it’s possible to establish the reliability of these findings. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Yet, a tradeoff of this study is that the high control over variables reduces our ability to presume that this is what we would observe in real life, outside of the confines of a lab. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nIn the case study by Neisser and Harsch, after the Challenger explosion, around one hundred college students were invited to complete a questionnaire about where they were, what they were doing, and how they found out about the event. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Two years later they were asked to complete the same surveys again - this makes this a longitudinal, prospective study. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ They were also asked to report their confidence that their memories surrounding the explosion were correct. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ There were large discrepancies between the accuracy of their memories and their confidence: even when they had very different recollections than their first survey, confidence tended to be high. The researchers say this is one thing that distinguishes flashbulb memory $\\text{\\textendash}$ not high accuracy, but very high confidence in the accuracy of those memories. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nIn this study, which is an example of prospective, longitudinal research, the researchers were able to compare their responses to both the original and follow-up survey years later. In other studies that ask participants to share and describe the qualities of potential flashbulb memories, it can be difficult to measure or summarize the extent to which memories are accurate. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Neisser and Harsch is also an example of a naturalistic study. It provides a realistic sense of how memory of a real life, significant event changes over time. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ We can assume decent transferability to other national or international events, such as 9/11 or the Fukushima nuclear incident. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Similarly, there is no way to measure the exact emotional state without relying on self-reporting in Neisser and Harsch. It could be the case that people with the least accurate recall could be those who were least emotional about the event. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nOverall, both studies face their own strengths and limitations in attempting to describe factors that contribute to the formation of flashbulb memories. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nWord count: 780\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\nMcGaugh, J. L., Cahill, L., \\& Roozendaal, B. (1996). Involvement of the amygdala in memory storage: interaction with other brain systems. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America, 93(24), 13508–13514. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.93.24.13508\n\nNeisser U, Harsch N. Phantom flashbulbs: False recollections of hearing the news about Challenger. In: Winograd E, Neisser U, editors. Affect and accuracy in recall. Cambridge, UK: Cambridge University Press; 1992. pp. 9–31."}]},{"name":"Reliability of Cognitive Processes","questions":[{"ref":"PS0308","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0308\n\nExplain one ethical consideration in studying the reliability of one cognitive process.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the responses include all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question is provided in the first few sentences (*e.g. “An ethical consideration which is common in studying the reliability of cognitive processes is protection from physical or psychological harm.”*)\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of ethical issues that are related to the topic of reliability on cognitive processes in the cognitive approach.\n\nEthical considerations to be defined or focused on are some of the following: *the protection of participants from physical or psychological harm, consent, right to withdraw, confidentiality, deception, and debriefing.* \n\nThe answer is focused on the command term ‘explain’, which gives a detailed account, including reasons and causes related to ethical considerations in research on the reliability of cognitive processes (*e.g. Defining a specific ethical consideration, stating ethical concern and its description*). This also requires giving a detailed account of one ethical consideration concerning one research study linked to the reliability of cognitive processes (e.g., *reconstructive memory, eyewitness testimony*). Ethical considerations can be positive (*what guidelines could be followed*) or negative (*what guidelines were not followed*).\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study $\\text{\\textemdash}$ including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion, where the study’s aim is linked to ethical considerations on the topic of reliability of cognitive processes.\\\n\n*Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n- Loftus and Palmer (1974) investigated the role of information provided on influencing the eyewitness testimony of participants (protection from mental/physical harm) \n\n- Yuille & Cutshall (1986) investigated the accuracy in recall of eyewitness testimony using real eyewitnesses from a real crime  (informed consent) \n\n- Loftus, Miller and Burns (1978) investigated how verbal information supplied after an event influences a witness’s visual memory for that event (anonymity & confidentiality)\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the appropriate study to the topic (*e.g. Through the research conducted by Loftus and Palmer, it is seen that participants can be affected mentally as an exposure to traumatic events. Research in psychology needs to follow all ethical considerations.*).\n\n[9 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nEthical considerations ensure that participants are not harmed and that the research follows the standards set. Ethical standards in psychology are usually implemented by the American Psychology Association (APA) for all research done on humans and animals.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nProtection from mental or physical harm is an important ethical consideration that has to be followed to ensure the well$\\text{\\textemdash}$being of all participants. Protection from psychological harm is when the researchers ensure that participants are not exposed to any violence or traumatic events, which leaves them in a negative mindset after the research. On the other hand, an example of protection from physical harm is allowing participants to be ingested with any harmful substance or externally giving them anything for the research.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nThe ethical considerations include informed consent of the participants, right to withdraw anytime during the research, anonymity and confidentiality of the information shared by the participants, protection from mental and physical harm at all times, briefing and debriefing at the start and end of the research and deception to avoid social desirability effect.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nProtection from mental or physical harm is an important ethical consideration that has to be followed to ensure the well$\\text{\\textemdash}$being of all participants. Protection from mental harm is when the researchers ensure that participants are not exposed to any violence or traumatic events which leaves them in a negative mindset after the research. Protection from physical harm also means that the participants are not injected or ingested with anything that can harm them. $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nAn experiment was conducted by Loftus and Palmer in 1974. The research aimed to investigate the effect of leading questions on eyewitness testimony of an event. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ 45 participants were divided into 5 groups (smashed, collided, bumped, hit and contacted). The participants were shown 7 videos of car crashes for about 5$\\text{\\textemdash}$30 seconds. After each video, they were asked to fill a questionnaire which had one critical question “How fast were the cars going when they?” The verb in the critical question was changed to smashed/ collided/ hit/ bumped/ contacted and participants were asked to estimate the speed of the cars. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe ethical consideration of protection from mental or physical harm was not followed. Since the participants were exposed to accidents in the videos they saw, it could have mentally affected the participants in a negative way. The car crash could have resulted in making the participants remember their own traumatic experiences with similar incidents. To avoid mental harm, participants can be asked at the beginning of the experiment if they have gone through a similar experience and if they would still like to participate in the study. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nThe results of the experiment showed that higher intensity verbs such as smashed estimated higher speed by the participants and low intensity verbs such as contacted and hit estimated lower speed. The speed estimation in the research was as follows $\\text{\\textemdash}$ \\\n\nSmashed $\\text{\\textemdash}$ 40.5\\\n\nCollided $\\text{\\textemdash}$ 39.3\\\n\nBumped $\\text{\\textemdash}$ 38.1\\\n\nHit $\\text{\\textemdash}$ 34.0\\\n\nContacted $\\text{\\textemdash}$ 31.8 $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThrough the research conducted by Loftus and Palmer, it is seen that participants can be affected mentally as an exposure to traumatic events. Research in Psychology needs to follow all ethical considerations.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$  \n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link"},{"ref":"PS0520","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0520\n\nDescribe reconstructive memory, with reference to one study.\n\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the answer includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question is provided in the first few sentences. The question uses the command term describe; therefore, the response should provide a detailed account or description of reconstructive memory in relation to a research study (*e.g., $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The theory of reconstructive memory suggests that memory is not a passive retrieval of information from our long-term memory but is rather an active recreation of an event in the mind every time it is remembered.$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$*). \n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of key topics and supporting details are expressed in the answer. A minimum of 2 key terms should be used in the answer. Important concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define could include, but are not limited to: \n\n- Reconstructive memory: a theory which proposes that memory is an active process that involves the reconstruction of information, rather than being the passive retrieval of information\n\n- Schema: a mental framework that helps organise and interpret information, such as when participants in Loftus $\\&$ Palmer's study used their schema of car accidents to recall the event\n\n- Misinformation effect: the distortion of memory due to misleading information, as seen when Loftus $\\&$Palmer's participants recalled different speeds based on whether they heard $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$smashed$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$ or $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hit$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$ in the recall question\n\n- Eyewitness testimonies: accounts given by individuals who witnessed an event, which Loftus $\\&$ Palmer demonstrated can be influenced by the way questions are phrased\n\n  \n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nThe answer includes a clear focus on describing one study, including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study, where the study is linked to the topic. Relevant studies that could be referred to include, but are not limited to:\n\n- The effect of context to comprehension and memory of a passage: Bradford $\\&$ Johnson (1972)\n\n- Social and cultural effects on schemas: Bartlett (1932)\n\n- Leading questions on schema theory: Loftus $\\&$ Palmer (1974)\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study to reconstructive memory is present (*e.g., “This suggests that the reliability of memory recall can be quite poor as the process of reconstructing a memory is prone to interference from previous knowledge, as well as influenced by external cues. A particular study that highlights this is Loftus & Palmer's (1974) study into eyewitness testimony.”*).\n\n**Marking limits:**\n\n- If the answer provides a clear explanation of one relevant study and explains key terms relevant to the topic without an explicit link between the result of the study and the topic in question, a maximum of 6 marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer focuses on explaining a relevant study only, without explaining reconstructive memory, a maximum of 5 marks will be awarded. \n\n- If the answer focuses on a study that is not relevant to the topic, a maximum of 4 marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer focuses only on describing reconstructive memory, with no mention of a relevant study, a maximum of 3 marks will be awarded. \n\n- If the answer describes an incorrect topic, no marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer describes two relevant studies, the marks will only be given on the first study mentioned.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nThe theory of reconstructive memory suggests that memory is not a passive retrieval of information from our long-term memory but is rather an active recreation of an event in the mind every time it is remembered.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$   It recognises two pieces of information: information obtained during the perception of the event and external information obtained after the event. So, reconstructive memory proposes that memory can be changed during storage, processing, and retrieval, for example, due to schema processing.\n\nWe attempt to construct a memory using the information available to us from our schemas. This is a cognitive framework that helps organize and interpret information and allows us to make inferences about our environment. There are two processes involved with schemas: assimilation and accommodation. Assimilation is the process of a new memory or experience being added to our schemas to create a new mental framework. Accommodation is the process of altering the original memory with new information that conflicts with the previously known information.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nIt has been shown that numerous variables affect reconstructive memory, one of them being the misinformation effect. This could include the phrasing of a question or information supplied after an event. This suggests that the reliability of memory recall can be quite poor, as the process of reconstructing a memory is prone to interference from previous knowledge, as well as influenced by external cues.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nA particular study that highlights this is Loftus & Palmer's (1974) study on eyewitness testimony. The aim of this study was to investigate whether memory could be altered through the use of misleading questions about an event in an eyewitness situation.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The researchers  used a sample of 45 university students, who were split into various groups and shown a short video of a car crash. The only difference between the groups was the verb in the question they were asked after they had witnessed the recording: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hit$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$bumped$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$smashed$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$collided,$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$ or $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$contacted$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$ $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The participants were asked to report their estimation of the car's speed when the verb occurred.\n\nThe conclusions showed that the estimated speed reported was affected by the verb used.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ This could be because the verb implied information about the speed, which systematically affected the participants' memories of the accident.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Participants in the “smashed” condition reported significantly higher speeds than those in the “contacted” condition.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ This suggests that the verb conveyed an impression of the speed at which the car was travelling, and thus altered the participants' perception of the event. Therefore, this study suggests that leading questions act as false information, and they distort memory. This is an example of the misinformation effect and is one way to show that memories are reconstructive. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link"},{"ref":"PS0521","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0521\n\nExplain one cognitive bias, making use of one research study.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question provided in the first few sentences *(e.g. “Cognitive biases are illlogical, systematic errors in thinking that negatively affect decision making. there are several cognitive biases such as framing effects use of information judgment and anchoring bias.”)*\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of cognitive bias\\\n\nImportant concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define include: \n\n- *Fundamental attribution bias - when a favourable outcome is seen as resulting from actions under personal control and unfavourable ones as outside personal control.*\n\n- *Illusion of control - when the understanding of how events occur becomes distorted (creating superstitions).*\n\n- *Hindsight bias - when outcomes are perceived as predictable even when there is no evidence.*\n\nThe command term $`$explain' is addressed. This requires students to give a detailed account of the theory, which includes possible reasons or causes for the theory in relation to one research study linked to the cognitive approach to understanding behaviour. \n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study $\\text{\\textendash}$ including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study\\\n\n*Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n- Marsh and Hanlon (2007) confirmation bias in observations \n\n- Brandt et al. (1975) self-serving bias in teachers of students success or failure\n\n- Fluke at al. (2010) illusions of control in superstitions\n\n- Englich and Mussweiler (2001) Anchoring bias in jury verdicts\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link exploring cognitive bias to one study and in reference to the question *(e.g. $`$An example of cognitive bias is anchoring bias. Anchoring bias is when we use the first piece of information we receive to influence our decisions. For example, in Englich and Mussweiler's (2001) study, the decisions the jury made were biased by the original recommendations they received in court. The timeframe created an anchor to help them make a decision on an ambiguous concept. They used this as an anchor in which to create their cognitive judgment, thus showing cognitive bias.')*\n\nMarking limits:\n\n- Cognitive bias and its related parts are not explained in the opening paragraph.\n\n- Responses should clearly relate the theory to the cognitive approach and must focus on the cognitive aspects of the research.\n\n- Explaining more than one study will mean marks are only given to the first study described.\n\n- Describing a study but cognitive bias is not explicitly addressed *(maximum 3 marks awarded)*\n\n- Explaining cognitive bias but does not refer to an appropriate study *(maximum 4 marks awarded)*\n\n- Clear links between the theory of cognitive bias, the related parts and the study are needed to gain marks in the top mark band. *(If the link is only implicit a maximum of 6 marks will be awarded)*\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nCognitive biases are illogical, systematic errors in thinking that negatively affect decision-making. When making decisions, we often rely system one thinking, to make quick intuitive judgements. $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ In order to reduce the amount of thinking required, system one makes use of heuristics or mental shortcuts to make decisions. While heuristics can help us to make decisions quickly, they sometimes lead to illogical or incorrect decisions or conclusions. $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nOne example of a cognitive bias that results from the use of heuristics is anchoring bias. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$  Anchoring bias is when the first piece of information we receive influences our decisions. $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ For example, when we hear that the original price for an item was \\$100 but is now marked down to \\$80, we find that more attractive than walking in and seeing the same item for \\$80. The value of the goods is ambiguous $\\text{\\textendash}$ that is, we don't know how much it should be, so this information helps us to form an opinion and make a decision.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nEnglich and Mussweiler (2001) explored the idea of anchoring bias in jury decision-making. They aimed to see if anchoring bias would affect a jury's verdict.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ A sample of Law students was given a rape case and asked, based on the evidence, how long the punishment should be in one group they were told that the prosecution recommended a 34-month sentence and in the other group - a 2-month sentence.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The study showed that when given the suggestion of 34 months, the students recommended on average a longer sentence.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Since they would not have an absolute sense of how many months to punish the offender, they use the anchor that they were given to make their decision this is an example of how cognitive bias may affect one's behaviour.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link"},{"ref":"PS0541","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0541\n\nEvaluate one or more studies investigating reconstructive memory.\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained and addressed consistently throughout the response. The question used the command term evaluate paired with a research question stem, therefore, the response should identify, explain, and weigh the strengths and limitations of one or more studies on reconstructive memory. These responses can also discuss advancements and challenges in completing research on the topic, though this should ideally be linked to the studies presented.\n\n**1 mark** if the question is either simply restated, misinterpreted (though still related to reconstructive memory, *e.g. This essay will evaluate reconstructive memory*), or if the overall response does not address the prompt (*e.g. the candidate writes in their introduction that they will evaluate research, but only discusses reconstructive memory*).\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6** marks if relevant & appropriate concepts and terminology related to reconstructive memory are explained in detail (e.g. reconstructive memory itself, schema, episodic memory, levelling, sharpening, misinformation effect, etc.). Because the question asks to evaluate studies, marks will also be awarded for criterion B for knowledge of research methods and how they are used in the study context.\n\n**3-4** marks if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors (*e.g. reconstructive memory is described accurately but additional vocabulary terms - such as the schema, the misinformation effect, etc - are not correctly applied*), and some knowledge of key terms linked to research methods are explained but has minor errors or lacks in detail.\n\n**1-2** marks if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited (*e.g. incorrectly explaining how schema relates to reconstructive memory, or conflating reconstructive memory with other concepts such as false memories or a model of memory*), and if the answer only demonstrates general or superficial knowledge and understanding of research methodology.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6** marks if one or more relevant studies (*e.g., aim, procedures, results $\\&$ conclusion*) are thoroughly explained and used to support the answer or the arguments stated. Studies to be used are but not limited to:\n\n-   Brewer and Treyens (1981), on reconstructive memory of expected, missing, and unexpected office items\n-   Loftus and Palmer (1974), on false reconstructions of broken glass based on the schema associated with intense verb phrasings\n-   Neisser and Harsch (1992), on how the accuracy and confidence of flashbulb memories change over time\n\n**3-4** marks if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors (*e.g. the aim, procedure, results $\\&$ conclusions of the study were not clearly presented*).\n\n**1-2** marks if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments (*e.g. the study was not linked to the evaluation points mentioned or one of the multiple studies presented is not related to the topic*).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6** marks if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed evaluation points (*e.g. around 2-3 strengths and limitations*) of research on reconstructive memory. While this depends on the studies selected, common trends in research on reconstructive memory include high internal and low ecological validity, varied construct definitions for flashbulb memory, and possible cultural bias.\n\n**3-4 marks** if the evaluation points (*e.g. around 1-2 strengths $\\&$ limitations*) are present and clearly explained but are not sustained or if the response focused on the strengths only or the limitations only.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation or discussion points are superficial and mainly descriptive (*e.g. the evaluation points were only stated or described but not linked to the study*).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organisation}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is an organised & coherent explanation of one or more relevant studies and an evaluation of those studies (see Criterion C). (*e.g. first paragraph introduces the focus on the question and related terminology, the succeeding paragraphs talk about the studies to be evaluated as well as the actual evaluation of one or more research studies, and the closing paragraphs or statements link the arguments back to the question*)\n\n**1 mark** if organisation and clarity were not sustained or if the response is incomplete or unfinished.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\nMemory is not photographic; it is reconstructive - meaning that memories are constructed by piecing together information from various sources. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Cognitive research can shine a light on patterns that exist in how we reconstruct those memories. Two studies that explore reconstructive memory are Brewer and Treyens (1981) and Loftus and Palmer (1974). The former explores how our schema or cognitive frameworks, for environments, can influence the accuracy of the objects we recall from that environment. The latter investigates how post-event information, even in the phrasing of questions, can shape our recall and cause us to reconstruct memories that distort our recollection of details. Each study can be evaluated in order to determine the validity of the research and its findings. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nBrewer and Treyens' study aimed to understand how schema may play a role in the encoding and recollection of memories related to an office scene. Participants were seated in an office without knowing that the experiment had taken place to reduce participant variability's effect on working memory or effort. Some of the objects were $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$schema consistent\" with an object, such as desks, a coffee pot, and paper. Some of the objects were schema inconsistent and would be unexpected to see in an office, such as tree bark. After spending time in the office, participants were invited to a new room and asked to recall the objects within that office either by writing what they remembered, drawing what they remembered, or by responding yes or no to verbal prompts from a researcher. Their findings showed that participants often recalled items consistent with their schema of an office regardless of the recall condition. Participants who drew or wrote objects tended to forget objects that were schema-inconsistent. When listening to a list of objects, they were more likely to remember seeing schema-inconsistent objects but also tended to falsely recall objects that were typical of offices but were not in the room. These patterns of distortion suggest that schema does shape our encoding and recollection of objects. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThere are a number of strengths and limitations that impact the validity of this study. Brewer and Treyens utilised a questionnaire to determine whether or not participants may have been alert to the goals of the study while in the staged office area (they generally were not). This suggests that demand characteristics would likely not have influenced participant behaviour. Had they realised that the study had already started, they may have tried to memorise items in the office. While minor deception was used in this study, the collection of accurate data would not have been possible if participants were aware of the true aim of the study. No psychological harm was caused as a result of the study and participants were fully debriefed at the conclusion of the research. The use of minor deception was therefore ethical and helped to reduce the likelihood of demand characteristics. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nIn contrast, Loftus and Palmer's research focused on how question phrasing could predictably alter participants' memories of an event.  In this experiment, participants watched footage of a car accident and were then asked about the speed of the cars using different verbs to describe the collision (*e.g., $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hit,\" $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$smashed\"*). The results of this first study demonstrated that the verb used influenced participants' perception of the accident's severity, with participants who were asked the question phrasing involving the verb $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$smashed'' reporting higher speeds than those asked with the verb $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hit.\" When asked a week later whether they saw broken glass in the footage (there was none), those who were asked with the term $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$smashed\" were more likely to report seeing it than both the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hit” and control conditions. It is likely that participants integrated the intensity of the verb into their memories of the event, and because the more intense verbs are associated with more destructive accidents, they tended to recall broken glass because it is a feature of severe accidents. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nLoftus and Palmer's experiment was rigorously controlled, with manipulation of an IV to see the effect on the DV, with random allocation of participants to groups. Some examples of controlled variables include: the use of identical questions (other than the critical question), and identical videos across each condition. So, it was possible to isolate the effect of the IV and establish a cause-effect relationship between the independent variable (the critical words) and the dependent variable (estimation of speed). The experiment, however, was conducted in a laboratory setting, as such, there may be a problem of ecological validity. On many occasions, this type of memory reporting comes after shocking or otherwise emotionally arousing events, such as being asked to provide eyewitness testimony after a traumatic accident. The fact that the experiment used students as participants has also been criticized because students are not representative of the wider population. Importantly, students may be less experienced drivers and therefore less confident in their ability to estimate speeds. As such, their estimates may be based on guesswork, rather than the influence of the verb. While the findings of this study were significant, there are some contradictory findings, with other studies suggesting that leading questions or other post-event information does not distort memory accuracy for most participants. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nIn conclusion, both studies contribute to our understanding of reconstructive memory by highlighting two important aspects: the role of schemas and the influence of suggestive information. Brewer and Treyen's study demonstrates that our memories are not just recordings of sensory information but are constructed from both what we perceive, as well as being influenced by our schemas. Loftus and Palmer's research suggests that memory reconstruction may be impacted by leading questions and post-event information. While both studies contain strengths, ecological and external validity, along with contradictory findings should be considered. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nWord count: 939\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\nBrewer, W. F., & Treyens, J. C. (1981). Role of schemata in memory for places. Cognitive Psychology, 13(2), 207-230.  https://doi:10.1016/0010-0285(81)90008-6 \n\nLoftus, E. F., & Palmer, J. C. (1974). Reconstruction of automobile destruction: An example of the interaction between language and memory. Journal of Verbal Learning and Verbal Behavior, 13(5), 585-589.  https://doi:10.1016/s0022-5371(74)80011-3"},{"ref":"PS0542","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0542\n\nExplain one study on the reliability of one cognitive process.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question provided in the first few sentences. The command term in focus is ‘explain’ which gives a detailed account including reasons and causes related to the reliability of cognitive processes with focus on one study. (*e.g. One study that explains how one cognitive process may not be reliable is the study by Loftus & Palmer which investigated the reconstructive nature of memory.*)\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of the topic of the reliability of one cognitive process. To answer the question, the answer may focus on one but not limited to the following cognitive processes: *reconstructive memory (memories that are reconstructed or reorganized based on various influences such as experiences, suggestions), effect of schema on memory (schema influences memory through th process of encoding, retrieval and storage), false memories and its influence on recall (this happens when recolleting incorrect information, memory reconstruction by filling the gaps and by interfering with the reality*).\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one  study on the reliability of one cognitive process  - including the  aim, procedure, results & conclusion of the study, where the study is linked to the topic.\\\n\n*Relevant studies include but are not limited to the following:*\n\n-   Loftus and Pickrell (1995) - Did a study on false memories of autobiographical events can be created through the power of suggestion.\n\n-   Loftus and Palmer (1974) - Investigated whether the language used in eyewitness testimony can alter memory. \n\n-   Shaw and Porter (2015) - Investigated if it is possible to implant a false memory of committing a crime.\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link between the results of the study to the reliability of one cognitive process is stated. (*e.g.The study concluded that people can be made to believe in certain memories even if it has not occurred in reality. It is possible for memories to be unreliable due toaltered by suggestion as explained bythe reconstructive nature of memory.*)\n\n[9 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nCognitive processes may be unreliable for a number of reasons. One way of understanding the reliability of one cognitive process is through reconstructive memory.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ Reconstructive memory explains that memories consist of what is encoded and stored primarily but it is also affected by prior knowledge in the form of schema.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ A schema is a mental representation of something based on prior knowledge and experience. With the help of schema, information is organized and gaps are filled in our mind, but this can be distorted.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$Memory distortion is caused by cognitive processes such as memory, thinking and decision making and these processes interfere with the encoding of the memory schema and may result in false memories.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ \n\nA study conducted by Loftus and Pickrell (1995) explains the process of reconstructive nature of memory.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$  The aim of the research was to study if false memories of autobiographical events can be created through the power of suggestion. The sample consisted of 24 participants, 3 males and 21 females with the age between 18-53 years. The procedure consisted of asking two questions to the parent or the sibling of the participant, “Could you retell childhood memories of the participant?” and “Do you remember a time when the participant was lost in a mall?” $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe participants then received a questionnaire in the mail which consisted of 3 events that were real while 1 event was about “getting lost in the mall”. All participants were asked to write about the four events and submit their responses back to the researchers. They were also instructed that if they didn't remember either of the events, an appropriate response would be ‘I don’t remember this incident’. The participants were then interviewed twice over a period of four weeks and were asked to recall information about the four events. They were also asked to rate their level of confidence about the memories on a scale of 1-10. After this, a second interview was conducted and then they were debriefed and asked if they could guess which memory was the false memory out of the four events.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nResults of the study found that 25% of the participants “recalled” the false memory but the participants also ranked this memory as less confident than the other memories. It was also found that they also wrote less information about this memory in the questionnaire.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe study concluded that people can be made to believe in certain memories even if it has not occurred in reality. It is possible for memories to be unreliable due to suggestion as explained by the reconstructive nature of memory. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{References}}$:\\\n\nLoftus, E. F., & Pickrell, J. E. (1995). The formation of false memories. Psychiatric Annals, \n\n25(12), 720–725. https://doi.org/10.3928/0048-5713-19951201-07"},{"ref":"PS0563","marks":"22","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"#  PS0563\n\nEvaluate one or more research study/ies on one or more biases in thinking and decision-making.\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The \n\nquestion used the command term *evaluate*, therefore, the response should \n\nidentify, explain, and weigh the strengths and limitations of one or more studies on cognitive bias/es.\n\n**1 mark** if the command term is not accurately explained or limited explanation.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant $\\&$ appropriate terminology is explained in detail (e.g. Defining thinking and decision making, system 1 $\\&$ system 2 thinking, biases). Since the question asks to evaluate studies, marks will also be awarded for criterion B for knowledge of research methods and how they are used in the study context.\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors and if the response only showed knowledge about the research \n\nmethod (e.g. brief explanation of systems of thinking and biases with little focus on evaluation of study). \n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited with no evaluation of studies.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (e.g., aim, procedures, results $\\&$ \n\nconclusion) are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments \n\nstated. Studies to be used are but not limited to: \n\n-   Framing effect (Tversky and Kahneman,1981), \n\n-   Confirmation bias (Hill et al 2008), \n\n-   Asymmetric dominance (Huber, Payne and Puto, 1982) \n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (e.g. the aim, procedure, results $\\&$ conclusions of the study was not clearly presented) \n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. (e.g. the study was not linked to the evaluation points mentioned) \n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed evaluation points (e.g. around 2-3 strengths and limitations) of research on one bias (there is high internal validity as a cause and effect relationship can be observed between the IV and DV). \n\n**3-4 marks** of the evaluation points (e.g. around 1-2 strengths $\\&$ limitations) are present and clearly explained but are not sustained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation or discussion points are superficial (evaluation points only stated but not explained properly).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if a clear link was made at the end of the answer focusing on the bias and the study used. The link can be made with the help of a bias and how it is shown in the research used to explain that bias. The answer must be organized and clear with appropriate information provided. \n\n**1 mark** if the organization and clarity were not sustained throughout the answer.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nThinking and decision-making are higher-order cognitive processes. Thinking is a complex process of analyzing and synthesizing information for understanding $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. Decision-making is a process of selecting an option from a variety of alternatives present $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. Both of these cognitive processes are crucial to understanding and interpreting information on a daily basis.\n\nThere are two types of thinking: System 1 and System 2. System 1 thinking is a type of automatic and fast thinking that is usually instinct-based, whereas System 2 thinking is more detail-oriented and logical, requiring time before coming to a conclusion $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. Both of these thinking types lead to cognitive biases such as the framing effect, confirmation bias, and asymmetric dominance. These cognitive biases are mistakes resulting from mental shortcuts (heuristics) that affect the process of decision-making $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$.\n\nConfirmation bias occurs when humans tend to seek out information that supports their opinion or belief and overlook information that does not support it $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. This bias is explained in the research conducted by Hill et al. (2008)$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$. The aim was to investigate whether an expectation of guilt can affect the interviewing of a suspect in a crime situation$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. There were a total of 61 participants (mean age of 21 years). In Study 1, all participants formulated questions they wanted to ask a suspect to determine if they cheated on a task. In Study 2, 45 $`$innocent' and $`$guilty' participants were accused of cheating on a task. They were interviewed with guilt questions or neutral questions. In Study 3, a total of 58 participants listened to audiotaped interviews from Study 2 $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. Following this, they rated aspects of the suspects' verbal behavior. The results of the study were as follows: in Study 1, more guilt-presumptive questions were formed even though participants were free to form their own questions. In Study 2, a significant connection was found between guilt/innocence and whether they confessed or denied. While in Study 3, all the ratings of independent observers who listened to tape recordings of the suspect interviews showed an influence of questioning style on the suspects' verbal behavior $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. These questions were then rated by people who were unaware of the two conditions on a scale of 0-6, where 0 was extremely presumptive of innocence and 6 was extremely presumptive of guilt $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. The study explains confirmation bias because participants holding an expectation of guilt generated more guilt-presumptive questions for the interview $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$.\n\nThe strengths of the study are high internal validity, as a cause-and-effect relationship can be observed between the independent variable (IV) and dependent variable (DV), and there is no evidence of researcher or participant bias in the study. The limitations of the study are that it can only be generalized to undergraduate students with a mean age of 21 years, as the sample was restricted to that $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. There are no ethical concerns in the study since there is no mental or physical harm done to students. There is evidence of deception, as the true aim was not shared with the participants to avoid demand characteristics $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$.\n\nThe study explained above shows how a cognitive bias (confirmation bias) affects decision-making by only focusing on selected information that confirms their belief/opinion $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$.\n\nAnother cognitive bias is called the framing effect, wherein people make choices based on the framing of the choice in a positive and negative manner or gain and loss $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. In an experiment conducted by Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky in 1981, they aimed to observe the influence of the way a decision is made based on how a problem is framed in scenarios involving risks $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. The sample consisted of 307 university students who were given a questionnaire with the question $`$Imagine that the USA is preparing for an outbreak of an unusual Asian Disease, which is expected to kill 600 people $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$.' There were two options provided for two groups: Group 1 had Program A: 200 people will be saved, and Program B: There is a 1/3 possibility that 600 people will be saved, and a 2/3 probability that no people will be saved. Group 2 had Program A: 400 people will die, and Program B: There is a 1/3 probability that nobody will die and a 2/3 probability that 600 people will die $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. The study results found that Program A was selected by 72$\\%$ of people in Group 1 and 22$\\%$ in Group 2, whereas Program B was selected by 28$\\%$ of people in Group 1 and 78$\\%$ of people in Group 2. It is seen through this study that the outcomes of the programs were the same, but only because they were framed in gains and losses format, participants gave different responses $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$.\n\nOne strength of the study is that no physical harm was done, and informed consent was acquired from the participants $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. There is evidence of high internal validity, which is supported by how Tversky and Kahneman designed their experiments with careful control over extraneous variables. They manipulated framing by presenting information differently to various groups, ensuring that any observed differences in decision-making were attributable to the framing manipulation rather than other factors.$\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nThe study has some limitations, such as low ecological validity since it was a lab experiment conducted under controlled conditions $\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. It cannot be generalized to all age groups since the experiment was conducted on university students only.$\\textcolor{#1974D2}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nBoth studies effectively show the influence of cognitive biases on thinking and decision-making.\n\nWord count: 865\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nHill, C., Memon, A., & McGeorge, P. (2008). The role of confirmation bias in suspect interviews: A systematic evaluation. Legal and Criminological Psychology, 13(2), 357$-$371. https://doi.org/10.1348/135532507X238682\n\nTversky, A., & Kahneman, D. (1981). The framing of decisions and the psychology of choice. Science, 211(4481), 453$-$458. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.7455683"},{"ref":"PS0645","marks":"22","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0645\n\nDiscuss one or more research methods used in studies related to the reliability of one or more cognitive processes.\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The question used the command term *discuss* paired with the *research* methods question stem, therefore, the response should present a balanced discussion regarding the use of one or more specific research methods (*experiments, interviews, questionnaires, etc*) in producing knowledge regarding the topic.\n\n**1 mark** if the question is either simply restated, misinterpreted (though still related to the reliability of one or more cognitive processes, *e.g. This essay will evaluate research related to errors in thinking and decision-making*), or if the overall response does not address the prompt (*e.g. the candidate writes in their introduction that they will discuss research methods, but only discuss cognitive bias*).\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if candidates accurately and clearly explain one or more research methods, using relevant terms (*i.e. in a response discussing interviews, a candidate discusses iterative questioning, rapport, interview schedules, etc.*). Because the question asks candidates to discuss research methods in the context of the reliability of one or more cognitive processes, marks are also given for the relevant \\& appropriate use of content terminology (*e.g. cognitive bias, the misinformation effect, heuristics, etc*).\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant research method terminology is explained and has minimal errors, or if the response primarily demonstrates sustained knowledge of cognitive processes.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited (*e.g. having inaccurate definitions of natural experiments*) and if the answer does not demonstrate knowledge and understanding of cognitive processes.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (*e.g., aim, procedures, results \\& conclusion*) are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments stated. Studies to be used are but not limited to:\n- Neisser and Harsch (1992), a case study which explored how the accuracy and confidence of flashbulb memories change over time\n- Brewer and Treyens (1981), a true experiment on reconstructive memory of expected, missing, and unexpected office items\n- Loftus and Palmer (1974), a true experiment on false reconstructions of broken glass based on the schema associated with intense verb phrasings\n- Englich and Mussweiler (2001), a true experiment on anchoring bias\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (*e.g. the aim, procedure, results \\& conclusions of the study were not clearly presented*)\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. (*e.g the study was not linked to the evaluation points mentioned*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed discussion points regarding the use of research methods (*e.g. strengths and limitations of the method as applied to the study of the topic specified in the question*).\n\n**3-4 marks** of the discussion points (*e.g. 1-2*) are present and clearly explained but are not sustained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the discussion points are superficial and mainly descriptive (*e.g. if the discussion points were only stated or described but not linked back to the study*).\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is an organised \\& coherent explanation of one or more research methods, an evaluation of one or more relevant studies (*see Criterion C*), and a discussion of the use of methods in developing knowledge within the topic area. (*e.g. first paragraph introduces the focus on the question and related terminology, the succeeding paragraphs talk about the studies to be evaluated as well as the actual evaluation of one or more research studies, and the closing paragraphs or statements link the arguments back to the question*)\n\n**1 mark** if organisation and clarity were not sustained.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nUnderstanding the reliability of cognitive processes such as memory requires the use of multiple research methods. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ Two methods - experiments and case studies - present different strengths and limitations that supplement one another as we seek to establish factors that may make memory more or less reliable. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ Studies like Brewer and Treyens use experimental methods to take advantage of random allocation and other controls to improve internal validity but suffer from reduced ecological validity. Case studies, such as Neisser and Harsch, present opportunities to study memory in realistic settings.\n\nBrewer and Treyens sought to understand how schema may play a role in the encoding and recollection of memories related to an office scene. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Participants were seated in an office without knowing that the experiment had taken place, in order to reduce the effect of participant variability in working memory or effort. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Some objects were ‘schema consistent’ with an object, such as desks, a coffee pot, and paper. Some objects, such as tree bark, were inconsistent with the schema of an office and would be unexpected to see in an office. After spending time in the office, participants were invited to a new room and asked to recall the objects within that office either by writing what they remembered, drawing what they remembered, or by responding yes or no to verbal prompts from a researcher. Their findings showed that participants often recalled items consistent with their schema of an office regardless of the recall condition. Participants who drew or wrote objects tended to forget objects that were schema-inconsistent. When listening to a list of objects, they were more likely to remember seeing schema-inconsistent objects but also tended to falsely recall objects that were typical of offices but were not in the room. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Because of the trends in recall across participants, this true experiment helps highlight how memory can be shaped by our preexisting knowledge, and can therefore be unreliable. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nBrewer and Treyens involve an experimental design, $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$, which permits the use of random allocation and other strong controls to reduce the impact of confounding variables. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ In other words, internal validity is quite high: we can be relatively sure that with statistically significant findings, the independent variable (schema congruence/incongruence and recall method) is what is causing the difference between groups. Further, we can infer causation between the independent and dependent variables in true experiments. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ One limitation of this particular study is that it was not a repeated measures design, which could mean that some of the differences in performance could be due to individual differences in memory and schemas.\n\nNeisser and Harsch, in contrast, is a case study. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Case studies gather rich data using multiple methods over a long period of time for individuals or unique groups. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ After the Challenger explosion, around one hundred college students were invited to complete a questionnaire about where they were, what they were doing, and how they found out about the event. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Two years later, they were asked to complete the same surveys again, and some participants were asked to take part in interviews to understand their results further. They were also asked to report their confidence that their memories surrounding the explosion were correct. There were large discrepancies between the accuracy of their memories and their confidence: even when they had very different recollections than their first survey, confidence tended to be high. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The researchers say this is one thing that distinguishes flashbulb memory - not high accuracy, but very high confidence in the accuracy of those memories.\n\nChoosing to use a case study to investigate flashbulb memories is a useful way of addressing one recurring limitation of retrospective memory research: when asking participants to report memories, researchers often have no way of being able to verify those memories. In Neisser and Harsch, which is an example of prospective, longitudinal research, the researchers were able to compare their responses to both the original and follow-up survey years later. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ It is also an example of a naturalistic study. It provides a realistic sense of how memory of a significant event changes over time. We can assume decent transferability to similar, national events, such as 9/11. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nBoth studies, despite their diverging methods, have two core issues. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ For Brewer and Treyens, we have to make an assumption of what the schema is for each participant. There is no way to measure it and understand how schema has influenced each participant while recollecting. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Similarly, there is no way to measure the exact emotional state without relying on self-reporting in Neisser and Harsch. It could be the case that people with the least accurate recall could be those who were least emotional about the event. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ These are both unknowns that cannot necessarily be addressed through skilful use of research methods alone.\n\nWord count: 743\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\nBrewer, W. F., \\& Treyens, J. C. (1981). Role of schemata in memory for places. Cognitive Psychology, 13(2), 207-230. doi:10.1016/0010-0285(81)90008-6.\n\nNeisser U, Harsch N. Phantom flashbulbs: False recollections of hearing the news about Challenger. In: Winograd E, Neisser U, editors. Affect and accuracy in recall. Cambridge, UK: Cambridge University Press; 1992. pp. 9–31."},{"ref":"PS0814","marks":"2","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0814\n\nOutline the concept of cognitive bias.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nA cognitive bias is a predictable error in decision-making or thinking (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) that can systematically occur when an individual is processing and interpreting information. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded definitions to a maximum of 2 marks."},{"ref":"PS0815","marks":"4","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0815\n\nDefine confirmation bias and explain how it can lead to errors in thinking and decision-making. \n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nConfirmation bias is a cognitive bias that causes us to actively seek out or search for evidence or examples that confirm our pre-existing beliefs or ideas. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) It also involves a failure to search for contradictory evidence. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) In doing so, our currently held beliefs are strengthened, even if these beliefs are false. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This usually occurs as a shortcut in the thinking process, which reduces mental effort, but can lead to skewed perspectives and faulty decision-making. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for each accurate point or similarly worded point up to a maximum of 4 marks. The response should include both a definition and an explanation but these do not need to be evenly weighted. If a response only includes a definition or an explanation then a maximum of 2 marks should be awarded."},{"ref":"PS0816","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0816\n\nExplain one $`$real-life example' of how confirmation bias could result in faulty decision-making. \n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nMinnie's favourite YouTuber always uses a particular brand of headphones, and as a result, Minnie thinks that they are high-quality headphones. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Before buying the headphones, Minnie reads and watches a load of online reviews for the headphones. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) When doing so, Minnie searches for positive reviews (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) but does not read or watch any negative reviews (even though there are plenty of them available). (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) In this situation, confirmation bias has resulted in Minnie actively seeking out evidence that confirms their pre-existing beliefs about the quality of the headphones. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) It also meant that Minnie failed to search for contradictory evidence. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) As a result, Minnie's currently held belief that they are high-quality headphones was strengthened, (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) even though this belief may be false. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded example that is explained to a maximum of 6 marks. Any additional $`$real life' accurate example that is explained should also be credited."},{"ref":"PS0817","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0817\n\nExplain one way in which you might be able to avoid making faulty judgements based on confirmation bias. \n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nBy being aware that confirmation bias exists (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) we might be able to avoid it by actively seeking out contradictory evidence before making a judgement. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded or additional accurate point that is explained, up to a maximum of 2 marks."},{"ref":"PS0818","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0818\n\nExplain two factors that might contribute to confirmation bias.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nCognitive effort may be a factor that contributes to confirmation bias. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)  People may avoid seeking out contradictory evidence, as this process requires greater mental effort. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nEmotional attachment may also contribute the confirmation bias. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) If people are emotionally invested in a particular belief or point of view they will have less desire to seek out contradictory evidence. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded or additional accurate explanation up to a maximum of 4 marks (with 2 marks being allocated for each explanation)."},{"ref":"PS0819","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0819\n\nDefine anchoring bias and explain how it can lead to errors in thinking and decision-making.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nAnchoring bias occurs when an individual relies too heavily on the first piece of information presented to them  when making judgements. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) The initial information acts as a reference point, forming the basis of the decision-making process, (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) even if the initial information is misleading, inaccurate, or not significant. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) The relevance or importance of the initial piece of information is not necessarily significant; it is the placement of the information at initial introduction that helps it become a framework around which understanding is built.  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded points to a maximum of 4 marks. A maximum of 2 marks should be awarded for the definition and a maximum of 2 marks should be awarded for the explanation."},{"ref":"PS0820","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0820\n\nExplain one $`$real-life example' of how anchoring bias could result in faulty decision-making.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nRetailers will often set the price of a product at \\$19.99 instead of \\$20.  The “19” acts as an anchor, so the product seems cheaper than it really is. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) This can result in faulty decision-making because customers may believe they are getting a bargain and decide to buy the item, even though the difference in cost is only one cent. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded examples that are explained to a maximum of 4 marks. Additional $`$real life' accurate examples that are explained should also be credited."},{"ref":"PS0821","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0821\n\nExplain one reason why it might be difficult to avoid anchoring bias.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nAnchoring bias is considered to be an unconscious bias. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) As such, it is difficult to avoid something that you are not aware is impacting your decision-making processes. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded or additional accurate reason that is explained, up to a maximum of 2 marks."},{"ref":"PS0822","marks":"4","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0822\n\nExplain two potential causes of, or reasons for anchoring bias.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nAnchoring bias might be caused by selective attention. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) When focusing on the anchor, people might selectively attend to this information and ignore other information. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\nAnchoring bias might be caused by using system 1 thinking, which often relies on heuristics (mental shortcuts).  (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Using the first piece of information as a starting point for providing an estimate or making a decision can act as a mental shortcut, which aims to speed up the decision-making process. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded or additional accurate explanation up to a maximum of 4 marks (with 2 marks being allocated for each explanation)."},{"ref":"PS0823","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0823\n\nDefine the availability heuristic and explain how it can lead to errors in thinking and decision-making.\n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nThe availability heuristic is a cognitive bias that involves making use of information or examples that most readily or easily come to mind when making judgements (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) about the likelihood of a specific event occurring in the future. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) In doing so, the more readily available examples about a specific event occurring are given more weight (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) than examples of events that are more difficult to bring to mind (or less available). (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) It is assumed that if examples of events are hard to bring to mind, (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) then they must be unlikely to occur. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) While this logic may sometimes result in correct predictions (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$), it can in some instances, systematically lead to inaccurate predictions. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for each accurate point or similarly worded point up to a maximum of 6 marks. The response should include both a definition and an explanation but these do not need to be evenly weighted. If a response only includes a definition or an explanation then a maximum of 3 marks should be awarded."},{"ref":"PS0824","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0824\n\nExplain one $`$real-life example' of how the availability heuristic could result in faulty decision-making.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nBernice is planning a family holiday to Italy. She joins an Italy travel planning group on social media. The day before she leaves for her trip, a person in the group creates a post recounting their recent experience of being pickpocketed while walking around a busy piazza in Rome. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) As a result of the availability heuristic, Bernice becomes excessively anxious while on her trip and is always worried when in busy places (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$). As this recounted story was fresh in her mind (easily available), (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$) Bernice may significantly overestimate the actual risk of being pickpocketed in Italy. (:span$\\color{red}\\checkmark$)\n\n(Overestimating the likelihood of a terrorist attack or a plane crash would also be suitable examples, particularly if these have recently been reported on the news.)\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Marking Guidance}}$:\\\n\nMarks should be awarded for any similarly worded example that is explained to a maximum of 4 marks. Any additional $`$real life' accurate example that is explained should also be credited."}]},{"name":"Technology & Cognitive Processing","questions":[{"ref":"PS0235","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"?","type":"MCQ","body":"# PS0235\n\nIn the study by Mueller and Oppenheimer (2014) on typewriting vs. handwriting notes, the results of the study supported __________.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The positive effects of technology\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The negative effects of technology\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Technology does not influence cognition in any way\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The effect of technology depends on the way it is used","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe results of the study by Mueller and Oppenheimer indicated that students who took notes by hand did significantly better in their comprehension skills than those who took notes with a laptop. This shows a negative effect of technology on cognition.\n\nThe other options are incorrect as the results of the study did not support it.\n\nMueller, P., & Oppenheimer, D. M. (2014). The pen is mightier than the keyboard. *Psychological Science*, *25(6)*, 1159–1168. https://doi.org/10.1177/095679761452458"},{"ref":"PS0580","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0580\n\nEvaluate one or more studies that explore how the use of technology can affect cognition.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The question used the command term evaluate, therefore, therefore, the response should identify, explain, and weigh the strengths and limitations of technology[s effect on cognition. The response can also include an explanation of why it is important to evaluate research studies that investigate technology's effect on cognition.\n\n**1 mark** if the question was briefly described in less detail or if the question was restated. (*e.g. This essay will evaluate two studies that evaluate how technology affects human cognition*)\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant & appropriate terminology is explained in detail without errors and is linked to the study and the strengths and limitations of the study discussed (*e.g. social comparison theory is when we evaluate our personal worth in how we compare to others around us, often in a downward manner which can affect self-esteem*). Because the question asks to evaluate studies, marks will also be awarded for criterion B for knowledge of research methods and how they are used in the study context.\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors (*e.g. Social comparison theory is when we compare ourselves to others*) and if the response only showed knowledge about the theory rather than exploring the methodology in the studies.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited (*e.g.Social comparison theory often happens on social media…*) and If the answer only shows knowledge related to technology and cognition and did not explore studies that support it. \n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (*e.g., aim, procedures, results $\\&$ conclusion*) are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments stated. Studies to be used are but not limited to:\n\n - Chou and Edge (2012) - studied the impact of using Facebook on perception of other people’s lives\n\n - Bavelier et al. (2011) - research selective attention on video games\n\n - Sparrow et al. (2011) - studied the Google effect on memory\n\n - Storm et al. (2016) - investigated cognitive loading and digital technology\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (*e.g the aim, procedure, results & conclusions of the study was not clearly presented*)\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the arguments. (*e.g the study was not linked to the evaluation points mentioned*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent and well-developed evaluation points (e.g around 2-3 strengths and limitations) of research on technology's effect on cognition. (*e.g. in Storm's (2016) study is in a highly controlled environment and there is a clear cause and effect relationship between the variables*)\n\n**3-4 marks** of the evaluation points (*e.g around 1-2 strengths $\\&$ limitations*) are present and clearly explained but are not sustained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation or discussion points are superficial and mainly descriptive (*e.g if the theory was evaluated instead of the study, or if the evaluation points were only stated or described but not linked back to the study*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n**2 marks** if there is an organised & coherent explanation of the positive and negative effects of technology's effect on cognition and an evaluation of one or more relevant studies (see Criterion C). (*e.g. first paragraph introduces the focus on the question, has a clear thesis and related terminology is defined, the succeeding paragraphs link back to the argument stated in the introduction, an evaluation is made based on the strengths and weaknesses of the research studies covered*)\n\n**1 mark** if the organisation and clarity were not sustained throughout the answer. (*e.g unfinished response, incomplete description of key terms and studies*)\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nThinking is a cognitive process that involves using information and knowledge to consider concepts and ideas. Whereas decision making makes use of thinking processes to make choices. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ With the rapid advancement of various forms of technology, technology has become so pervasive in our lives that psychologists have begun exploring its effects on our decision-making abilities, notably system 1 thinking, which is a fast, intuitive, and automatic thinking process $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. Bavelier et al (2011) explored how playing computer games improves the accuracy of rapid decision-making among its users, dispelling widely held beliefs that playing games entails no redeeming effects. However, with the rise of social media use, Chou and Edge (2012) have demonstrated that through the utilisation of the availability heuristic, social media platforms cause a rise in our tendency to evaluate our self-worth according to how we compare with the success and positive traits of others, known as social comparison theory $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. Often, this mechanism can cause downward comparison where people feel bad about themselves in comparison, or to put down others and feel better about themselves. It is clear that technology has both positive and negative effects on our thinking and, yet its integration into our lives has been so rapid and recent that its true effects are unprecedented. It is worth evaluating each of these studies to better understand how useful they are in shedding light on the effect of technology on cognitive processes.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nChou and Edge (2012) aimed to assess the effect of Facebook use on thinking and the availability heuristic, by focusing on how this shortcut leads individuals to make social comparisons $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. 425 undergraduate students took part in a survey that had a series of 10-point Likert scale questions centred around comparisons the participants made between themselves and people they see on their newsfeeds. Results demonstrated that the more people spent on Facebook each week, the more likely they were to evaluate others as being happier than they were. The conclusion can be made that the more time spent online, the more that users see the heavily curated lives of their friends and acquaintances, the less likely they are to be critical about what they see and more likely to attribute snapshots of time to someone's feed to their level of happiness. This flawed thinking can be explained in terms of the availability heuristic as people use the information that is most $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$available\" to them when making evaluative decisions about others' happiness compared to their own $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. Therefore, this suggests that the use of social media, which is a form of digital technology can result in flawed thinking.\n\nIn the above study, Chou & Edge used a Likert scale for participants to respond to the survey. While this allows data to be easily collated and quantified, it is difficult to capture the complexity of someone's lived experience on a numbered scale and lack of granularity means it is difficult to make distinctions between the response numbers $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. Different individuals will interpret the scale differently, which causes some construct invalidity in the concepts that are attached to the scale. While there is a clear relationship between the time that someone spends on Facebook and the way they negatively evaluate their happiness in relation to others, the relationship is correlational, and so no cause-and-effect relationship can be established $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. It is difficult to rule out other variables that are having an effect; a more controlled study would provide more reliable data $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. \n\nBavelier et al. (2011) aimed to explore the relationship between playing action video games and decision-making skills. In a group of men and women whose average age is 26 and who had not played video games in one year, half were asked to play a video game for two hours a day where they had to make decisions about a character's life, and half were not asked to play a game. After a set period of time, all participants were asked to play a video game where they had to intuit the movement of dots on a screen. While all participants were able to complete the task successfully, the group who had played the action game were able to do so faster, while making fewer errors. The researchers concluded that the group who had played the game were able to deal with large amounts of visual information more quickly, had improved spatial reasoning, and were able to make faster decisions than the group who had not played the game. Highlighting a potential positive effect of technology on the cognitive process of decision-making.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$.\n\nBavelier's (2011) study was standardised, meaning the design is replicable and can be carried out by other researchers, which strengthens the reliability of the results. Also, the design is a true experiment, meaning a cause-and-effect relationship can be established $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. However, the participants were split randomly into the experimental and the control conditions, meaning participant variability may have been the cause of the differences in results between both conditions $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. Perhaps a baseline test could have been introduced to assess speed of decision-making before the study began, as this would have provided information about the improvement of skills of the experimental group after playing the character game. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nWhile these two studies suggest that the use of technology can have both positive and negative effects on our cognitive processes, both studies are limited in scope in that they only focused on Facebook and action video games. They were also only focused on thinking and decision making, which means they fail to take into account other cognitive processes. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$  It may be that the impact of digital technology on cognitive processes is influenced by the type of technology, how it is used and what you are measuring. It is, therefore, difficult to make informed conclusions about the impact of technology on cognitive processes from just these two studies. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ The experimental nature of Bavelier's study benefits from tight controls and being able to establish a cause-and-effect relationship, both of which are lacking in the study conducted by Chou & Edge. However, both studies lack an examination of the long term effects of technology and are limited in scope. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nBavelier, Daphne & Green, C & Han, Doug & Renshaw, Perry & Merzenich, Michael & Gentile, Douglas. (2011). Brains on video games. Nature reviews. Neuroscience. 12. 763-8. 10.1038/nrn3135.\n\nChou, H.-T. G., & Edge, N. (2012). \"They are happier and having better lives than I am\": The impact of using Facebook on perceptions of others' lives. Cyberpsychology, Behavior, and Social Networking, 15(2), 117-120. https://doi.org/10.1089/cyber.2011.0324"},{"ref":"PS0585","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0585\n\nDiscuss research methods used to investigate the effect of technology on cognitive processing.\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question was explained. The command term used in the question is *discuss*, therefore the response should provide a balanced review of one or more research methods used to investigate the effect of technology on cognitive processing (*e.g. thinking, memory and/or decision making*). The main focus of the response should be on the research methods, rather than on the effect of technology on cognitive processing. Focus on the answer is maintained throughout the response.\n\n**1 mark** if the question was briefly described in less detail or if the question was just restated. (*e.g. This essay will discuss research methods used to investigate the effect of digital technology on cognitive processing*) Or if focus on the answer is not maintained throughout the response.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant $\\&$ appropriate terminology is explained in detail (*e.g. details relating to true experiments, correlational studies, quasi-experiments, case-studies etc*). Because the focus of the question is on research methods, knowledge and understanding should focus on describing and explaining concepts related to research studies. Some explanations and knowledge related to the effects of digital technology and cognitive processing would also be suitable but this should not be the main focus.\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors *but is lacking in some detail or there is more detail about cognitive processing and technology and not enough detail about the research methods*.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited (*e.g. inaccurate statements about specific research methods*) and If the answer only shows knowledge related to digital technology and cognitive processes.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies (*e.g. aim, procedures, results & conclusion*) are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments stated. Studies to be used are but not limited to:\n- Meshi et al. (2019): Correlational study - excessive Facebook use and decision-making\n- Sparrow et al., (2011): True experiment - The Google Effect: influence of technology on memory.\n- Oh et al., (2017): True experiment - the impact of smartphone anti-aging apps on memory.\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study was explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors. (*e.g. the aim, procedure, results *$\\&$* conclusions of the study was not clearly presented*)\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research was used with superficial links to the question. (*e.g. the study does not have a clear research method or the study has a clear research method but it is not investigating cognitive processing*)\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer showed relevant, consistent, and well-developed critical thinking points related to the use of the chosen research method to investigate the effects of technology on cognitive processing. The critical thinking should be able to consider both the strengths and limitations of the research method in the chosen study or studies (*around 3-4 critical thinking points*). (*e.g. While this experiment allowed for the manipulation and control of variables, it was conducted in a highly artificial environment that only tested recall of trivia facts. This reduces ecological validity and mundane realism, meaning that the findings may not accurately predict how functions in complex, real-world settings.*)\n\nThere must be at least one critical thinking point that links specifically to the use of one of the methods in studying the effects of technology on cognitive processing. *(e.g. “As cognitive processing is not directly observable, experiments allow for a scientific and robust method for testing hypotheses related to the impact of technology on cognitive processing”.)*\n\n**3-4 marks** if the critical thinking points *(e.g. around 1-3 strengths $\\&$ limitations of using the chosen research method in the study)* are present and clearly explained but are not sustained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation or discussion points are superficial and mainly descriptive *(or if the study is evaluated without focusing on the research method used in the study)*.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organisation}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if there is an organized *$\\&$* coherent explanation of one or more research methods and an evaluation of one or more relevant studies with a focus on evaluating the research method used in the study (see Criterion C). (*e.g. the first paragraph introduces the focus on the question and related terminology, the succeeding paragraphs talk about the studies that used the stated research method as well as the actual evaluation of one or more research studies, and the closing paragraphs or statements link the arguments back to the question*)\n\n**1 mark** if organisation and clarity were not sustained.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\nCognitive processing refers to the actions that take place in our mind such as the processes of memory, thinking, and decision-making.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ Humans are surrounded by, and engage with, technology on a daily basis. Given its extensive usage, psychologists need to have an understanding of how technology might influence our cognitive processing. Psychologists use a variety of research methods to help them investigate the influence of technology on cognitive processes, with the choice of research method being dependent on the scope and requirements of the research. The use of true experiments and correlational studies to investigate the effect of technology on cognitive processing can be discussed with reference to research conducted by Sparrow et al., (2011)  and Mehsi et al., (2019). $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nTrue experiments are a quantitative method that usually aims to test a cause-and-effect relationship between two or more variables. They involve the manipulation of an independent variable to see the effect on a dependent variable and the random allocation of participants to conditions. Experiments are usually performed under highly controlled conditions where researchers attempt to control extraneous variables so they can isolate the effects of the IV. As cognitive processing is not directly observable, experiments allow for a scientific and robust method for testing hypotheses related to the impact of technology on cognitive processing. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nOne research study that made use of a true experiment to investigate the effect of technology on cognitive processing is Sparrow et al. The aim was to investigate whether continual access to Google was having any impact on memory. The study used an independent groups design and participants were asked to input recently learned trivia facts into a computer. The IV was what the participants were told would happen to the information they were typing. Some participants were told that the information would be saved and accessible later and some were told that it would be erased. In each condition, half of the participants were explicitly asked to try and remember the information they were typing. Later, all participants were given a surprise recall test, with the results of the test being the dependent variable. The results showed that those participants who believed the information would be erased had significantly better recall while being explicitly asked to remember the information had no impact on levels of recall. The researchers concluded that continuous access to search engines may impair recall due to the expectation of instant information availability. This has been referred to as the Google Effect or Digital Amnesia.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe use of the experimental method allowed the researchers to manipulate two different independent variables. The first was whether participants were told that the data would be saved or erased and the other was whether they were explicitly asked to remember the information. They were also able to tightly control other variables, such as making sure that the newly learned trivia facts were identical across each condition. This allowed the researchers to isolate the effects of the IV and establish a cause-and-effect relationship between reliance on digital technology to act as a backup storage system and how memory performance. As cognitive processing is not directly observable, the experimental method allowed the researchers to make inferences about memory based on the data collected (DV). Finally, while this experiment allowed for the manipulation and control of variables, it was conducted in a highly artificial environment that only tested recall of trivia facts. This reduces ecological validity and mundane realism, meaning that the findings may not accurately predict how functions in complex, real world settings. These are limitations that are often shared by studies that make use of the experimental method.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nAnother quantitative research method used to investigate the effects of technology on cognitive processing is correlational studies. When using a correlational study, the researchers do not actively manipulate an independent variable. Instead, they analyse two existing variables, to see how they might influence each other. While the numerical data collected can also be analysed for significance, cause-and-effect relationships cannot be established between variables, as the researchers have not manipulated or controlled any variables.$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nMeshi et al., (2019) made use of a correlational study to investigate the effect of technology on cognitive processing. The study aimed to examine the relationship between excessive online social networking use and decision making. The study involved 71 participants taking a Facebook Addiction Scale questionnaire (BFAS) to assess their reliance on Facebook. All participants then completed 100 trials of the Iowa Gambling Task (IGT), which is a commonly utilised measure of decision-making. Finally, the researchers examined the relationship between scores on the BFAS and performance on the IGT. The results showed a negative correlation between the BFAS score and performance in the IGT. This means the more addicted participants were to Facebook, the worse they did on the gambling task. The researcher concluded that extreme social media use is associated with worse decision-making skills (risky decision-making). Highlighting a negative influence of digital technology on cognitive processes.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThe use of the correlational method in this study allowed the researchers to quickly collect data about the long-term effects of Facebook without having to manipulate variables for an extended period of time. It also provided an ethical and practical alternative to manipulating how much time a person spends using social media. A limitation of this correlational study and correlational studies, in general, is that the researchers cannot control or manipulate variables, therefore, while the findings are interesting and not impacted by an artificial setting, no cause-and-effect relationship can be established between excessive Facebook use and diminished decision-making skills. As such bi-directional ambiguity is a significant issue. The researchers have no way of knowing whether it was pre-existing deficits in decision-making that led to excessive Facebook use or vice versa. Furthermore, there could have been additional variables that have not been accounted for that have impacted decision-making ability. Finally, data in this particular study was collected via self-reports which means that problems with honesty and memory may have negatively impacted the data collected.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nIn conclusion, true experiments and correlational studies are two different quantitative research methods that allow researchers to investigate the influence of technology on cognitive processing. A true experiment, which was utlised in Sparrow’s study, allow researchers to establish cause-and-effect relationships between variables, however, there are some concerns with ecological and predictive validity. On the other hand, correlational studies, as seen in Meshi’s research provide a practical and ethical alternative to manipulating variables, while benefiting from being higher in ecological validity.  However, they suffer from an inability to establish causation. Finally, both research methods can be impacted by the data collection techniques that are utilised in a specific research study. Which method is best for investigating the influence of technology on cognitive processing will depend on the scope, limits, and requirements of the research. It is worth keeping in mind that both research methods are somewhat limited by the fact that cognitive processes are not directly observable and researchers will need to make inferences from the findings obtained.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nWord count: 1151\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\nMeshi, D., Elizarova, A., Bender, A., & Verdejo-Garcia, A. (2019). Excessive social media users demonstrate impaired decision-making in the Iowa Gambling Task. Journal of Behavioral Addictions, 8(1), 169–173.  https://doi.org/10.1556/2006.7.2018.138 \n\nSparrow, B., Liu, J., & Wegner, D. M. (2011). Google effects on memory: Cognitive consequences of having information at our fingertips. Science, 333(6043)"},{"ref":"PS0683","marks":"22","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0683\n\nTo what extent does digital technology have a positive effect on cognitive processes? \n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nHumans are surrounded by and engage with technology daily. Given its extensive usage, it is important to have an understanding of how technology might influence cognitive processes. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ Cognitive processes are the mental processes that are essential for everyday tasks, such as memory, thinking, and decision-making. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ While it is imperative to know whether the use of technology is harming our cognitive processes, it is equally important to understand any potential positive effects. An understanding of the positive effects can open the door for us to harness and embrace any potential benefits. The extent to which digital technology has a positive effect on cognitive processes can be considered with reference to research studies conducted by Oh et al. (2017) and Sparrow et al. (2011). $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ \n\nA study conducted by Oh et al. (2017) aimed to examine the effectiveness of a smartphone app (SMART) designed to improve age-related memory concerns. 53 participants ranging in age from 50-68 were randomly assigned to either the SMART app intervention condition or the waitlist (control) condition. Participants in the intervention condition used the smartphone app 5 days a week, for 15-20 minutes each day, for a total of 8 weeks. The effectiveness of the app was assessed through the use of a series of objective, standardised measures to evaluate changes in working memory (WM) and attention. Participants also completed self-report surveys to assess their subjective memory complaints. All tests took place at the start of the study and again after 8 weeks. The results showed that while there was no significant difference in self-reported subjective memory complaints, the objective working memory scores of the smartphone intervention group increased significantly compared to the wait list (control condition). This study suggests that some forms of digital technology have the potential to have positive effects on cognitive processes. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThis study benefits from its experimental nature, where the researchers randomly allocated participants to conditions and manipulated an IV (use of the SMART App or No use of the App) in order to examine the effect on the DV (objective and subjective memory scores). However, as the study was conducted in natural, real-world settings, the researchers could not control all variables that might influence memory. Therefore, the causal relationships established may not be as strong as in a laboratory experiment. While this study supports the idea that digital technology can be used to enhance cognitive abilities such as memory, it is just one study that investigated a single smartphone app. We cannot be certain that all smartphone apps aimed at improving memory are effective or that the benefits were necessarily due to the use of technology specifically. Is it possible that the same types of activities in a non-digital format, would have been just as effective? Furthermore, the study did not investigate or measure any potential negative consequences associated with smartphone usage. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nIt is also worth investigating the positive and/or negative influences of other forms of digital technology on cognitive processes. A study conducted by Sparrow et al, (2011) aimed to investigate whether continual access to Google was having any impact on memory. The study used an independent groups design, and participants were asked to input recently learned trivia facts into a computer. The IV was what the participants were told would happen to the information they were typing. Some participants were told that the information would be saved and accessible later, and some were told that it would be erased. In each condition, half of the participants were explicitly asked to try and remember the information they were typing. Later, all participants were given a surprise recall test (DV). The results showed that those participants who believed the information would be erased had significantly better recall.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Being explicitly asked to remember the information had no impact on levels of recall. The researchers concluded that continuous access to search engines may impair recall due to the expectation of instant information availability. This has been referred to as the Google Effect or Digital Amnesia. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nThe use of a true experiment in this study allowed the researchers to randomly allocate participants to conditions and to manipulate two different independent variables. The first was whether participants were told the data would be saved or erased, and the other was whether they were explicitly asked to remember the information. They also tightly controlled other variables, such as ensuring the newly learned trivia facts were identical across each condition. This allowed the researchers to isolate the effects of the IV and establish a cause-and-effect relationship between reliance on digital technology (as a backup storage system) and memory performance. However, tightly controlling variables in this way means that the study was conducted in a highly artificial environment that only tested recall of trivia facts. This reduces ecological validity and mundane realism, meaning the findings may not accurately predict how our memory functions in complex, real-world settings. Use of the internet in real-world settings varies widely in terms of how it is used and for how long. This means that the scope of this particular study may be too narrow to generalise to all internet use. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nAt first glance, the robust findings of Sparrow’s study appear to suggest that digital technology, such as the internet, has a negative influence on cognitive processes. However, such conclusions need to be treated with caution. While it is clear from this study that reliance on internet access may mean that we are retaining less information, this may not necessarily be a completely negative thing. By relying on the internet as an external storage device, we may be able to free up important cognitive space for higher-order cognitive processes such as critical thinking. This could result in more efficient cognitive processing. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nIn conclusion, humans are surrounded by and engage with technology daily. Research conducted by Oh et al. (2007) suggests that some forms of digital technology (such as memory-enhancing smartphone apps) have the potential to positively affect cognitive processes. Research conducted by Sparrow suggests that reliance on digital technology, such as the internet, may negatively impact memory. However, this may also free up space for more efficient critical thinking. While both studies are interesting and helpful, they are limited in scope. As there are so many different types of technology being used in diverse ways, it is difficult to draw a strong conclusion as to the extent to which digital technology can have a positive influence on cognitive processes. It may be that the extent to which digital technology positively or negatively influences cognition may depend on the type of technology used, how it is used, and the individual who is using it. Therefore, it would be safe to conclude that technology positively influences cognitive processes to a moderate extent.  $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$\n\nWord count: 1101\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nOh, S. J., Seo, S., Lee, J. H., Song, M. J., & Shin, M.-S. (2017). Effects of smartphone-based memory training for older adults with subjective memory complaints: A randomized controlled trial. Aging & Mental Health, 22(4), 526-534. https://doi.org/10.1080/13607863.2016.1274373 \n\nSource: Sparrow, B., Liu, J., & Wegner, D. M. (2011). Google effects on memory: Cognitive consequences of having information at our fingertips. Science, 333(6043), 776-778. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1207745 \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Markscheme}}$:\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}}$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question is explained. The \n\nthe question uses the command term to what extent; therefore, the response should consider both sides of the argument (i.e., positive and negative influences of digital technology on cognitive processes) and draw a conclusion as to how much digital technology positively influences cognitive processes. While it is essential to address both sides of the argument, your consideration of positive and negative influences does not need to be evenly balanced. Focus on the question should be maintained throughout the response. \n\n**1 mark** if the question is not accurately explained, has limited explanation, or is simply restated (e.g., the essay with consider the positive effects of digital technology on cognitive processes). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer is not relevant to the question.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant and appropriate knowledge and terminology related to the effects of digital technology on cognitive processes are explained in detail without errors (e.g., “Cognitive processes are the mental processes that are essential for everyday tasks, such as memory, thinking, and decision making.$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$ Continuous access to search engines may impair recall due to the expectation of instant information availability. This is known as digital amnesia or the Google Effect).\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors but is lacking in some detail or shows only a brief explanation of the theory/topic (e.g., the Google Effect or digital amnesia are mentioned but not defined or they are defined but neither of the terms are explicitly identified).\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited or superficial in nature (e.g.,on if the response suggested that digital technology has negative or positive effects of cognitive processes, but no specific cognitive processes or digital technology is identified). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if no relevant terminology is explained or if the response describes and evaluates a model of memory instead of a model of thinking and decision-making.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments stated, and a specific link is made between the findings of the study(ies) and the question (i.e., it should be clear how the study links to our understanding of the positive effects of digital technology on cognitive processes). Relevant details, including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusions, from one or more studies, should be included. Studies that could be referred to include, but are not limited to:\n\n- An investigation on the effectiveness of a smartphone app (SMART) designed to improve age-related memory concerns: Oh et al. (2007)\n\n- A study on the Google Effect: Sparrow et al. (2011)\n\n- A study on the role of action video games to improved working memory: Blacker et al. (2014)\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study is explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors (e.g., the aim, procedure, results, and conclusions of the study are not presented, or there are some errors with regard to the relevance of the findings or details of the methodology).\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research is used with insufficient detail, errors, or superficial links to the question. No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer does not include any relevant studies.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer shows relevant, consistent, and well-developed critical thinking points. Critical thinking points could include a combination of the following: strengths and limitations of the research methods used in supporting research studies and their conclusions, potential applications of the findings of the studies, contradictory explanations, and/or an acknowledgment of complexities and alternative arguments. At least 2-3 discussion or critical thinking points are explained (e.g., The use of experimental method can be a strength as it allows for cause-and-effect to be established. Or, by relying on the internet as an external storage device, we may be able to free up important cognitive space for other cognitive processes.)\n\n**3-4 marks** if relevant critical thinking points are presented (as above), but they are not always fully developed. They may be too generic, or they may be limited in number. For example, a response might state that the study is low in internal validity without explaining what this means in reference to the specific study. Or, a response might state that the study is low in ecological validity without explaining why or how. At least 1-2 discussion or critical thinking points are explained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if critical thinking points are superficial or contain inaccuracies (e.g., evaluation points only stated but not explained properly). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer does not contain relevant critical thinking points, discussion, or evaluation. \n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}}$\n\n**2 marks** if the essay has an organised structure, including a clear introduction (which outlines the focus of the essay), body paragraphs (which outline the relevant study or studies as described in Criterion C; followed by evaluation of those studies), and concluding paragraphs or statements (which link back to the question by providing a conclusion as to the extent to which digital technology positively affects cognitive processes).\n\n**1 mark** if the organisation and clarity are not sustained throughout the answer (e.g., unfinished response, lack of a clear introduction, lack of body paragraphs, and/or lack of a conclusion; a structure that is not logical or easy to follow; or incomplete description of key terms and studies). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the response does not meet any of the standards above.\n\n[6 marks]"}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"PS0516","marks":"9","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0516\n\nDescribe one or more ethical considerations related to one research study on cognitive processing.\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9 marks** are awarded if the response includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question provided in the first few sentences *(e.g. “One example of an ethical consideration related to research on cognitive processing  is…”)*\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of ethical issues that are related to the cognitive approach\n\nImportant ethical issues to use and/or define include: \n\n- *$`$In psychology, ethics must be considered to ensure participants (humans and animals) are not harmed and that the research conducted is ethically valid.'*\n\n- *$`$Researchers should always conduct research in an ethical manner and studies should always be critically evaluated for ethical issues.'* \n\n- *$`$Ethical standards are made by the American Psychology Association (APA) that all research done in psychology must abide by.'*\n\nThese ethical considerations are: *the protection of participants from physical or psychological harm, consent, right to withdraw, confidentiality, deception, and debriefing.* \n\nThe command term $`$describe' is addressed. This requires giving a detailed account of one or more ethical considerations in relation to one research study linked to the cognitive approach to understanding behaviour. Ethical considerations can be positive (what guidelines could be followed) or negative (what guidelines were not followed). \n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nA clear focus on describing one study $\\text{--}$ including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study\n\n*Relevant studies include but are not limited to:*\n\n- $`$Genie' Curtiss (1981) - On social deprivations long term affects.\n\n- Clive Wearing Sacks (2007) - A case study on hippocampus damage\n\n- Blakemore & Cooper (1970) “Kitten carrousel” - Investigated the visual pursuit of moving objects.\n\n- Gardner and Gardner (1969) “Washoe” - Investigated if primates can learn a language\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link exploring the ethical considerations of the study in reference to the question (e.g. *$`$Protection of participants means participants should be protected from harm. Gene was protected from harm during the study, but once the study was concluded, Gene was sent to live in an adult foster home where she may have experienced feelings of abandonment and mental distress at the significant change of environment.'*)\n\nMarking limits:\n\n- Describing more than one study will mean marks are only given to the first study described.\n\n- Describing more than one ethical consideration mean marks are only given to the first ethical consideration described. (see sample below)\n\n- Describing a study but an ethical consideration is not addressed *(maximum 3 marks awarded)*\n\n- Addressing one or more ethical considerations but does not refer to an appropriate study *(maximum 4 marks awarded)*\n\n- Clear links between the ethical consideration and the study are needed to gain marks in the top mark band. *(If the link is only implicit a maximum of 6 marks will be awarded)*\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nEthics is the moral correctness of specific conduct - in this case, the moral correctness of animal and human research at the cognitive approach. Ethical considerations related to psychological research, which are also applied in the cognitive approach, are the following: protection of participants, consent, right to withdraw, confidentiality, deception, and debriefing. These ethical standards are made by the American Psychology Association (APA) and must be abided by. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$\n\nThe study of Genie was conducted by Curtis (1981). Genie was a young girl who had been deprived of normal exposure to language early in life. She had no apparent language skills when she was discovered at the age of 13. The researcher's aim was to investigate the sensitive period hypothesis as to whether there is a sensitive learning period (before puberty closed) during which language must be acquired to develop normally. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The researchers encouraged her to verbalize and socialise. They communicated with her, taught her sign language, and provided a caring environment for Genie, while collecting data on how her language ability developed. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThere were, however, ethical issues not abided by in this study. Participants should be protected from physical and mental harm and distress, such as humiliation, stress, and injury, and participants should not be forced to reveal personal information. Although, Genie was protected from harm during the study, when the researchers concluded the study, Genie was left to live in an adult foster home, and she may have experienced mental distress from the dramatic change in environment and carers and being abandoned by the researchers. She was also asked personal and dramatic questions, which may have caused her to relive some of her past trauma. Genie was also unable to give informed consent.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$  Genie could not be fully informed or consent to the study due to language restrictions and general cognitive functioning. Genie was not in a healthy state of mind to  comprehend the nature and aims of the research, thus, it was not possible to gain fully informed consent.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Genie would also have been unable to have the right to withdraw. She would not have been able to express any desire to withdraw from the study due to her language restrictions and low levels of cognitive functioning caused by years of deprivation.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ Alongside this, confidentiality was also breached. Although her identity was kept anonymous, as Genie is not her real name, her case was exposed to the world of psychology, and she was readily available in video footage. Genie was also unaware she was being studied (deception) and was not debriefed at the end of the study. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ \n\nIn addition to these ethical considerations, it is largely considered that the behaviour of the researcher was inappropriate, in that they had a very personal attachment and very close relationship with Genie.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ This is inappropriate for scientific research, and one of the guiding principles of the cognitive level of analysis is that mental processes can be studied scientifically.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ This leads to questions about the researchers' objectivity and aims for studying Genie. \n\nOverall, this study does not meet the ethical requirements necessary for research into cognitive approaches to understanding human behaviour and would probably not be approved by the ethics board of universities today. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link"},{"ref":"PS0517","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# PS0517\n\nDescribe how and why one research method is used in the cognitive approach, with reference to one study.\n\n[9 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA maximum of **9** marks are awarded if the answer includes all of the following:\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{1. Direct Answer}\\ [\\textbf{DA}]}$\\\n\nA direct answer to the question is provided in the first few sentences. The question uses the command term describe; therefore, the response should provide a detailed account or description in relation to a research study (*e.g., $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$One research method used in the cognitive approach is. . .$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$*). \n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{2. Key Terms}\\ [\\textbf{KT}]}$\\\n\nAccurate knowledge and understanding of key topics and supporting details are expressed in the answer. A minimum of 2 key terms should be used in the answer. Important concepts and vocabulary terms to use and/or define could include, but are not limited to:\n\n- Case study: an in-depth investigation of a single individual, family, event, or other entity; multiple types of data (psychological, physiological, biographical, environmental) are assembled, for example, to understand an individual's background, relationships, and behaviour; will usually collect qualitative, as well as, quantitative data\n\n- Dependent variable (DV): the variable in a laboratory experiment that the researcher predicts as the effect of the phenomenon\n\n- Empiricism: idea that research findings should be observable using the five senses\n\n- Independent variable (IV): the variable that researchers change in a laboratory experiment; what the researcher predicts as the cause of the phenomenon\n\n- Laboratory experiment: an experiment that uses a carefully controlled setting and standardised procedure to accurately measure how changes in the independent variable (IV) affect the dependent variable (DV) and establish causal relationships between the variables\n\n- Reliability: idea that if a study were to be replicated, similar results should be found; established by using a standardised procedure in lab experiments\n\n- Validity: idea that the investigation accurately measures what it intends to\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{3. Study}\\ [\\textbf{S}]}$\\\n\nThe answer includes a clear focus on describing one study, including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion of the study, where the study is linked to the topic. Relevant studies that could be referred to include, but are not limited to:\n\n- Leading questions' affect on memory: Lofus $\\&$ Palmer (1974)\n\n- Schema influence on perception: Bruner $\\&$ Mintern (1955)\n\n- Acoustic and semantic similarities on memory: Baddeley (1966)\n\n- Short-term memory (STM) maximum duration of 30 seconds: Peterson $\\&$ Peterson (1959)\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{4. Link}\\ [\\textbf{L}]}$\\\n\nAn explicit link connecting the results of the study to the topic is present (*e.g., $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$One of the guiding principles of the cognitive approach is that mental processes can be studied scientifically. The research method used in the cognitive approach that most reflects the scientific study of behaviour, like that of Loftus & Palmer's (1974) study is a laboratory experiment.$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$*)\n\nMarking Limits:\n\n- If the answer provides a clear explanation of one relevant study and explains key terms relevant to the topic without an explicit link between the result of the study and the topic in question, a maximum of 6 marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer focuses on explaining a relevant study only, without explaining the cognitive approach, a maximum of 5 marks will be awarded. \n\n- If the answer focuses on a study that is not relevant to the topic, a maximum of 4 marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer focuses only on describing the cognitive approach, with no mention of a relevant study, a maximum of 3 marks will be awarded. \n\n- If the answer describes an incorrect topic, no marks will be awarded.\n\n- If the answer describes two relevant studies, the marks will only be given on the first study mentioned.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nOne of the guiding principles of the cognitive approach is that mental processes can be studied scientifically. Perhaps the research method of the cognitive approach that most reflects this scientific study is the laboratory experiment. $\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ A laboratory experiment is a scientific procedure carried out with the goal of verifying, refuting, or establishing the validity of a hypothesis. All psychologists must work to accept their null hypothesis so as not to show bias in their research. Experiments are used to establish cause and effect by determining whether changes to one variable lead to changes in another. $\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$\n\nOne example of a laboratory experiment is Loftus & Palmer’s (1974) study on how leading questions may affect one's memory of an automobile crash.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The aim of the study was to determine whether the use of leading questions could affect recall in a situation in which participants were asked to estimate speed at the time of the crash.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ The researchers hypothesized that using questions with verbs associated with greater speed (e.g., smashed), in contrast with those associated with lesser speed (e.g., contacted), would result in greater estimates of speed at impact when participants were asked to recall the accident. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nIn order to standardize the procedure and control the environment, participants watched the same 7 videos (in which two cars hit one another), in the same conditions. The only change to the condition came when the participants were asked to estimate the speed of a car in miles per hour (the dependent variable, orDV). After each video, the participants described what they saw and answered a number of questions about it. The independent variable (IV) was a change of verb in the critical question: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$About how fast were the cars going when they _______ each other?$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$ There were 5 experimental conditions associated with the verbs used: contacted, hit, bumped into, collided with, and smashed into. The researchers used an independent sample design, so each participant only experienced one condition. Otherwise, there may have been participant bias.$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nBy using a laboratory experiment, Loftus & Palmer (1974) were able to have high levels of variable control. Only the verb was manipulated (smashed, hit, collided, etc.) and having an objective measurement unit increased the accuracy of the response. For example, miles per hour (mph) is a very standard measurement, in that 30 mph is less than 40 mph. This increases the objectivity of the results, meaning the accuracy of measurement can be tested independently from the researcher who first reported it. $\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$\n\nThis study is also easy to replicate, and this increases the reliability of the results. The more times an experiment can be repeated in different cognitive laboratories with different participant groups and still show robust cause-and-effect relationships in the results, the higher the validity of the cognitive theory it shows. For example, after years of experimental studies, it is now almost certain that the use of leading questions can affect the recall of an event.$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ However, there are certain problems with lab experiments having highly controlled environments, including artificiality and a lack of ecological validity (when the methods, materials, and settings of the study approximate the real world that is being examined). Watching a video of a car crash is far removed from the experience of observing one in a real-life situation. Because of this difference, we cannot be certain that the same effect would be obtained in real-life circumstances.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{DA}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Direct answer\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{L}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Link"},{"ref":"PS0612","marks":"22","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# PS0612\n\nDiscuss ethical considerations related to one or more studies that explore the reliability of cognitive processes.\n\n[22 marks]","markScheme":"$\\textcolor{#000000}{\\textbf{Criterion A: Focus on the question}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the issue or problem raised in the question is explained. The question uses the command term discuss; therefore, the response should offer a balanced review considering various factors, arguments, and hypotheses. The response can also explain why it is essential to evaluate ethical considerations that investigate the reliability of cognitive processes.\n\n**1 mark** if the question is not accurately explained, has limited explanation, or is simply restated *(e.g., This essay will evaluate two studies that pose ethical considerations when exploring the reliability of cognitive processing*). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer is not relevant to the question.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#F04438}{\\textbf{Criterion B: Knowledge \\& understanding}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if relevant and appropriate knowledge and terminology are explained in detail without errors and are explicitly linked to the studies in Criterion C (*e.g., protection, rights, undue stress or harm, consent, debrief, anonymity, right to withdraw*).\n\n**3-4 marks** if the relevant terminology is explained and has minimal errors but is lacking in some detail or shows only knowledge about research methods, such as a brief explanation of the topic with little focus on evaluation of study (*e.g., consent is the practice in which individuals agree they will be involved and understand the consequences of being involved: this is vital in psychological practice*).\n\n**1-2 marks** if the relevant terminology has errors and/or is limited or superficial in nature (*e.g., a study is explored, such as Loftus & Pickrell (1995), and the most prominent ethical considerations are not identified*), or f the answer only shows knowledge related to reliability of cognitive processing and does not explore studies that support it. No marks will be awarded for this criterion if no relevant terminology is explained. \n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#008c3a}{\\textbf{Criterion C: Use of research}}$\n\n**5-6 marks** if one or more relevant studies are explained and used to support the answer or the arguments stated, and a specific link is made between the findings of the study(ies) and the question. Relevant details, including the aim, procedure, results, and conclusion, from one or more studies, should be included. Studies that could be referred to include, but are not limited to:\n\n- Study on the effect of leading questions: Loftus & Palmer (1974)\n\n- Study on implanting false memories: Loftus & Pickrell (1995) \n\n- Study on memory suggestion in witnesses to a real crime scene: Yuille $\\&$ Cutshall (1986) \n\n- Study on false memories of committing a crime: Shaw & Porter (2015)\n\n**3-4 marks** if a relevant study is explained and used to develop arguments but with some errors (*e.g., the aim, procedure, results, and conclusions of the study are not presented, or there are some errors with regard to the relevance of the findings or details of the methodology*).\n\n**1-2 marks** if limited relevant research is used with insufficient detail, errors, or superficial links to the question (*e.g., the study is not linked to the evaluation points mentioned*). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer does not include any relevant studies.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{\\textbf{Criterion D: Critical thinking}\\ }$\n\n**5-6 marks** if the answer shows relevant, consistent, and well-developed critical thinking points on ethical consideration. Critical thinking points could include a combination of the following: strengths and limitations of the research methods used in supporting research studies and their conclusions, strengths and limitations of the relevant theories, potential applications of the findings, or an acknowledgment of complexities and alternative arguments. At least 2-3 discussion or critical thinking points are explained (*e.g., psychologists need to avoid harm on participants at all costs, therefore implanting false memories or making people question the reliability of their memory may have long-lasting effects that need to be considered carefully, etc.*)\n\n**3-4 marks** if relevant critical thinking points are presented (as above), but they are not always fully developed. They may not be directly linked to the specific study or theory, or they may be limited in number. For example, a response might state that the study is low in internal validity without explaining what this means in reference to the specific study. At least 1-2 discussion or critical thinking points are explained.\n\n**1-2 marks** if the evaluation points are superficial or contain inaccuracies  and mainly descriptive or just stated (*e.g., evaluation points only stated but not explained properly*). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the answer does not contain relevant critical thinking points, discussion, or evaluation.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\textcolor{#999999}{\\textbf{Criterion E: Clarity \\& organization}\\ }$\n\n**2 marks** if the essay has an organised structure, including a clear introduction (which outlines the focus of the essay), body paragraphs (which outline the relevant study or studies as described in Criterion C; followed by evaluation of those studies and how researchers incorporated ethical practices or how ethical considerations could have been made or made differently), and concluding paragraphs or statements (which link back to the question).\n\n**1 mark** if the organisation and clarity are not sustained throughout the answer (*e.g. unfinished response, lack of a clear introduction, lack of body paragraphs, and/or lack of a conclusion; a structure that is not logical or easy to follow; or incomplete description of key terms and studies*). No marks will be awarded for this criterion if the response does not meet any of the standards above.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\nA code of ethics is a set of moral principles that governs the field of psychology and helps to maintain high standards of research and integrity for the discipline.$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$. Carrying out research on humans that could potentially help a vast majority of people has its risks; testing humans can result in the harm of those individuals; and psychological discoveries, although well-meaning, can have negative consequences.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. That is why it is the duty of any practising psychologist for ethical considerations to be at the forefront of any study; before, during, and afterwards. Researchers need to make sure that participants give consent, are kept anonymous, will be free from undue stress or harm, can withdraw at any time, and are fully debriefed about the purpose of the experiment and how the data will be used when the experiment is finished. $\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$. It is vitally important to adhere to ethical codes, such as those established by the British Psychological Society or the American Psychological Association, to ensure that psychology as a discipline maintains its high standards and protects participants. This is especially important in studies that might lead to someone questioning the reliability of their memory, such as in Loftus $\\&$ Pickrell (1995), or studies that cause the psychological stress of reliving trauma, as in Yuille $\\&$ Cutshall (1986).$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$When experiences such as these can have long-lasting effects on participants, ethical considerations should be weighed against the benefits of the study.\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. \n\nYuille $\\&$ Cutshall (1986) aimed to study whether leading questions would affect the memory of eyewitnesses to a real crime scene.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. In this experiment, 13 eyewitnesses to a violent robbery were interviewed using two leading questions. Half of the participants were asked if they saw $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$\n\na broken headlight,$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$\n\nand the other half were asked if they saw $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$\n\nthe broken headlight$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$ on the getaway car. Additionally, the same question was asked about the car's $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$\n\nyellow panel.$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$ The getaway car had neither of these features. The participants were also asked to rate their stress on the day of the event. The results of this study demonstrated that participants' corroboration with original accounts given in police reports was between 79$\\%$ and 84$\\%$. $\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. Eyewitnesses during this real-life event were therefore highly reliable. We can therefore conclude that emotional response to the event may have caused vividness in memory recall, increasing resistance to distortion.\n\nThe fact that recall was accurate and less open to distortion after the event is evidence that the emotions surrounding being witness to this crime were fairly strong , and perhaps indicate the amount of times the participants had thought about it since., $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. This in itself is an indication of the shock, or even trauma, of this experience. Care needs to be taken in studies to ensure that painful memories, or traumatic experiences, are not brought to the surface for participants to relive.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. If it is vital to the study that this happens, then care should be taken in the debrief to make sure the participants are supported after the fact.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. dvice should be given on which agencies, or people of contact, to refer to in moments of stress after the study. Concerningly, participants in Yuille $\\&$ Cutshall (1986) were not aware of the true nature of the experiment, to protect the study's aim. In order to ensure this protection, the participants could not have been aware of the true risks or consequences of becoming involved.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. While omitting some details from participants is vital to protect results from demand characteristics, the benefits of doing so should not be at the expense of deception when participants' harm from involvement is a potential risk. $\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$.\n\nLoftus $\\&$ Pickrell (1995) aimed to study whether false autobiographical memories could be implanted in people.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. 3 males and 21 females agreed to take part in the study and, in its initial stages, relatives were contacted and asked to recount childhood memories of the participants. The participants received a questionnaire in the mail and were asked to write about 4 childhood memories: 3 real ones and 1 fabricated time when they were $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$\n\nlost in the mall$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$\n\nAfter that, the participants were interviewed 2 times over a period of 4 weeks. They were asked to recall as much as they could about these 4 events and rate their confidence about each memory on a scale of 1-10. The participants were debriefed afterwards and asked whether they identified the false memory. Around 25$\\%$ of the participants recalled the false memory, however, they also reported feeling less confident about this memory.$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$. While there were low levels of people who actually recalled the memory, the effect on those who were open to suggestion could be feelings of shame, or worse, cause them to question their cognitive capabilities.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nIn the above study, there is clear deception of the participants: they would not have been told the true aim of the experiment and would not have been properly informed about the procedure beforehand so that the researchers could prevent demand characteristics.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. While the integrity of the data is very important, this study, more so than Yuille $\\&$ Cutshall's, had the potential to harm participants.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$. Those who did succumb to the false memory might become alarmed about how easily influenced they had been, and may have begun to worry about the lack of robustness of the memories they had from their own childhoods.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ It is a worrying prospect that with a bit of encouragement from those in positions of trust and responsibility, in this case the researchers, our own sense of our past can become distorted. Researchers did note that some of those who recalled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$\n\nbeing lost in the mall$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$\n\nmay have done so because of a real memory they had or as an extension of an early memory they might have had.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ The propensity for harm to the participants needs to be carefully considered when embarking upon a study of this nature. The conclusions of this study are important to note for fields like counselling, psychology, and law enforcement, so the study is valuable. However, no study should come at the expense of participant safety.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ \n\nIt can be seen through the exploration of both experiments that there exists an ethical dilemma in psychological research: true scientific experimentation is sometimes at odds with ethical treatment of participants.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ However, integrity in the discipline is vital for public trust. If there was no trust in psychology, its practitioners, and the research, then the discipline would become obsolete.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ Participant safety and comfort must come first, while weighing how best to preserve reliable data through methodological practices.$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$\n\nWord Count: 1087\n\n$\\textcolor{#000000}{[\\textbf{F}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Focus on the question\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#C34A2C}{[\\textbf{KT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Key terms\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#056608}{[\\textbf{S}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Study\\\n\n$\\textcolor{#4472C4}{[\\textbf{CT}]}$ $\\rightarrow$ Critical Thinking\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Reference/s}}$:\\\n\nLoftus, E. F., $\\&$ Pickrell, J. E. (1995). The formation of false memories. Psychiatric Annals, 25(12), 720-725. https://doi.org/10.3928/0048-5713-19951201-07\n\nYuille, J. C., $\\&$ Cutshall, J. L. (1986). A case study of eyewitness memory of a crime. Journal of Applied Psychology, 71(2), 291-301. https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.71.2.291"}]}]}]};
